{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"You are a Flint ingest atomizer. Task: atomize *Supertraining* (Siff and Verkhoshansky, 6th Expanded Edition) Chapter 6, batch 6d: sections 6.12 \"Principles of Programming and Organising Training\" (6.12.1-6.12.3), 6.13 \"Primary Aims in Programming Training\", 6.14 \"Models for Structuring Annual Training\" (6.14.1 Explosive Strength sports, 6.14.2 Medium Duration Endurance, 6.14.3 Long Duration Endurance, 6.14.4 Tricyclic Periodisation, 6.14.5 Practical Principles of Programming), 6.15 \"A Sequence for Programming Annual Training\", 6.16 \"Managing the Training Process\" and 6.17 \"The Future of Programming Training\", PDF idx 379-403 (printed 368-392). Stop at the end of Chapter 6 (Chapter 7 starts at PDF 404). Three other atomizers are working at the same time on 6.1-6.3 (PDF 324-346), 6.4-6.8 (PDF 347-362) and 6.9-6.11 (PDF 363-378); do not atomize their pages. The 6.14.1 explosive-strength model matters most for Eric's football work: be especially careful with every number and figure there.\n\n## Read first, in full\n1. `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md` (every rule is checked by a reviewer; Windows paths in it, use the Mac translations below).\n2. `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/ingest-book/SKILL.md` (schema, frontmatter keys, file kinds).\n3. `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/method-baseline.md` (Eric's approved baseline; quote lines verbatim with line number AND tag; it has positions on the football year, in-season dose, and heavy work ending 72 h before kickoff).\n4. `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md` (map; page key PDF idx = printed + 11, 0-based).\n5. The Chapter 5 reviews, to avoid their errors: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md`, `..._ch5b_opus.md`, `..._ch5c_opus.md` and their `_round2` versions. Common misses: unlabelled writer judgments, \"contested\" set for things that are not real contradictions, misread figure values, baseline quotes without tags, invented examples.\n6. Two existing entries in the output folder as style references.\n\n## Mac paths\n- Vault library (read-only, dedup greps): `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`\n- Staging library: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library`\n- Output folder: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`\n- Source PDF (noisy text layer): `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`\n- Text: `~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"import pymupdf; d=pymupdf.open('<pdf>'); print(d[N].get_text())\"`. Render every page with a figure, table or number to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_renders/6d_pN.png` (`d[N].get_pixmap(dpi=200).save(...)`) and open it with Read. Write \"about\" for values read off a graph. Only claim \"verified against the page image\" if you opened it.\n\n## Hard rules\n- Add NEW entry files only. Do NOT edit any existing entry and do NOT edit `_book.md`. Dedup against vault and staging: annual planning, block models and concentrated loading are covered by Issurin (block periodization, ATR, Issurin 2016 critique of Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model), Bompa, Bondarchuk and Supertraining Ch5 (5.3.3 conjugate sequence, `framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading`, `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next`). Add an entry only for a genuinely new angle and link the existing one in Related; where another library source disagrees, record it and set `contested` with the named source. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your report instead of editing other files.\n- Use the source's own examples, otherwise start the Example with \"Inference:\". No mechanism the authors do not give. `contested` only for a real contradiction or a named library source that disagrees; give both pages. Locators \"PDF p. X / printed p. Y\" on every number, definition and quote. \"The authors\" unless a passage clearly belongs to one author.\n- Frontmatter `chapter:` uses the section number. `created: 2026-10-03`. No em dashes in titles or body (section headers keep `**What it is** —`). No review history. Never wrap a [[wikilink]] across lines.\n- Quality over coverage; if 25 pages are too many, stop at a clean section boundary and say exactly where.\n- Write a scope file `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6d.txt` listing your new filenames, one per line.\n- Lint: `cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky`; fix anything flagged in YOUR files and paste the lines for your files.\n- No git. No vault edits.\n\n## Final reply\nEntries by kind and confidence; entry count per section with printed and PDF page ranges (for `_book.md`); where you stopped if not at PDF 403; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots; anything you could not read; lint output.\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \"tool not found\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\n    ToolSearch(query: \"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\")\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, ToolSearch it and retry — do NOT fall back to Bash/Read just because the schema was not loaded yet.\n  </deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n</context_window_protection>"},"uuid":"1bf27951-5d8d-4b35-86ab-d9f44f33432e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:06:57.224Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
