supplied physiology the book never states. Label it \"Inference:\" or leave it out.\n- **Do not add \"well established\" or \"modern research shows\" without a citation.** Name the paper, or delete the phrase.\n\n## Lessons from the 2026-09-27 review wave (Supertraining Ch2-3, Issurin Ch3, Bondarchuk, Evans, Old Bull)\n\n- **Invented Examples are the most common failure.** 34 of 36 Supertraining Ch2-3a Examples were unlabelled inventions. Use the source's own example where it gives one; otherwise start the Example with \"Inference:\".\n- **\"Verified against the page image\" means you opened the image.** Four Issurin Ch3 entries claimed it for figures they had misread (Fig 3.7 inverted, a Table 3.7 row swapped). Sparse slides are not \"bare diagrams\" until you have looked.\n- **Name the population and method the source gives.** Bondarchuk's data are 7,000+ native and foreign athletes, questionnaires, r >= 0.349, steroid-free; entries that said \"Soviet athletes\" were wrong everywhere.\n- **Check what a cited paper actually says.** Issurin 2016's critique targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R; Herzog & Leonard 2000 shows force enhancement, against the book's sentence. When the book misreports a study, record both.\n- **No review history in entries.** \"Prior version\", \"corrected from a NO-GO finding\" and similar belong in `staging/fixes_*_applied.md`, never in the entry body.\n- **Fix logs must match the files.** One fixer log claimed fixes that were not made. Fixers run their own grep checks and paste the output.\n- **Section headers use the vault form** `**What it is** —` (with the em dash). Do not add suffixes such as \": mandatory\". Em dashes are banned only in titles and body text. Never wrap a `[[wikilink]]` across lines.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Every number, definition and quote carries a checkable locator: PDF page (and printed page if it differs), or file plus timestamp. Never write \"confirmed in PDF\" unless you opened that page.\n- Never invent a number. Keep ambiguity visible: mark unclear audio or OCR as unclear, and do not harmonize two conflicting numbers from the source; record both with their locators.\n- OCR sources (`staging/ocr/*.md`): pages listed as low_confidence in the header must be checked against the page image before any number from them is cited. Use the Read tool on the PDF page, or render it.\n- **OCR confidence scores miss the worst errors** (checked 2026-09-27). Zatsiorsky 2e: the OCR interleaves the two columns and the sidebars line by line, so its text is not readable prose; use it only to find content, and read and quote from the page image. *CFTS*: single-column prose that mostly reads in order, but upside-down lines come out as gibberish, some lines are dropped, and figures are scrambled, all at \"0 low-confidence pages\". For any OCR source, quote only after checking the page image, and read every figure and table from the image.\n\n## Confidence\n\n- Practitioner courses and lectures: ceiling `supported`.\n- Books: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics. Soviet-era or single-lab empirical findings and training prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for single cases and anecdotes. `contested` where another library source or modern evidence disagrees (say which).\n\n## Dedup and converge\n\nBefore each entry, grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library` and the staging library (`C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\staging\\library`) for the concept. If it is covered, add a new entry only for a genuinely new angle, and link the existing one in Related. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your final report. Never edit files in the vault or another agent's staging folder.\n\n## Baylor context\n\nRead `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\method-baseline.md` (approved). Use it in When to apply, label