{"parentUuid":"c39e1808-b86d-48af-8c22-6af5881733e9","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffkPm6WPc1nuuLALCxM6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0125dU37YCHDZkGi1NDYwZpn","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs; sed -n 5,40p method-baseline.md | cut -c1-600; echo; grep -n \"^#\" method-baseline.md","description":"Read baseline provenance and headers"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3878,"cache_read_input_tokens":58479,"output_tokens":192,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":48},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3878},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":192,"cache_read_input_tokens":58479,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3878,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3878,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0125dU37YCHDZkGi1NDYwZpn":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs; sed -n 5,40p method-baseline.md | cut -c1-600; echo; grep -n \"^#\" method-baseline.md","description":"Read baseline provenance and headers"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffkPkoPR6PPAn8JPsaDo","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"ef8e3d52-3802-487f-a918-72eda9622fb1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:07:03.944Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ef8e3d52-3802-487f-a918-72eda9622fb1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0125dU37YCHDZkGi1NDYwZpn","type":"tool_result","content":"## Provenance note (read this before the bullets)\n\nNot all 23 documents are Eric's own words, and the two richest sources (the class transcripts) have a diarization problem. Read this once, then use these tags:\n\n- **Eric-authored, high confidence**: the two SportSmith articles, the Performance Playbook Development Plan, the Force Plate Testing V3 and Iso Seated Calf protocols, the Applied Performance Playbook (departmental document under his unit), and the HD ODSF metrics note (adopted practice, though the framework itself is Hawkin Dynamics' own).\n- **Course slide decks (Week 1, Week 3, Class 4)**: co-branded to Ruf/Rash/Wenger on every title slide. Per `spring-2026-teaching.md`, Eric was the assigned instructor for the Feb. 5 and Feb. 12 sessions (Week 3 Lecture and Class 4 2026 respectively), so bullets from those two decks are marked **Explicit**. Week 1 (Jan. 22, \"All\") and Class 8/Lesson 8 (Mar. 19, Wenger's session) are marked **Inferred** — shared team material, not confirmed as Eric's individual position.\n- **Both class transcripts (Class 1, Class 3) are diarized entirely under one Fireflies speaker label, \"Chris Ruf,\" for every line, across 100+ minutes combined.** This is true even for Class 3 (Feb. 5), which the syllabus assigns solo to Rash. Fireflies likely defaulted to the meeting organizer's name rather than doing real per-speaker diarization. That means specific sentences in these transcripts cannot be confidently attributed to Rash vs. Ruf vs. Wenger. Bullets drawn from the transcripts are marked **Inferred**, with a citation note, even where the statement itself is a direct, confident\n  - **Confirmed by Eric, 2026-09-26:** HHPR 5355 is taught each spring by Chris Ruf, Eric and Mike Wenger. The lecture material is split among the three of them. This confirms the course is partly Eric's, but it cannot say who spoke a given transcript line. So transcript bullets stay tagged Inferred and read as **course team positions**. Each one becomes Eric's own only when he claims it, in review or in the interview.\n- **Reference material, not Eric's position**: `intencurriculumn.md` is Jason Avedesian's (Clemson) authored curriculum end to end, used by the Development Plan as a benchmark, not as something Eric wrote or necessarily agrees with. The two Unit Goal Programming Standards documents are explicitly staff templates filled in (thinly) by Geyer and Skodinski, not Eric. The AP Assessment Manual V12 and the TechnologyTemplateSOP (TMG) have uncertain/likely-legacy authorship (see Tensions below) and are marked accordingly.\n\nSections below use **Explicit** (the source states Eric holds this position, with confidence in authorship per the tiers above) and **Inferred** (implied by protocol design, shared course material, or transcript content with unconfirmed speaker attribution).\n\nTags added 2026-09-28 from the method interview:\n\n- **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Qn)**: Eric stated or claimed the position in the interview. His words are quoted as \"Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Qn):\". Q1 claimed exactly four course positions (Vermeil hierarchy, undulating periodization, Dynamic Correspondence, the football energy-system priority stack); other course material stays Inferred.\n- **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Qn)**: Eric said the source or practice is not current. The old content is kept and marked, not deleted, so a synthesis note can see what changed.\n- **Superseded by interview (Qn)**: a documented position, sometimes from Eric's own slides, that his interview answer replaces.\n\n---\n\n## Philosophy and decision principles\n\n- Applied Performance's stated mission is to \"drive elite performance ... by leveraging science and technology through education and relationships,\" enabling the high-performance team to answer questions, ask better questions, and discover new ones — not to hand down answers unilaterally. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]\n- The department's four operating principles are Athlete Monitoring and Analysis, Athlete Evaluation and Return-to-Performance, Performance Team Collaboration, and Research and Development. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]\n- Institutional framing: \"Prepare Champions for Life\" across four pillars (academic, athletic, social, spiritual), operationalized through six \"One Standard, One Accord\" principles. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md pp.3-4]\n- Data collection is judged on cost versus insight: \"Data collection has a cost in time, athlete compliance, and staff attention. When the insight stops justifying that cost, it's time to stop collecting.\" **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n- A test can be scientifically valid and still be the wrong choice operationally: \"The lesson wasn't about the test. It was about the infrastructure required to make a test useful ... even a valid test stops being valuable\" without staff ownership and consistent collection. **Explicit** [2026-02-20-sportsmith-6otb-nordbord-er.md]\n- General rule for any test or technology, stated as a standing rule rather than a fresh call each time. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q3): \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)**\n- The coach's eye and objective data work in sequence: the eye flags, data investigates. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q6): \"There is a place for the coach's eye: when something looks off, it can direct you where to look and gather data to investigate further.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q6)**\n- \"Inconsistent data from a good test is still bad data\" — a test's validity doesn't rescue it if it can't be collected consistently at scale. **Explicit** [2026-02-20-sportsmith-6otb-nordbord-er.md]\n- Reject ownership language (\"my team,\" \"my athletes\"): the athlete's development is shared across every department that touches them, not owned by any one coach. **Inferred (transcript, speaker unconfirmed)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.5, \"we got to get away from like the me and my\"]\n- Attribution in practice: outside frameworks are absorbed into the team's own way of working rather than credited to one person. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q27): \"We fold it into the mantra of 'this is how we do our business.'\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q27)** Inference: this is how Eric applies the \"we/our\" bullet above, so that bullet reads as practice, though the transcript line itself is still unclaimed.\n- The \"five expectations\" culture rules (Golden Rule, Be Disciplined, Be Relentless, Communicate, No Excuses) were presented in the course as absolute [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.1-2]. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q26): \"These are more a relic of another coaching era that has held over. I would say none [no deliberate exception], but they are rarely mentioned around here any more.\" **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q26)**\n- Injury risk can be reduced, not eliminated, through sound programming, progression, and collaboration with Athletic Medicine — an explicit refusal to promise injury prevention. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.4]\n- Be skeptical of social-media-driven training trends: a viral clip is \"just a brief snapshot,\" stripped of the context that would tell you whether it's sound. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.3]\n- Recovery has to be earned by training hard first; a culture of fear grew out of early GPS/load-monitoring work that pushed teams toward under-training in the name of \"recovery.\" **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.9-10]\n\n1:# Eric's method baseline (draft for Eric's review)\n5:## Provenance note (read this before the bullets)\n25:## Philosophy and decision principles\n43:## Department model and roles\n54:## Testing and assessment\n71:## Force plate and neuromuscular monitoring\n82:## Load monitoring and GPS\n91:## Speed and sprint\n99:## Strength and power\n111:## Conditioning and energy systems\n127:## Rehab and return to play\n138:## Injury risk and prevention\n149:## Recovery and sleep\n157:## Nutrition and body composition\n167:## Coaching, skill acquisition and constraints-led approach\n177:## Periodization and programming\n190:## Data, technology and reporting\n200:## Education and staff development\n209:## No material found\n217:## Tensions and contradictions\n267:## Named influences","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"61610084-e8ec-4595-b73b-db14144a15ae","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:07:04.143Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"## Provenance note (read this before the bullets)\n\nNot all 23 documents are Eric's own words, and the two richest sources (the class transcripts) have a diarization problem. Read this once, then use these tags:\n\n- **Eric-authored, high confidence**: the two SportSmith articles, the Performance Playbook Development Plan, the Force Plate Testing V3 and Iso Seated Calf protocols, the Applied Performance Playbook (departmental document under his unit), and the HD ODSF metrics note (adopted practice, though the framework itself is Hawkin Dynamics' own).\n- **Course slide decks (Week 1, Week 3, Class 4)**: co-branded to Ruf/Rash/Wenger on every title slide. Per `spring-2026-teaching.md`, Eric was the assigned instructor for the Feb. 5 and Feb. 12 sessions (Week 3 Lecture and Class 4 2026 respectively), so bullets from those two decks are marked **Explicit**. Week 1 (Jan. 22, \"All\") and Class 8/Lesson 8 (Mar. 19, Wenger's session) are marked **Inferred** — shared team material, not confirmed as Eric's individual position.\n- **Both c