{"parentUuid":"763a6b92-9425-4095-a850-1b6a97cf686f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012k8ok5ynLRzUnGEE4DtBAG","type":"tool_result","content":"## Strength and power\n\n- The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next) is repeatedly described as the department's programming \"compass.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript + shared course material) [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.9-10; week1-lecture.md pp.23,26]\n- Exercise selection is organized on a spectrum from general preparation through specific preparation, specific development, to competitive exercises (Bondarchuk), replacing the loose and often-misapplied idea of \"sport specific.\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.13-16; week1-lecture.md pp.32-36]\n- The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript + slides) [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.14; week1-lecture.md p.34]\n- Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints); single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.13; week1-lecture.md p.31]\n- Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work. In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule. **Inferred (transcript + shared slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.20-21; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.21] Not claimed in the interview. The in-season dose itself is unknown: Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q18): \"I don't have the specific sets and reps.\"\n- Undulating/non-linear periodization (Parker/Miller/Panariello), waving volume week to week (e.g., medium-high, medium, high, low over four weeks) is stated as the personally preferred model over strict linear progression, specifically to avoid staleness while still building tolerance for higher loads. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript, speaker unconfirmed but first-person: \"For me personally, this is a more preferential model to use\") [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.24-26]\n- Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block, used consistently across strength and power teaching. **Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.19; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.15] Inference: with no true 1RM testing (Q10), any %1RM prescription rests on projected maxes. Kept on that explicit basis (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02).\n- Strength's residual training effect is treated as roughly 30 days; a minimal retention dose is still trained within that window, even during lower-strength-emphasis blocks. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.24-25] Inference: consistent with Eric siding with Issurin (Q16), whose strength residual is 30 ± 5 days, though Q16 was about speed and he did not claim this bullet.\n- Tracy Fober's maxim (\"use a barbell like a chisel, not a sledgehammer\") is adopted as a guiding standard: prioritize movement quality and confidence-through-competence over chasing hypertrophy or 1RM numbers, especially early in a training cycle. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.21; week1-lecture.md p.46]\n\n## Conditioning and energy systems\n\n- Conditioning is defined two ways: (1) how well an athlete can meet the energy requirements of their specific sport/activity, and (2) how well they sustain strength, speed, and power output across a full competition. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md p.22]\n- All three energy systems (alactic ~10-12s highest power, glycolytic/anaerobic-lactic ~60-90s medium capacity, aerobic hours-long lowest power/highest capacity) are always active simultaneously; programming targets the adaptation, not the drill label. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md p.19; class-4-2026.md p.15]\n- A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once — priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands (e.g., for football: prepared for fall camp first, then the first game). **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md p.31]\n- For football specifically, the stated priority stack is: Goal 1, alactic and aerobic capacity/power for fall-camp demands; Goal 2, the same two systems for game demands; glycolytic conditioning is treated as a \"worst case scenario\" to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target. **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md pp.34, 39]; claimed again in the interview, **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**\n- Glycolytic conditioning is still not a primary target in season, but staff do program it in the off-season. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q19): \"It does hold up, but our staff does program it in the off-season.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)**\n- Anaerobic (lactate) threshold heart-rate zones are assigned by athlete body size rather than a single number: roughly 165 bpm for big/tall athletes, 170 for medium, 175 for small, with Zone 1-4 bands set as percentages of that individual threshold. **Superseded by interview (Q20)**; was Explicit [class-4-2026.md pp.28-29]\n- The anaerobic-threshold heart rate in use is the age-based method (the slide deck's max HR = 220-age for men, 226-age for women, threshold at about 85% of that [class-4-2026.md p.27]). Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q20): \"Age-based.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q20)** Resolves Tension 6.\n- The traits easiest to build (aerobic) are lost fastest under detraining; the traits hardest to build (alactic/speed) are lost slowest; this ordering is used to sequence what needs the most frequent re-exposure. **Superseded by interview (Q16)**; was Explicit [class-4-2026.md p.47] Eric now sides with Issurin (speed fades fastest). See Tension 11.\n- Training intensities between roughly 75-90% of maximal effort are labeled (via Francis) \"no man's land\" — too fast to build alactic power cleanly, too slow to build real aerobic capacity — and are treated as a zone to generally avoid as a primary training target. **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md p.48]\n- \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\": lifting, agility work, and skill drills each load a specific energy system (power vs. capacity, alactic/glycolytic/aerobic) and should be classified and counted as conditioning, not treated as separate from it. **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md p.31, tables pp.35-42]\n- For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery (regenerating ATP between bouts), not distance capacity — a better-developed aerobic system means more available energy on play 10, 11, or 12 of a drive, not longer continuous running ability. **Inferred (transcript)** [2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md p.9]\n- Aerobic-system training is kept below anaerobic threshold with roughly a 1:1 to 1:2 work:rest ratio and a general speed ceiling around 4.5 m/s, specifically to avoid spilling over into glycolytic-system stress. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md p.51; 2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.11-12]\n- Joel Jamieson's *Ultimate MMA Conditioning* is recommended as the single best practical resource for energy-system development, on the basis that its methods transfer cleanly to non-combat sports despite the MMA framing. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md pp.49-50; 2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.11-12]\n\n----\n## Periodization and programming\n\n- Macro/meso/microcycle structure is used as the standard planning language, with an explicit reminder that a \"training week\" does not have to equal 7 calendar days — it can be extended to fit training themes around a real competition schedule. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.17-18; week1-lecture.md p.39]\n- The preparatory period is sequenced hypertrophy/work-capacity, then basic strength, then strength-to-power, each with defined intensity/volume bands (e.g., hypertrophy phase 50-75% intensity, 3-6 sets of 5-20 reps; strength phase 80-90% intensity, moderate volume), before a transition into competition. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.19-21; week1-lecture.md pp.43-48]\n- In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.21]\n- Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block — \"you can't ride two horses with one saddle.\" Compatible qualities are trained together; the rest are trained at a retention dose. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.28-29; week1-lecture.md p.70]\n- An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract: lay out the competition calendar, key non-training dates (holidays, exam weeks), and phase-by-phase volume/intensity waves, then adapt the route as circumstances change while still reaching the same destination. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.22-23; 2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.13-15]\n- Training residual effects set the retraining cadence: aerobic capacity and max strength both need re-exposure roughly every 30 days; speed needs re-exposure roughly every 5 days or tissue readiness for max-velocity work degrades. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.27] Inference: these are Issurin's figures, and Eric sides with Issurin (interview 2026-09-28, Q16), so the speed half reads as his; the aerobic and strength halves were not asked directly.\n- In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q14): \"in football you want to peak every Saturday, but definitely throughout the month of November as you get closer to the postseason ... That's when players need to be at or near their peak as they head into the postseason and playoffs.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)** He did not say whether the postseason gets a distinct taper.\n- The in-season game week: Sunday off; Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday; lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**\n- Bye weeks: three practice days, extra rest and recovery, slightly higher practice volume than a game week, a speed and strength top-up, and harder work for players with few game snaps. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q17): \"guys that aren't getting a ton of play will probably practice harder that week than guys that have gotten a ton of snaps.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)**\n- The head coach's system of play and roster identity (e.g., a \"home run hitters\" lineup vs. a \"steal bases\" lineup) is treated as a direct input into whether a team's programming leans toward maximal strength or toward speed/power emphasis. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.16]\n\n----\n## Tensions and contradictions\n\nStatus after the interview (2026-09-28): resolved 1 (partly), 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 9; still open 7 (partly), 8, 10. New tensions from the interview are 11 to 16. Resolved by Eric 2026-10-02: 12, 16 and 18 (COD/FMS gates). Each entry below carries its status on its last line.\n\n1. **The task's own premise doesn't fully hold.** Both class transcripts (Class 1 and Class 3) are diarized entirely under a single Fireflies speaker label, \"Chris Ruf,\" for every line — including Class 3, which the Spring 2026 teaching schedule assigns solo to Rash (`spring-2026-teaching.md`: \"Feb. 5 | Conditioning - Alactic-Aerobic | Rash\"). Fireflies likely defaulted to the meeting organizer's name instead of doing real per-speaker diarization. This means roughly 30,000 words of the richest, most first-person material in this corpus cannot be confidently attributed to Rash specifically versus Ruf or Wenger. Every transcript-sourced bullet above is marked Inferred for this reason, even whe\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): partly resolved.** Q1 claimed four positions (Vermeil hierarchy, undulating periodization, Dynamic Correspondence, the football energy-system stack), now Explicit. Every other transcript bullet stays Inferred.\n\n2. **Department reporting model is not internally consistent.** The Playbook (p.6) frames Applied Performance as a peer discipline inside Health & Wellness that \"enables\" Athletics Performance, Athletic Medicine, Nutrition, and Mental Health to answer their own questions. But Week 1 course material lists \"Applied Performance\" as one of many training sub-categories nested under Athletic Performance itself (p.11), and the same transcript narration describes Rash's role as \"helping program and plan the training\" — a support-staff framing, not a peer-department framing. Which model actually governs day-to-day authority is not reconciled anywhere in these documents.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric (Q5): \"Both.\" Both framings are true at once.\n\n3. **Coach's-eye-first versus objective-first is argued both ways.** The course material repeatedly argues that \"you don't need a fancy test... you have to have a good coach's eye\" and criticizes the GPS/load-monitoring era for breeding a fear-driven under-training culture. Eric's own SportSmith writing moves the opposite direction on daily monitoring specifically: he replaced subjective wellness surveys with objective force-plate mRSI because subjective self-report couldn't be trusted. Both share a skepticism of trendy or convenient data, but they land on opposite defaults (trust the trained eye vs. require an objective number) for the daily-monitoring question specifically.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric (Q6): the coach's eye flags where to look; data then investigates. The two are sequential, not rival defaults.\n\n4. **The Assessment Manual (V12) reads as an older document than the current technology stack.** It builds its 1RM-estimation protocol around GymAware, and its CMJ description references a \"contact mat\" for jump height. The current Playbook technology stack and the Development Plan both center velocity-based training on Perch, not GymAware, and the current Force Plate Testing V3 protocol runs all CMJ variants exclusively on ForceDecks force plates, with no contact mat mentioned. This suggests the Assessment Manual predates the current VALD/Perch-centered stack and may be a legacy document rather than the currently operative protocol — worth confirming directly with Eric before treating any o\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric (Q8): V12 \"is not current\"; a new manual built on the NCAA technology and sport-science recommendations is in development. All V12 bullets are marked Legacy.\n\n5. **Testing cueing instructions conflict across two protocol documents for the same test.** The Assessment Manual's CMJ instructions say explicitly: \"Do not coach the strategy of jumping.\" The current Force Plate Testing V3 protocol gives testers specific verbal cues to give athletes during CMJ, squat-jump, and hop-test administration (\"keep chest up,\" \"land softly,\" \"land stiff-legged on only the toes\"). These may be reconcilable (safety/technique cues vs. jump-strategy cues are different things), but the two documents read differently on how much a tester should say out loud.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved by Q8.** Inference: with V12 not current, the Force Plate Testing V3 cues govern. Eric did not address the cueing question itself.\n\n6. **Two different anaerobic-threshold heart-rate methods appear back to back in the same slide deck without being reconciled.** One slide gives an age-based formula (max HR = 220-age for men, 226-age for women, threshold at ~85% of that), and the very next slides give a body-size-based table (big/medium/small athlete, ~165/170/175 bpm threshold) as the operational standard used elsewhere throughout the conditioning material. Which one is actually used day to day is not stated. [class-4-2026.md pp.27-29]\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric (Q20): \"Age-based.\" The body-size table is marked Superseded. Note that the body-size table was from Eric's own assigned session, so his slides and his practice differ.\n\n7. **The Development Plan's own critique cuts against the corpus it's part of.** It states the current Baylor Playbook \"has no educational component whatsoever\" and reads \"like a vendor product reference manual,\" even though Eric personally co-teaches a full graduate-level course covering much of the same material the Playbook is missing. The department's teaching capacity clearly exists; it just hasn't been captured back into the Playbook or a formal intern curriculum yet.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): still open, confirmed.** Eric (Q30): the intern curriculum \"has not been built yet.\"\n\n8. **The Playbook's NordBord documentation has not caught up with practice.** The Playbook's Technology Overview and Workflows still document a full, unqualified, roster-style NordBord data-collection SOP. The SportSmith piece describes explicitly retiring roster-wide NordBord testing for football starting after January 2023, keeping it only for small, targeted return-to-play groups. The written SOP has not been updated to reflect that decision.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): open.** Not asked. Inference: the Q3 rule (\"if it doesn't answer a question anyone has...\") would retire the roster-wide SOP.\n\n9. **A personally-stated periodization preference is stated inside an unreliable-speaker transcript.** \"For me personally, this is a more preferential model to use\" (about undulating over linear periodization) is a strong, direct, first-person preference claim — but it comes from the same unreliably-diarized Class 1 transcript described in Tension 1, so it cannot be confirmed as Eric's own stated preference without his direct confirmation.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric claimed undulating periodization (Q1).\n\n10. **Older vs. current unit-goal templates show almost no adopted content.** The Geyer and Skodinski Unit Goal Programming Standards documents are staff-authored templates for individual sport programming philosophy; Geyer's is mostly filled in (periodization model, weekly split, KPI tests for softball), Skodinski's is nearly blank (just the section headers). Neither is attributed to Eric, and there's no equivalent document in this corpus showing Eric's own answers to the same template questions — a real gap if Flint wants Eric's own unit-goal-style programming philosophy in his own words.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): open.** Not asked.\n\nNew tensions raised by the interview (2026-09-28):\n\n11. **Eric's own slide against his interview on residual effects.** The class-4 slide (p. 47), from a session Eric taught, says aerobic fitness is lost fastest and speed slowest. In the interview he sides with Issurin, speed fades fastest (Q16), and says Issurin drives speed programming. The slide bullet is marked Superseded. Inference: the slide still circulates in HHPR 5355 and should be reconciled before next spring. The peaking synthesis keeps the counter-evidence both ways.\n12. **Daily mRSI testing against a Wednesday read.** The SportSmith piece describes mRSI from daily CMJ testing as the daily readiness marker [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]. Q2 names Wednesday as the day. Inference: daily collection with one weekly decision day would reconcile them, but Eric did not say which; worth one follow-up.\n    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** Daily jumps are collected to build the rolling baseline and SWC; Wednesday is the single decision read that changes the week.\n13. **\"We don't assess readiness\" against mRSI as the readiness marker.** Q22 says Applied Performance does not assess readiness in RTP; the SportSmith piece and Q2 describe mRSI readiness reads. Inference: Q22 is scoped to RTP, so these probably do not conflict, but the RTP templates should be checked.\n14. **\"Support\" role against \"it doesn't really end\".** The Playbook frames RTP input as support to Athletic Medicine and Athletic Performance decisions [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]. Q4 describes ongoing testing, progression and shared loading decisions with no end point. Inference: compatible (support can be continuous), but the Playbook wording understates the role.\n15. **The Q3 rule against current practice.** Eric's rule is that a test with no question or need has no place (Q3). Yet ACWR is still looked at (\"We dabble with ACWR\") though he does not \"trust it for anything\", and movement screens are \"just collected\" without changing decisions (Q12). Inference: by his own rule both are candidates to retire or to be given a question.\n16. **No true 1RM against %1RM prescription.** No sport tests true 1RM (Q10), while Prilepin's chart and the phase bands are written in %1RM [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.19; week1-lecture.md pp.43-48]. Inference: loads are percentages of projected maxes, so their error carries into every prescription. Not a contradiction in practice, but worth stating.\n    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** Keep %1RM and Prilepin prescription, stated explicitly as built on projected maxes (observation plus history, Q10), not tested 1RMs.\n17. **DSI as a plyo-vs-strength switch against the evidence.** Eric uses DSI to choose plyometric versus max-strength emphasis (Q21). The evidence check (`docs/synthesis/_work/dsi-evidence-check.md`) finds that IMTP PF explains most of DSI's variance, that the 0.60/0.80 cutoffs were never validated, and that the one controlled trial found a benefit only in its strength arm. Inference: keep the test, but decide on CMJ PF and IMTP PF alongside the ratio, and set cutoffs from the roster.\n18. **COD and FMS gates in the RTP templates against Q12/Q13.** The LE template gates Evaluation on the 10-5 COD test and on FMS > 14 (`docs/interview/2026-09-28-method-interview.md`), while Q13 says COD is not tested in most sports and Q12 says screens do not change decisions.\n    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** The gates stay in the RTP templates. The baseline names who runs them (Athletic Medicine or the sport coach), not Applied Performance, consistent with \"the template answers readiness\" (Q22).\n\n---\n\n\n51:- A three-phase roadmap (Foundation, weeks 1-4; Operational Depth, weeks 5-10; Education and Culture, weeks 11-16+) is the stated plan to close those gaps, ending with an annual Playbook review cycle each May. **Explicit** [performance-playbook-de\n89:- Daily subjective wellness surveys, rolled out across multiple sports via the AMS app starting summer 2022, were abandoned after roughly one academic year because athletes learned to game the consistent, low-effort answers the survey rewarded; th\n97:- Issurin's residual training effects (speed fades fastest, 5 ± 3 days; Block Periodization PDF p. 35, as cited in `docs/synthesis/peaking-and-taper-in-season-football.md`) drive speed programming. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q16): \"I believe Issu\n105:- Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work. In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying \n107:- Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block, used consistently across strength and power teaching. **Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)** [01192022-hp535\n108:- Strength's residual training effect is treated as roughly 30 days; a minimal retention dose is still trained within that window, even during lower-strength-emphasis blocks. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.24-2\n117:- Glycolytic conditioning is still not a primary target in season, but staff do program it in the off-season. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q19): \"It does hold up, but our staff does program it in the off-season.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, \n181:- In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.m\n182:- Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block — \"you can't ride two horses with one s\n183:- An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract: lay out the competition calendar, key non-training dates (holidays, exam weeks), and phase-by-phase volume/intensity waves, then adapt the route as circumstances change whil\n186:- The in-season game week: Sunday off; Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday; lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**\n276:| Vladimir Issurin (*Block Periodization*) | Residual training effects (speed fades fastest); drives off-season speed programming and in-season sprint recommendations | interview 2026-09-28, Q16; class1-transcript.md p.27 and week1-lecture.md p.6","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"4ae65716-375a-4a1a-a9d6-6ea26f31c10a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:07:06.780Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"## Strength and power\n\n- The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next) is repeatedly described as the department's programming \"compass.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript + shared course material) [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.9-10; week1-lecture.md pp.23,26]\n- Exercise selection is organized on a spectrum from general preparation through specific preparation, specific development, to competitive exercises (Bondarchuk), replacing the loose and often-misapplied idea of \"sport specific.\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.13-16; week1-lecture.md pp.32-36]\n- The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript + slides) [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.14; week1-lecture.md p.34]\n- Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints); single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.13; week1-lecture.md p.31]\n- Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work. In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule. **Inferred (transcript + shared slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.20-21; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.21] Not claimed in the interview. The in-season dose itself is unknown: Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q18): \"I don't have the specific sets and reps.\"\n- Undulating/non-linear periodization (Parker/Miller/Panariello), waving volume week to week (e.g., medium-high, medium, high, low over four weeks) is stated as the personally preferred model over strict linear progression, specifically to avoid staleness while still building tolerance for higher loads. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript, speaker unconfirmed but first-person: \"For me personally, this is a more preferential model to use\") [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.24-26]\n- Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block, used consistently across strength and power teaching. **Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.19; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.15] Inference: with no true 1RM testing (Q10), any %1RM prescription rests on projected maxes. Kept on that explicit basis (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02).\n- Strength's residual training effect is treated as roughly 30 days; a minimal retention dose is still trained within that window, even during lower-strength-emphasis blocks. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.24-25] Inference: consistent with Eric siding with Issurin (Q16), whose strength residual is 30 ± 5 days, though Q16 was about speed and he did not claim this bullet.\n- Tracy Fober's maxim (\"use a barbell like a chisel, not a sledgehammer\") is adopted as a guiding standard: prioritize movement quality and confidence-through-competence over chasing hypertrophy or 1RM numbers, especially early in a training cycle. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.21; week1-lecture.md p.46]\n\n## Conditioning and energy systems\n\n- Conditioning is defined two ways: (1) how well an athlete can meet the energy requirements of their specific sport/activity, and (2) how well they sustain strength, speed, and power output across a full competition. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md p.22]\n- All three energy systems (alactic ~10-12s highest power, glycolytic/anaerobic-lactic ~60-90s medium capacity, aerobic hours-long lowest power/highest capacity) are always active simultaneously; programming targets the adaptation, not the drill label. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md p.19; class-4-2026.md p.15]\n- A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once — priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands (e.g., for football: prepared for fall camp first, then the first game). **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md p.31]\n- For football specifically, the stated priority stack is: Goal 1, alactic and aerobic capacity/power for fall-camp demands; Goal 2, the same two systems for game demands; glycolytic conditioning is treated as a \"worst case scenario\" to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target. **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md pp.34, 39]; claimed again in the interview, **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**\n- Glycolytic conditioning is still not a primary target in season, but staff do program it in the off-season. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q19): \"It does hold up, but our staff does program it in the off-season.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)**\n- Anaerobic (lactate) threshold heart-rate zones are assigned by athlete body size rather than a single number: roughly 165 bpm for big/tall athletes, 170 for medium, 175 for small, with Zone 1-4 bands set as percentages of that individual threshold. **Superseded by interview (Q20)**; was Explicit [class-4-2026.md pp.28-29]\n- The anaerobic-threshold heart rate in use is the age-based method (the slide deck's max HR = 220-age for men, 226-age for women, threshold at about 85% of that [class-4-2026.md p.27]). Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q20): \"Age-based.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q20)** Resolves Tension 6.\n- The traits easiest to build (aerobic) are lost fastest under detraining; the traits hardest to build (alactic/speed) are lost slowest; this ordering is used to sequence what needs the most frequent re-exposure. **Superseded by interview (Q16)**; was Explicit [class-4-2026.md p.47] Eric now sides with Issurin (speed fades fastest). See Tension 11.\n- Training intensities between roughly 75-90% of maximal effort are labeled (via Francis) \"no man's land\" — too fast to build alactic power cleanly, too slow to build real aerobic capacity — and are treated as a zone to generally avoid as a primary training target. **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md p.48]\n- \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\": lifting, agility work, and skill drills each load a specific energy system (power vs. capacity, alactic/glycolytic/aerobic) and should be classified and counted as conditioning, not treated as separate from it. **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md p.31, tables pp.35-42]\n- For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery (regenerating ATP between bouts), not distance capacity — a better-developed aerobic system means more available energy on play 10, 11, or 12 of a drive, not longer continuous running ability. **Inferred (transcript)** [2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md p.9]\n- Aerobic-system training is kept below anaerobic threshold with roughly a 1:1 to 1:2 work:rest ratio and a general speed ceiling around 4.5 m/s, specifically to avoid spilling over into glycolytic-system stress. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md p.51; 2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.11-12]\n- Joel Jamieson's *Ultimate MMA Conditioning* is recommended as the single best practical resource for energy-system development, on the basis that its methods transfer cleanly to non-combat sports despite the MMA framing. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md pp.49-50; 2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.11-12]\n\n----\n## Periodization and programming\n\n- Macro/meso/microcycle structure is used as the standard planning language, with an explicit reminder that a \"training week\" does not have to equal 7 calendar days — it can be extended to fit training themes around a real competition schedule. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.17-18; week1-lecture.md p.39]\n- The preparatory period is sequenced hypertrophy/work-capacity, then basic strength, then strength-to-power, each with defined intensity/volume bands (e.g., hypertrophy phase 50-75% intensity, 3-6 sets of 5-20 reps; strength phase 80-90% intensity, moderate volume), before a transition into competition. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.19-21; week1-lecture.md pp.43-48]\n- In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.21]\n- Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block — \"you can't ride two horses with one saddle.\" Compatible qualities are trained together; the rest are trained at a retention dose. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.28-29; week1-lecture.md p.70]\n- An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract: lay out the competition calendar, key non-training dates (holidays, exam weeks), and phase-by-phase volume/intensity waves, then adapt the route as circumstances change while still reaching the same destination. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.22-23; 2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.13-15]\n- Training residual effects set the retraining cadence: aerobic capacity and max strength both need re-exposure roughly every 30 days; speed needs re-exposure roughly every 5 days or tissue readiness for max-velocity work degrades. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.27] Inference: these are Issurin's figures, and Eric sides with Issurin (interview 2026-09-28, Q16), so the speed half reads as his; the aerobic and strength halves were not asked directly.\n- In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q14): \"in football you want to peak every Saturday, but definitely throughout the month of November as you get closer to the postseason ... That's when players need to be at or near their peak as they head into the postseason and playoffs.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)** He did not say whether the postseason gets a distinct taper.\n- The in-season game week: Sunday off; Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday; lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**\n- Bye weeks: three practice days, extra rest and recovery, slightly higher practice volume than a game week, a speed and strength top-up, and harder work for players with few game snaps. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q17): \"guys that aren't getting a ton of play will probably practice harder that week than guys that have gotten a ton of snaps.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)**\n- The head coach's system of play and roster identity (e.g., a \"home run hitters\" lineup vs. a \"steal bases\" lineup) is treated as a direct input into whether a team's programming leans toward maximal strength or toward speed/power emphasis. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.16]\n\n----\n## Tensions and contradictions\n\nStatus after the interview (2026-09-28): resolved 1 (partly), 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 9; still open 7 (partly), 8, 10. New tensions from the interview are 11 to 16. Resolved by Eric 2026-10-02: 12, 16 and 18 (COD/FMS gates). Each entry below carries its status on its last line.\n\n1. **The task's own premise doesn't fully hold.** Both class transcripts (Class 1 and Class 3) are diarized entirely under a single Fireflies speaker label, \"Chris Ruf,\" for every line — including Class 3, which the Spring 2026 teaching schedule assigns solo to Rash (`spring-2026-teaching.md`: \"Feb. 5 | Conditioning - Alactic-Aerobic | Rash\"). Fireflies likely defaulted to the meeting organizer's name instead of doing real per-speaker diarization. This means roughly 30,000 words of the richest, most first-person material in this corpus cannot be confidently attributed to Rash specifically versus Ruf or Wenger. Every transcript-sourced bullet above is marked Inferred for this reason, even whe\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): partly resolved.** Q1 claimed four positions (Vermeil hierarchy, undulating periodization, Dynamic Correspondence, the football energy-system stack), now Explicit. Every other transcript bullet stays Inferred.\n\n2. **Department reporting model is not internally consistent.** The Playbook (p.6) frames Applied Performance as a peer discipline inside Health & Wellness that \"enables\" Athletics Performance, Athletic Medicine, Nutrition, and Mental Health to answer their own questions. But Week 1 course material lists \"Applied Performance\" as one of many training sub-categories nested under Athletic Performance itself (p.11), and the same transcript narration describes Rash's role as \"helping program and plan the training\" — a support-staff framing, not a peer-department framing. Which model actually governs day-to-day authority is not reconciled anywhere in these documents.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric (Q5): \"Both.\" Both framings are true at once.\n\n3. **Coach's-eye-first versus objective-first is argued both ways.** The course material repeatedly argues that \"you don't need a fancy test... you have to have a good coach's eye\" and criticizes the GPS/load-monitoring era for breeding a fear-driven under-training culture. Eric's own SportSmith writing moves the opposite direction on daily monitoring specifically: he replaced subjective wellness surveys with objective force-plate mRSI because subjective self-report couldn't be trusted. Both share a skepticism of trendy or convenient data, but they land on opposite defaults (trust the trained eye vs. require an objective number) for the daily-monitoring question specifically.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric (Q6): the coach's eye flags where to look; data then investigates. The two are sequential, not rival defaults.\n\n4. **The Assessment Manual (V12) reads as an older document than the current technology stack.** It builds its 1RM-estimation protocol around GymAware, and its CMJ description references a \"contact mat\" for jump height. The current Playbook technology stack and the Development Plan both center velocity-based training on Perch, not GymAware, and the current Force Plate Testing V3 protocol runs all CMJ variants exclusively on ForceDecks force plates, with no contact mat mentioned. This suggests the Assessment Manual predates the current VALD/Perch-centered stack and may be a legacy document rather than the currently operative protocol — worth confirming directly with Eric before treating any o\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric (Q8): V12 \"is not current\"; a new manual built on the NCAA technology and sport-science recommendations is in development. All V12 bullets are marked Legacy.\n\n5. **Testing cueing instructions conflict across two protocol documents for the same test.** The Assessment Manual's CMJ instructions say explicitly: \"Do not coach the strategy of jumping.\" The current Force Plate Testing V3 protocol gives testers specific verbal cues to give athletes during CMJ, squat-jump, and hop-test administration (\"keep chest up,\" \"land softly,\" \"land stiff-legged on only the toes\"). These may be reconcilable (safety/technique cues vs. jump-strategy cues are different things), but the two documents read differently on how much a tester should say out loud.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved by Q8.** Inference: with V12 not current, the Force Plate Testing V3 cues govern. Eric did not address the cueing question itself.\n\n6. **Two different anaerobic-threshold heart-rate methods appear back to back in the same slide deck without being reconciled.** One slide gives an age-based formula (max HR = 220-age for men, 226-age for women, threshold at ~85% of that), and the very next slides give a body-size-based table (big/medium/small athlete, ~165/170/175 bpm threshold) as the operational standard used elsewhere throughout the conditioning material. Which one is actually used day to day is not stated. [class-4-2026.md pp.27-29]\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric (Q20): \"Age-based.\" The body-size table is marked Superseded. Note that the body-size table was from Eric's own assigned session, so his slides and his practice differ.\n\n7. **The Development Plan's own critique cuts against the corpus it's part of.** It states the current Baylor Playbook \"has no educational component whatsoever\" and reads \"like a vendor product reference manual,\" even though Eric personally co-teaches a full graduate-level course covering much of the same material the Playbook is missing. The department's teaching capacity clearly exists; it just hasn't been captured back into the Playbook or a formal intern curriculum yet.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): still open, confirmed.** Eric (Q30): the intern curriculum \"has not been built yet.\"\n\n8. **The Playbook's NordBord documentation has not caught up with practice.** The Playbook's Technology Overview and Workflows still document a full, unqualified, roster-style NordBord data-collection SOP. The SportSmith piece describes explicitly retiring roster-wide NordBord testing for football starting after January 2023, keeping it only for small, targeted return-to-play groups. The written SOP has not been updated to reflect that decision.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): open.** Not asked. Inference: the Q3 rule (\"if it doesn't answer a question anyone has...\") would retire the roster-wide SOP.\n\n9. **A personally-stated periodization preference is stated inside an unreliable-speaker transcript.** \"For me personally, this is a more preferential model to use\" (about undulating over linear periodization) is a strong, direct, first-person preference claim — but it comes from the same unreliably-diarized Class 1 transcript described in Tension 1, so it cannot be confirmed as Eric's own stated preference without his direct confirmation.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric claimed undulating periodization (Q1).\n\n10. **Older vs. current unit-goal templates show almost no adopted content.** The Geyer and Skodinski Unit Goal Programming Standards documents are staff-authored templates for individual sport programming philosophy; Geyer's is mostly filled in (periodization model, weekly split, KPI tests for softball), Skodinski's is nearly blank (just the section headers). Neither is attributed to Eric, and there's no equivalent document in this corpus showing Eric's own answers to the same template questions — a real gap if Flint wants Eric's own unit-goal-style programming philosophy in his own words.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): open.** Not asked.\n\nNew tensions raised by the interview (2026-09-28):\n\n11. **Eric's own slide against his interview on residual effects.** The class-4 slide (p. 47), from a session Eric taught, says aerobic fitness is lost fastest and speed slowest. In the interview he sides with Issurin, speed fades fastest (Q16), and says Issurin drives speed programming. The slide bullet is marked Superseded. Inference: the slide still circulates in HHPR 5355 and should be reconciled before next spring. The peaking synthesis keeps the counter-evidence both ways.\n12. **Daily mRSI testing against a Wednesday read.** The SportSmith piece describes mRSI from daily CMJ testing as the daily readiness marker [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]. Q2 names Wednesday as the day. Inference: daily collection with one weekly decision day would reconcile them, but Eric did not say which; worth one follow-up.\n    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** Daily jumps are collected to build the rolling baseline and SWC; Wednesday is the single decision read that changes the week.\n13. **\"We don't assess readiness\" against mRSI as the readiness marker.** Q22 says Applied Performance does not assess readiness in RTP; the SportSmith piece and Q2 describe mRSI readiness reads. Inference: Q22 is scoped to RTP, so these probably do not conflict, but the RTP templates should be checked.\n14. **\"Support\" role against \"it doesn't really end\".** The Playbook frames RTP input as support to Athletic Medicine and Athletic Performance decisions [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]. Q4 describes ongoing testing, progression and shared loading decisions with no end point. Inference: compatible (support can be continuous), but the Playbook wording understates the role.\n15. **The Q3 rule against current practice.** Eric's rule is that a test with no question or need has no place (Q3). Yet ACWR is still looked at (\"We dabble with ACWR\") though he does not \"trust it for anything\", and movement screens are \"just collected\" without changing decisions (Q12). Inference: by his own rule both are candidates to retire or to be given a question.\n16. **No true 1RM against %1RM prescription.** No sport tests true 1RM (Q10), while Prilepin's chart and the phase bands are written in %1RM [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.19; week1-lecture.md pp.43-48]. Inference: loads are percentages of projected maxes, so their error carries into every prescription. Not a contradiction in practice, but worth stating.\n    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** Keep %1RM and Prilepin prescription, stated explicitly as built on projected maxes (observation plus history, Q10), not tested 1RMs.\n17. **DSI as a plyo-vs-strength switch against the evidence.** Eric uses DSI to choose plyometric versus max-strength emphasis (Q21). The evidence check (`docs/synthesis/_work/dsi-evidence-check.md`) finds that IMTP PF explains most of DSI's variance, that the 0.60/0.80 cutoffs were never validated, and that the one controlled trial found a benefit only in its strength arm. Inference: keep the test, but decide on CMJ PF and IMTP PF alongside the ratio, and set cutoffs from the roster.\n18. **COD and FMS gates in the RTP templates against Q12/Q13.** The LE template gates Evaluation on the 10-5 COD test and on FMS > 14 (`docs/interview/2026-09-28-method-interview.md`), while Q13 says COD is not tested in most sports and Q12 says screens do not change decisions.\n    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** The gates stay in the RTP templates. The baseline names who runs them (Athletic Medicine or the sport coach), not Applied Performance, consistent with \"the template answers readiness\" (Q22).\n\n---\n\n\n51:- A three-phase roadmap (Foundation, weeks 1-4; Operational Depth, weeks 5-10; Education and Culture, weeks 11-16+) is the stated plan to close those gaps, ending with an annual Playbook review cycle each May. **Explicit** [performance-playbook-de\n89:- Daily subjective wellness surveys, rolled out across multiple sports via the AMS app starting summer 2022, were abandoned after roughly one academic year because athletes learned to game the consistent, low-effort answers the survey rewarded; th\n97:- Issurin's residual training effects (speed fades fastest, 5 ± 3 days; Block Periodization PDF p. 35, as cited in `docs/synthesis/peaking-and-taper-in-season-football.md`) drive speed programming. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q16): \"I believe Issu\n105:- Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work. In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying \n107:- Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block, used consistently across strength and power teaching. **Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)** [01192022-hp535\n108:- Strength's residual training effect is treated as roughly 30 days; a minimal retention dose is still trained within that window, even during lower-strength-emphasis blocks. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.24-2\n117:- Glycolytic conditioning is still not a primary target in season, but staff do program it in the off-season. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q19): \"It does hold up, but our staff does program it in the off-season.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, \n181:- In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.m\n182:- Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block — \"you can't ride two horses with one s\n183:- An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract: lay out the competition calendar, key non-training dates (holidays, exam weeks), and phase-by-phase volume/intensity waves, then adapt the route as circumstances change whil\n186:- The in-season game week: Sunday off; Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday; lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**\n276:| Vladimir Issurin (*Block Periodization*) | Residual training effects (speed fades fastest); drives off-season speed programming and in-season sprint recommendations | interview 2026-09-28, Q16; class1-transcript.md p.27 and week1-lecture.md p.6","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"763a6b92-9425-4095-a850-1b6a97cf686f","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
