5.3 | Application of Special Strength Training Means (lead-in) | 287-290 | 298-301 | done | 5 |\n| 5.3.1 | Interaction between Different Training Means | 291 | 302 | done | 1 |\n| 5.3.2 | A Sequential System of Training Means | 291-292 | 302-303 | done | 1 |\n| 5.3.3 | The Conjugate Sequence System of Training Means | 292-294 | 303-305 | done | 3 |\n| 5.4 | The Principal Aims of Special Strength Training (lead-in) | 294 | 305 | done | 1 |\n| 5.4.1 | Converging the Partial Effects of Strength Training Means | 294-296 | 305-307 | done | 2 |\n| 5.4.2 | Acceleration of Specific Adaptation | 296 | 307 | done | 1 |\n| 5.4.3 | Specific Correspondence of the Training Effect | 296-297 | 307-308 | done | 1 |\n| 5.4.4 | Maintaining the Strength Training Effect | 297-298 | 308-309 | done | 1 |\n| 5.5 | Cross Training in Sport | 298-300 | 309-311 | done | 2 |\n| 5.6 | Circuit Training | 300-312 | 311-323 | done | 8 |\n| 6 | Organisation of Training | 313-392 | 324-403 | pending | 0 |\n| 7 | Strength Training Methods | 393-420 | 404-431 | pending | 0 |\n| 8 | Designing Sport Specific Strength Programmes | 421-480 | 432-491 | pending | 0 |\n| — | References and Bibliography | 481-494 | 492-505 | skip | n/a (reference list, not atomized) |\n| — | Index | 495-502 | 506-513 | skip | n/a |\n| 9 | Addendum (9.1 Laws of Sports Mastery, 9.2 The Shock Method for Developing Power) | 503-579 (verify) | 514-590 | pending | 0 |\n\nThis row-level table is the resume checkpoint: each 1.x row is set to `done` with its\nreal entry count immediately after that section is atomized, never batched.\n\n_Batch boundary: Chapter 2 is done (12 entries). Chapter 3 is now fully done: sections 3.1\nthrough 3.4.10 (32 entries, prior session) plus 3.5 Flexibility and Sporting Performance\nthrough 3.5.9, 3.6 The Stretching Matrix System, and 3.7 The Movement Matrix System (28\nentries, this session covering printed 174-200, PDF idx 185-211) for 60 entries total in\nChapter 3. One dedup note from this session: the 3.5.6 restatement (printed 181) of \"bony\ninsertion weakest at slow loading rate, soft tissue weakest at fast loading rate\" was NOT\nre-atomized, since it duplicates [[claim--collagen-loading-curve-and-injury-risk]] (Ch1,\nKennedy 1976/Frankel & Nordin 1980); only the genuinely new adjacent detail (the graded\nbone-tendon transitional-stiffness system and its disruption by anabolic steroids) got its\nown entry, [[claim--tendon-insertion-transitional-stiffness-gradient-disrupted-by-steroids]].\nChapters 4-9 remain pending for later sessions; resume at Chapter 4, The Means of Special\nStrength Training (printed 201, PDF idx 212)._\n\n_Batch boundary, this session: Chapter 4 section 4.1 \"The Problem of Training Means\" (4.1.1\nCharacteristics of Strength Increase, 4.1.2 The Effect of the Strength Training Means;\nprinted 201-206, PDF idx 212-217) is done, 6 entries. The short unnumbered lead-in before\n4.1 (page 201/PDF 212, history-of-the-field narrative) produced no entry of its own; it is\nscene-setting with no discrete claim. One dedup note: the 4.1.1 passage at printed 202\n(PDF 213) repeats, via the same Verkhoshansky 1977 citation chain, the\nKorobkov/Gerasimov/Vasiliev \"strength gains are roughly uniform at 20/40/60/80% of 1RM in\nearly training\" finding and the Hettinger & Muller (1953) one-third-max threshold finding\nalready captured in [[claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus]] (Ch1, 1.2); that\nidentical material was NOT re-atomized. The genuinely new adjacent material in 4.1.1\n(Zimkin 1961 and Muller & Hettinger 1957 training-age-dependent load percentages;\nChernyavsky 1966 light/heavy load-proportion result) became\n[[claim--strength-and-speed-training-response-shifts-with-training-age]]. Two further new\nclaim entries cover delayed onset of strength gains\n([[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]]) and detraining/\nretention timelines, including an unreconciled internal contradiction between the\nChernyavsky 20-session decay curve and the Vasiliev/McMorris & Eklins 40-session retention\ndata ([[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]]). Section 4.1.2's \"Algebraic\nRelations\" passage (Commutative/Associative Laws applied to training order) and its\nAcute/Delayed Training Effect and Particular/Cumulative Effect taxonomy became two framework\nentries ([[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]],\n[[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]]); the remaining\n\"Steps Towards Structuring the Training Process\" material (training load's five\ncharacteristics; volume's two functions; structured system beats random combination even at\nlower volume, citing Verkhoshansky 1966/1970, Tatyan 1974, Khodykin 1975; Figure 4.1,\nverified against the page image) became\n[[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]]. Chapter\n4 sections 4.2 (Different Means of Strength Development: electrostimulation, resistance and\nkinetic-energy training, isometric, eccentric, isokinetic, machines, symmetric training,\nmuscle isolation), 4.3 (Dynamic Correspondence) and 4.4 (Strength Training and General\nEndurance) remain pending; resume at 4.2, printed 207 / PDF idx 218, \"4.2 Different Means of\nStrength Development\" / \"4.2.1 Electrostimulation.\"_\n\n_Batch boundary, this session: Chapter 4 section 4.2 \"Different Means of Strength\nDevelopment\" is now fully done, all nine subsections (4.2.1 Electrostimulation through 4.2.9\nMuscle Isolation; printed 207-241, PDF idx 218-252), 35 entries total (8+4+3+7+2+6+3+1+1 per\nthe section rows above). Every table and figure encountered was opened as a page image\n(Figs 4.2, 4.3, 4.6, 4.7, 4.8, 4.11, 4.12, 4.13, 4.14, 4.16) before being cited; Figs 4.4,\n4.5, 4.9 and 4.10 are also cited and were checked against the page images (PDF idx 230 and\n234). Fig 4.15 (PDF idx 239, isometric endurance against forearm-immersion temperature,\nClarke et al 1958; its caption is image-only) is cited in\n[[claim--isometric-endurance-limits-and-recovery-phases]] and was checked against the page\nimage. Voice: 4.2.1\nElectrostimulation has first-person Siff material only on printed p.215: \"the ES training\napplied to the author by Serge Reding\" (Siff as the subject) and \"Work that I witnessed\" in\nRatov's Moscow laboratory. The printed p.209 Reding passage names Siff in the third person\n(\"one of the authors (Siff)\"), and the printed p.208 Ratov passage is the authors'.\n[[case-study--reding-electrostimulation-protocol-siff-account]] and\n[[claim--ratov-functional-electrostimulation-emg-triggered-sport-specific]] attribute each\npassage accordingly. The MACS/MICS \"integrated theory\" of\nelectrostimulation (4.2.1.4-4.2.1.5, citing Becker, Nordenstrom and Pilla on bioelectric\ncell-signalling) is rated `contested`, with its mainstream-status judgment labelled\nInference, rather than presented as settled physiology;\nsee [[claim--macs-mics-integrated-theory-of-electrostimulation]]. Two forward/backward\ncross-references the book itself makes were completed as reciprocal links: the 4.2.2 passage\non load-dependent proximal/distal muscle recruitment explicitly forward-references 4.2.9\n(now [[claim--muscle-recruitment-consistency-and-proximal-distal-shift-with-load]] linked to\n[[claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load]]), and the\nCh3.3.4.4 entry [[claim--natural-asymmetry-caution-against-isokinetic-symmetry-correction]]\nexplicitly deferred \"symmetric training\" to 4.2.8, now fulfilled by\n[[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]] (reciprocal link\nrecommended on the Ch3 entry, not made since it is outside this session's assigned\nwrite-scope). [[heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity]]\n(4.2.6.2) substantially extends the existing Ch2 entry\n[[heuristic--isometric-dynamometry-misrepresents-dynamic-functional-strength]] with a\ndetailed, itemised mechanism list; a reciprocal link on that Ch2 entry is recommended but not\nmade (outside this session's write-scope). No entries were needed for chapter 4's own\nelectrostimulation/isometric/eccentric/isokinetic/symmetric-training/muscle-isolation\nconcepts already covered elsewhere in this folder or in the vault; dedup was checked against\nMoyer's `framework--isometric-training-variables-and-adaptations` (vault, a distinct\ndose-variable framework, not duplicated), Handford 2024's eccentric-training-methods paper\n(vault, not duplicated, no direct overlap found), and Bishop 2016's asymmetry paper (vault,\nalready linked from the Ch3 entry this session's 4.2.8 entry completes). Chapter 4 sections\n4.3 (Dynamic Correspondence as a Means of Strength) and 4.4 (Strength Training and General\nEndurance) remain pending; resume at 4.3, printed 241 / PDF idx 252, \"4.3 Dynamic\nCorrespondence as a Means of Strength\" / \"4.3.1 The Amplitude and Direction of Movement.\"_\n\n_Batch boundary, this session: Chapter 4 sections 4.3 \"Dynamic Correspondence as a Means of\nStrength Training\" (4.3.1-4.3.6, including the \"Parachute Resistance Training\" subsection;\nprinted 241-248, PDF idx 252-259) and 4.4 \"Strength Training and General Endurance\" (with its\nunnumbered subsections Oxidative Capacity and Muscular Endurance, Strength Training and General\nEndurance, The Process of Functional Specialisation, Factor Analysis; printed 248-253, PDF idx\n259-264) are done: 13 and 10 entries. Two page notes for the rows above: 4.3.6 runs onto\nprinted 248 (PDF 259), where 4.4 starts mid-page; and printed 254 (PDF 265) is blank. Figs\n4.17-4.24 and Tables 4.1-4.2 were checked against page images (PDF 253-257, 260-264).\nSupertraining had no entry for the five dynamic-correspondence criteria themselves (only the\nCh1.4 ten-dimension specificity entry and Evans's applied case study);\n[[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]] is now the anchor. Contested in this\nbatch: [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]]\n(raise VO2 max p.249 vs not p.250; less glycolysis p.249 vs high glycolytic productivity\np.253) and [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]]\n(Pattengale & Holloszy 1967 is a treadmill-running study by its title). Chapter 4 is now\ncomplete; resume at Chapter 5, The Methods of Special Strength Training, printed 255 / PDF idx\n266._\n\n_Batch boundary, 2026-10-03 (Mac): Chapter 5 complete in three parallel batches (5a: 5.1-5.2.3, 19 entries; 5b: 5.2.4-5.2.5, 30; 5c: 5.3-5.6, 26), each through an independent round-1 review, fixes, and a round-2 review (reviews and fix logs in `staging/review_supertraining_ch5{a,b,c}_opus*.md` and `staging/fixes_supertraining_ch5{a,b,c}_applied.md`). Section boundaries fall mid-page at PDF 266, 278, 296 and 298. Resume at Chapter 6, Organisation of Training, printed 313 / PDF idx 324._\n     253\n_book.md\ncase-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md\ncase-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md\ncase-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means.md\ncase-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification.md\ncase-study--junior-sprinters-short-and-long-jump-training-effects.md\ncase-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments.md\ncase-study--reding-electrostimulation-protocol-siff-account.md\ncase-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md\ncase-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions.md\nclaim--400m-stride-length-held-by-local-strength-endurance.md\nclaim--absolute-leg-strength-connects-to-sprint-speed-only-indirectly-via-jump.md\nclaim--active-flexibility-correlates-more-with-sporting-proficiency-than-passive.md\nclaim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation.md\nclaim--all-round-athletes-main-regime-cyclic-and-phasic-tonic.md\nclaim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-n