{"parentUuid":"5af68c5d-ac89-417c-a752-345a269dfdba","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016eWR3AUGAe2BJzw5W787nn","type":"tool_result","content":"review_supertraining_ch5a_opus_round2.md\nreview_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md\nreview_supertraining_ch5b_opus_round2.md\nreview_supertraining_ch5b_opus.md\nreview_supertraining_ch5c_opus_round2.md\nreview_supertraining_ch5c_opus.md\n   14298 review_supertraining_ch5a_opus_round2.md\n   23089 review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md\n   20813 review_supertraining_ch5b_opus_round2.md\n   22692 review_supertraining_ch5b_opus.md\n   15940 review_supertraining_ch5c_opus_round2.md\n   29162 review_supertraining_ch5c_opus.md\n  125994 total\n# Review: Supertraining Ch 5c (sections 5.3-5.6), 26 entries\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent, instructed to falsify). Date: 2026-10-03.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 298-323 (printed 287-312), plus PDF 91, 375, 381, 536.\nPages opened as images by the reviewer (renders in `staging/_renders/r5c_p*.png`): 298, 300 (plus a 300 dpi crop of Fig 5.12), 301, 302, 303, 304, 305, 306, 307, 308, 309, 310, 312, 313, 314, 315, 316, 317, 318, 319, 320, 321, 322, 323, 375. Pages 381 and 536 were read from the text layer only (clean text, no figures or tables used from them). PDF 91 was checked in the text layer for the Ch1 quote.\nAlso checked: every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote against `docs/method-baseline.md` (lines 32, 61, 76, 102, 103, 104, 105, 107, 115, 119, 122, 123, 124, 129, 142, 151, 173, 182, 183, 184 and Tension 17 at line 261; all quotes and evidence tags match); all 74 wikilinks resolve in the vault or staging; the Issurin 2016 vault brief; the Issurin 2008 entries; the Bompa vault entries; the Bondarchuk transfer-ceiling entry; and Bompa & Haff 5e itself (Drive copy, PDF idx 61) for the Bompa finding.\n\n## Entry table\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means.md | FIX | Fig 5.14 reading is incomplete. Entry: \"Ss and Se both rise through stages 1-3, and Ss falls back from its stage-3 high at the stage-4 test.\" Image (PDF 301 / p.290): within stage 3 both curves fall (Ss about 700 to about 650; Se about 430 to about 375), and at the stage-4 test Se (about 390) is also below its stage-3 start, not only Ss. The rises happen across the rest weeks (8 and 16), which is the text's point. Everything else checked: stage layout weeks 1-7 / 8 / 9-15 / 16 / 17-21 / 22 / 23-24, Fmax about 103 to 110 to about 125 kgf, tmax about 0.42 to about 0.27 s, all quotes, \"complete rest\" wording, Deniskin 1976. | Replace the last sentence of the figure description with: \"Ss and Se jump after each rest week (weeks 8 and 16) and are highest at the start of stage 3; both then decline through stage 3 and sit below that peak at the stage-4 test (Ss about 600-620, Se about 390). Values read by eye from the image.\" |\n| case-study--junior-sprinters-short-and-long-jump-training-effects.md | FIX | Bar values misread. From a 300 dpi crop of Fig 5.12 (PDF 300 / p.289; left axis 0-0.5 s, right panel 0-1.0 s), short / long / combined: 30 m LS about 0.39 / 0.08 / 0.24-0.25 (entry: 0.29 combined); 30 m SS about 0.08 / 0.14 / 0.19 (entry 0.07 / 0.13 / 0.20, fine); 60 m LS about 0.27 / 0.20 / 0.29 (entry 0.29 / 0.22 / 0.33); 60 m SS about 0.07 / 0.38 / 0.42 (fine); 100 m LS about 0.29 / 0.48 / 1.0 (entry 0.27 / 0.50 / 1.0, close). With the corrected values, combined is below the sum at 30 m SS (0.19 vs 0.22), 60 m LS (0.29 vs 0.47) and roughly equal at 60 m SS (0.42 vs 0.45); only 100 m exceeds the sum. The contested reading itself holds and is stronger: short beats combined at 30 m LS by about 0.15 s. Note also an unexplained white outlined bar at the right edge of the 100 m panel reaching 1.0 (may be the frame); treat as unclear. Quotes, \"eight months\", group definitions checked. | Replace the bar-height sentence with: \"30 m LS about 0.39 / 0.08 / 0.25 s; 30 m SS about 0.08 / 0.14 / 0.19 s; 60 m LS about 0.27 / 0.20 / 0.29 s; 60 m SS about 0.07 / 0.38 / 0.42 s; 100 m LS about 0.29 / 0.48 / 1.0 s. An outlined bar at the right edge of the 100 m panel also reaches 1.0; it may be part of the frame and is unclear.\" In When NOT to apply replace the comparison sentences with: \"the short-jump group, not the combined group, shows the largest drop at 30 m LS (about 0.39 vs 0.25 s). Against the sum of the two single groups, the combined bar is below it at 30 m SS (0.19 vs 0.22) and 60 m LS (0.29 vs 0.47), about equal at 60 m SS (0.42 vs 0.45), and above it only at 100 m LS (1.0 vs 0.77).\" Update the Example to \"about 0.29 s for short jumps alone and about 0.48 s for long jumps alone\". |\n| case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments.md | FIX | One of the three \"contested\" strands is a misreading. Entry: p.300's \"slightly smaller effect\" for mixed sessions vs p.299's \"A relatively larger effect\" is called a contradiction. It is not: p.299's comparator is \"the above-mentioned variants\" (strength-first vs speed-strength-first across the preparatory period); p.300's comparator is the best variant, the Fig 5.13 complexes. Mixed > order variants and best > mixed are compatible. The other two strands are real and on the cited pages: p.300 ranks strength-then-speed-strength in one session \"Least effective\" while p.299 found \"no statistically significant difference\"; p.299 says order \"has no real significance for novices\" while experiment 3 on the same page favours weights then depth jumps. Missed sharper strand: PDF 288 / p.277 (already atomized in `claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers`) says resistance plus plyometrics beats plyometrics alone \"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions,\" against p.299's within-workout novice null result (different populations, but the book states the session-order rule flatly). Also missing: p.299 defines the mixed regime as separate sessions \"with a rest interval between sessions on the same day.\" Names checked against the image (Purvin, Khodykin, Deniskin, Tatian, Chernousov). Baseline line 173 checked. | Delete the sentence beginning \"The first sentence ranks mixed sessions below the best variant, while PDF p. 299 says mixed sessions gave 'A relatively larger effect';\" and keep the two real strands. Add: \"The book also states, in 5.2.4, that resistance plus plyometrics works best 'provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions' (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277), which sits against experiment 4's null within-session result in novices.\" In item 2 add: \"(the mixed regime used separate sessions 'with a rest interval between sessions on the same day', PDF p. 299).\" Add `[[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]]` to Related. |\n| claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md | FIX | Inference misstates the source. Entry: \"Inference: the authors' warning is about volume, not intensity, which fits that rule.\" PDF 302 / p.291 also blames intensity: Filinov, \"excessively heavy strength loads diminish the force and speed of boxers' punches,\" and \"excessive maximum strength training can impair speed-strength and technical skill.\" Everything else checked against the image: 30% \"as much as\", Kotz, Filinov, Deniskin 1976, 18 weeks (54 workouts), 50-60 sessions / about 12 weeks, \"rubber-stamp\", Siff attribution for the Verkhoshansky-naming passage (correct). | Replace the inference with: \"Inference: the authors' warning covers both prolonged high volume (Deniskin) and excessive heaviness (Filinov; 'excessive maximum strength training'), so it supports the in-season 'reduce volume' half of that rule but does not by itself endorse holding heavy intensity during specialised speed phases.\" |\n| claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits.md | FIX | Unlabelled writer judgment. Entry: \"10-15 repetitions at over 80% of 1RM is not possible for most lifters; treat that number as unclear.\" The image (PDF 313 / p.302) confirms the book's wording; the impossibility claim is the writer's. All quotes checked against PDF 312 / p.301 and 313 / p.302 images: \"a moderate compromise\", Morgan and Adamson 1953 Leeds (PDF 311 text), \"It has been shown\", \"A literature review reveals\", alternation on separate days superior, \"This is not to state that circuit training is of little value\". Baseline line 115 checked. | Replace with: \"Inference: 10-15 repetitions at over 80% of 1RM is beyond most lifters' capacity, so treat that figure as doubtful even though the image confirms the wording.\" |\n| framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters.md | FIX | Contested is overstated in one strand. The PDF 317 / p.306 scheme is explicitly \"applicable to the average programme designed to moderately enhance muscle endurance and cardiovascular performance,\" a different target from the anaerobic column, so it is not a third figure for the same quantity. The real clash is within one category: anaerobic circuits defined as 3-8 reps at over 70% 1RM, then the anaerobic muscle-endurance sub-case at about 50% 1RM and about 50 reps (image confirms \"50\"). Every table value checked against the image, PDF 315 / p.304: 20-40, 30%, 5-10, 1-4 min, 3-8, 3-5, 70%, 50% / 50 / 4, 75% / 5 / 2 min / 3, 20-30 s. PDF 316 agility/restoration and PDF 317 40-50% of 10RM/5RM/1RM and 80% checked. Baseline line 124 checked. | Replace the contested paragraph opening with: \"**Contested inside the book (muscle-endurance figures).** The anaerobic column says 3-8 reps at over 70% of 1RM, but the muscle-endurance sub-case of the same anaerobic category says about 50% of 1RM for about 50 reps (the image confirms '50'), which falls outside both columns. The 40-50% of 10RM, 5RM or 1RM scheme on PDF p. 317 is for a different target ('moderately enhance muscle endurance and cardiovascular performance') and is a separate prescription, not a contradiction.\" Keep the rest. |\n| framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour.md | FIX | Unlabelled application in When to apply: \"The superset equivalence is useful when writing programmes: a two-exercise superset can be planned and progressed with the same rules as a circuit.\" The book only states the equivalence (PDF 320 / p.309, Fig 5.29). Figures 5.29-5.33 checked against images PDF 320-321; pyramid 40-80% 1RM and 10 to 4 reps, stripping about 10 kg, 60 kg x10 to 100 kg x6 then 90/80/70 kg, spotter quote checked at PDF 322; water resistance quote checked at PDF 317; dripping water at PDF 318. | Prefix: \"Inference: the superset equivalence is useful when writing programmes ...\" |\n| framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading.md | GO | Checked: Kudelin 1980, Budokho 1978, Mironenko 1980, 4-12 weeks, 20% macrocycle saving, Fedorova 1975 / Platonov & Monograov 1977 / Verkhoshansky 1977, low intensity of means, 23-25% of yearly volume, all at PDF 305 / p.294 (image); repeat wording at PDF 375 / p.364 (image); dual definition of \"acceleration\" at PDF 307 (image). Issurin contest points to the conjugate entry, whose Issurin 2016 summary matches the vault brief and targets Verkhoshansky's CU model, not A-T-R. Issurin 2008 \"minimal number of targets\" matches the vault principle entry (PDF 37). Example labelled Inference. Baseline line 184 checked. | None. Optional: PDF 381 / p.370 adds \"a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" for concentrated loading, which could join item 1. |\n| framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means.md | GO | Checked: two systems, concurrent definition, \"coupled successive system\", Verkhoshansky 1978, \"only average results\" for elites, SPP sentence, all at PDF 303 / p.292 (image; the writer had not opened this page, wording confirmed); \"degree of contrast\", complex of means for qualified athletes, speed-endurance and speed-strength sequences, Fig 5.15 labels, caption and overlap note, at PDF 304 / p.293 (image). Issurin 2016 claims match the vault brief (pp. 6-7, 11: CU evidence insufficient for combat, team and swimming; fitness gains without team practice or competition outcomes; suited to narrow-target sports) and are explicitly about Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model; the brief records the A-T-R model as Issurin's own and the one with 28-study support. Issurin 2008 PDF 44 / p.33 \"high-level athletes\" matches the vault entry. Naming-overlap note on \"complex system\" is accurate. Baseline line 182 checked. | None. |\n| framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification.md | GO | Checked: CCT / ICT / MCT and six subtypes, 15 min aerobic add-on, 10-15 min warm-up circuit, 5-10 min cool-down, \"super circuit\", fartlek, at PDF 313 / p.302 (image); circuit types list at PDF 314 / p.303 (image); technique quote at PDF 315; Table 5.4 at PDF 322 / p.311 (image): Circuit A 14 stations from 12-min fixed cycle, Circuit B 11 stations from 4-min fixed cycle (the text layer's \"Hart squats\" is \"Half squats\"). PDF 323 limit quote checked. Baseline line 122 checked. | None. |\n| framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation.md | GO | Checked: initial-stage general response, \"transfer of fitness is very distinct\", selective adaptation, \"determined entirely\", the four principles and \"do not exclude all of the traditional principles\", at PDF 305 / p.294 (image). Example labelled Inference. Baseline line 103 checked (quote truncated at a natural point, no distortion). | None. |\n| framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means.md | GO | Checked at PDF 298 / p.287 (image): cumulative-effect sentence, two variants, two observations, \"assess ... categorise\", endurance sequence and citations (Ozolin 1959, 1970; Volkov 1963; Yakovlev 1957; Borisov 1979), \"no definitive practical recommendations\". Image reads \"simply a summation\" (text layer \"amply\"), as the entry says. Synergy sentence continues at PDF 299. The non-contradiction with the 5.3.3 speed-endurance order is argued correctly. Baseline line 182 checked. | None. |\n| framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks.md | GO | Checked: definition, \"selectively reacts\", specialisation of systems / of ability, four tasks verbatim, at PDF 307 / p.296 (image); Fig 5.17 boxes and the six-ability list at PDF 308 / p.297 (image). Flexibility-strength at PDF 323 checked. Baseline line 76 and Tension 17 (line 261, unvalidated 0.60/0.80 cut-offs) checked. Example labelled Inference. | None. |\n| heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md | FIX | The cross-library point is right but aimed at the wrong place. Entry: \"Bompa's entry ... attributes to Siff and Verkhoshansky that 'gains may appear 4 to 12 weeks after the concentrated block ends.'\" The vault entry faithfully reports Bompa & Haff 5e, which says (Drive PDF idx 61): \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase (figure 2.10).\" So the misreading is in Bompa & Haff's book; the vault entry inherits it and then sharpens it into a delay (\"the longer/larger the loading, the longer the delay\"; \"delayed-rebound timing (4-12 wk)\"). All Supertraining figures checked: 1-3 week latency after 4-8 week blocks and italic restorative rule at PDF 305 / p.294 (image); LDTE \"observed to last from 4-12 weeks\", T2 about equal to T1, \"individualised\", intensive vs extensive at PDF 375 / p.364 (image, with Fig 6.31 showing T2 starting at the end of T1 and gains rising during T2); 4-12 week stages at PDF 381 and 536 (text layer). Also missing the in-library supporting case for a short latency. Baseline line 183 checked. | Replace the Bompa sentence with: \"**Contested across the library:** Bompa & Haff (5th ed.) write that 'Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase,' and the Bompa entry [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]] repeats this as a 4-12 week delay. That matches neither Supertraining statement: 5.3.3 gives a 1-3 week latency to gains; Chapter 6 gives 4-12 weeks as how long the delayed effect lasts, starting when the block ends (Fig 6.31).\" Add `[[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]]` (4.1: speed-strength up 18-25% after a 10-day rest, PDF 213) to Related. |\n| heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label.md | GO | Checked: definition, concurrent/sequential, Russian practice, \"It is inadequate ...\", volleyball/basketball, periodised supplementary sports, at PDF 309 / p.298 (image); Siff attribution for the Verkhoshansky-naming sentence, \"Kuzwecov\", fitness-quality list, specialisation sentence, Enoka 1988, \"combinational or complementary training\", motivation example, at PDF 310 / p.299 (image); four questions at PDF 311. Baseline line 32 checked. Advisory: 5.5 as a whole reads as Siff's (Western framing, rugby and American football), but the rule only requires the naming passage to be attributed, and it is. | None. |\n| heuristic--circuit-progression-load-prescription-and-novice-pre-testing.md | GO | Checked at PDF 316 / p.305 (image): \"no such entity as the best circuit\", three levels, KISS, progression levers, 1 hour CCT limit, Russian (%) and Bulgarian (kg below) systems, pre-testing, 5 kg / 10 kg, three weeks then 6RM or 10RM, three months then 1RM. At PDF 317 / p.306 (image): 40-50 / 50-65 / 65-80% of (220 - age), monitoring sentence, Fig 5.20 caption \"A basic multi-level circuit\", 2 then 3 then up to 6 laps A-B, 3-6 laps A-C, 3-5 laps A-D. Baseline lines 61 and 119 checked. Inferences labelled. | None. |\n| heuristic--circuits-cannot-develop-sport-specific-strength-qualities.md | GO | Checked at PDF 323 / p.312 (image): both italic sentences, quality list, station-count sentence. Contested holds on the cited pages: over 75% 1RM / 5 reps / 2 min at PDF 315, \"as much as 80%\" at PDF 317, power and plyometric circuits at PDF 314, against \"not possible ... medium heavy, near maximal or explosive loading\" at PDF 323. Ch1 quote checked at PDF 91 (text layer): \"does not enhance strength and power sufficiently ... except possibly during the early off-season.\" Example labelled Inference. Baseline line 104 checked. | None. |\n| heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport.md | GO | Checked: Verkhoshansky 1963, 1972; \"forced training\"; predisposition examples; first and second stage (italic); team-sport competitions; \"firstly according to ability\", at PDF 306 / p.295 (image); single school or club at PDF 307 (image). Bompa comparison checked against the vault LTAD entry (specialisation generally not before about 14-15; three longitudinal studies). Baseline line 173 checked. | None. |\n| heuristic--equip-circuits-with-free-weights-and-pulleys-over-multistation-machines.md | GO | Checked at PDF 314 / p.303 (image): lowest priority for multi-purpose machines, hi-lo pulleys, \"fraction of the price\", safety \"minimal\", space \"inconsequential\", free weights \"far more effective\", load-change advantage, two-dimensional patterns. At PDF 315 / p.304 (image): seven machines for a clean, three for a squat, weakest joint position, named machines, partner hands grips, spotter. Isokinetic limited use at PDF 311; dumbbell and pulley circuits at PDF 322 (image). Baseline line 104 checked. | None. |\n| heuristic--no-supplementary-sport-is-categorically-good-or-bad.md | GO | Checked at PDF 310 / p.299 (image): full \"Some authors have been swift ...\" passage, counterproductive cardiovascular combinations, bodybuilding-style training for marathoners, variety and overtraining, \"intelligent circumspection\". Baseline lines 123 and 124 checked. Inferences labelled. | None. |\n| heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades.md | GO | Checked at PDF 308 / p.297 (image): preservation definition, Fig 1.32 reference, Fig 4.1 plan, \"experimental assessment ... categorisation\", Verkhoshansky 1969, 1970, cyclical system, means A-E; Fig 5.18 order jumping exercises, barbell exercises, jumps with weights, depth jumping, with dashed switch lines. Fig 5.19 at PDF 309 (image): year 1 A, B, C; year 2 B, C, D; year 3 C, D, E, each starting higher; Verkhoshansky 1977. \"long-term use of the same means\" at PDF 304 (image). Baseline line 107 checked. | None. |\n| heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages.md | FIX | Same misread strand as the novice case: \"it puts mixed sessions below the best variant while PDF p. 299 says mixed sessions gave 'A relatively larger effect'\" (different comparators, no contradiction). Missed the clearest internal tension for this entry's own rule 1: \"Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises in training\" (PDF 301 / p.290) against p.299 \"the sequence of means in the preparatory period (beginning with strength, then speed-strength means, or vice-versa) has no real significance for novices,\" and against PDF 288's \"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions.\" Fig 5.13 reading checked against the image (PDF 300): group 3 rises through all stages, group 2 flat in stage 3, group 1 flattens in stage 2 and dips in stage 3; no numeric axis. Verkhoshansky & Aganin 1970, \"average qualification\", \"natural training conditions\" checked. Baseline line 142 checked. | Replace the mixed-sessions clause with: \"and the first rule, 'Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises in training' (PDF p. 301), sits against the novice finding that this order 'has no real significance for novices' (PDF p. 299) and against 5.2.4's statement that resistance work should precede plyometrics 'in individual sessions' (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\" Keep the \"Least effective\" vs \"no statistically significant difference\" strand. Add `[[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]]` to Related. |\n| heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing.md | GO | Checked at PDF 319 / p.308 (image): Setchinov passage verbatim; Fig 5.25 AS and RS one-for-one; Fig 5.27 one AS then two RS (AS1, RS1, RS2, AS2, RS3, RS4); Fig 5.28 one AS then three RS; text for 5.27-5.28 at PDF 320 (image). Mechanism gap labelled Inference. | None. Optional: Fig 5.26 (starts with RS1, then AS1, AS2, RS2) is not described. |\n| principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation.md | FIX | Confidence. The entry rests `contested` on two definitions of the term and then says \"The two can coexist (reach the peak sooner with less volume).\" Ambiguous terminology is not a disagreement, and no other source or modern evidence is cited against the principle. As a Soviet-era prescription with no data on the page, the ceiling is `supported`. Quotes checked at PDF 307 / p.296 (image): definition, modelling, contingency plan, four means, statistical data, relative and starting strength claims; 20% sentence at PDF 305 (image). Baseline line 129 quoted faithfully (the baseline itself says \"Athletics Performance\"). Example labelled Inference. | Set `confidence: supported`. Change the heading \"**Contested inside the book (two definitions).**\" to \"**Two definitions in the book.**\" and keep the paragraph. |\n| principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means.md | GO | Checked at PDF 305 (definition) and PDF 306 / p.295 (image): \"far apart\", \"firstly according to ability\", convergence sentences, criterion, role split. Fig 5.16 (image): Sport A starts highest, M below it, M1-M3 lower, all converge at the right. Bondarchuk inference matches the vault entry (narrow performance bands in most events, snatch exception in men's 110 m hurdles). Baseline line 102 checked. | None. |\n| principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect.md | FIX | Locator points to the wrong sentence. Entry: the ranking \"requires 'experimental assessment' (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288).\" PDF 299's sentence is \"Experimental assessment of the cumulative effects of different variations of the combination of means is necessary,\" about combinations. The matching sentence is at PDF 308 / p.297: \"experimental assessment of the training effect and categorisation of the specific complex of means is necessary.\" All other quotes checked at PDF 302-303 (images): 5.3.2 opening, prerequisites, magnitude rule, two errors, repertoire, novelty, \"only once or twice\". Baseline line 102 checked. Example inference labelled. | Change to: \"which the authors say requires 'experimental assessment of the training effect and categorisation of the specific complex of means' (PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297).\" |\n\n## Duplicates\n\nNo entry restates an existing vault or staging entry without a new angle. Checked overlaps: the circuit claim vs Ch1 `claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals` (Ch5 adds the CWT verdict and rest-interval mechanism, and links it); concentrated loading vs Bompa and Issurin entries (different author, linked); early specialisation vs Bompa LTAD and the Russian sport-school table (linked). Batch 5a/5b entries cover 5.1-5.2 and do not duplicate 5.3-5.6.\n\nReciprocal links to add (not blocking): novice case and sequence heuristic to `claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers`; restoration heuristic to `claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear`.\n\n## Missing ideas (covered pages, not captured)\n\n1. PDF 299 / p.288: the \"mixed sessions\" regime is defined as strength and speed-strength sessions on the same day separated by a rest interval, with the authors crediting the after-effect and the \"contrasting of stimuli.\" No entry states what the regime actually was. (Folded into the novice-case fix above.)\n2. PDF 312 / p.301: one shared circuit can be individualised per client by contraction type, acceleration and velocity, posture, joint angle, hand and foot spacing. Not in any circuit entry; a short addition to the classification or progression entry would cover it.\n\nNothing else of weight is missing. The 5.3.1 principles of coupled effects and unity of physical and technical training are already quoted in the excess-volume claim; the ES-plus-plyometrics paragraph on PDF 301 is already atomized in the 4.2.1.6 electrostimulation entry.\n\n## Bompa finding (vault entry, out of scope for edits)\n\n`periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching` attributes to Siff and Verkhoshansky that gains \"may appear **4 to 12 weeks** after the concentrated block ends,\" and treats 4-12 weeks as the delay to the rebound. The vault entry reports its own source faithfully: Bompa & Haff 5e (PDF idx 61 of the Drive copy) says \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase.\" But against Supertraining 6e the attribution is wrong as a latency claim:\n- PDF 305 / p.294: gains after 4-8 week concentrated blocks \"may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks.\"\n- PDF 375 / p.364: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks\"; its duration T2 is \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage (T1)\"; Fig 6.31 shows T2 beginning at the end of T1 with gains rising during T2.\n- PDF 381 / p.370 and 305: 4-12 weeks is the length of effective unidirectional training periods. PDF 536 / p.525: \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks.\"\n\nSo in Supertraining 4-12 weeks is a duration (of the block or of the delayed effect), never the wait before gains appear. The error originates in Bompa & Haff; the vault entry inherits it and sharpens it (\"the longer/larger the loading, the longer the delay\"). Caveat: reference 83 could not be read in the Drive copy (no reference list found; the figure credit on PDF 66 cites Siff 2003, Supertraining). The 6e pages checked here are consistent with the 1999 Siff & Verkhoshansky figures they reproduce. Suggested vault fix, for Eric: \"Bompa & Haff cite Siff and Verkhoshansky for gains 4 to 12 weeks after the block; Supertraining itself gives a 1-3 week latency and 4-12 weeks as the duration of the delayed effect (PDF 305, 375).\"\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** 15 GO, 11 FIX, 0 NO-GO.\n\nRequired fixes:\n1. Deniskin: correct the Fig 5.14 Ss/Se description (both fall within stage 3; Se also ends below its stage-3 start).\n2. Junior sprinters: correct Fig 5.12 bar values (30 m LS combined about 0.25, 60 m LS 0.27 / 0.20 / 0.29, 100 m long about 0.48) and the sum comparisons; flag the 100 m edge bar as unclear.\n3. Novice sequencing: drop the mixed-sessions \"contradiction\"; add the PDF 288 session-order strand, the same-day definition of mixed sessions, and the link.\n4. Excess heavy strength: rewrite the \"volume, not intensity\" inference.\n5. Traditional circuit claim: label the 10-15 reps at over 80% judgment as Inference.\n6. Aerobic vs anaerobic circuits: narrow the contested strand to the anaerobic column vs its muscle-endurance sub-case; the PDF 317 scheme is a different target.\n7. Layout variants: label the superset-planning sentence as Inference.\n8. Allow restoration: place the 4-12 week misreading on Bompa & Haff's book, describe the Fig 6.31 timing, add the 4.1 delayed-lag link.\n9. Sequence jumps-barbell-depth: drop the mixed-sessions strand; add rule 1 vs PDF 299 and PDF 288; add the link.\n10. Accelerating specific adaptation: `contested` to `supported`; retitle the two-definitions note.\n11. Sequentially introduce means: move the \"experimental assessment\" locator from PDF 299 to PDF 308 / p.297.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"dde00e09-51d5-4b06-a6d0-62d0a54cde3b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:07:13.007Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"review_supertraining_ch5a_opus_round2.md\nreview_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md\nreview_supertraining_ch5b_opus_round2.md\nreview_supertraining_ch5b_opus.md\nreview_supertraining_ch5c_opus_round2.md\nreview_supertraining_ch5c_opus.md\n   14298 review_supertraining_ch5a_opus_round2.md\n   23089 review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md\n   20813 review_supertraining_ch5b_opus_round2.md\n   22692 review_supertraining_ch5b_opus.md\n   15940 review_supertraining_ch5c_opus_round2.md\n   29162 review_supertraining_ch5c_opus.md\n  125994 total\n# Review: Supertraining Ch 5c (sections 5.3-5.6), 26 entries\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent, instructed to falsify). Date: 2026-10-03.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 298-323 (printed 287-312), plus PDF 91, 375, 381, 536.\nPages opened as images by the reviewer (renders in `staging/_renders/r5c_p*.png`): 298, 300 (plus a 300 dpi crop of Fig 5.12), 301, 302, 303, 304, 305, 306, 307, 308, 309, 310, 312, 313, 314, 315, 316, 317, 318, 319, 320, 321, 322, 323, 375. Pages 381 and 536 were read from the text layer only (clean text, no figures or tables used from them). PDF 91 was checked in the text layer for the Ch1 quote.\nAlso checked: every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote against `docs/method-baseline.md` (lines 32, 61, 76, 102, 103, 104, 105, 107, 115, 119, 122, 123, 124, 129, 142, 151, 173, 182, 183, 184 and Tension 17 at line 261; all quotes and evidence tags match); all 74 wikilinks resolve in the vault or staging; the Issurin 2016 vault brief; the Issurin 2008 entries; the Bompa vault entries; the Bondarchuk transfer-ceiling entry; and Bompa & Haff 5e itself (Drive copy, PDF idx 61) for the Bompa finding.\n\n## Entry table\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means.md | FIX | Fig 5.14 reading is incomplete. Entry: \"Ss and Se both rise through stages 1-3, and Ss falls back from its stage-3 high at the stage-4 test.\" Image (PDF 301 / p.290): within stage 3 both curves fall (Ss about 700 to about 650; Se about 430 to about 375), and at the stage-4 test Se (about 390) is also below its stage-3 start, not only Ss. The rises happen across the rest weeks (8 and 16), which is the text's point. Everything else checked: stage layout weeks 1-7 / 8 / 9-15 / 16 / 17-21 / 22 / 23-24, Fmax about 103 to 110 to about 125 kgf, tmax about 0.42 to about 0.27 s, all quotes, \"complete rest\" wording, Deniskin 1976. | Replace the last sentence of the figure description with: \"Ss and Se jump after each rest week (weeks 8 and 16) and are highest at the start of stage 3; both then decline through stage 3 and sit below that peak at the stage-4 test (Ss about 600-620, Se about 390). Values read by eye from the image.\" |\n| case-study--junior-sprinters-short-and-long-jump-training-effects.md | FIX | Bar values misread. From a 300 dpi crop of Fig 5.12 (PDF 300 / p.289; left axis 0-0.5 s, right panel 0-1.0 s), short / long / combined: 30 m LS about 0.39 / 0.08 / 0.24-0.25 (entry: 0.29 combined); 30 m SS about 0.08 / 0.14 / 0.19 (entry 0.07 / 0.13 / 0.20, fine); 60 m LS about 0.27 / 0.20 / 0.29 (entry 0.29 / 0.22 / 0.33); 60 m SS about 0.07 / 0.38 / 0.42 (fine); 100 m LS about 0.29 / 0.48 / 1.0 (entry 0.27 / 0.50 / 1.0, close). With the corrected values, combined is below the sum at 30 m SS (0.19 vs 0.22), 60 m LS (0.29 vs 0.47) and roughly equal at 60 m SS (0.42 vs 0.45); only 100 m exceeds the sum. The contested reading itself holds and is stronger: short beats combined at 30 m LS by about 0.15 s. Note also an unexplained white outlined bar at the right edge of the 100 m panel reaching 1.0 (may be the frame); treat as unclear. Quotes, \"eight months\", group definitions checked. | Replace the bar-height sentence with: \"30 m LS about 0.39 / 0.08 / 0.25 s; 30 m SS about 0.08 / 0.14 / 0.19 s; 60 m LS about 0.27 / 0.20 / 0.29 s; 60 m SS about 0.07 / 0.38 / 0.42 s; 100 m LS about 0.29 / 0.48 / 1.0 s. An outlined bar at the right edge of the 100 m panel also reaches 1.0; it may be part of the frame and is unclear.\" In When NOT to apply replace the comparison sentences with: \"the short-jump group, not the combined group, shows the largest drop at 30 m LS (about 0.39 vs 0.25 s). Against the sum of the two single groups, the combined bar is below it at 30 m SS (0.19 vs 0.22) and 60 m LS (0.29 vs 0.47), about equal at 60 m SS (0.42 vs 0.45), and above it only at 100 m LS (1.0 vs 0.77).\" Update the Example to \"about 0.29 s for short jumps alone and about 0.48 s for long jumps alone\". |\n| case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments.md | FIX | One of the three \"contested\" strands is a misreading. Entry: p.300's \"slightly smaller effect\" for mixed sessions vs p.299's \"A relatively larger effect\" is called a contradiction. It is not: p.299's comparator is \"the above-mentioned variants\" (strength-first vs speed-strength-first across the preparatory period); p.300's comparator is the best variant, the Fig 5.13 complexes. Mixed > order variants and best > mixed are compatible. The other two strands are real and on the cited pages: p.300 ranks strength-then-speed-strength in one session \"Least effective\" while p.299 found \"no statistically significant difference\"; p.299 says order \"has no real significance for novices\" while experiment 3 on the same page favours weights then depth jumps. Missed sharper strand: PDF 288 / p.277 (already atomized in `claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers`) says resistance plus plyometrics beats plyometrics alone \"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions,\" against p.299's within-workout novice null result (different populations, but the book states the session-order rule flatly). Also missing: p.299 defines the mixed regime as separate sessions \"with a rest interval between sessions on the same day.\" Names checked against the image (Purvin, Khodykin, Deniskin, Tatian, Chernousov). Baseline line 173 checked. | Delete the sentence beginning \"The first sentence ranks mixed sessions below the best variant, while PDF p. 299 says mixed sessions gave 'A relatively larger effect';\" and keep the two real strands. Add: \"The book also states, in 5.2.4, that resistance plus plyometrics works best 'provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions' (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277), which sits against experiment 4's null within-session result in novices.\" In item 2 add: \"(the mixed regime used separate sessions 'with a rest interval between sessions on the same day', PDF p. 299).\" Add `[[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]]` to Related. |\n| claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md | FIX | Inference misstates the source. Entry: \"Inference: the authors' warning is about volume, not intensity, which fits that rule.\" PDF 302 / p.291 also blames intensity: Filinov, \"excessively heavy strength loads diminish the force and speed of boxers' punches,\" and \"excessive maximum strength training can impair speed-strength and technical skill.\" Everything else checked against the image: 30% \"as much as\", Kotz, Filinov, Deniskin 1976, 18 weeks (54 workouts), 50-60 sessions / about 12 weeks, \"rubber-stamp\", Siff attribution for the Verkhoshansky-naming passage (correct). | Replace the inference with: \"Inference: the authors' warning covers both prolonged high volume (Deniskin) and excessive heaviness (Filinov; 'excessive maximum strength training'), so it supports the in-season 'reduce volume' half of that rule but does not by itself endorse holding heavy intensity during specialised speed phases.\" |\n| claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits.md | FIX | Unlabelled writer judgment. Entry: \"10-15 repetitions at over 80% of 1RM is not possible for most lifters; treat that number as unclear.\" The image (PDF 313 / p.302) confirms the book's wording; the impossibility claim is the writer's. All quotes checked against PDF 312 / p.301 and 313 / p.302 images: \"a moderate compromise\", Morgan and Adamson 1953 Leeds (PDF 311 text), \"It has been shown\", \"A literature review reveals\", alternation on separate days superior, \"This is not to state that circuit training is of little value\". Baseline line 115 checked. | Replace with: \"Inference: 10-15 repetitions at over 80% of 1RM is beyond most lifters' capacity, so treat that figure as doubtful even though the image confirms the wording.\" |\n| framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters.md | FIX | Contested is overstated in one strand. The PDF 317 / p.306 scheme is explicitly \"applicable to the average programme designed to moderately enhance muscle endurance and cardiovascular performance,\" a different target from the anaerobic column, so it is not a third figure for the same quantity. The real clash is within one category: anaerobic circuits defined as 3-8 reps at over 70% 1RM, then the anaerobic muscle-endurance sub-case at about 50% 1RM and about 50 reps (image confirms \"50\"). Every table value checked against the image, PDF 315 / p.304: 20-40, 30%, 5-10, 1-4 min, 3-8, 3-5, 70%, 50% / 50 / 4, 75% / 5 / 2 min / 3, 20-30 s. PDF 316 agility/restoration and PDF 317 40-50% of 10RM/5RM/1RM and 80% checked. Baseline line 124 checked. | Replace the contested paragraph opening with: \"**Contested inside the book (muscle-endurance figures).** The anaerobic column says 3-8 reps at over 70% of 1RM, but the muscle-endurance sub-case of the same anaerobic category says about 50% of 1RM for about 50 reps (the image confirms '50'), which falls outside both columns. The 40-50% of 10RM, 5RM or 1RM scheme on PDF p. 317 is for a different target ('moderately enhance muscle endurance and cardiovascular performance') and is a separate prescription, not a contradiction.\" Keep the rest. |\n| framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour.md | FIX | Unlabelled application in When to apply: \"The superset equivalence is useful when writing programmes: a two-exercise superset can be planned and progressed with the same rules as a circuit.\" The book only states the equivalence (PDF 320 / p.309, Fig 5.29). Figures 5.29-5.33 checked against images PDF 320-321; pyramid 40-80% 1RM and 10 to 4 reps, stripping about 10 kg, 60 kg x10 to 100 kg x6 then 90/80/70 kg, spotter quote checked at PDF 322; water resistance quote checked at PDF 317; dripping water at PDF 318. | Prefix: \"Inference: the superset equivalence is useful when writing programmes ...\" |\n| framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading.md | GO | Checked: Kudelin 1980, Budokho 1978, Mironenko 1980, 4-12 weeks, 20% macrocycle saving, Fedorova 1975 / Platonov & Monograov 1977 / Verkhoshansky 1977, low intensity of means, 23-25% of yearly volume, all at PDF 305 / p.294 (image); repeat wording at PDF 375 / p.364 (image); dual definition of \"acceleration\" at PDF 307 (image). Issurin contest points to the conjugate entry, whose Issurin 2016 summary matches the vault brief and targets Verkhoshansky's CU model, not A-T-R. Issurin 2008 \"minimal number of targets\" matches the vault principle entry (PDF 37). Example labelled Inference. Baseline line 184 checked. | None. Optional: PDF 381 / p.370 adds \"a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" for concentrated loading, which could join item 1. |\n| framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means.md | GO | Checked: two systems, concurrent definition, \"coupled successive system\", Verkhoshansky 1978, \"only average results\" for elites, SPP sentence, all at PDF 303 / p.292 (image; the writer had not opened this page, wording confirmed); \"degree of contrast\", complex of means for qualified athletes, speed-endurance and speed-strength sequences, Fig 5.15 labels, caption and overlap note, at PDF 304 / p.293 (image). Issurin 2016 claims match the vault brief (pp. 6-7, 11: CU evidence insufficient for combat, team and swimming; fitness gains without team practice or competition outcomes; suited to narrow-target sports) and are explicitly about Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model; the brief records the A-T-R model as Issurin's own and the one with 28-study support. Issurin 2008 PDF 44 / p.33 \"high-level athletes\" matches the vault entry. Naming-overlap note on \"complex system\" is accurate. Baseline line 182 checked. | None. |\n| framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification.md | GO | Checked: CCT / ICT / MCT and six subtypes, 15 min aerobic add-on, 10-15 min warm-up circuit, 5-10 min cool-down, \"super circuit\", fartlek, at PDF 313 / p.302 (image); circuit types list at PDF 314 / p.303 (image); technique quote at PDF 315; Table 5.4 at PDF 322 / p.311 (image): Circuit A 14 stations from 12-min fixed cycle, Circuit B 11 stations from 4-min fixed cycle (the text layer's \"Hart squats\" is \"Half squats\"). PDF 323 limit quote checked. Baseline line 122 checked. | None. |\n| framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation.md | GO | Checked: initial-stage general response, \"transfer of fitness is very distinct\", selective adaptation, \"determined entirely\", the four principles and \"do not exclude all of the traditional principles\", at PDF 305 / p.294 (image). Example labelled Inference. Baseline line 103 checked (quote truncated at a natural point, no distortion). | None. |\n| framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means.md | GO | Checked at PDF 298 / p.287 (image): cumulative-effect sentence, two variants, two observations, \"assess ... categorise\", endurance sequence and citations (Ozolin 1959, 1970; Volkov 1963; Yakovlev 1957; Borisov 1979), \"no definitive practical recommendations\". Image reads \"simply a summation\" (text layer \"amply\"), as the entry says. Synergy sentence continues at PDF 299. The non-contradiction with the 5.3.3 speed-endurance order is argued correctly. Baseline line 182 checked. | None. |\n| framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks.md | GO | Checked: definition, \"selectively reacts\", specialisation of systems / of ability, four tasks verbatim, at PDF 307 / p.296 (image); Fig 5.17 boxes and the six-ability list at PDF 308 / p.297 (image). Flexibility-strength at PDF 323 checked. Baseline line 76 and Tension 17 (line 261, unvalidated 0.60/0.80 cut-offs) checked. Example labelled Inference. | None. |\n| heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md | FIX | The cross-library point is right but aimed at the wrong place. Entry: \"Bompa's entry ... attributes to Siff and Verkhoshansky that 'gains may appear 4 to 12 weeks after the concentrated block ends.'\" The vault entry faithfully reports Bompa & Haff 5e, which says (Drive PDF idx 61): \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase (figure 2.10).\" So the misreading is in Bompa & Haff's book; the vault entry inherits it and then sharpens it into a delay (\"the longer/larger the loading, the longer the delay\"; \"delayed-rebound timing (4-12 wk)\"). All Supertraining figures checked: 1-3 week latency after 4-8 week blocks and italic restorative rule at PDF 305 / p.294 (image); LDTE \"observed to last from 4-12 weeks\", T2 about equal to T1, \"individualised\", intensive vs extensive at PDF 375 / p.364 (image, with Fig 6.31 showing T2 starting at the end of T1 and gains rising during T2); 4-12 week stages at PDF 381 and 536 (text layer). Also missing the in-library supporting case for a short latency. Baseline line 183 checked. | Replace the Bompa sentence with: \"**Contested across the library:** Bompa & Haff (5th ed.) write that 'Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase,' and the Bompa entry [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]] repeats this as a 4-12 week delay. That matches neither Supertraining statement: 5.3.3 gives a 1-3 week latency to gains; Chapter 6 gives 4-12 weeks as how long the delayed effect lasts, starting when the block ends (Fig 6.31).\" Add `[[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]]` (4.1: speed-strength up 18-25% after a 10-day rest, PDF 213) to Related. |\n| heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label.md | GO | Checked: definition, concurrent/sequential, Russian practice, \"It is inadequate ...\", volleyball/basketball, periodised supplementary sports, at PDF 309 / p.298 (image); Siff attribution for the Verkhoshansky-naming sentence, \"Kuzwecov\", fitness-quality list, specialisation sentence, Enoka 1988, \"combinational or complementary training\", motivation example, at PDF 310 / p.299 (image); four questions at PDF 311. Baseline line 32 checked. Advisory: 5.5 as a whole reads as Siff's (Western framing, rugby and American football), but the rule only requires the naming passage to be attributed, and it is. | None. |\n| heuristic--circuit-progression-load-prescription-and-novice-pre-testing.md | GO | Checked at PDF 316 / p.305 (image): \"no such entity as the best circuit\", three levels, KISS, progression levers, 1 hour CCT limit, Russian (%) and Bulgarian (kg below) systems, pre-testing, 5 kg / 10 kg, three weeks then 6RM or 10RM, three months then 1RM. At PDF 317 / p.306 (image): 40-50 / 50-65 / 65-80% of (220 - age), monitoring sentence, Fig 5.20 caption \"A basic multi-level circuit\", 2 then 3 then up to 6 laps A-B, 3-6 laps A-C, 3-5 laps A-D. Baseline lines 61 and 119 checked. Inferences labelled. | None. |\n| heuristic--circuits-cannot-develop-sport-specific-strength-qualities.md | GO | Checked at PDF 323 / p.312 (image): both italic sentences, quality list, station-count sentence. Contested holds on the cited pages: over 75% 1RM / 5 reps / 2 min at PDF 315, \"as much as 80%\" at PDF 317, power and plyometric circuits at PDF 314, against \"not possible ... medium heavy, near maximal or explosive loading\" at PDF 323. Ch1 quote checked at PDF 91 (text layer): \"does not enhance strength and power sufficiently ... except possibly during the early off-season.\" Example labelled Inference. Baseline line 104 checked. | None. |\n| heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport.md | GO | Checked: Verkhoshansky 1963, 1972; \"forced training\"; predisposition examples; first and second stage (italic); team-sport competitions; \"firstly according to ability\", at PDF 306 / p.295 (image); single school or club at PDF 307 (image). Bompa comparison checked against the vault LTAD entry (specialisation generally not before about 14-15; three longitudinal studies). Baseline line 173 checked. | None. |\n| heuristic--equip-circuits-with-free-weights-and-pulleys-over-multistation-machines.md | GO | Checked at PDF 314 / p.303 (image): lowest priority for multi-purpose machines, hi-lo pulleys, \"fraction of the price\", safety \"minimal\", space \"inconsequential\", free weights \"far more effective\", load-change advantage, two-dimensional patterns. At PDF 315 / p.304 (image): seven machines for a clean, three for a squat, weakest joint position, named machines, partner hands grips, spotter. Isokinetic limited use at PDF 311; dumbbell and pulley circuits at PDF 322 (image). Baseline line 104 checked. | None. |\n| heuristic--no-supplementary-sport-is-categorically-good-or-bad.md | GO | Checked at PDF 310 / p.299 (image): full \"Some authors have been swift ...\" passage, counterproductive cardiovascular combinations, bodybuilding-style training for marathoners, variety and overtraining, \"intelligent circumspection\". Baseline lines 123 and 124 checked. Inferences labelled. | None. |\n| heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades.md | GO | Checked at PDF 308 / p.297 (image): preservation definition, Fig 1.32 reference, Fig 4.1 plan, \"experimental assessment ... categorisation\", Verkhoshansky 1969, 1970, cyclical system, means A-E; Fig 5.18 order jumping exercises, barbell exercises, jumps with weights, depth jumping, with dashed switch lines. Fig 5.19 at PDF 309 (image): year 1 A, B, C; year 2 B, C, D; year 3 C, D, E, each starting higher; Verkhoshansky 1977. \"long-term use of the same means\" at PDF 304 (image). Baseline line 107 checked. | None. |\n| heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages.md | FIX | Same misread strand as the novice case: \"it puts mixed sessions below the best variant while PDF p. 299 says mixed sessions gave 'A relatively larger effect'\" (different comparators, no contradiction). Missed the clearest internal tension for this entry's own rule 1: \"Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises in training\" (PDF 301 / p.290) against p.299 \"the sequence of means in the preparatory period (beginning with strength, then speed-strength means, or vice-versa) has no real significance for novices,\" and against PDF 288's \"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions.\" Fig 5.13 reading checked against the image (PDF 300): group 3 rises through all stages, group 2 flat in stage 3, group 1 flattens in stage 2 and dips in stage 3; no numeric axis. Verkhoshansky & Aganin 1970, \"average qualification\", \"natural training conditions\" checked. Baseline line 142 checked. | Replace the mixed-sessions clause with: \"and the first rule, 'Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises in training' (PDF p. 301), sits against the novice finding that this order 'has no real significance for novices' (PDF p. 299) and against 5.2.4's statement that resistance work should precede plyometrics 'in individual sessions' (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\" Keep the \"Least effective\" vs \"no statistically significant difference\" strand. Add `[[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]]` to Related. |\n| heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing.md | GO | Checked at PDF 319 / p.308 (image): Setchinov passage verbatim; Fig 5.25 AS and RS one-for-one; Fig 5.27 one AS then two RS (AS1, RS1, RS2, AS2, RS3, RS4); Fig 5.28 one AS then three RS; text for 5.27-5.28 at PDF 320 (image). Mechanism gap labelled Inference. | None. Optional: Fig 5.26 (starts with RS1, then AS1, AS2, RS2) is not described. |\n| principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation.md | FIX | Confidence. The entry rests `contested` on two definitions of the term and then says \"The two can coexist (reach the peak sooner with less volume).\" Ambiguous terminology is not a disagreement, and no other source or modern evidence is cited against the principle. As a Soviet-era prescription with no data on the page, the ceiling is `supported`. Quotes checked at PDF 307 / p.296 (image): definition, modelling, contingency plan, four means, statistical data, relative and starting strength claims; 20% sentence at PDF 305 (image). Baseline line 129 quoted faithfully (the baseline itself says \"Athletics Performance\"). Example labelled Inference. | Set `confidence: supported`. Change the heading \"**Contested inside the book (two definitions).**\" to \"**Two definitions in the book.**\" and keep the paragraph. |\n| principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means.md | GO | Checked at PDF 305 (definition) and PDF 306 / p.295 (image): \"far apart\", \"firstly according to ability\", convergence sentences, criterion, role split. Fig 5.16 (image): Sport A starts highest, M below it, M1-M3 lower, all converge at the right. Bondarchuk inference matches the vault entry (narrow performance bands in most events, snatch exception in men's 110 m hurdles). Baseline line 102 checked. | None. |\n| principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect.md | FIX | Locator points to the wrong sentence. Entry: the ranking \"requires 'experimental assessment' (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288).\" PDF 299's sentence is \"Experimental assessment of the cumulative effects of different variations of the combination of means is necessary,\" about combinations. The matching sentence is at PDF 308 / p.297: \"experimental assessment of the training effect and categorisation of the specific complex of means is necessary.\" All other quotes checked at PDF 302-303 (images): 5.3.2 opening, prerequisites, magnitude rule, two errors, repertoire, novelty, \"only once or twice\". Baseline line 102 checked. Example inference labelled. | Change to: \"which the authors say requires 'experimental assessment of the training effect and categorisation of the specific complex of means' (PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297).\" |\n\n## Duplicates\n\nNo entry restates an existing vault or staging entry without a new angle. Checked overlaps: the circuit claim vs Ch1 `claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals` (Ch5 adds the CWT verdict and rest-interval mechanism, and links it); concentrated loading vs Bompa and Issurin entries (different author, linked); early specialisation vs Bompa LTAD and the Russian sport-school table (linked). Batch 5a/5b entries cover 5.1-5.2 and do not duplicate 5.3-5.6.\n\nReciprocal links to add (not blocking): novice case and sequence heuristic to `claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers`; restoration heuristic to `claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear`.\n\n## Missing ideas (covered pages, not captured)\n\n1. PDF 299 / p.288: the \"mixed sessions\" regime is defined as strength and speed-strength sessions on the same day separated by a rest interval, with the authors crediting the after-effect and the \"contrasting of stimuli.\" No entry states what the regime actually was. (Folded into the novice-case fix above.)\n2. PDF 312 / p.301: one shared circuit can be individualised per client by contraction type, acceleration and velocity, posture, joint angle, hand and foot spacing. Not in any circuit entry; a short addition to the classification or progression entry would cover it.\n\nNothing else of weight is missing. The 5.3.1 principles of coupled effects and unity of physical and technical training are already quoted in the excess-volume claim; the ES-plus-plyometrics paragraph on PDF 301 is already atomized in the 4.2.1.6 electrostimulation entry.\n\n## Bompa finding (vault entry, out of scope for edits)\n\n`periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching` attributes to Siff and Verkhoshansky that gains \"may appear **4 to 12 weeks** after the concentrated block ends,\" and treats 4-12 weeks as the delay to the rebound. The vault entry reports its own source faithfully: Bompa & Haff 5e (PDF idx 61 of the Drive copy) says \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase.\" But against Supertraining 6e the attribution is wrong as a latency claim:\n- PDF 305 / p.294: gains after 4-8 week concentrated blocks \"may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks.\"\n- PDF 375 / p.364: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks\"; its duration T2 is \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage (T1)\"; Fig 6.31 shows T2 beginning at the end of T1 with gains rising during T2.\n- PDF 381 / p.370 and 305: 4-12 weeks is the length of effective unidirectional training periods. PDF 536 / p.525: \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks.\"\n\nSo in Supertraining 4-12 weeks is a duration (of the block or of the delayed effect), never the wait before gains appear. The error originates in Bompa & Haff; the vault entry inherits it and sharpens it (\"the longer/larger the loading, the longer the delay\"). Caveat: reference 83 could not be read in the Drive copy (no reference list found; the figure credit on PDF 66 cites Siff 2003, Supertraining). The 6e pages checked here are consistent with the 1999 Siff & Verkhoshansky figures they reproduce. Suggested vault fix, for Eric: \"Bompa & Haff cite Siff and Verkhoshansky for gains 4 to 12 weeks after the block; Supertraining itself gives a 1-3 week latency and 4-12 weeks as the duration of the delayed effect (PDF 305, 375).\"\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** 15 GO, 11 FIX, 0 NO-GO.\n\nRequired fixes:\n1. Deniskin: correct the Fig 5.14 Ss/Se description (both fall within stage 3; Se also ends below its stage-3 start).\n2. Junior sprinters: correct Fig 5.12 bar values (30 m LS combined about 0.25, 60 m LS 0.27 / 0.20 / 0.29, 100 m long about 0.48) and the sum comparisons; flag the 100 m edge bar as unclear.\n3. Novice sequencing: drop the mixed-sessions \"contradiction\"; add the PDF 288 session-order strand, the same-day definition of mixed sessions, and the link.\n4. Excess heavy strength: rewrite the \"volume, not intensity\" inference.\n5. Traditional circuit claim: label the 10-15 reps at over 80% judgment as Inference.\n6. Aerobic vs anaerobic circuits: narrow the contested strand to the anaerobic column vs its muscle-endurance sub-case; the PDF 317 scheme is a different target.\n7. Layout variants: label the superset-planning sentence as Inference.\n8. Allow restoration: place the 4-12 week misreading on Bompa & Haff's book, describe the Fig 6.31 timing, add the 4.1 delayed-lag link.\n9. Sequence jumps-barbell-depth: drop the mixed-sessions strand; add rule 1 vs PDF 299 and PDF 288; add the link.\n10. Accelerating specific adaptation: `contested` to `supported`; retitle the two-definitions note.\n11. Sequentially introduce means: move the \"experimental assessment\" locator from PDF 299 to PDF 308 / p.297.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5af68c5d-ac89-417c-a752-345a269dfdba","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
