{"parentUuid":"98c7cbb1-818d-4d7d-ab6f-e2715471e8a9","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffkQp9bGVAjY8qig7RU3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012sfhLCpciUc8Q1duJdHABo","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; cat framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading.md; echo =====; cat heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md","description":"Read two style reference entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2222,"cache_read_input_tokens":90982,"output_tokens":204,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2222},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":204,"cache_read_input_tokens":90982,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2222,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2222,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_012sfhLCpciUc8Q1duJdHABo":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; cat framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading.md; echo =====; cat heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md","description":"Read two style reference entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffkQoryK1vELymbknYU6","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"0dd9c0b5-7373-4b21-ac08-5b2d044f975f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:07:18.280Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"0dd9c0b5-7373-4b21-ac08-5b2d044f975f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Exit code 1\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3.3\"\ntopic: concentrated loading conditions\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, block-periodization, volume, programming, advanced]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# When Concentrated Unidirectional Loading Works: A Diverse Complex of Means, Low-Intensity Means, and 23-25% of the Year's Volume in One Month\n\n**What it is** — The authors set out the working conditions for the concentrated phases of the conjugate sequence system (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image):\n1. **Scale and duration.** \"Unidirectional emphasis on qualities such as strength or endurance may be used not only in individual workouts but also during each microcycle (Kudelin, 1980; Budokho et al, 1978; Mironenko, 1980). Research has shown that unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective, especially if the intensity is gradually increased for each form of loading in the given cycle.\"\n2. **Diversity within one emphasis.** \"unidirectional loading is effective only if one uses a diverse complex of means with a single primary emphasis, together with a variety of training methods (Fedorova et al, 1975; Platonov & Monograov, 1977; Verkhoshansky, 1977).\"\n3. **Low intensity of the means.** \"an important condition for implementing concentrated loading is the relatively low intensity of the means, since the frequent use of these means already intensifies the training.\"\n4. **Definition.** \"the loading may be considered concentrated if its volume in the month in which it is applied constitutes 23-25% of the general volume for the year.\"\n5. **Payoff claimed.** \"the effectiveness of the conjugate sequence system with advanced athletes is such that the training volume over a macrocycle can be reduced by as much as 20%, a phenomenon referred to as the acceleration of specific adaptation (see 5.4.2).\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' one stated reason is condition 3: concentration \"already intensifies the training,\" so intense means on top of it would overload. Chapter 6 repeats this in the same words (\"Concentration of unidirectional loading already intensifies the training process, and one should not intensify it further with high intensity means\"; PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). No reason is given for the 23-25% threshold beyond the definition itself, and no source is named for the 20% volume saving.\n\n**When to apply** — Planning a concentrated block for an advanced athlete: fill it with several different exercises that share one emphasis, keep each one moderate in intensity, and expect the month's volume to be about a quarter of the year's. Eric's baseline: \"Training residual effects set the retraining cadence: aerobic capacity and max strength both need re-exposure roughly every 30 days; speed needs re-exposure roughly every 5 days\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 184, Inferred). Inference: a 4-12 week unidirectional block runs past those windows for the qualities it does not train, so the baseline's retention dose would still be needed alongside it; the authors do not address retention of other qualities here.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors warn on the same page that \"concentrated loading is very stressful and, besides increasing the potential for overtraining, it can decrease motor qualities such as speed-strength\" (see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]). The 23-25% figure is a definition, not a target derived from data. The \"acceleration of specific adaptation\" is defined here as a volume saving of up to 20%, but 5.4.2 defines it differently, as \"advancing the stage when maximum sports proficiency is expected to be produced by the current training regime\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296); keep both meanings in view. **Contested by Issurin** for team and combat sports (see [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]). Issurin's own block model also concentrates workloads but on a minimal number of targets per block, not a single one ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]).\n\n**Example** — Inference: a 4-week concentrated strength block in which squats, pulls, presses and loaded jumps all serve the strength emphasis, each at moderate loads, with that month carrying close to a quarter of the year's strength volume. The authors give the rule, not a worked plan.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]]\n(eval):1: ==== not found","is_error":true,"tool_use_id":"toolu_012sfhLCpciUc8Q1duJdHABo"}]},"uuid":"2bcdaff8-b13a-475f-b14d-f7eb06036803","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:07:18.386Z","toolUseResult":"Error: Exit code 1\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3.3\"\ntopic: concentrated loading conditions\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, block-periodization, volume, programming, advanced]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# When Concentrated Unidirectional Loading Works: A Diverse Complex of Means, Low-Intensity Means, and 23-25% of the Year's Volume in One Month\n\n**What it is** — The authors set out the working conditions for the concentrated phases of the conjugate sequence system (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image):\n1. **Scale and duration.** \"Unidirectional emphasis on qualities such as strength or endurance may be used not only in individual workouts but also during each microcycle (Kudelin, 1980; Budokho et al, 1978; Mironenko, 1980). Research has shown that unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective, especially if the intensity is gradually increased for each form of loading in the given cycle.\"\n2. **Diversity within one emphasis.** \"unidirectional loading is effective only if one uses a diverse complex of means with a single primary emphasis, together with a variety of training methods (Fedorova et al, 1975; Platonov & Monograov, 1977; Verkhoshansky, 1977).\"\n3. **Low intensity of the means.** \"an important condition for implementing concentrated loading is the relatively low intensity of the means, since the frequent use of these means already intensifies the training.\"\n4. **Definition.** \"the loading may be considered concentrated if its volume in the month in which it is applied constitutes 23-25% of the general volume for the year.\"\n5. **Payoff claimed.** \"the effectiveness of the conjugate sequence system with advanced athletes is such that the training volume over a macrocycle can be reduced by as much as 20%, a phenomenon referred to as the acceleration of specific adaptation (see 5.4.2).\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' one stated reason is condition 3: concentration \"already intensifies the training,\" so intense means on top of it would overload. Chapter 6 repeats this in the same words (\"Concentration of unidirectional loading already intensifies the training process, and one should not intensify it further with high intensity means\"; PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). No reason is given for the 23-25% threshold beyond the definition itself, and no source is named for the 20% volume saving.\n\n**When to apply** — Planning a concentrated block for an advanced athlete: fill it with several different exercises that share one emphasis, keep each one moderate in intensity, and expect the month's volume to be about a quarter of the year's. Eric's baseline: \"Training residual effects set the retraining cadence: aerobic capacity and max strength both need re-exposure roughly every 30 days; speed needs re-exposure roughly every 5 days\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 184, Inferred). Inference: a 4-12 week unidirectional block runs past those windows for the qualities it does not train, so the baseline's retention dose would still be needed alongside it; the authors do not address retention of other qualities here.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors warn on the same page that \"concentrated loading is very stressful and, besides increasing the potential for overtraining, it can decrease motor qualities such as speed-strength\" (see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]). The 23-25% figure is a definition, not a target derived from data. The \"acceleration of specific adaptation\" is defined here as a volume saving of up to 20%, but 5.4.2 defines it differently, as \"advancing the stage when maximum sports proficiency is expected to be produced by the current training regime\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296); keep both meanings in view. **Contested by Issurin** for team and combat sports (see [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]). Issurin's own block model also concentrates workloads but on a minimal number of targets per block, not a single one ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]).\n\n**Example** — Inference: a 4-week concentrated strength block in which squats, pulls, presses and loaded jumps all serve the strength emphasis, each at moderate loads, with that month carrying close to a quarter of the year's strength volume. The authors give the rule, not a worked plan.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]]\n(eval):1: ==== not found","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0dd9c0b5-7373-4b21-ac08-5b2d044f975f","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
