{"parentUuid":"59e2d6c7-c481-4c4e-bdab-8d1851af86e6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F9HyhN3QhLERLvr4XhgWen","type":"tool_result","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: elite-sports-performance--evans\nchapter: \"U3 L4, U3 L5\"\ntopic: monocycle, bicycle, and tricycle annual plan structures\ncategory: [evans-citing, periodization, annual-plan]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# Bompa's Three Annual-Plan Cycle Types: Monocycle, Bicycle, Tricycle\n\n**What it is** — Evans's slides (U3 L4 p.25-30, repeated identically in U3 L5 p.3-8), cited\nthroughout to \"Bompa, 2009,\" present three annual-plan structures. Monocycle: \"Only ONE\ncompetition phase and one major peak,\" recommended \"FOR NOVICE AND JUNIOR ATHLETES. LONG\nPREPARATORY PHASE TO ENSURE DEVELOPMENT OF THE TECHNICAL/TACTICAL/PHYSICAL DOMAINS,\" with a\nstated week distribution per 52-week cycle: Preparatory >= 32 weeks, Competitive 10-15 weeks,\nTransition 5 weeks. Bicycle: \"TWO competition phases and two major peaks,\" for \"ADVANCED OR\nELITE ATHLETES WHO QUALIFY FOR NATIONAL CHAMPIONSHIPS. TRY TO KEEP THE PREPARATORY PHASES AS\nLONG AS POSSIBLE TO ENSURE DEVELOPMENT,\" distribution per 26-week cycle: Preparatory 13 weeks,\nCompetitive 5-10 weeks, Transition 3 weeks. Tricycle: \"THREE competition phases and three\nmajor peaks,\" for \"ADVANCED OR INTERNATIONAL-LEVEL ATHLETES. SOLID FOUNDATION IS INSTALLED AND\nTHEY CAN TOLERATE MULTIPLE PEAKS. 3 OR MORE,\" distribution per 17-18-week cycle: Preparatory\n>= 8 weeks, Competitive 3-5 weeks, Transition 2-3 weeks. No book title beyond \"Bompa\" and the\nyear 2009 is given on the slides.\n\n**Why it works** — The slides state the recommended athlete level and week distribution for\neach cycle type but do not explain the physiological reasoning for why, for example, a\ntricycle's shorter preparatory phase (>=8 weeks versus a monocycle's >=32) is appropriate only\nfor athletes who already have \"a solid foundation... installed.\" The slides give no mechanism.\nInference: a trained athlete needing less time to re-establish base qualities before\nspecializing toward a peak is implied by the athlete-level guideline but not stated\nexplicitly on these slides.\n\n**When to apply** — As a first-pass structural choice when designing an annual plan: how many\ndistinct peaks does the competition calendar actually require, and does the athlete's training\nage support the correspondingly shorter preparatory phases of a multi-cycle plan? Evans's own\n\n#####\n---\n\n# Evans's Ten-Step Process for Building an Annual Plan (American Football Wide Receiver Example)\n\n**What it is** — Evans presents a ten-step sequence for building an annual plan (U3 L7),\napplied throughout to \"Sport: American Football, Position: Wide Receiver\" (p.2). Step One,\n\"Build Profile\": a table of System, Notes, and Rationale covering Autonomic NS\n(parasympathetic dominance, for \"fast recovery abilities, resiliency\"), Central NS (high\ncentral drive, for \"speed of movement, reaction time, tactical decision making\"),\nNeuromuscular (large fast-twitch percentage especially lower body, large concentric/eccentric\nloading, for \"speed of movement, deceleration qualities\"), Energy Supply Systems (per\ntime-motion analysis: alactic and aerobic capacity), Common Injuries (ankle, knee, hip,\nshoulder, fingers, informing \"exercise prescription, loading strategies\"), and\nAnthropometrics (low body fat percent, moderate cross-sectional area for force production and\ncollisions). Step Two: \"Setup Calendar\" (competitions, travel, intensive practice periods,\norganizational dates, holidays, training camps/facilities). Step Three: \"Setup Periodization\nModel\" (single/double/triple periodization, team-sport considerations - the same\nmonocycle/bicycle/tricycle menu named in\n[[framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]], though this lecture does not say which one the\n#####\n---\n\n# Bondarchuk Publishes 40 Normative Tables Linking Assistance-Exercise Results to Competitive-Result Bands, by Age and Qualification\n\n**What it is** — Tables 1-34 run PDF pp. 44-60, printed pp. 27-43 (this ingest sampled\nrepresentative tables rather than transcribing all 34); Tables 35-40 plus surrounding\nprose run PDF pp. 61-64, printed pp. 44-47, so the section holds 40 tables. Tables 1-34 give normative\n\"model characteristics\": for a given competitive-result band (e.g., 100m in 10.00-10.20s\nvs. 10.80-11.00s), a matching range of expected results in assistance exercises. Examples\npulled directly: **Table 1** ties 100m sprint splits (10m through 100m segments) to\nfive 100m-time bands. **Table 2** ties speed-strength markers (standing long jump,\ntriple/5-fold/10-fold jump from place, single-leg and alternate-leg jumps for time) to\nthose same five 100m bands; for example a 10.00-10.20s sprinter should stand-long-jump\nblock-periodization--issurin/:\n_book.md\ncase-study--bondarchuk-hammer-throw-block-mesocycles.md\ncase-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay.md\ncase-study--issurin-kaverin-canoe-kayak-block-periodization.md\ncase-study--klementiev-multiyear-workload-decline-with-podium-longevity.md\ncase-study--popov-aerobic-strength-microcycle.md\ncase-study--popov-competition-shortens-residual-effects.md\ncase-study--postactivation-potentiation-exercise-order-squat-study.md\ncase-study--sedykh-hammer-throw-explosive-strength-microcycle.md\ncase-study--sozin-canoe-kayak-age-14-15-performance-benchmark.md\ncase-study--troup-swimmers-maturation-timing-and-longevity.md\ncase-study--warmup-cooldown-injury-reduction-soccer.md\nclaim--morning-resistance-training-no-decrement-after-6hr-rest.md\nclaim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field.md\nclaim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability.md\nframework--accumulation-mesocycle-duration-and-monitoring.md\nframework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles.md\nframework--anaerobic-glycolytic-microcycle-design.md\nframework--athletic-talent-four-factor-heredity-model.md\nframework--in-season-team-sport-mini-block-microcycle.md\nframework--key-workout-and-key-exercise-concept.md\nframework--long-term-preparation-four-stage-model-and-directions.md\nframework--microcycle-types-and-load-wave-design.md\nframework--pre-competitive-microcycle-tuning.md\nframework--prolonging-residual-effects-with-mini-blocks.md\nframework--quadrennial-cycle-four-year-directions.md\nframework--realization-mesocycle-taper-prescription.md\nframework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle.md\nframework--transmutation-mesocycle-duration-and-overtraining-monitoring.md\nframework--workout-load-classification-and-rpe-mapping.md\nheuristic--dual-approach-initial-level-and-improvement-rate-giftedness.md\nheuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state.md\nheuristic--microcycle-construction-key-workout-rules.md\nheuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence.md\nheuristic--older-vs-younger-athlete-quadrennial-workload-differentiation.md\nheuristic--sensitive-periods-by-motor-ability-and-sex.md\nheuristic--two-a-day-workout-sequencing-by-dominant-ability.md\nprinciple--concentration-of-training-workloads.md\nprinciple--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout.md\n\nperiodization--bompa/:\n_book.md\nframework--eight-training-objectives.md\nframework--ltad-sequential-model.md\nframework--step-loading-paradigm.md\nframework--three-training-effects.md\nheuristic--biological-vs-training-age.md\nheuristic--fatigue-fitness-3to1-ratio.md\nprinciple--concentrated-loading-overreaching.md\nprinciple--individualization.md\nprinciple--multilateral-before-specialization.md\nprinciple--progressive-overload-threshold.md\nprinciple--specificity-of-adaptation.md\nprinciple--supercompensation-cycle.md","is_err