30; echo ####; sed -n 14,40p strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence.md | cut -c1-500","description":"Check Issurin 2016 brief and nine-step heuristic"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffkg5ykU3uHkNWa6Rqah","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"45c9bb0d-f93a-48b9-b008-3a61a9833573","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:10:54.370Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"45c9bb0d-f93a-48b9-b008-3a61a9833573","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WE8j5YmbXFaJ2KVgZ7q3rr","type":"tool_result","content":"15:evidence-locator: page\n26:This review distinguishes concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block models and provides a useful conceptual vocabulary for discussing concentrated workloads and sequencing.\n34:- The review emphasizes elite, subelite, and well-trained athletes across endurance, team, combat, dual, jumping, and strength/power settings. [p. 11]\n36:- It is a training-theory review, not evidence specific to collegiate American football.\n40:- Narrative review contrasting concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block-periodization models.\n46:- Concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance. [p. 6]\n48:- The author judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation. [p. 6]\n50:- The concentrated-unidirectional model was presented as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities. [p. 11]\n51:- Whose model is whose: the concentrated-unidirectional (CU) model is Verkhoshansky's; the multi-targeted model with 28-study support is Issurin's own A-T-R block model. [pp. 6-7, 11] So this review does not dispute Issurin's 2008 book; it repeats the book's critique of traditional planning. The real 2008-2016 difference is on lower-level athletes: the book (p. 33) says they do fine with medium\n58:- Evidence spans dissimilar sports and training levels; it does not supply validated block durations or effect estimates for collegiate American football.\n59:- The strong conclusion favoring multi-targeted block periodization is the author's synthesis and should not be treated as settled comparative evidence. [p. 11]\n65:## Evidence map\n67:| Claim | Locator | Evidence type |\n70:| Concentrated-unidirectional evidence and performance gaps | p. 6 | narrative synthesis |\n71:| Multi-targeted evidence claim and applicability boundaries | p. 11 | narrative synthesis/limitation |\n77:- [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]: Supertraining 5.3.3's system of successive concentrated blocks; it cites this review's limits for team, combat and swimming preparation as the case against using that system there","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"bfc5a603-ac7c-44f2-a75c-40f335bd3918","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:10:54.612Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"15:evidence-locator: page\n26:This review distinguishes concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block models and provides a useful conceptual vocabulary for discussing concentrated workloads and sequencing.\n34:- The review emphasizes elite, subelite, and well-trained athletes across endurance, team, combat, dual, jumping, and strength/power settings. [p. 11]\n36:- It is a training-theory review, not evidence specific to collegiate American football.\n40:- Narrative review contrasting concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block-periodization models.\n46:- Concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance. [p. 6]\n48:- The author judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation. [p. 6]\n50:- The concentrated-unidirectional model was presented as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities. [p. 11]\n51:- Whose model is whose: the concentrated-unidirectional (CU) model is Verkhoshansky's; the multi-targeted model with 28-study support is Issurin's own A-T-R block model. [pp. 6-7, 11] So this review does not dispute Issurin's 2008 book; it repeats the book's critique of traditional planning. The real 2008-2016 difference is on lower-level athletes: the book (p. 33) says they do fine with medium\n58:- Evidence spans dissimilar sports and training levels; it does not supply validated block durations or effect estimates for collegiate American football.\n59:- The strong conclusion favoring multi-targeted block periodization is the author's synthesis and should not be treated as settled comparative evidence. [p. 11]\n65:## Evidence map\n67:| Claim | Locator | Evidence type |\n70:| Concentrated-unidirectional evidence and performance gaps | p. 6 | narrative synthesis |\n71:| Multi-targeted evidence claim and applicability boundaries | p. 11 | narrative synthesis/limitation |\n77:- [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]: Supertraining 5.3.3's system of successive concentrated blocks; it cites this review's limits for team, combat and swimming preparation as the case against using that system there","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"45c9bb0d-f93a-48b9-b008-3a61a9833573","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
