{"parentUuid":"3d501f4e-297c-441e-b9b4-b981f824d1c8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014zso2x1Bbh6rieXS5zJq93","type":"tool_result","content":"**What it is** — Issurin gives a fixed nine-step sequence for constructing an annual\nprogram (Table 4.2, PDF p. 138, printed p. 127), illustrated stepwise in Figures 4.1-4.4\n(PDF pp. 139-141, printed pp. 128-130; verified against the page images): (1) determine\nthe target and mandatory competitions, normally taken from the international/national/\nregional calendar; (2) determine the terms and duration of training stages and periods\n(\"in terms of target competitions and methodology\"); (3) divide training stages into\nseveral meso- and microcycles; (4) plan additional competitions and trials to \"finalize\nthe training stages and to diversify the preparatory program\"; (5) plan training camps\n(objectives, terms, places), which also covers altitude preparation (Ch. 5); (6) plan\nmedical and sport-specific tests, with the initial medical exam early season; (7)\ncalculate integrative workload characteristics per month (total mileage, number of\nlifts, throws, etc.); (8) calculate the same for the full annual cycle, compared against\nprevious years to reveal \"weak\" points; (9) correct the plan following that review (\"as\na rule, the first version needs correction and this operation is obligatory\"). Figures\n4.1-4.4 plot competitions on four levels against a month-by-month grid that fills in\nstages/periods, meso/microcycles and training camps in the later figures as each step\nis completed. Figure 4.1 places the national championship (NCh) as the Level A target,\nthe national cup (NCup) at Level B, and the winter cup (WC) and regional trials (RT) at\nLevel C (caption: \"targeted (level A) and mandatory competitions (Levels B and C)\").\nLevel D is empty in Figures 4.1-4.2 and is filled only in Figures 4.3-4.4, with the\ndotted arrows of the additional competitions and trials planned at step 4 (PDF pp.\n139-141, printed pp. 128-130). The **optimal design places important competitions at the end of a\ntraining stage** (Fig. 4.2 caption, PDF p. 139, printed p. 128). The plan is not\nconsidered final at step 9: \"it must first be reviewed by the administration, colleagues\nand athletes and with repeated analysis, will lead to corrections\" (PDF p. 141, printed\np. 130) before it \"receives the final version status that is used for realization.\"\n\nLayered on this procedure is a seasonal content trend (Table 4.3, PDF p. 142, printed p.\n131; verified against the page image): early season favors continuous/uniform/\nalternating methods in the accumulation mesocycle and a wide range of fitness\n**What it is** — Issurin's annual cycle is built from a sequence of **training stages**,\neach one containing all three mesocycle-blocks (accumulation, transmutation, realization) in\norder, ending in a competition (PDF p. 38, printed p. 27). Each training stage \"gives the\nappearance of an annual cycle in miniature\": a block resembling a preparatory period\n(accumulation), one resembling a competition period (transmutation), and a tapering block\nending in competitive performance (realization) (PDF p. 39, printed p. 28). The duration of\na training stage is set by how long the constituent residual training effects can be\nsuperimposed (Figure 1.8; see\n[[claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]]) and \"should be about two\nmonths,\" though Issurin notes stages can run shorter close to season peaking or longer\nearlier in the season, in which case \"special measures should be used to prolong residual\ntraining effects\" (developed in Chapter 3, now ingested; see\n[[framework--prolonging-residual-effects-with-mini-blocks]] and `_book.md`).\nThe number of training stages in a season \"usually varies from four to seven,\" depending on\nthe sport's competition calendar (PDF p. 40, printed p. 29, Figure 1.9). Issurin lists four\nbenefits of this design over the traditional model at this point in the text (PDF p. 40,\nprinted p. 29): the model allows a reduction in total mileage and time spent training\nwithout substantially changing the total number of workouts; monitoring of trainedness is\nmore purposeful and effective, because the reduced number of targeted abilities allows more\nappropriate tests and an easier dose-effect analysis; psychological traits improve because","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"82b74521-90f9-43a0-8590-7f53888fa45a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:11:06.720Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"**What it is** — Issurin gives a fixed nine-step sequence for constructing an annual\nprogram (Table 4.2, PDF p. 138, printed p. 127), illustrated stepwise in Figures 4.1-4.4\n(PDF pp. 139-141, printed pp. 128-130; verified against the page images): (1) determine\nthe target and mandatory competitions, normally taken from the international/national/\nregional calendar; (2) determine the terms and duration of training stages and periods\n(\"in terms of target competitions and methodology\"); (3) divide training stages into\nseveral meso- and microcycles; (4) plan additional competitions and trials to \"finalize\nthe training stages and to diversify the preparatory program\"; (5) plan training camps\n(objectives, terms, places), which also covers altitude preparation (Ch. 5); (6) plan\nmedical and sport-specific tests, with the initial medical exam early season; (7)\ncalculate integrative workload characteristics per month (total mileage, number of\nlifts, throws, etc.); (8) calculate the same for the full annual cycle, compared against\nprevious years to reveal \"weak\" points; (9) correct the plan following that review (\"as\na rule, the first version needs correction and this operation is obligatory\"). Figures\n4.1-4.4 plot competitions on four levels against a month-by-month grid that fills in\nstages/periods, meso/microcycles and training camps in the later figures as each step\nis completed. Figure 4.1 places the national championship (NCh) as the Level A target,\nthe national cup (NCup) at Level B, and the winter cup (WC) and regional trials (RT) at\nLevel C (caption: \"targeted (level A) and mandatory competitions (Levels B and C)\").\nLevel D is empty in Figures 4.1-4.2 and is filled only in Figures 4.3-4.4, with the\ndotted arrows of the additional competitions and trials planned at step 4 (PDF pp.\n139-141, printed pp. 128-130). The **optimal design places important competitions at the end of a\ntraining stage** (Fig. 4.2 caption, PDF p. 139, printed p. 128). The plan is not\nconsidered final at step 9: \"it must first be reviewed by the administration, colleagues\nand athletes and with repeated analysis, will lead to corrections\" (PDF p. 141, printed\np. 130) before it \"receives the final version status that is used for realization.\"\n\nLayered on this procedure is a seasonal content trend (Table 4.3, PDF p. 142, printed p.\n131; verified against the page image): early season favors continuous/uniform/\nalternating methods in the accumulation mesocycle and a wide range of fitness\n**What it is** — Issurin's annual cycle is built from a sequence of **training stages**,\neach one containing all three mesocycle-blocks (accumulation, transmutation, realization) in\norder, ending in a competition (PDF p. 38, printed p. 27). Each training stage \"gives the\nappearance of an annual cycle in miniature\": a block resembling a preparatory period\n(accumulation), one resembling a competition period (transmutation), and a tapering block\nending in competitive performance (realization) (PDF p. 39, printed p. 28). The duration of\na training stage is set by how long the constituent residual training effects can be\nsuperimposed (Figure 1.8; see\n[[claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]]) and \"should be about two\nmonths,\" though Issurin notes stages can run shorter close to season peaking or longer\nearlier in the season, in which case \"special measures should be used to prolong residual\ntraining effects\" (developed in Chapter 3, now ingested; see\n[[framework--prolonging-residual-effects-with-mini-blocks]] and `_book.md`).\nThe number of training stages in a season \"usually varies from four to seven,\" depending on\nthe sport's competition calendar (PDF p. 40, printed p. 29, Figure 1.9). Issurin lists four\nbenefits of this design over the traditional model at this point in the text (PDF p. 40,\nprinted p. 29): the model allows a reduction in total mileage and time spent training\nwithout substantially changing the total number of workouts; monitoring of trainedness is\nmore purposeful and effective, because the reduced number of targeted abilities allows more\nappropriate tests and an easier dose-effect analysis; psychological traits improve because","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3d501f4e-297c-441e-b9b4-b981f824d1c8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
