{"parentUuid":"53a9e79b-1fab-4bd2-9d6c-6e40c9aabd36","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SJwa1ZtDWay4WEqd9dMuqw","type":"tool_result","content":"##### framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means.md\nchapter: \"5.3.3\"\nconfidence: contested\n# The Conjugate Sequence System: Successive Blocks of Unidirectional Loading, Each Built on the Delayed Effect of the Last\n**What it is** — The authors name \"two main systems of organising long term training: the concurrent system and the conjugate sequence system\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292). The concurrent system is \"the parallel training of several motor abilities, such as strength, speed and endurance, over the same period.\" The conjugate sequence system (\"sometimes translated from the Russian as the coupled successive system\") \"involves successively introducing into the training programme separate, specific means, each of which has a progressively stronger training effect, and coupling them sequentially to create favourable conditions for eliciting the cumulative effect of all the training loads.\" It us\n##### claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md\nchapter: \"5.3.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n# Too Much Heavy Strength Work Lowers Speed, Explosiveness and Technique; Speed-Strength Gains Stop After About 12-18 Weeks\n**What it is** — The authors grant that general heavy resistance exercises \"enable the athlete to reach the necessary level of absolute strength, to provide general physical conditioning and to train the neuromuscular system non-specifically,\" but \"it is undesirable to use them extensively during specialised training phases, since they can diminish speed and explosiveness. High volume strength loads used after a phase of specialised jump training can substantially reduce the existing level of explosive strength (by as much as 30%)\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291; all figures in this entry checked against the page image). Supporting reports, all cited without dates except Deniskin: Kotz, \"pro\n##### framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing.md\nchapter: \"1.16\"\nconfidence: supported\n# The Two-Factor Model Can Be Formalized Mathematically, and Its Research Base Argues Against Heavy Training Right Before Competition\n**What it is** — The Two-Factor (Fitness-Fatigue) Model treats preparedness at time t as\n##### heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages.md\nchapter: \"5.3\"\nconfidence: contested\n# Across Stages: Jumps, Then Barbell Work, Then Depth Jumps; Staying on One Means Stalls or Reverses Progress\n**What it is** — Three \"equivalent groups of jumpers of average qualification\" trained \"Under natural training conditions\" (Verkhoshansky & Aganin 1970; Fig 5.13, verified against the page image; PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289). All used special jumping exercises in stage 1. Group 1 stayed on jumps for all three stages. Group 2 switched to barbell exercises in stage 2 and stayed there. Group 3 switched to barbell exercises in stage 2 and to depth jumps in stage 3. \"The special strength training indicators displayed a clear tendency to stabilise in groups 1 and 2 after the first and second stages respectively, and in group 1, even to decrease during the third stage.\" The figure shows group 3's\n##### claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear.md\nchapter: \"4.1.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n# Strength Gains From a Training Stimulus Show a Delay Before They Appear\n**What it is** — The authors describe \"the phasic nature of the response to intense strength","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"df080c19-6a07-4123-b2bb-3edc5cb174c2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:11:26.895Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"##### framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means.md\nchapter: \"5.3.3\"\nconfidence: contested\n# The Conjugate Sequence System: Successive Blocks of Unidirectional Loading, Each Built on the Delayed Effect of the Last\n**What it is** — The authors name \"two main systems of organising long term training: the concurrent system and the conjugate sequence system\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292). The concurrent system is \"the parallel training of several motor abilities, such as strength, speed and endurance, over the same period.\" The conjugate sequence system (\"sometimes translated from the Russian as the coupled successive system\") \"involves successively introducing into the training programme separate, specific means, each of which has a progressively stronger training effect, and coupling them sequentially to create favourable conditions for eliciting the cumulative effect of all the training loads.\" It us\n##### claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md\nchapter: \"5.3.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n# Too Much Heavy Strength Work Lowers Speed, Explosiveness and Technique; Speed-Strength Gains Stop After About 12-18 Weeks\n**What it is** — The authors grant that general heavy resistance exercises \"enable the athlete to reach the necessary level of absolute strength, to provide general physical conditioning and to train the neuromuscular system non-specifically,\" but \"it is undesirable to use them extensively during specialised training phases, since they can diminish speed and explosiveness. High volume strength loads used after a phase of specialised jump training can substantially reduce the existing level of explosive strength (by as much as 30%)\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291; all figures in this entry checked against the page image). Supporting reports, all cited without dates except Deniskin: Kotz, \"pro\n##### framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing.md\nchapter: \"1.16\"\nconfidence: supported\n# The Two-Factor Model Can Be Formalized Mathematically, and Its Research Base Argues Against Heavy Training Right Before Competition\n**What it is** — The Two-Factor (Fitness-Fatigue) Model treats preparedness at time t as\n##### heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages.md\nchapter: \"5.3\"\nconfidence: contested\n# Across Stages: Jumps, Then Barbell Work, Then Depth Jumps; Staying on One Means Stalls or Reverses Progress\n**What it is** — Three \"equivalent groups of jumpers of average qualification\" trained \"Under natural training conditions\" (Verkhoshansky & Aganin 1970; Fig 5.13, verified against the page image; PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289). All used special jumping exercises in stage 1. Group 1 stayed on jumps for all three stages. Group 2 switched to barbell exercises in stage 2 and stayed there. Group 3 switched to barbell exercises in stage 2 and to depth jumps in stage 3. \"The special strength training indicators displayed a clear tendency to stabilise in groups 1 and 2 after the first and second stages respectively, and in group 1, even to decrease during the third stage.\" The figure shows group 3's\n##### claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear.md\nchapter: \"4.1.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n# Strength Gains From a Training Stimulus Show a Delay Before They Appear\n**What it is** — The authors describe \"the phasic nature of the response to intense strength","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"53a9e79b-1fab-4bd2-9d6c-6e40c9aabd36","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
