edium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"2f258eb7-13f1-490a-90b8-faa9ffa82527","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0126SmBEyanw9zq4DZyVNtmE","type":"tool_result","content":"- Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker in place of subjective wellness questionnaires, because it is \"objective, consistent, and actionable in a way the wellness surveys never quite were.\" **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n- The mRSI decision read happens on Wednesdays, against individualized thresholds, not a single cut-off or position-group thresholds. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q2): \"Wednesdays, the thresholds should be individualized.\" Individualized against: Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q2a): \"Percent change from baseline or a rolling average. We've used standard deviations and SWC to diagnose signal versus noise.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q2, Q2a)** Inference: \"should be\" may describe the intended rule rather than a fully running one; no numeric threshold was given. See Tension 12 (daily testing versus a Wednesday read), resolved 2026-10-02: daily jumps build the baseline and SWC, Wednesday is the decision read.\n- Daily subjective wellness surveys, rolled out across multiple sports via the AMS app starting summer 2022, were abandoned after roughly one academic year because athletes learned to game the consistent, low-effort answers the survey rewarded; the data looked clean but carried no real signal. Wellness surveys are still used, deliberately, in return-to-play scenarios and with athletes managing chronic health conditions, where subjective context still adds value objective data can't supply. **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n- Issurin's residual training effects (speed fades fastest, 5 ± 3 days; Block Periodization PDF p. 35, as cited in `docs/synthesis/peaking-and-taper-in-season-football.md`) drive speed programming. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q16): \"I believe Issurin. It drives our speed programming in the off-season and what drives sprinting recommendations in season.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q16)** See the residual-effects bullets under Conditioning and Periodization, and Tension 11.\n4:Checks: body em dashes (header separators allowed), unresolved [[links]] (against staging\n13:HEADER = re.compile(r\"^\\*\\*[^*]+\\*\\* —\")\n14:HISTORY = re.compile(r\"prior version|earlier draft|NO-GO|round[- ]1|round[- ]2|fix pass|fixer|this revision|previous version\", re.I)\n31:                     if \"—\" in HEADER.sub(\"\", line)]\n33:            issues.append(f\"body em dash lines {body_dash[:8]}\")\n34:        for link in re.findall(r\"\\[\\[([^\\]|#]+)\", text):\n39:            m = re.search(r\"^confidence:\\s*(\\S+)\", text, re.M)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d9acf2cc-4681-49fe-91d4-d51572309918","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:12:37.966Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"- Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker in place of subjective wellness questionnaires, because it is \"objective, consistent, and actionable in a way the wellness surveys never quite were.\" **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n- The mRSI decision read happens on Wednesdays, against individualized thresholds, not a single cut-off or position-group thresholds. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q2): \"Wednesdays, the thresholds should be individualized.\" Individualized against: Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q2a): \"Percent change from baseline or a rolling average. We've used standard deviations and SWC to diagnose signal versus noise.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q2, Q2a)** Inference: \"should be\" may describe the intended rule rather than a fully running one; no numeric threshold was given. See Tension 12 (daily testing versus a Wednesday read), resolved 2026-10-02: daily jumps build the baseline and SWC, Wednesday is the decision read.\n- Daily subjective wellness surveys, rolled out across multiple sports via the AMS app starting summer 2022, were abandoned after roughly one academic year because athletes learned to game the consistent, low-effort answers the survey rewarded; the data looked clean but carried no real signal. Wellness surveys are still used, deliberately, in return-to-play scenarios and with athletes managing chronic health conditions, where subjective context still adds value objective data can't supply. **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n- Issurin's residual training effects (speed fades fastest, 5 ± 3 days; Block Periodization PDF p. 35, as cited in `docs/synthesis/peaking-and-taper-in-season-football.md`) drive speed programming. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q16): \"I believe Issurin. It drives our speed programming in the off-season and what drives sprinting recommendations in season.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q16)** See the residual-effects bullets under Conditioning and Periodization, and Tension 11.\n4:Checks: body em dashes (header separators allowed), unresolved [[links]] (against staging\n13:HEADER = re.compile(r\"^\\*\\*[^*]+\\*\\* —\")\n14:HISTORY = re.compile(r\"prior version|earlier draft|NO-GO|round[- ]1|round[- ]2|fix pass|fixer|this revision|previous version\", re.I)\n31:                     if \"—\" in HEADER.sub(\"\", line)]\n33:            issues.append(f\"body em dash lines {body_dash[:8]}\")\n34:        for link in re.findall(r\"\\[\\[([^\\]|#]+)\", text):\n39:            m = re.search(r\"^confidence:\\s*(\\S+)\", text, re.M)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2f258eb7-13f1-490a-90b8-faa9ffa82527","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBr