{"parentUuid":"86ffdae2-e35e-401b-a3df-951459eee85f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffm77mMB39sRqs6ibwqv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3770,"cache_read_input_tokens":263210,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3770,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1157,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffm638Gk8TC45N2CdDhq","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"66ca1e1e-2f80-4437-bcc9-3c194be8b7fc","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:16:25.329Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"66ca1e1e-2f80-4437-bcc9-3c194be8b7fc","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffm77mMB39sRqs6ibwqv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NjPbV1cpehDXR8rwcN2HSn","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: technique during strength blocks\ncategory: [technique, concentrated-loading, skill-learning, speed-strength, periodization]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Keep Technique Work in a Concentrated Strength Block: Learn Components at Low Intensity During the Block, Perfect the Whole Skill at High Intensity as the Delayed Effect Appears\n\n**What it is** — The authors reject reading block loading as strength only: \"*Although there is a primary emphasis on a specific motor quality at different stages of training, this does not mean that technique work is totally omitted during the stage of concentrated strength loading*\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 382 / printed p. 371). Their rules (PDF pp. 382-383 / printed pp. 371-372, both checked against the page images):\n1. **Daily technique, with freshness for the hard parts.** \"One must work on improving technique daily, especially in speed-strength and complex-technical types of sports, with 'freshness' of the body being an important prerequisite for development of technique (Dyachkov, 1966; Ozolin, 1970; Ratov, 1979). Separate tasks of technical training which do not require maximum intensity of effort can be performed with the body in a lowered functional state, but the execution of other tasks under such conditions is unacceptable.\"\n2. **Two stages of technical work in the preparatory period.** \"During the first stage, one perfects those *components of technique* which need improvement and during the second, the emphasis is on executing *the sport exercise as a whole* with high intensity of effort\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 383). High-intensity whole-skill work \"should begin in the preparatory period, since it would be too late were it to commence in the competitive period\" (PDF p. 382).\n3. **During the block: motor learning at low intensity.** One should \"primarily emphasize motor learning, i.e. mastering the more precise variants of technique, oriented to that specific level of speed-strength preparedness which will be provided by the concentrated loading... this task is carried out at low intensity,\" by \"special supplementary exercises, by imitation of the rhythmic pattern (but not the tempo) and its related individual elements, and by execution of the whole sports exercise, not at full strength, but under less demanding conditions\" (PDF p. 383).\n4. **After the block: perfecting at high intensity.** \"During the LDTE stage of strength loading, the athlete begins perfecting technique and adapting it to his increasing speed-strength preparedness. This is where one models competition conditions in training to stabilise the perfected technique of the competition exercise at the necessary high intensity of effort\" (PDF p. 383).\n\nFig 6.32(a) (PDF p. 383, checked against the page image) draws this for speed-strength and complex-technical sports: a shaded concentrated strength block on the left, labelled \"Learning\" below the axis, a dotted line, then \"Perfecting.\" The \"STATE\" curve (overall fitness state) drops below the zero line during the block and rises after it to the highest level at the right; the Technique and GPP curves continue through both stages. The figure has no numeric scale.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the lowered functional state during concentrated loading (\"Noting that one's functional state will be lowered during this stage, this task is carried out at low intensity,\" PDF p. 383) and the later rise in speed-strength, which the technique is \"oriented to.\" They add that each cycle of technical improvement consists of \"the preliminary development of the motor qualities which the athlete needs to reproduce in the near future, accompanied by the immediate accommodation of technique to that level,\" and that with this system \"the athlete will not be unduly concerned about technique and his achievements will not stagnate\" (PDF p. 383). No study is cited for the two-stage scheme itself.\n\n**When to apply** — Any off-season strength block for a speed-strength athlete: keep a daily technique slot, load it with drills, rhythm work and sub-maximal whole movements while the block is heavy, and move full-speed, competition-like technique to the weeks after the block. The 6.14.1 model states the same rule (technique \"is included at reduced intensity using the separate elements and their links,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383). Eric's baseline: \"The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next) is repeatedly described as the department's programming \\\"compass.\\\"\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 101, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: the authors' scheme does not wait for strength to be built before skill work starts; it runs low-intensity skill learning alongside the strength block, which is a different reading of \"foundation\" than a strict stepwise hierarchy.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Speed sports with a cyclic structure separate the two more sharply: \"Speed work is excluded totally from the concentrated strength loading stage,\" though intermuscular coordination work is allowed (PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373; see [[framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks]]). The rule concerns the authors' concentrated-block method for qualified athletes; it says nothing about technique learning in novices outside a block. The authors do not give a dose or a proportion of time for the technique slot. Inference: in team sports the sport coach, not the strength staff, usually owns technical practice, so the strength staff can control only the timing of their own heavy work relative to it.\n\n**Example** — The authors give Fig 6.32(a) as their illustration, not a named athlete. Inference: a long jumper in a six-week strength block does approach-rhythm runs and short-approach jumps at moderate effort three or four days a week, then after the block moves to full-approach jumps at competition speed as speed-strength rises.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice]], [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities]], [[heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3770,"cache_read_input_tokens":263210,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3770,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01NjPbV1cpehDXR8rwcN2HSn":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: technique during strength blocks\ncategory: [technique, concentrated-loading, skill-learning, speed-strength, periodization]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Keep Technique Work in a Concentrated Strength Block: Learn Components at Low Intensity During the Block, Perfect the Whole Skill at High Intensity as the Delayed Effect Appears\n\n**What it is** — The authors reject reading block loading as strength only: \"*Although there is a primary emphasis on a specific motor quality at different stages of training, this does not mean that technique work is totally omitted during the stage of concentrated strength loading*\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 382 / printed p. 371). Their rules (PDF pp. 382-383 / printed pp. 371-372, both checked against the page images):\n1. **Daily technique, with freshness for the hard parts.** \"One must work on improving technique daily, especially in speed-strength and complex-technical types of sports, with 'freshness' of the body being an important prerequisite for development of technique (Dyachkov, 1966; Ozolin, 1970; Ratov, 1979). Separate tasks of technical training which do not require maximum intensity of effort can be performed with the body in a lowered functional state, but the execution of other tasks under such conditions is unacceptable.\"\n2. **Two stages of technical work in the preparatory period.** \"During the first stage, one perfects those *components of technique* which need improvement and during the second, the emphasis is on executing *the sport exercise as a whole* with high intensity of effort\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 383). High-intensity whole-skill work \"should begin in the preparatory period, since it would be too late were it to commence in the competitive period\" (PDF p. 382).\n3. **During the block: motor learning at low intensity.** One should \"primarily emphasize motor learning, i.e. mastering the more precise variants of technique, oriented to that specific level of speed-strength preparedness which will be provided by the concentrated loading... this task is carried out at low intensity,\" by \"special supplementary exercises, by imitation of the rhythmic pattern (but not the tempo) and its related individual elements, and by execution of the whole sports exercise, not at full strength, but under less demanding conditions\" (PDF p. 383).\n4. **After the block: perfecting at high intensity.** \"During the LDTE stage of strength loading, the athlete begins perfecting technique and adapting it to his increasing speed-strength preparedness. This is where one models competition conditions in training to stabilise the perfected technique of the competition exercise at the necessary high intensity of effort\" (PDF p. 383).\n\nFig 6.32(a) (PDF p. 383, checked against the page image) draws this for speed-strength and complex-technical sports: a shaded concentrated strength block on the left, labelled \"Learning\" below the axis, a dotted line, then \"Perfecting.\" The \"STATE\" curve (overall fitness state) drops below the zero line during the block and rises after it to the highest level at the right; the Technique and GPP curves continue through both stages. The figure has no numeric scale.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the lowered functional state during concentrated loading (\"Noting that one's functional state will be lowered during this stage, this task is carried out at low intensity,\" PDF p. 383) and the later rise in speed-strength, which the technique is \"oriented to.\" They add that each cycle of technical improvement consists of \"the preliminary development of the motor qualities which the athlete needs to reproduce in the near future, accompanied by the immediate accommodation of technique to that level,\" and that with this system \"the athlete will not be unduly concerned about technique and his achievements will not stagnate\" (PDF p. 383). No study is cited for the two-stage scheme itself.\n\n**When to apply** — Any off-season strength block for a speed-strength athlete: keep a daily technique slot, load it with drills, rhythm work and sub-maximal whole movements while the block is heavy, and move full-speed, competition-like technique to the weeks after the block. The 6.14.1 model states the same rule (technique \"is included at reduced intensity using the separate elements and their links,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383). Eric's baseline: \"The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next) is repeatedly described as the department's programming \\\"compass.\\\"\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 101, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: the authors' scheme does not wait for strength to be built before skill work starts; it runs low-intensity skill learning alongside the strength block, which is a different reading of \"foundation\" than a strict stepwise hierarchy.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Speed sports with a cyclic structure separate the two more sharply: \"Speed work is excluded totally from the concentrated strength loading stage,\" though intermuscular coordination work is allowed (PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373; see [[framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks]]). The rule concerns the authors' concentrated-block method for qualified athletes; it says nothing about technique learning in novices outside a block. The authors do not give a dose or a proportion of time for the technique slot. Inference: in team sports the sport coach, not the strength staff, usually owns technical practice, so the strength staff can control only the timing of their own heavy work relative to it.\n\n**Example** — The authors give Fig 6.32(a) as their illustration, not a named athlete. Inference: a long jumper in a six-week strength block does approach-rhythm runs and short-approach jumps at moderate effort three or four days a week, then after the block moves to full-approach jumps at competition speed as speed-strength rises.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice]], [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities]], [[heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffm638Gk8TC45N2CdDhq","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"4dca9255-483a-4512-a738-87c8ed063a34","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:16:44.850Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"4dca9255-483a-4512-a738-87c8ed063a34","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NjPbV1cpehDXR8rwcN2HSn","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"dedb4c94-c0ae-448d-a640-a8debcef87a2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:16:44.943Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: technique during strength blocks\ncategory: [technique, concentrated-loading, skill-learning, speed-strength, periodization]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Keep Technique Work in a Concentrated Strength Block: Learn Components at Low Intensity During the Block, Perfect the Whole Skill at High Intensity as the Delayed Effect Appears\n\n**What it is** — The authors reject reading block loading as strength only: \"*Although there is a primary emphasis on a specific motor quality at different stages of training, this does not mean that technique work is totally omitted during the stage of concentrated strength loading*\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 382 / printed p. 371). Their rules (PDF pp. 382-383 / printed pp. 371-372, both checked against the page images):\n1. **Daily technique, with freshness for the hard parts.** \"One must work on improving technique daily, especially in speed-strength and complex-technical types of sports, with 'freshness' of the body being an important prerequisite for development of technique (Dyachkov, 1966; Ozolin, 1970; Ratov, 1979). Separate tasks of technical training which do not require maximum intensity of effort can be performed with the body in a lowered functional state, but the execution of other tasks under such conditions is unacceptable.\"\n2. **Two stages of technical work in the preparatory period.** \"During the first stage, one perfects those *components of technique* which need improvement and during the second, the emphasis is on executing *the sport exercise as a whole* with high intensity of effort\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 383). High-intensity whole-skill work \"should begin in the preparatory period, since it would be too late were it to commence in the competitive period\" (PDF p. 382).\n3. **During the block: motor learning at low intensity.** One should \"primarily emphasize motor learning, i.e. mastering the more precise variants of technique, oriented to that specific level of speed-strength preparedness which will be provided by the concentrated loading... this task is carried out at low intensity,\" by \"special supplementary exercises, by imitation of the rhythmic pattern (but not the tempo) and its related individual elements, and by execution of the whole sports exercise, not at full strength, but under less demanding conditions\" (PDF p. 383).\n4. **After the block: perfecting at high intensity.** \"During the LDTE stage of strength loading, the athlete begins perfecting technique and adapting it to his increasing speed-strength preparedness. This is where one models competition conditions in training to stabilise the perfected technique of the competition exercise at the necessary high intensity of effort\" (PDF p. 383).\n\nFig 6.32(a) (PDF p. 383, checked against the page image) draws this for speed-strength and complex-technical sports: a shaded concentrated strength block on the left, labelled \"Learning\" below the axis, a dotted line, then \"Perfecting.\" The \"STATE\" curve (overall fitness state) drops below the zero line during the block and rises after it to the highest level at the right; the Technique and GPP curves continue through both stages. The figure has no numeric scale.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the lowered functional state during concentrated loading (\"Noting that one's functional state will be lowered during this stage, this task is carried out at low intensity,\" PDF p. 383) and the later rise in speed-strength, which the technique is \"oriented to.\" They add that each cycle of technical improvement consists of \"the preliminary development of the motor qualities which the athlete needs to reproduce in the near future, accompanied by the immediate accommodation of technique to that level,\" and that with this system \"the athlete will not be unduly concerned about technique and his achievements will not stagnate\" (PDF p. 383). No study is cited for the two-stage scheme itself.\n\n**When to apply** — Any off-season strength block for a speed-strength athlete: keep a daily technique slot, load it with drills, rhythm work and sub-maximal whole movements while the block is heavy, and move full-speed, competition-like technique to the weeks after the block. The 6.14.1 model states the same rule (technique \"is included at reduced intensity using the separate elements and their links,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383). Eric's baseline: \"The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next) is repeatedly described as the department's programming \\\"compass.\\\"\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 101, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: the authors' scheme does not wait for strength to be built before skill work starts; it runs low-intensity skill learning alongside the strength block, which is a different reading of \"foundation\" than a strict stepwise hierarchy.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Speed sports with a cyclic structure separate the two more sharply: \"Speed work is excluded totally from the concentrated strength loading stage,\" though intermuscular coordination work is allowed (PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373; see [[framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks]]). The rule concerns the authors' concentrated-block method for qualified athletes; it says nothing about technique learning in novices outside a block. The authors do not give a dose or a proportion of time for the technique slot. Inference: in team sports the sport coach, not the strength staff, usually owns technical practice, so the strength staff can control only the timing of their own heavy work relative to it.\n\n**Example** — The authors give Fig 6.32(a) as their illustration, not a named athlete. Inference: a long jumper in a six-week strength block does approach-rhythm runs and short-approach jumps at moderate effort three or four days a week, then after the block moves to full-approach jumps at competition speed as speed-strength rises.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice]], [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities]], [[heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4dca9255-483a-4512-a738-87c8ed063a34","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"dedb4c94-c0ae-448d-a640-a8debcef87a2","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14730526 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"0e46de44-34d0-4312-9923-6f0f142b946c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:16:44.952Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14730526 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"0e46de44-34d0-4312-9923-6f0f142b946c","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffm9EqKHvuyaPAnnqPn5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2620,"cache_read_input_tokens":266980,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2620,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":998,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffm8mM672xqFBuZi3y2y","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"bb0e1f3a-a725-41ba-b25e-600a30603286","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:16:54.126Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"bb0e1f3a-a725-41ba-b25e-600a30603286","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffm9EqKHvuyaPAnnqPn5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019JzUHcfYNVWQUk5sLwm7Qw","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.4\"\ntopic: enhancing the delayed training effect\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, ldte, plyometrics, speed-strength, periodization, sequencing]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Follow a High-Volume, Low-Intensity Concentrated Block With Moderate-Volume Explosive Work to Enlarge the Delayed Training Effect\n\n**What it is** — Under the heading \"More Advanced Use of Concentrated Loading\" (PDF p. 397 / printed p. 386, checked against the page image) the authors modify the separated-block models of 6.14: \"in speed-strength sports, the magnitude of the delayed training effect (LDTE) may be enhanced further if the concentrated loading is followed by specialised work of moderate volume, which includes higher power or more explosive muscle contraction regimes, such as jumps, plyometrics and maximal attempts.\" The loads overlap: \"There is an overlapping of the different training volumes, which peak smoothly in each case to ensure optimal increase in the LDTE\" (PDF p. 398 / printed p. 387). In the bi-cyclic version, \"stages A and C refer to concentrated periods of high volume, low intensity work and stages B and D to moderate volumes of specialised, higher intensity speed-strength work. This scheme has been recommended to ensure continuous increase in special strength over a long period (Verkhoshansky, 1978)\" (PDF p. 398).\n\nThe figures, read from the page images (no numeric axes):\n- **Fig 6.43** (PDF p. 398, monocyclic large mesocycle): a tall wave A in the preparatory phase, a smaller wave B starting as A falls and peaking at about the start of the competition phase. The speed-strength curve sags while A is high, then rises through B and keeps rising into the competition phase.\n- **Fig 6.44** (PDF p. 399 / printed p. 388, bi-cyclic): tall A, small B, tall C, small D in sequence. Speed-strength dips during A, rises during B, flattens or dips slightly during C, and rises again after it to its highest level at the right.\n- **Fig 6.45** (PDF p. 399, a large mesocycle for speed-strength sports): the text says \"the concentrated strength loading block occupies the first half of the large mesocycle, following the groundwork laid by general physical preparation (GPP) and the peak amount of aerobic work... Technique and speed training occupies the second half.\" The figure shows an aerobic-work curve peaking early, a large shaded wave over the first half, a second shaded wave over the second half labelled \"Technique and Speed,\" and a fitness-state curve that dips then rises to its highest point at the right.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give a principle rather than a measured mechanism: overlapping loads that \"peak smoothly\" serve the LDTE, and \"the inclusion of regular passive and active restoration periods between stages of greater loading is important to facilitate development of the LDTE\" (PDF p. 398). They also argue for putting the bulk of strength work early: \"Since large volumes of strength and technique training generally are incompatible, the greatest quantity of strength work should be done during the preparatory period, with more emphasis being placed on technique work during the competitive phase when the LDTE rises markedly\" (PDF p. 399). No data are shown; the figures are schematics credited to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999).\n\n**When to apply** — Speed-strength athletes coming out of a high-volume strength block: bring in moderate volumes of jumps, plyometrics and maximal attempts as the block tapers, rather than dropping straight to technique and speed alone. The authors say the boundaries are loose: \"stage B does not necessarily begin where shown, but may commence far earlier, while stage A may tail off right near the end of phase B\" (PDF p. 398; \"phase B\" is printed as such). Siff ties this to Matveyev: \"it is sometimes perfectly appropriate to have elements of each intermingled in various 'doses'\" (PDF p. 398). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the A-then-B wave is one way to stage that shift within one off-season.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The scheme is stated for speed-strength sports. It assumes the A stage is \"high volume, low intensity,\" consistent with the low-intensity condition for concentrated loading ([[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]); an A stage built from heavy, intense lifting is a different situation the authors do not cover. **Unclear figure labels:** the caption of Fig 6.45 says \"A is the block of concentrated strength loading, while B and C refer to the types of training identified in the diagram,\" but in the figure the label \"GPP\" sits under wave A and \"Strength Load\" sits under wave B (PDF p. 399). Fig 6.46 has the same mismatch (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389). Which wave is the strength block cannot be settled from the page. No dose for the B stage is given beyond \"moderate volume.\"\n\n**Example** — The authors give the schematics only. Inference: after a four-week block of high-volume, moderate-load squats, pulls and loaded jumps, a jumper adds two short weekly sessions of bounding, a few depth jumps and one or two near-maximal lifts, with that work peaking about when the first competitions begin.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect]], [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2620,"cache_read_input_tokens":266980,"output_tokens":2457,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":172},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2620},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2457,"cache_read_input_tokens":266980,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2620,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2620,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_019JzUHcfYNVWQUk5sLwm7Qw":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.4\"\ntopic: enhancing the delayed training effect\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, ldte, plyometrics, speed-strength, periodization, sequencing]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Follow a High-Volume, Low-Intensity Concentrated Block With Moderate-Volume Explosive Work to Enlarge the Delayed Training Effect\n\n**What it is** — Under the heading \"More Advanced Use of Concentrated Loading\" (PDF p. 397 / printed p. 386, checked against the page image) the authors modify the separated-block models of 6.14: \"in speed-strength sports, the magnitude of the delayed training effect (LDTE) may be enhanced further if the concentrated loading is followed by specialised work of moderate volume, which includes higher power or more explosive muscle contraction regimes, such as jumps, plyometrics and maximal attempts.\" The loads overlap: \"There is an overlapping of the different training volumes, which peak smoothly in each case to ensure optimal increase in the LDTE\" (PDF p. 398 / printed p. 387). In the bi-cyclic version, \"stages A and C refer to concentrated periods of high volume, low intensity work and stages B and D to moderate volumes of specialised, higher intensity speed-strength work. This scheme has been recommended to ensure continuous increase in special strength over a long period (Verkhoshansky, 1978)\" (PDF p. 398).\n\nThe figures, read from the page images (no numeric axes):\n- **Fig 6.43** (PDF p. 398, monocyclic large mesocycle): a tall wave A in the preparatory phase, a smaller wave B starting as A falls and peaking at about the start of the competition phase. The speed-strength curve sags while A is high, then rises through B and keeps rising into the competition phase.\n- **Fig 6.44** (PDF p. 399 / printed p. 388, bi-cyclic): tall A, small B, tall C, small D in sequence. Speed-strength dips during A, rises during B, flattens or dips slightly during C, and rises again after it to its highest level at the right.\n- **Fig 6.45** (PDF p. 399, a large mesocycle for speed-strength sports): the text says \"the concentrated strength loading block occupies the first half of the large mesocycle, following the groundwork laid by general physical preparation (GPP) and the peak amount of aerobic work... Technique and speed training occupies the second half.\" The figure shows an aerobic-work curve peaking early, a large shaded wave over the first half, a second shaded wave over the second half labelled \"Technique and Speed,\" and a fitness-state curve that dips then rises to its highest point at the right.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give a principle rather than a measured mechanism: overlapping loads that \"peak smoothly\" serve the LDTE, and \"the inclusion of regular passive and active restoration periods between stages of greater loading is important to facilitate development of the LDTE\" (PDF p. 398). They also argue for putting the bulk of strength work early: \"Since large volumes of strength and technique training generally are incompatible, the greatest quantity of strength work should be done during the preparatory period, with more emphasis being placed on technique work during the competitive phase when the LDTE rises markedly\" (PDF p. 399). No data are shown; the figures are schematics credited to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999).\n\n**When to apply** — Speed-strength athletes coming out of a high-volume strength block: bring in moderate volumes of jumps, plyometrics and maximal attempts as the block tapers, rather than dropping straight to technique and speed alone. The authors say the boundaries are loose: \"stage B does not necessarily begin where shown, but may commence far earlier, while stage A may tail off right near the end of phase B\" (PDF p. 398; \"phase B\" is printed as such). Siff ties this to Matveyev: \"it is sometimes perfectly appropriate to have elements of each intermingled in various 'doses'\" (PDF p. 398). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the A-then-B wave is one way to stage that shift within one off-season.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The scheme is stated for speed-strength sports. It assumes the A stage is \"high volume, low intensity,\" consistent with the low-intensity condition for concentrated loading ([[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]); an A stage built from heavy, intense lifting is a different situation the authors do not cover. **Unclear figure labels:** the caption of Fig 6.45 says \"A is the block of concentrated strength loading, while B and C refer to the types of training identified in the diagram,\" but in the figure the label \"GPP\" sits under wave A and \"Strength Load\" sits under wave B (PDF p. 399). Fig 6.46 has the same mismatch (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389). Which wave is the strength block cannot be settled from the page. No dose for the B stage is given beyond \"moderate volume.\"\n\n**Example** — The authors give the schematics only. Inference: after a four-week block of high-volume, moderate-load squats, pulls and loaded jumps, a jumper adds two short weekly sessions of bounding, a few depth jumps and one or two near-maximal lifts, with that work peaking about when the first competitions begin.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect]], [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffm8mM672xqFBuZi3y2y","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"47ed48d0-7931-43c6-8f78-9d392faabd3b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:17:12.652Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"47ed48d0-7931-43c6-8f78-9d392faabd3b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019JzUHcfYNVWQUk5sLwm7Qw","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"8f7074ff-13d3-4cee-b2de-16474ec87c94","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:17:12.767Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.4\"\ntopic: enhancing the delayed training effect\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, ldte, plyometrics, speed-strength, periodization, sequencing]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Follow a High-Volume, Low-Intensity Concentrated Block With Moderate-Volume Explosive Work to Enlarge the Delayed Training Effect\n\n**What it is** — Under the heading \"More Advanced Use of Concentrated Loading\" (PDF p. 397 / printed p. 386, checked against the page image) the authors modify the separated-block models of 6.14: \"in speed-strength sports, the magnitude of the delayed training effect (LDTE) may be enhanced further if the concentrated loading is followed by specialised work of moderate volume, which includes higher power or more explosive muscle contraction regimes, such as jumps, plyometrics and maximal attempts.\" The loads overlap: \"There is an overlapping of the different training volumes, which peak smoothly in each case to ensure optimal increase in the LDTE\" (PDF p. 398 / printed p. 387). In the bi-cyclic version, \"stages A and C refer to concentrated periods of high volume, low intensity work and stages B and D to moderate volumes of specialised, higher intensity speed-strength work. This scheme has been recommended to ensure continuous increase in special strength over a long period (Verkhoshansky, 1978)\" (PDF p. 398).\n\nThe figures, read from the page images (no numeric axes):\n- **Fig 6.43** (PDF p. 398, monocyclic large mesocycle): a tall wave A in the preparatory phase, a smaller wave B starting as A falls and peaking at about the start of the competition phase. The speed-strength curve sags while A is high, then rises through B and keeps rising into the competition phase.\n- **Fig 6.44** (PDF p. 399 / printed p. 388, bi-cyclic): tall A, small B, tall C, small D in sequence. Speed-strength dips during A, rises during B, flattens or dips slightly during C, and rises again after it to its highest level at the right.\n- **Fig 6.45** (PDF p. 399, a large mesocycle for speed-strength sports): the text says \"the concentrated strength loading block occupies the first half of the large mesocycle, following the groundwork laid by general physical preparation (GPP) and the peak amount of aerobic work... Technique and speed training occupies the second half.\" The figure shows an aerobic-work curve peaking early, a large shaded wave over the first half, a second shaded wave over the second half labelled \"Technique and Speed,\" and a fitness-state curve that dips then rises to its highest point at the right.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give a principle rather than a measured mechanism: overlapping loads that \"peak smoothly\" serve the LDTE, and \"the inclusion of regular passive and active restoration periods between stages of greater loading is important to facilitate development of the LDTE\" (PDF p. 398). They also argue for putting the bulk of strength work early: \"Since large volumes of strength and technique training generally are incompatible, the greatest quantity of strength work should be done during the preparatory period, with more emphasis being placed on technique work during the competitive phase when the LDTE rises markedly\" (PDF p. 399). No data are shown; the figures are schematics credited to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999).\n\n**When to apply** — Speed-strength athletes coming out of a high-volume strength block: bring in moderate volumes of jumps, plyometrics and maximal attempts as the block tapers, rather than dropping straight to technique and speed alone. The authors say the boundaries are loose: \"stage B does not necessarily begin where shown, but may commence far earlier, while stage A may tail off right near the end of phase B\" (PDF p. 398; \"phase B\" is printed as such). Siff ties this to Matveyev: \"it is sometimes perfectly appropriate to have elements of each intermingled in various 'doses'\" (PDF p. 398). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the A-then-B wave is one way to stage that shift within one off-season.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The scheme is stated for speed-strength sports. It assumes the A stage is \"high volume, low intensity,\" consistent with the low-intensity condition for concentrated loading ([[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]); an A stage built from heavy, intense lifting is a different situation the authors do not cover. **Unclear figure labels:** the caption of Fig 6.45 says \"A is the block of concentrated strength loading, while B and C refer to the types of training identified in the diagram,\" but in the figure the label \"GPP\" sits under wave A and \"Strength Load\" sits under wave B (PDF p. 399). Fig 6.46 has the same mismatch (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389). Which wave is the strength block cannot be settled from the page. No dose for the B stage is given beyond \"moderate volume.\"\n\n**Example** — The authors give the schematics only. Inference: after a four-week block of high-volume, moderate-load squats, pulls and loaded jumps, a jumper adds two short weekly sessions of bounding, a few depth jumps and one or two near-maximal lifts, with that work peaking about when the first competitions begin.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect]], [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"47ed48d0-7931-43c6-8f78-9d392faabd3b","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
