{"parentUuid":"c67c463b-1f53-4a16-b404-1ab50142dabc","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QGEPYq3sb5L4jPe9txHedC","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"1bc64a40-159b-4bb4-a8b4-7ecc1feeec64","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:17:28.107Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work.md","oldString":"Siff ties this to Matveyev:","newString":"The authors tie this to Matveyev's \"non-discreteness of the preparatory and competitive phases\":","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.4\"\ntopic: enhancing the delayed training effect\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, ldte, plyometrics, speed-strength, periodization, sequencing]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Follow a High-Volume, Low-Intensity Concentrated Block With Moderate-Volume Explosive Work to Enlarge the Delayed Training Effect\n\n**What it is** — Under the heading \"More Advanced Use of Concentrated Loading\" (PDF p. 397 / printed p. 386, checked against the page image) the authors modify the separated-block models of 6.14: \"in speed-strength sports, the magnitude of the delayed training effect (LDTE) may be enhanced further if the concentrated loading is followed by specialised work of moderate volume, which includes higher power or more explosive muscle contraction regimes, such as jumps, plyometrics and maximal attempts.\" The loads overlap: \"There is an overlapping of the different training volumes, which peak smoothly in each case to ensure optimal increase in the LDTE\" (PDF p. 398 / printed p. 387). In the bi-cyclic version, \"stages A and C refer to concentrated periods of high volume, low intensity work and stages B and D to moderate volumes of specialised, higher intensity speed-strength work. This scheme has been recommended to ensure continuous increase in special strength over a long period (Verkhoshansky, 1978)\" (PDF p. 398).\n\nThe figures, read from the page images (no numeric axes):\n- **Fig 6.43** (PDF p. 398, monocyclic large mesocycle): a tall wave A in the preparatory phase, a smaller wave B starting as A falls and peaking at about the start of the competition phase. The speed-strength curve sags while A is high, then rises through B and keeps rising into the competition phase.\n- **Fig 6.44** (PDF p. 399 / printed p. 388, bi-cyclic): tall A, small B, tall C, small D in sequence. Speed-strength dips during A, rises during B, flattens or dips slightly during C, and rises again after it to its highest level at the right.\n- **Fig 6.45** (PDF p. 399, a large mesocycle for speed-strength sports): the text says \"the concentrated strength loading block occupies the first half of the large mesocycle, following the groundwork laid by general physical preparation (GPP) and the peak amount of aerobic work... Technique and speed training occupies the second half.\" The figure shows an aerobic-work curve peaking early, a large shaded wave over the first half, a second shaded wave over the second half labelled \"Technique and Speed,\" and a fitness-state curve that dips then rises to its highest point at the right.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give a principle rather than a measured mechanism: overlapping loads that \"peak smoothly\" serve the LDTE, and \"the inclusion of regular passive and active restoration periods between stages of greater loading is important to facilitate development of the LDTE\" (PDF p. 398). They also argue for putting the bulk of strength work early: \"Since large volumes of strength and technique training generally are incompatible, the greatest quantity of strength work should be done during the preparatory period, with more emphasis being placed on technique work during the competitive phase when the LDTE rises markedly\" (PDF p. 399). No data are shown; the figures are schematics credited to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999).\n\n**When to apply** — Speed-strength athletes coming out of a high-volume strength block: bring in moderate volumes of jumps, plyometrics and maximal attempts as the block tapers, rather than dropping straight to technique and speed alone. The authors say the boundaries are loose: \"stage B does not necessarily begin where shown, but may commence far earlier, while stage A may tail off right near the end of phase B\" (PDF p. 398; \"phase B\" is printed as such). Siff ties this to Matveyev: \"it is sometimes perfectly appropriate to have elements of each intermingled in various 'doses'\" (PDF p. 398). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the A-then-B wave is one way to stage that shift within one off-season.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The scheme is stated for speed-strength sports. It assumes the A stage is \"high volume, low intensity,\" consistent with the low-intensity condition for concentrated loading ([[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]); an A stage built from heavy, intense lifting is a different situation the authors do not cover. **Unclear figure labels:** the caption of Fig 6.45 says \"A is the block of concentrated strength loading, while B and C refer to the types of training identified in the diagram,\" but in the figure the label \"GPP\" sits under wave A and \"Strength Load\" sits under wave B (PDF p. 399). Fig 6.46 has the same mismatch (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389). Which wave is the strength block cannot be settled from the page. No dose for the B stage is given beyond \"moderate volume.\"\n\n**Example** — The authors give the schematics only. Inference: after a four-week block of high-volume, moderate-load squats, pulls and loaded jumps, a jumper adds two short weekly sessions of bounding, a few depth jumps and one or two near-maximal lifts, with that work peaking about when the first competitions begin.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect]], [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":22,"oldLines":7,"newStart":22,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors give a principle rather than a measured mechanism: overlapping loads that \"peak smoothly\" serve the LDTE, and \"the inclusion of regular passive and active restoration periods between stages of greater loading is important to facilitate development of the LDTE\" (PDF p. 398). They also argue for putting the bulk of strength work early: \"Since large volumes of strength and technique training generally are incompatible, the greatest quantity of strength work should be done during the preparatory period, with more emphasis being placed on technique work during the competitive phase when the LDTE rises markedly\" (PDF p. 399). No data are shown; the figures are schematics credited to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999)."," ","-**When to apply** — Speed-strength athletes coming out of a high-volume strength block: bring in moderate volumes of jumps, plyometrics and maximal attempts as the block tapers, rather than dropping straight to technique and speed alone. The authors say the boundaries are loose: \"stage B does not necessarily begin where shown, but may commence far earlier, while stage A may tail off right near the end of phase B\" (PDF p. 398; \"phase B\" is printed as such). Siff ties this to Matveyev: \"it is sometimes perfectly appropriate to have elements of each intermingled in various 'doses'\" (PDF p. 398). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the A-then-B wave is one way to stage that shift within one off-season.","+**When to apply** — Speed-strength athletes coming out of a high-volume strength block: bring in moderate volumes of jumps, plyometrics and maximal attempts as the block tapers, rather than dropping straight to technique and speed alone. The authors say the boundaries are loose: \"stage B does not necessarily begin where shown, but may commence far earlier, while stage A may tail off right near the end of phase B\" (PDF p. 398; \"phase B\" is printed as such). The authors tie this to Matveyev's \"non-discreteness of the preparatory and competitive phases\": \"it is sometimes perfectly appropriate to have elements of each intermingled in various 'doses'\" (PDF p. 398). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the A-then-B wave is one way to stage that shift within one off-season."," "," **When NOT to apply** — The scheme is stated for speed-strength sports. It assumes the A stage is \"high volume, low intensity,\" consistent with the low-intensity condition for concentrated loading ([[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]); an A stage built from heavy, intense lifting is a different situation the authors do not cover. **Unclear figure labels:** the caption of Fig 6.45 says \"A is the block of concentrated strength loading, while B and C refer to the types of training identified in the diagram,\" but in the figure the label \"GPP\" sits under wave A and \"Strength Load\" sits under wave B (PDF p. 399). Fig 6.46 has the same mismatch (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389). Which wave is the strength block cannot be settled from the page. No dose for the B stage is given beyond \"moderate volume.\""," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c67c463b-1f53-4a16-b404-1ab50142dabc","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
