{"parentUuid":"0e7c24fb-ace6-4592-8d76-0bd59905585d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmCwvgYwPJVDyCnVAbh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1205,"cache_read_input_tokens":272189,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1205,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1209,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffmC4pdxut6PQVzZv2Zo","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"a7c263f7-8c36-445d-99e1-c54cf3497a49","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:17:44.727Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"a7c263f7-8c36-445d-99e1-c54cf3497a49","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmCwvgYwPJVDyCnVAbh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ht1f41f1Br9HkHTLrory5u","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.3\"\ntopic: GPP as restoration\ncategory: [gpp, recovery, concentrated-loading, periodization, restoration]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# In the Concentrated-Loading Models, GPP Is Mainly a Low-Intensity Restoration Tool, and Its Largest Relative Share Falls in the Competitive Periods\n\n**What it is** — In these models general physical preparation (GPP) is assigned a restorative job rather than a fitness-building one. For mesocycle Variant B (PDF p. 386 / printed p. 375, checked against the page image): \"The basic purpose of this GPP is to facilitate the transition to activities which differ from the special work and thereby promote restoration. The GPP means solve the problem of restoring work-capacity during extensive concentrated loading and during appearance of the LDTE, being used for restoration, especially after high intensity technique and speed work. On the whole, the GPP means are executed at low intensity. However, to prepare the body to switch to training to develop speed and technical skill during the stage of reduced concentrated loading, the intensity of the GPP means is increased slightly.\" For the explosive-strength annual model (PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383, checked against the page image): \"Within each block, they offer variety and aid restoration after high volume strength loading, while at the LDTE stage, the GPP assists restoration after intense specialised loading. It should always be remembered that this procedure constitutes part of pedagogical restoration, which facilitates recovery alongside other methods such as sports massage (see 8.5). The largest relative volume of GPP means occurs during the competitive periods.\" In the medium-duration endurance model the aerobic work plays the same role in the second competition stage: \"There is an increase in the volume of aerobic loading again during the second competition stage, its role being to promote restoration\" (PDF p. 395 / printed p. 384).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated reason is switching: GPP moves the athlete to \"activities which differ from the special work and thereby promote restoration\" (PDF p. 386). They give no physiological mechanism for why a change of activity speeds recovery and cite no study for it here. Inference: this is the active-rest idea applied to block planning.\n\n**When to apply** — Inside a concentrated strength block, and after high-intensity technique or speed sessions: use low-intensity general work as the recovery filler rather than adding more special work, and raise its intensity slightly as the block ends and speed and technique work begins. In Fig 6.33(c) (PDF p. 386) the shaded GPP area for Variant B rises steadily across the whole mesocycle, while in Variant A's panel (b) GPP peaks early and then declines (read from the image; no numeric scale).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors do not give a dose, a modality list or an intensity figure beyond \"low.\" GPP in this sense is not the general base-building of a novice's preparatory period; the models are for qualified athletes. Inference: whether general work restores or adds fatigue depends on what it is, and a hard conditioning session labelled GPP would not fit the authors' \"low intensity\" condition.\n\n**Example** — The authors name sports massage as a companion method (PDF p. 394) but give no worked session. Inference: after a full-speed sprint and technique day during the delayed-effect stage, an easy swim, mobility circuit or light bike session the next morning fills the GPP slot.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1205,"cache_read_input_tokens":272189,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1205,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Ht1f41f1Br9HkHTLrory5u":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.3\"\ntopic: GPP as restoration\ncategory: [gpp, recovery, concentrated-loading, periodization, restoration]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# In the Concentrated-Loading Models, GPP Is Mainly a Low-Intensity Restoration Tool, and Its Largest Relative Share Falls in the Competitive Periods\n\n**What it is** — In these models general physical preparation (GPP) is assigned a restorative job rather than a fitness-building one. For mesocycle Variant B (PDF p. 386 / printed p. 375, checked against the page image): \"The basic purpose of this GPP is to facilitate the transition to activities which differ from the special work and thereby promote restoration. The GPP means solve the problem of restoring work-capacity during extensive concentrated loading and during appearance of the LDTE, being used for restoration, especially after high intensity technique and speed work. On the whole, the GPP means are executed at low intensity. However, to prepare the body to switch to training to develop speed and technical skill during the stage of reduced concentrated loading, the intensity of the GPP means is increased slightly.\" For the explosive-strength annual model (PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383, checked against the page image): \"Within each block, they offer variety and aid restoration after high volume strength loading, while at the LDTE stage, the GPP assists restoration after intense specialised loading. It should always be remembered that this procedure constitutes part of pedagogical restoration, which facilitates recovery alongside other methods such as sports massage (see 8.5). The largest relative volume of GPP means occurs during the competitive periods.\" In the medium-duration endurance model the aerobic work plays the same role in the second competition stage: \"There is an increase in the volume of aerobic loading again during the second competition stage, its role being to promote restoration\" (PDF p. 395 / printed p. 384).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated reason is switching: GPP moves the athlete to \"activities which differ from the special work and thereby promote restoration\" (PDF p. 386). They give no physiological mechanism for why a change of activity speeds recovery and cite no study for it here. Inference: this is the active-rest idea applied to block planning.\n\n**When to apply** — Inside a concentrated strength block, and after high-intensity technique or speed sessions: use low-intensity general work as the recovery filler rather than adding more special work, and raise its intensity slightly as the block ends and speed and technique work begins. In Fig 6.33(c) (PDF p. 386) the shaded GPP area for Variant B rises steadily across the whole mesocycle, while in Variant A's panel (b) GPP peaks early and then declines (read from the image; no numeric scale).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors do not give a dose, a modality list or an intensity figure beyond \"low.\" GPP in this sense is not the general base-building of a novice's preparatory period; the models are for qualified athletes. Inference: whether general work restores or adds fatigue depends on what it is, and a hard conditioning session labelled GPP would not fit the authors' \"low intensity\" condition.\n\n**Example** — The authors name sports massage as a companion method (PDF p. 394) but give no worked session. Inference: after a full-speed sprint and technique day during the delayed-effect stage, an easy swim, mobility circuit or light bike session the next morning fills the GPP slot.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffmC4pdxut6PQVzZv2Zo","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f87f11dc-19de-4670-ac31-305a7bdb8270","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:17:57.714Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f87f11dc-19de-4670-ac31-305a7bdb8270","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ht1f41f1Br9HkHTLrory5u","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"c6d29700-9735-44f3-909a-0ef86ed8b662","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:17:57.850Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.3\"\ntopic: GPP as restoration\ncategory: [gpp, recovery, concentrated-loading, periodization, restoration]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# In the Concentrated-Loading Models, GPP Is Mainly a Low-Intensity Restoration Tool, and Its Largest Relative Share Falls in the Competitive Periods\n\n**What it is** — In these models general physical preparation (GPP) is assigned a restorative job rather than a fitness-building one. For mesocycle Variant B (PDF p. 386 / printed p. 375, checked against the page image): \"The basic purpose of this GPP is to facilitate the transition to activities which differ from the special work and thereby promote restoration. The GPP means solve the problem of restoring work-capacity during extensive concentrated loading and during appearance of the LDTE, being used for restoration, especially after high intensity technique and speed work. On the whole, the GPP means are executed at low intensity. However, to prepare the body to switch to training to develop speed and technical skill during the stage of reduced concentrated loading, the intensity of the GPP means is increased slightly.\" For the explosive-strength annual model (PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383, checked against the page image): \"Within each block, they offer variety and aid restoration after high volume strength loading, while at the LDTE stage, the GPP assists restoration after intense specialised loading. It should always be remembered that this procedure constitutes part of pedagogical restoration, which facilitates recovery alongside other methods such as sports massage (see 8.5). The largest relative volume of GPP means occurs during the competitive periods.\" In the medium-duration endurance model the aerobic work plays the same role in the second competition stage: \"There is an increase in the volume of aerobic loading again during the second competition stage, its role being to promote restoration\" (PDF p. 395 / printed p. 384).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated reason is switching: GPP moves the athlete to \"activities which differ from the special work and thereby promote restoration\" (PDF p. 386). They give no physiological mechanism for why a change of activity speeds recovery and cite no study for it here. Inference: this is the active-rest idea applied to block planning.\n\n**When to apply** — Inside a concentrated strength block, and after high-intensity technique or speed sessions: use low-intensity general work as the recovery filler rather than adding more special work, and raise its intensity slightly as the block ends and speed and technique work begins. In Fig 6.33(c) (PDF p. 386) the shaded GPP area for Variant B rises steadily across the whole mesocycle, while in Variant A's panel (b) GPP peaks early and then declines (read from the image; no numeric scale).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors do not give a dose, a modality list or an intensity figure beyond \"low.\" GPP in this sense is not the general base-building of a novice's preparatory period; the models are for qualified athletes. Inference: whether general work restores or adds fatigue depends on what it is, and a hard conditioning session labelled GPP would not fit the authors' \"low intensity\" condition.\n\n**Example** — The authors name sports massage as a companion method (PDF p. 394) but give no worked session. Inference: after a full-speed sprint and technique day during the delayed-effect stage, an easy swim, mobility circuit or light bike session the next morning fills the GPP slot.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f87f11dc-19de-4670-ac31-305a7bdb8270","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"c6d29700-9735-44f3-909a-0ef86ed8b662","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmCwvgYwPJVDyCnVAbh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Vgq8pmF4KQ5jBVhAfTk8Ga","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: volume reduction after large loads\ncategory: [ldte, unloading, restoration, concentrated-loading, overtraining]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# After Large Volumes, the Delayed Training Effect Needs a Prolonged Reduction in Loading, Far Longer Than the Usual 1-2 Week Unload\n\n**What it is** — The authors (PDF p. 383 / printed p. 372, checked against the page image): \"A relatively prolonged stage of reduction in the volume of training is necessary to promote the LDTE. However, in practice, coaches usually aim to achieve large volumes of accumulated loading and do not always emphasize the role of restoration. Although they provide recuperation pauses within the microcycle and the so-called overload microcycles to provide some rest, they still do not effectively utilise prolonged reduction of loading after large volumes.\" Such methods are needed \"not only for restoration, but also for the development and optimal use of those physiological processes which underlie adaptation. These processes occur relatively slowly and an extended period is necessary for their completion, which is far longer than the 1-2 weeks usually allocated for reduction of loading.\" They add a warning about extra work: \"By overestimating the loading required for developing special preparedness, athletes frequently not only undertake an excessive volume of work, but also execute this volume when it is clearly contraindicated, namely when restoration is vital. Therefore, additional volumes of loading not only do not offer increased training benefits, but also become a major barrier to stimulating the restoration which is sorely needed by the body.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that the adaptive processes behind the LDTE \"occur relatively slowly.\" They do not name the processes on this page or give a measured duration here. Inference: this matches the book's Chapter 6 figure of 4-12 weeks for the long-term delayed effect, cited in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]] (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), but this page itself gives only \"far longer than the 1-2 weeks.\"\n\n**When to apply** — After a concentrated, high-volume block: plan a stage of reduced volume measured in weeks, not a single deload week, and do not fill it with more volume. Speed and technique work rise during this stage in the authors' models ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The rule is about the period after large accumulated volumes, not routine week-to-week loading. Tension with another library source: Bompa's step-loading paradigm uses one unloading week after three loading weeks (3:1) as the standard wave ([[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]]). The two address different situations (a routine wave versus the aftermath of a concentrated block), so this is not recorded as a contradiction, but a 3:1 deload should not be taken as enough reduction for the LDTE in the authors' sense. The authors themselves report 3- and 4-week stages with a final restoration week as the most common practice and say \"all of them, depending on circumstances, can produce good results\" (PDF p. 385 / printed p. 374). Reduced volume is not reduced intensity: in the same chapter intensity rises during the LDTE stage.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case on this page. Inference: after a six-week concentrated strength block, a jumper keeps strength volume low for the following four to six weeks while jump and sprint intensity rises, instead of returning to full volume after one easy week.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]], [[heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1205,"cache_read_input_tokens":272189,"output_tokens":3269,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":232},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1205},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3269,"cache_read_input_tokens":272189,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1205,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1205,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Vgq8pmF4KQ5jBVhAfTk8Ga":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: volume reduction after large loads\ncategory: [ldte, unloading, restoration, concentrated-loading, overtraining]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# After Large Volumes, the Delayed Training Effect Needs a Prolonged Reduction in Loading, Far Longer Than the Usual 1-2 Week Unload\n\n**What it is** — The authors (PDF p. 383 / printed p. 372, checked against the page image): \"A relatively prolonged stage of reduction in the volume of training is necessary to promote the LDTE. However, in practice, coaches usually aim to achieve large volumes of accumulated loading and do not always emphasize the role of restoration. Although they provide recuperation pauses within the microcycle and the so-called overload microcycles to provide some rest, they still do not effectively utilise prolonged reduction of loading after large volumes.\" Such methods are needed \"not only for restoration, but also for the development and optimal use of those physiological processes which underlie adaptation. These processes occur relatively slowly and an extended period is necessary for their completion, which is far longer than the 1-2 weeks usually allocated for reduction of loading.\" They add a warning about extra work: \"By overestimating the loading required for developing special preparedness, athletes frequently not only undertake an excessive volume of work, but also execute this volume when it is clearly contraindicated, namely when restoration is vital. Therefore, additional volumes of loading not only do not offer increased training benefits, but also become a major barrier to stimulating the restoration which is sorely needed by the body.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that the adaptive processes behind the LDTE \"occur relatively slowly.\" They do not name the processes on this page or give a measured duration here. Inference: this matches the book's Chapter 6 figure of 4-12 weeks for the long-term delayed effect, cited in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]] (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), but this page itself gives only \"far longer than the 1-2 weeks.\"\n\n**When to apply** — After a concentrated, high-volume block: plan a stage of reduced volume measured in weeks, not a single deload week, and do not fill it with more volume. Speed and technique work rise during this stage in the authors' models ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The rule is about the period after large accumulated volumes, not routine week-to-week loading. Tension with another library source: Bompa's step-loading paradigm uses one unloading week after three loading weeks (3:1) as the standard wave ([[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]]). The two address different situations (a routine wave versus the aftermath of a concentrated block), so this is not recorded as a contradiction, but a 3:1 deload should not be taken as enough reduction for the LDTE in the authors' sense. The authors themselves report 3- and 4-week stages with a final restoration week as the most common practice and say \"all of them, depending on circumstances, can produce good results\" (PDF p. 385 / printed p. 374). Reduced volume is not reduced intensity: in the same chapter intensity rises during the LDTE stage.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case on this page. Inference: after a six-week concentrated strength block, a jumper keeps strength volume low for the following four to six weeks while jump and sprint intensity rises, instead of returning to full volume after one easy week.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]], [[heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffmC4pdxut6PQVzZv2Zo","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"89bd41e4-9f07-4471-bfdc-87804b969080","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:18:10.130Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"89bd41e4-9f07-4471-bfdc-87804b969080","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vgq8pmF4KQ5jBVhAfTk8Ga","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"47de14e5-bbbf-4753-a075-7276bc7f946d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:18:10.240Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: volume reduction after large loads\ncategory: [ldte, unloading, restoration, concentrated-loading, overtraining]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# After Large Volumes, the Delayed Training Effect Needs a Prolonged Reduction in Loading, Far Longer Than the Usual 1-2 Week Unload\n\n**What it is** — The authors (PDF p. 383 / printed p. 372, checked against the page image): \"A relatively prolonged stage of reduction in the volume of training is necessary to promote the LDTE. However, in practice, coaches usually aim to achieve large volumes of accumulated loading and do not always emphasize the role of restoration. Although they provide recuperation pauses within the microcycle and the so-called overload microcycles to provide some rest, they still do not effectively utilise prolonged reduction of loading after large volumes.\" Such methods are needed \"not only for restoration, but also for the development and optimal use of those physiological processes which underlie adaptation. These processes occur relatively slowly and an extended period is necessary for their completion, which is far longer than the 1-2 weeks usually allocated for reduction of loading.\" They add a warning about extra work: \"By overestimating the loading required for developing special preparedness, athletes frequently not only undertake an excessive volume of work, but also execute this volume when it is clearly contraindicated, namely when restoration is vital. Therefore, additional volumes of loading not only do not offer increased training benefits, but also become a major barrier to stimulating the restoration which is sorely needed by the body.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that the adaptive processes behind the LDTE \"occur relatively slowly.\" They do not name the processes on this page or give a measured duration here. Inference: this matches the book's Chapter 6 figure of 4-12 weeks for the long-term delayed effect, cited in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]] (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), but this page itself gives only \"far longer than the 1-2 weeks.\"\n\n**When to apply** — After a concentrated, high-volume block: plan a stage of reduced volume measured in weeks, not a single deload week, and do not fill it with more volume. Speed and technique work rise during this stage in the authors' models ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The rule is about the period after large accumulated volumes, not routine week-to-week loading. Tension with another library source: Bompa's step-loading paradigm uses one unloading week after three loading weeks (3:1) as the standard wave ([[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]]). The two address different situations (a routine wave versus the aftermath of a concentrated block), so this is not recorded as a contradiction, but a 3:1 deload should not be taken as enough reduction for the LDTE in the authors' sense. The authors themselves report 3- and 4-week stages with a final restoration week as the most common practice and say \"all of them, depending on circumstances, can produce good results\" (PDF p. 385 / printed p. 374). Reduced volume is not reduced intensity: in the same chapter intensity rises during the LDTE stage.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case on this page. Inference: after a six-week concentrated strength block, a jumper keeps strength volume low for the following four to six weeks while jump and sprint intensity rises, instead of returning to full volume after one easy week.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]], [[heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"89bd41e4-9f07-4471-bfdc-87804b969080","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"47de14e5-bbbf-4753-a075-7276bc7f946d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14723335 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b32a2b0f-21ca-4f2e-93da-3dfd6c0e3c0d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:18:10.253Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14723335 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"b32a2b0f-21ca-4f2e-93da-3dfd6c0e3c0d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmG2Q9Bo5vfPotPRaMC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3561,"cache_read_input_tokens":273394,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3561,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1102,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffmF3eXufcUVmLLraBym","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"191c0be8-c35c-43d9-8378-c447086bd8ae","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:18:26.142Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"191c0be8-c35c-43d9-8378-c447086bd8ae","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmG2Q9Bo5vfPotPRaMC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016Wn8C3Ht3ME5LoaAq18h6Q","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: strength volume and explosive qualities\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, explosive-strength, muscle-stiffness, injury-risk, jumpers, sprinters]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Antonova (Women Long Jumpers) and Levchenko (Sprinters): Months of Peak Strength Volume Lowered Explosive Qualities, Which Rose Again When Loading Fell\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Problems with Concentrated Loading\" the authors report two Soviet studies (PDF p. 382 / printed p. 371, checked against the page image). They open with the general cost: concentrated strength loading \"leads to a brief but consistent decrease in speed-strength, which diminishes the athlete's special work-capacity and complicates the task of improving technical skills and speed of movement.\"\n1. **Antonova (1982), women long jumpers.** The study analysed \"the connection between the organisation and the volume of training loads, the changes in the force-time characteristics of the take-off in jumping, the sport results and the explosive strength.\" \"It was found that in the months with the largest training volumes, there was a noticeable decrease in the special-strength preparedness and sporting results. However, these same qualities rose during the subsequent months of reduced loading.\"\n2. **Levchenko (1980), sprinters.** It examined \"the interdependence between the dynamics of the volume of special strength-training means, the elasticity of the muscles and the control results of the standing triple-jump of highly qualified sprinters during the spring-summer stage (15 weeks) of training.\" \"An increase in the volume of special strength-training means results in increased muscle stiffness and decreased explosive force. Unfavourable conditions were thus created for improving technique and running speed and the likelihood of injury increased significantly.\"\n\nThe authors add that \"voluminous strength loading is detrimental to the technique of the weightlifting movements (Khlystov, 1976; Vorobyev et al, 1978), javelin throwing (Ruvinsky, 1980) and punching speed in boxing (Filimonov, 1979),\" and conclude: \"Rather than negating the value of concentrated loading, these findings stress the importance of knowing when this method may be appropriate to use.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The book reports correlations between volume and outcomes; it gives no sample sizes, effect sizes or statistics for either study. The only mechanism offered is Levchenko's observed rise in \"muscle stiffness\" with strength volume. The book does not define how stiffness or elasticity was measured.\n\n**When to apply** — As the evidence behind expecting a temporary drop in jump and sprint qualities during high-volume strength phases, and a rebound when volume falls. The authors' remedy follows on the same page: \"if concentrated loading is used, then *cybernetic periodisation* or programming based on Perceived Ratings of Effort and Ratings of Technique, as well as formal testing, can assist greatly in preventing overtraining (see 6.2.2).\" The passage reads as Siff's (cybernetic periodisation is his term elsewhere in the book), and the cited section 6.2.2 is outside this batch. Eric's baseline: \"Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker in place of subjective wellness questionnaires\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 74, Explicit). Inference: a daily jump measure is the kind of \"formal testing\" that would catch the drop Antonova and Levchenko describe.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Both groups were qualified track athletes (\"highly qualified sprinters\" for Levchenko; the long jumpers' level is not stated). The injury statement (\"the likelihood of injury increased significantly\") gives no injury counts. The book does not say whether loads were heavy or moderate, only that volume rose. This duplicates in part the Chapter 5 warning in [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], which cites Kotz, Filinov and Deniskin; the Chapter 6 page adds the Antonova and Levchenko studies and spells the boxing author \"Filimonov, 1979.\"\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is the Antonova finding: results fell in the highest-volume months and rose in the reduced-loading months that followed (PDF p. 382).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]], [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3561,"cache_read_input_tokens":273394,"output_tokens":1981,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":139},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3561},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1981,"cache_read_input_tokens":273394,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3561,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3561,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_016Wn8C3Ht3ME5LoaAq18h6Q":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: strength volume and explosive qualities\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, explosive-strength, muscle-stiffness, injury-risk, jumpers, sprinters]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Antonova (Women Long Jumpers) and Levchenko (Sprinters): Months of Peak Strength Volume Lowered Explosive Qualities, Which Rose Again When Loading Fell\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Problems with Concentrated Loading\" the authors report two Soviet studies (PDF p. 382 / printed p. 371, checked against the page image). They open with the general cost: concentrated strength loading \"leads to a brief but consistent decrease in speed-strength, which diminishes the athlete's special work-capacity and complicates the task of improving technical skills and speed of movement.\"\n1. **Antonova (1982), women long jumpers.** The study analysed \"the connection between the organisation and the volume of training loads, the changes in the force-time characteristics of the take-off in jumping, the sport results and the explosive strength.\" \"It was found that in the months with the largest training volumes, there was a noticeable decrease in the special-strength preparedness and sporting results. However, these same qualities rose during the subsequent months of reduced loading.\"\n2. **Levchenko (1980), sprinters.** It examined \"the interdependence between the dynamics of the volume of special strength-training means, the elasticity of the muscles and the control results of the standing triple-jump of highly qualified sprinters during the spring-summer stage (15 weeks) of training.\" \"An increase in the volume of special strength-training means results in increased muscle stiffness and decreased explosive force. Unfavourable conditions were thus created for improving technique and running speed and the likelihood of injury increased significantly.\"\n\nThe authors add that \"voluminous strength loading is detrimental to the technique of the weightlifting movements (Khlystov, 1976; Vorobyev et al, 1978), javelin throwing (Ruvinsky, 1980) and punching speed in boxing (Filimonov, 1979),\" and conclude: \"Rather than negating the value of concentrated loading, these findings stress the importance of knowing when this method may be appropriate to use.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The book reports correlations between volume and outcomes; it gives no sample sizes, effect sizes or statistics for either study. The only mechanism offered is Levchenko's observed rise in \"muscle stiffness\" with strength volume. The book does not define how stiffness or elasticity was measured.\n\n**When to apply** — As the evidence behind expecting a temporary drop in jump and sprint qualities during high-volume strength phases, and a rebound when volume falls. The authors' remedy follows on the same page: \"if concentrated loading is used, then *cybernetic periodisation* or programming based on Perceived Ratings of Effort and Ratings of Technique, as well as formal testing, can assist greatly in preventing overtraining (see 6.2.2).\" The passage reads as Siff's (cybernetic periodisation is his term elsewhere in the book), and the cited section 6.2.2 is outside this batch. Eric's baseline: \"Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker in place of subjective wellness questionnaires\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 74, Explicit). Inference: a daily jump measure is the kind of \"formal testing\" that would catch the drop Antonova and Levchenko describe.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Both groups were qualified track athletes (\"highly qualified sprinters\" for Levchenko; the long jumpers' level is not stated). The injury statement (\"the likelihood of injury increased significantly\") gives no injury counts. The book does not say whether loads were heavy or moderate, only that volume rose. This duplicates in part the Chapter 5 warning in [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], which cites Kotz, Filinov and Deniskin; the Chapter 6 page adds the Antonova and Levchenko studies and spells the boxing author \"Filimonov, 1979.\"\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is the Antonova finding: results fell in the highest-volume months and rose in the reduced-loading months that followed (PDF p. 382).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]], [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffmF3eXufcUVmLLraBym","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"b5b6504e-a510-4185-aa8b-89ade126216e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:18:40.508Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"b5b6504e-a510-4185-aa8b-89ade126216e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016Wn8C3Ht3ME5LoaAq18h6Q","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"2e771be2-9f1e-417b-8e2f-fcda0686d0da","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:18:40.633Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: strength volume and explosive qualities\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, explosive-strength, muscle-stiffness, injury-risk, jumpers, sprinters]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Antonova (Women Long Jumpers) and Levchenko (Sprinters): Months of Peak Strength Volume Lowered Explosive Qualities, Which Rose Again When Loading Fell\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Problems with Concentrated Loading\" the authors report two Soviet studies (PDF p. 382 / printed p. 371, checked against the page image). They open with the general cost: concentrated strength loading \"leads to a brief but consistent decrease in speed-strength, which diminishes the athlete's special work-capacity and complicates the task of improving technical skills and speed of movement.\"\n1. **Antonova (1982), women long jumpers.** The study analysed \"the connection between the organisation and the volume of training loads, the changes in the force-time characteristics of the take-off in jumping, the sport results and the explosive strength.\" \"It was found that in the months with the largest training volumes, there was a noticeable decrease in the special-strength preparedness and sporting results. However, these same qualities rose during the subsequent months of reduced loading.\"\n2. **Levchenko (1980), sprinters.** It examined \"the interdependence between the dynamics of the volume of special strength-training means, the elasticity of the muscles and the control results of the standing triple-jump of highly qualified sprinters during the spring-summer stage (15 weeks) of training.\" \"An increase in the volume of special strength-training means results in increased muscle stiffness and decreased explosive force. Unfavourable conditions were thus created for improving technique and running speed and the likelihood of injury increased significantly.\"\n\nThe authors add that \"voluminous strength loading is detrimental to the technique of the weightlifting movements (Khlystov, 1976; Vorobyev et al, 1978), javelin throwing (Ruvinsky, 1980) and punching speed in boxing (Filimonov, 1979),\" and conclude: \"Rather than negating the value of concentrated loading, these findings stress the importance of knowing when this method may be appropriate to use.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The book reports correlations between volume and outcomes; it gives no sample sizes, effect sizes or statistics for either study. The only mechanism offered is Levchenko's observed rise in \"muscle stiffness\" with strength volume. The book does not define how stiffness or elasticity was measured.\n\n**When to apply** — As the evidence behind expecting a temporary drop in jump and sprint qualities during high-volume strength phases, and a rebound when volume falls. The authors' remedy follows on the same page: \"if concentrated loading is used, then *cybernetic periodisation* or programming based on Perceived Ratings of Effort and Ratings of Technique, as well as formal testing, can assist greatly in preventing overtraining (see 6.2.2).\" The passage reads as Siff's (cybernetic periodisation is his term elsewhere in the book), and the cited section 6.2.2 is outside this batch. Eric's baseline: \"Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker in place of subjective wellness questionnaires\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 74, Explicit). Inference: a daily jump measure is the kind of \"formal testing\" that would catch the drop Antonova and Levchenko describe.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Both groups were qualified track athletes (\"highly qualified sprinters\" for Levchenko; the long jumpers' level is not stated). The injury statement (\"the likelihood of injury increased significantly\") gives no injury counts. The book does not say whether loads were heavy or moderate, only that volume rose. This duplicates in part the Chapter 5 warning in [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], which cites Kotz, Filinov and Deniskin; the Chapter 6 page adds the Antonova and Levchenko studies and spells the boxing author \"Filimonov, 1979.\"\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is the Antonova finding: results fell in the highest-volume months and rose in the reduced-loading months that followed (PDF p. 382).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]], [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b5b6504e-a510-4185-aa8b-89ade126216e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
