{"parentUuid":"abe0fa8a-0c6c-40d2-bd6d-99642f87b3f6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0185sMMYWAvHDRnd3b2ENL1z","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"677f3732-8a6b-4d6f-88c1-35d899a9fa8b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:19:07.885Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities.md","oldString":"The passage reads as Siff's (cybernetic periodisation is his term elsewhere in the book), and the cited section 6.2.2 is outside this batch.","newString":"Section 6.2.2 defines cybernetic periodisation as a scheme in which \"the original preplanned periodisation scheme is regularly modified by subjective and objective feedback\" (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329, text layer).","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: strength volume and explosive qualities\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, explosive-strength, muscle-stiffness, injury-risk, jumpers, sprinters]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Antonova (Women Long Jumpers) and Levchenko (Sprinters): Months of Peak Strength Volume Lowered Explosive Qualities, Which Rose Again When Loading Fell\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Problems with Concentrated Loading\" the authors report two Soviet studies (PDF p. 382 / printed p. 371, checked against the page image). They open with the general cost: concentrated strength loading \"leads to a brief but consistent decrease in speed-strength, which diminishes the athlete's special work-capacity and complicates the task of improving technical skills and speed of movement.\"\n1. **Antonova (1982), women long jumpers.** The study analysed \"the connection between the organisation and the volume of training loads, the changes in the force-time characteristics of the take-off in jumping, the sport results and the explosive strength.\" \"It was found that in the months with the largest training volumes, there was a noticeable decrease in the special-strength preparedness and sporting results. However, these same qualities rose during the subsequent months of reduced loading.\"\n2. **Levchenko (1980), sprinters.** It examined \"the interdependence between the dynamics of the volume of special strength-training means, the elasticity of the muscles and the control results of the standing triple-jump of highly qualified sprinters during the spring-summer stage (15 weeks) of training.\" \"An increase in the volume of special strength-training means results in increased muscle stiffness and decreased explosive force. Unfavourable conditions were thus created for improving technique and running speed and the likelihood of injury increased significantly.\"\n\nThe authors add that \"voluminous strength loading is detrimental to the technique of the weightlifting movements (Khlystov, 1976; Vorobyev et al, 1978), javelin throwing (Ruvinsky, 1980) and punching speed in boxing (Filimonov, 1979),\" and conclude: \"Rather than negating the value of concentrated loading, these findings stress the importance of knowing when this method may be appropriate to use.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The book reports correlations between volume and outcomes; it gives no sample sizes, effect sizes or statistics for either study. The only mechanism offered is Levchenko's observed rise in \"muscle stiffness\" with strength volume. The book does not define how stiffness or elasticity was measured.\n\n**When to apply** — As the evidence behind expecting a temporary drop in jump and sprint qualities during high-volume strength phases, and a rebound when volume falls. The authors' remedy follows on the same page: \"if concentrated loading is used, then *cybernetic periodisation* or programming based on Perceived Ratings of Effort and Ratings of Technique, as well as formal testing, can assist greatly in preventing overtraining (see 6.2.2).\" The passage reads as Siff's (cybernetic periodisation is his term elsewhere in the book), and the cited section 6.2.2 is outside this batch. Eric's baseline: \"Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker in place of subjective wellness questionnaires\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 74, Explicit). Inference: a daily jump measure is the kind of \"formal testing\" that would catch the drop Antonova and Levchenko describe.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Both groups were qualified track athletes (\"highly qualified sprinters\" for Levchenko; the long jumpers' level is not stated). The injury statement (\"the likelihood of injury increased significantly\") gives no injury counts. The book does not say whether loads were heavy or moderate, only that volume rose. This duplicates in part the Chapter 5 warning in [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], which cites Kotz, Filinov and Deniskin; the Chapter 6 page adds the Antonova and Levchenko studies and spells the boxing author \"Filimonov, 1979.\"\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is the Antonova finding: results fell in the highest-volume months and rose in the reduced-loading months that followed (PDF p. 382).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]], [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":21,"oldLines":7,"newStart":21,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The book reports correlations between volume and outcomes; it gives no sample sizes, effect sizes or statistics for either study. The only mechanism offered is Levchenko's observed rise in \"muscle stiffness\" with strength volume. The book does not define how stiffness or elasticity was measured."," ","-**When to apply** — As the evidence behind expecting a temporary drop in jump and sprint qualities during high-volume strength phases, and a rebound when volume falls. The authors' remedy follows on the same page: \"if concentrated loading is used, then *cybernetic periodisation* or programming based on Perceived Ratings of Effort and Ratings of Technique, as well as formal testing, can assist greatly in preventing overtraining (see 6.2.2).\" The passage reads as Siff's (cybernetic periodisation is his term elsewhere in the book), and the cited section 6.2.2 is outside this batch. Eric's baseline: \"Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker in place of subjective wellness questionnaires\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 74, Explicit). Inference: a daily jump measure is the kind of \"formal testing\" that would catch the drop Antonova and Levchenko describe.","+**When to apply** — As the evidence behind expecting a temporary drop in jump and sprint qualities during high-volume strength phases, and a rebound when volume falls. The authors' remedy follows on the same page: \"if concentrated loading is used, then *cybernetic periodisation* or programming based on Perceived Ratings of Effort and Ratings of Technique, as well as formal testing, can assist greatly in preventing overtraining (see 6.2.2).\" Section 6.2.2 defines cybernetic periodisation as a scheme in which \"the original preplanned periodisation scheme is regularly modified by subjective and objective feedback\" (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329, text layer). Eric's baseline: \"Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker in place of subjective wellness questionnaires\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 74, Explicit). Inference: a daily jump measure is the kind of \"formal testing\" that would catch the drop Antonova and Levchenko describe."," "," **When NOT to apply** — Both groups were qualified track athletes (\"highly qualified sprinters\" for Levchenko; the long jumpers' level is not stated). The injury statement (\"the likelihood of injury increased significantly\") gives no injury counts. The book does not say whether loads were heavy or moderate, only that volume rose. This duplicates in part the Chapter 5 warning in [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], which cites Kotz, Filinov and Deniskin; the Chapter 6 page adds the Antonova and Levchenko studies and spells the boxing author \"Filimonov, 1979.\""," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"abe0fa8a-0c6c-40d2-bd6d-99642f87b3f6","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"677f3732-8a6b-4d6f-88c1-35d899a9fa8b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmJwej4U17Yj1UfPZCU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SahKivvKSKb6LSiiKjLogq","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: concentrated strength block evidence\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, block-periodization, strength, programming, advanced]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Concentrated Strength Loading: a Substitutable Block of Up to 2 Months, Claimed Best for Highly Qualified Athletes and to Cut Annual Strength Volume by 13-15%\n\n**What it is** — Chapter 6 restates the concentrated method and adds claims not in the Chapter 5 entry on its conditions (PDF pp. 381-382 / printed pp. 370-371, both checked against the page images):\n1. **Duration.** \"The main novelty of this method consists of exerting a concentrated training influence on the body with a high volume of unidirectional loading for a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF p. 381, citing Verkhoshansky, 1977). Earlier on the same page: \"the primary use of unidirectional training for prolonged stages (4-12 weeks) of training can be very effective.\" The two figures sit a paragraph apart; 12 weeks is longer than 2 months, and the book does not reconcile them (the 4-12 weeks refers to \"unidirectional training,\" the 2 months to \"concentrated\" loading).\n2. **The strength block as a planning unit.** \"Concentrated strength loading constitutes a relatively independent part or block of training to create a functional foundation for the subsequent enhancement of technical skill or those motor abilities which chiefly determine success in a given sport.\" The block's means must fit \"the training specifics of that type of sport,\" and \"The strength block should have a specific place in the annual cycle.\" \"To achieve the principal aims of organising loading in the annual cycle, the coach must select a certain strength block or substitute one block for another, depending on the situation and the individual. Obviously, in order to do this, he needs to prepare a detailed selection of appropriate strength blocks in advance\" (PDF p. 381). Concentrated strength loading matters \"not only for raising the athlete's physical fitness, but also for regulating the entire complex of loading over the annual cycle in all sports.\"\n3. **Evidence claimed.** \"The effectiveness of concentrated strength loading has been confirmed by many coaches and special research in a number of speed-strength, cyclic and team sports (Varakin, 1979; Zhdanovich, 1980; Rudokene, 1981; Naraliev, 1981; Ptushkin, 1981; Verkhoshanskaya, 1982; Kabachkova et al, 1982). In particular, it has been established that this system is most appropriate for highly qualified athletes. Moreover, the use of concentrated strength loading enables the general annual volume of strength loading to be reduced (by 13-15%), when compared with the conventional approach (Mironenko, 1981; Antonova, 1982)\" (PDF p. 382).\n4. **Advanced stages.** \"In more advanced stages, the concentrated unidirectional method of loading is the most effective way of further increasing the high level of physical fitness which athletes achieve with multi-year training,\" provided one finds \"regimes of concentrated loading which definitely do not produce negative training effects (Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" (PDF p. 382).\n\n**Two volume-saving figures.** Chapter 5 says \"the training volume over a macrocycle can be reduced by as much as 20%\" with the conjugate sequence system for advanced athletes (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; see [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]). Chapter 6 says the \"general annual volume of strength loading\" falls by 13-15%. These measure different things (all training volume over a macrocycle versus annual strength volume), so they are recorded side by side, not as a contradiction.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give a mechanism at PDF p. 381: the concentrated influence \"produces a brief, fairly stable state of incomplete restoration which is associated with the persistent and relatively prolonged disturbance of homeostasis. This triggers extensive functional changes which are prerequisites for subsequent supercompensation and the raising of the body to a higher level of special preparedness.\" The cited studies are named without design, sample size or effect size.\n\n**When to apply** — Building an annual plan for qualified athletes around one or two strength blocks, each designed for the sport and kept ready to swap in or out. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract: lay out the competition calendar, key non-training dates (holidays, exam weeks), and phase-by-phase volume/intensity waves, then adapt the route as circumstances change while still reaching the same destination\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' \"detailed selection of appropriate strength blocks in advance\" is a concrete way to make that roadmap adaptable.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin** for team sports. The book says concentrated strength loading was confirmed in \"team sports\" (PDF p. 382); the library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports \"improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance,\" and that Issurin judged the evidence \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6). See [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]. The method is stated as most appropriate for highly qualified athletes, not novices. It has costs, set out on the same page: a \"brief but consistent decrease in speed-strength\" ([[case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' sports examples are general (speed-strength, cyclic and team sports, PDF p. 382). Inference: a strength coach keeps three prepared strength blocks for a jumper (a general-strength block, a speed-strength block, a reactive block) and chooses which to run in the second preparation phase according to how the first block went.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]], [[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1616,"cache_read_input_tokens":279071,"output_tokens":2958,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":142},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1616},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2958,"cache_read_input_tokens":279071,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1616,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1616,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01SahKivvKSKb6LSiiKjLogq":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: concentrated strength block evidence\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, block-periodization, strength, programming, advanced]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Concentrated Strength Loading: a Substitutable Block of Up to 2 Months, Claimed Best for Highly Qualified Athletes and to Cut Annual Strength Volume by 13-15%\n\n**What it is** — Chapter 6 restates the concentrated method and adds claims not in the Chapter 5 entry on its conditions (PDF pp. 381-382 / printed pp. 370-371, both checked against the page images):\n1. **Duration.** \"The main novelty of this method consists of exerting a concentrated training influence on the body with a high volume of unidirectional loading for a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF p. 381, citing Verkhoshansky, 1977). Earlier on the same page: \"the primary use of unidirectional training for prolonged stages (4-12 weeks) of training can be very effective.\" The two figures sit a paragraph apart; 12 weeks is longer than 2 months, and the book does not reconcile them (the 4-12 weeks refers to \"unidirectional training,\" the 2 months to \"concentrated\" loading).\n2. **The strength block as a planning unit.** \"Concentrated strength loading constitutes a relatively independent part or block of training to create a functional foundation for the subsequent enhancement of technical skill or those motor abilities which chiefly determine success in a given sport.\" The block's means must fit \"the training specifics of that type of sport,\" and \"The strength block should have a specific place in the annual cycle.\" \"To achieve the principal aims of organising loading in the annual cycle, the coach must select a certain strength block or substitute one block for another, depending on the situation and the individual. Obviously, in order to do this, he needs to prepare a detailed selection of appropriate strength blocks in advance\" (PDF p. 381). Concentrated strength loading matters \"not only for raising the athlete's physical fitness, but also for regulating the entire complex of loading over the annual cycle in all sports.\"\n3. **Evidence claimed.** \"The effectiveness of concentrated strength loading has been confirmed by many coaches and special research in a number of speed-strength, cyclic and team sports (Varakin, 1979; Zhdanovich, 1980; Rudokene, 1981; Naraliev, 1981; Ptushkin, 1981; Verkhoshanskaya, 1982; Kabachkova et al, 1982). In particular, it has been established that this system is most appropriate for highly qualified athletes. Moreover, the use of concentrated strength loading enables the general annual volume of strength loading to be reduced (by 13-15%), when compared with the conventional approach (Mironenko, 1981; Antonova, 1982)\" (PDF p. 382).\n4. **Advanced stages.** \"In more advanced stages, the concentrated unidirectional method of loading is the most effective way of further increasing the high level of physical fitness which athletes achieve with multi-year training,\" provided one finds \"regimes of concentrated loading which definitely do not produce negative training effects (Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" (PDF p. 382).\n\n**Two volume-saving figures.** Chapter 5 says \"the training volume over a macrocycle can be reduced by as much as 20%\" with the conjugate sequence system for advanced athletes (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; see [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]). Chapter 6 says the \"general annual volume of strength loading\" falls by 13-15%. These measure different things (all training volume over a macrocycle versus annual strength volume), so they are recorded side by side, not as a contradiction.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give a mechanism at PDF p. 381: the concentrated influence \"produces a brief, fairly stable state of incomplete restoration which is associated with the persistent and relatively prolonged disturbance of homeostasis. This triggers extensive functional changes which are prerequisites for subsequent supercompensation and the raising of the body to a higher level of special preparedness.\" The cited studies are named without design, sample size or effect size.\n\n**When to apply** — Building an annual plan for qualified athletes around one or two strength blocks, each designed for the sport and kept ready to swap in or out. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract: lay out the competition calendar, key non-training dates (holidays, exam weeks), and phase-by-phase volume/intensity waves, then adapt the route as circumstances change while still reaching the same destination\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' \"detailed selection of appropriate strength blocks in advance\" is a concrete way to make that roadmap adaptable.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin** for team sports. The book says concentrated strength loading was confirmed in \"team sports\" (PDF p. 382); the library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports \"improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance,\" and that Issurin judged the evidence \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6). See [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]. The method is stated as most appropriate for highly qualified athletes, not novices. It has costs, set out on the same page: a \"brief but consistent decrease in speed-strength\" ([[case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' sports examples are general (speed-strength, cyclic and team sports, PDF p. 382). Inference: a strength coach keeps three prepared strength blocks for a jumper (a general-strength block, a speed-strength block, a reactive block) and chooses which to run in the second preparation phase according to how the first block went.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]], [[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffmJFTm2UUJQkMP3td1z","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"caf416fb-cd83-444a-9993-d7116d5994ec","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:19:26.453Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
