igh level of physical fitness which athletes achieve with multi-year training,\" provided one finds \"regimes of concentrated loading which definitely do not produce negative training effects (Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" (PDF p. 382).\n\n**Two volume-saving figures.** Chapter 5 says \"the training volume over a macrocycle can be reduced by as much as 20%\" with the conjugate sequence system for advanced athletes (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; see [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]). Chapter 6 says the \"general annual volume of strength loading\" falls by 13-15%. These measure different things (all training volume over a macrocycle versus annual strength volume), so they are recorded side by side, not as a contradiction.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give a mechanism at PDF p. 381: the concentrated influence \"produces a brief, fairly stable state of incomplete restoration which is associated with the persistent and relatively prolonged disturbance of homeostasis. This triggers extensive functional changes which are prerequisites for subsequent supercompensation and the raising of the body to a higher level of special preparedness.\" The cited studies are named without design, sample size or effect size.\n\n**When to apply** — Building an annual plan for qualified athletes around one or two strength blocks, each designed for the sport and kept ready to swap in or out. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract: lay out the competition calendar, key non-training dates (holidays, exam weeks), and phase-by-phase volume/intensity waves, then adapt the route as circumstances change while still reaching the same destination\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' \"detailed selection of appropriate strength blocks in advance\" is a concrete way to make that roadmap adaptable.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin** for team sports. The book says concentrated strength loading was confirmed in \"team sports\" (PDF p. 382); the library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports \"improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance,\" and that Issurin judged the evidence \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6). See [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]. The method is stated as most appropriate for highly qualified athletes, not novices. It has costs, set out on the same page: a \"brief but consistent decrease in speed-strength\" ([[case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' sports examples are general (speed-strength, cyclic and team sports, PDF p. 382). Inference: a strength coach keeps three prepared strength blocks for a jumper (a general-strength block, a speed-strength block, a reactive block) and chooses which to run in the second preparation phase according to how the first block went.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]], [[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"caf416fb-cd83-444a-9993-d7116d5994ec","sessionKind":"bg","use