{"parentUuid":"270e4f9b-17f5-4f6b-a4fb-4152ab7c5665","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_record","entries":[],"toolInputCopies":[{"id":"toolu_0185sMMYWAvHDRnd3b2ENL1z","copy":"wire"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"8beedc56-41fd-4e85-8776-1f62bda072e4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:19:39.297Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8beedc56-41fd-4e85-8776-1f62bda072e4","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmMQpfzJAAHNDFggPJQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3252,"cache_read_input_tokens":280687,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3252,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1205,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffmLg6AB9ZQcNNG2Xjyy","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"e2934415-9292-4498-a910-c031d0a133a8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:19:39.296Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"e2934415-9292-4498-a910-c031d0a133a8","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmMQpfzJAAHNDFggPJQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0164gMRoePhee58hocs91ENp","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: complex versus unidirectional training\ncategory: [complex-training, concurrent-training, unidirectional-loading, advanced, programming]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Complex (Concurrent) Training Suits Beginners and Average Athletes; for Highly Qualified Athletes the Authors Favour Single-Emphasis Workouts, Microcycles and Stages\n\n**What it is** — The authors define the two ways of organising training: \"Complex loading involves prescription of multi-faceted training regimes to achieve several different fitness objectives over the same period, whereas the concentrated loading method concentrates for a given period on producing a single major specific fitness quality via the use of a unidirectional regime of training\" (PDF p. 379 / printed p. 368). \"For instance, a complex workout might comprise resistance training, plyometrics and sprinting; a complex microcycle (typically a week) might employ those same training means on different days or during different sessions on the same day.\" Using complex means requires knowing how the means interact through \"the acute and delayed after-effects of each\" (PDF p. 379).\n\nTheir case against complex training for top athletes (PDF pp. 380-381 / printed pp. 369-370, all pages checked against the images):\n- **The supporting research is old and used weak athletes.** The early studies (Krestovnikov, 1951; Letunov et al., 1954; Zimkin, 1956; Korobkov et al, 1960; Matveyev, 1970) found complex loading gave balanced fitness and better strength, speed and endurance than unidirectional work. The authors call these concepts \"indisputable in principle,\" but \"the research supporting these ideas was done many years ago and utilised athletes of low qualification... under modern conditions this loading would be applicable only to beginners or athletes of average qualification\" (PDF p. 380).\n- **Verkhoshansky's verdict.** \"According to Verkhoshansky (1977), there generally appears to be little advantage for high-level athletes to utilise the complex system of training, although one should not dismiss their possible value at different stages of training with certain individuals in specific cases\" (PDF p. 380).\n- **Three deficiencies for highly qualified athletes.** (1) Their very high special preparedness needs \"strong and relatively prolonged training influences of appropriate emphasis. Complex training does not achieve this.\" (2) \"Complex training, with its multifaceted influence on the body, cannot create the conditions necessary for producing highly specific physical preparedness.\" (3) \"Extensive complex loading to simultaneously perfect sport technique and special physical preparedness inevitably leads to general fatigue and deterioration of this control\" (PDF p. 380). These deficiencies \"are not as noticeable in athletes of average qualification.\"\n- **Evidence for single-emphasis units.** Single-emphasis workouts make adaptation \"more marked\" (Handelsman & Stetsenko, 1980); sessions devoted solely to technique are \"very effective in improving technical skill\" (Struchkov, 1980; Mironenko, 1981; Petrovsky, 1977); single-ability sessions \"exert a more profound effect on special preparedness\" than multi-objective sessions (five studies cited); single-emphasis microcycles \"powerfully stimulate the trainability of swimmers (Kudelin, 1980)\" and strength-emphasis microcycles \"significantly raise the special physical preparedness of track-and-field jumpers (Mironenko, 1981)\" (PDF p. 381).\n- **The resolution: complexity over time.** \"*The conjugate sequence system does not reject the complex (concurrent) method as the most general principle of training, but only extends it so that it meets the requirements of conditioning highly qualified athletes.* Complexity should be understood in terms of its evolution over time, not its contents and structure at a given instant\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373). Complex preparation \"is limited as a general principle for training all athletes at all stages of development.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are the three deficiencies above: too weak a stimulus in any one direction, too little specificity, and fatigue that degrades technical control. Their stated mechanism for the alternative is \"the summation of positive, accumulated after-effects of preceding loading of different primary emphasis\" (PDF p. 384). No effect sizes are given for any cited study.\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding whether a well-trained athlete's week should mix every quality in each session or give whole sessions and whole weeks to one emphasis. Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182, Inferred (transcript + slides)). Inference: the baseline and the authors agree that not everything is trained hard at once; the baseline still trains other qualities \"at a retention dose,\" closer to Issurin's multi-targeted blocks than to a single emphasis.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin.** Issurin's multi-targeted block model trains a minimal number of targets per block, not one ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]), and the library's brief of Issurin 2016 records the multi-targeted model's 28-study support against insufficient evidence for the concentrated-unidirectional model in team, combat and swimming preparation ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], pp. 6-7, 11). Inside the book there is a caveat a few pages later: a swimming week built from single-emphasis workouts of different kinds still \"typifies the complex (multi-faceted) form of training organisation\" and \"fails to produce a definite increase in speed, aerobic or anaerobic productivity\" (PDF pp. 387-388 / printed pp. 376-377; see [[case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength]]). So single-emphasis workouts alone are not enough; the authors want the emphasis held across a microcycle or stage. Unidirectional loading \"is effective only if it forms part of a complex of several means of a single primary emphasis involving a variety of methods\" (PDF p. 381). The argument is for highly qualified athletes; for beginners the authors keep complex training.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example of a complex unit: a workout of \"resistance training, plyometrics and sprinting,\" or a week using those means on different days (PDF p. 379). Their single-emphasis examples are the swimmer and jumper microcycles of Kudelin (1980) and Mironenko (1981) (PDF p. 381).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]], [[case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength]], [[claim--strength-and-speed-training-response-shifts-with-training-age]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3252,"cache_read_input_tokens":280687,"output_tokens":2888,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":192},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3252},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2888,"cache_read_input_tokens":280687,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3252,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3252,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0164gMRoePhee58hocs91ENp":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: complex versus unidirectional training\ncategory: [complex-training, concurrent-training, unidirectional-loading, advanced, programming]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Complex (Concurrent) Training Suits Beginners and Average Athletes; for Highly Qualified Athletes the Authors Favour Single-Emphasis Workouts, Microcycles and Stages\n\n**What it is** — The authors define the two ways of organising training: \"Complex loading involves prescription of multi-faceted training regimes to achieve several different fitness objectives over the same period, whereas the concentrated loading method concentrates for a given period on producing a single major specific fitness quality via the use of a unidirectional regime of training\" (PDF p. 379 / printed p. 368). \"For instance, a complex workout might comprise resistance training, plyometrics and sprinting; a complex microcycle (typically a week) might employ those same training means on different days or during different sessions on the same day.\" Using complex means requires knowing how the means interact through \"the acute and delayed after-effects of each\" (PDF p. 379).\n\nTheir case against complex training for top athletes (PDF pp. 380-381 / printed pp. 369-370, all pages checked against the images):\n- **The supporting research is old and used weak athletes.** The early studies (Krestovnikov, 1951; Letunov et al., 1954; Zimkin, 1956; Korobkov et al, 1960; Matveyev, 1970) found complex loading gave balanced fitness and better strength, speed and endurance than unidirectional work. The authors call these concepts \"indisputable in principle,\" but \"the research supporting these ideas was done many years ago and utilised athletes of low qualification... under modern conditions this loading would be applicable only to beginners or athletes of average qualification\" (PDF p. 380).\n- **Verkhoshansky's verdict.** \"According to Verkhoshansky (1977), there generally appears to be little advantage for high-level athletes to utilise the complex system of training, although one should not dismiss their possible value at different stages of training with certain individuals in specific cases\" (PDF p. 380).\n- **Three deficiencies for highly qualified athletes.** (1) Their very high special preparedness needs \"strong and relatively prolonged training influences of appropriate emphasis. Complex training does not achieve this.\" (2) \"Complex training, with its multifaceted influence on the body, cannot create the conditions necessary for producing highly specific physical preparedness.\" (3) \"Extensive complex loading to simultaneously perfect sport technique and special physical preparedness inevitably leads to general fatigue and deterioration of this control\" (PDF p. 380). These deficiencies \"are not as noticeable in athletes of average qualification.\"\n- **Evidence for single-emphasis units.** Single-emphasis workouts make adaptation \"more marked\" (Handelsman & Stetsenko, 1980); sessions devoted solely to technique are \"very effective in improving technical skill\" (Struchkov, 1980; Mironenko, 1981; Petrovsky, 1977); single-ability sessions \"exert a more profound effect on special preparedness\" than multi-objective sessions (five studies cited); single-emphasis microcycles \"powerfully stimulate the trainability of swimmers (Kudelin, 1980)\" and strength-emphasis microcycles \"significantly raise the special physical preparedness of track-and-field jumpers (Mironenko, 1981)\" (PDF p. 381).\n- **The resolution: complexity over time.** \"*The conjugate sequence system does not reject the complex (concurrent) method as the most general principle of training, but only extends it so that it meets the requirements of conditioning highly qualified athletes.* Complexity should be understood in terms of its evolution over time, not its contents and structure at a given instant\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373). Complex preparation \"is limited as a general principle for training all athletes at all stages of development.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are the three deficiencies above: too weak a stimulus in any one direction, too little specificity, and fatigue that degrades technical control. Their stated mechanism for the alternative is \"the summation of positive, accumulated after-effects of preceding loading of different primary emphasis\" (PDF p. 384). No effect sizes are given for any cited study.\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding whether a well-trained athlete's week should mix every quality in each session or give whole sessions and whole weeks to one emphasis. Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182, Inferred (transcript + slides)). Inference: the baseline and the authors agree that not everything is trained hard at once; the baseline still trains other qualities \"at a retention dose,\" closer to Issurin's multi-targeted blocks than to a single emphasis.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin.** Issurin's multi-targeted block model trains a minimal number of targets per block, not one ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]), and the library's brief of Issurin 2016 records the multi-targeted model's 28-study support against insufficient evidence for the concentrated-unidirectional model in team, combat and swimming preparation ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], pp. 6-7, 11). Inside the book there is a caveat a few pages later: a swimming week built from single-emphasis workouts of different kinds still \"typifies the complex (multi-faceted) form of training organisation\" and \"fails to produce a definite increase in speed, aerobic or anaerobic productivity\" (PDF pp. 387-388 / printed pp. 376-377; see [[case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength]]). So single-emphasis workouts alone are not enough; the authors want the emphasis held across a microcycle or stage. Unidirectional loading \"is effective only if it forms part of a complex of several means of a single primary emphasis involving a variety of methods\" (PDF p. 381). The argument is for highly qualified athletes; for beginners the authors keep complex training.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example of a complex unit: a workout of \"resistance training, plyometrics and sprinting,\" or a week using those means on different days (PDF p. 379). Their single-emphasis examples are the swimmer and jumper microcycles of Kudelin (1980) and Mironenko (1981) (PDF p. 381).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]], [[case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength]], [[claim--strength-and-speed-training-response-shifts-with-training-age]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffmLg6AB9ZQcNNG2Xjyy","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"b83fa382-499b-4aff-bf4b-4d7df2136c8c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:19:59.682Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"b83fa382-499b-4aff-bf4b-4d7df2136c8c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0164gMRoePhee58hocs91ENp","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"9835efe2-01fd-4f88-916c-e7c55c7472ec","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:19:59.802Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: complex versus unidirectional training\ncategory: [complex-training, concurrent-training, unidirectional-loading, advanced, programming]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Complex (Concurrent) Training Suits Beginners and Average Athletes; for Highly Qualified Athletes the Authors Favour Single-Emphasis Workouts, Microcycles and Stages\n\n**What it is** — The authors define the two ways of organising training: \"Complex loading involves prescription of multi-faceted training regimes to achieve several different fitness objectives over the same period, whereas the concentrated loading method concentrates for a given period on producing a single major specific fitness quality via the use of a unidirectional regime of training\" (PDF p. 379 / printed p. 368). \"For instance, a complex workout might comprise resistance training, plyometrics and sprinting; a complex microcycle (typically a week) might employ those same training means on different