(Handelsman & Stetsenko, 1980); sessions devoted solely to technique are \"very effective in improving technical skill\" (Struchkov, 1980; Mironenko, 1981; Petrovsky, 1977); single-ability sessions \"exert a more profound effect on special preparedness\" than multi-objective sessions (five studies cited); single-emphasis microcycles \"powerfully stimulate the trainability of swimmers (Kudelin, 1980)\" and strength-emphasis microcycles \"significantly raise the special physical preparedness of track-and-field jumpers (Mironenko, 1981)\" (PDF p. 381).\n- **The resolution: complexity over time.** \"*The conjugate sequence system does not reject the complex (concurrent) method as the most general principle of training, but only extends it so that it meets the requirements of conditioning highly qualified athletes.* Complexity should be understood in terms of its evolution over time, not its contents and structure at a given instant\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373). Complex preparation \"is limited as a general principle for training all athletes at all stages of development.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are the three deficiencies above: too weak a stimulus in any one direction, too little specificity, and fatigue that degrades technical control. Their stated mechanism for the alternative is \"the summation of positive, accumulated after-effects of preceding loading of different primary emphasis\" (PDF p. 384). No effect sizes are given for any cited study.\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding whether a well-trained athlete's week should mix every quality in each session or give whole sessions and whole weeks to one emphasis. Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182, Inferred (transcript + slides)). Inference: the baseline and the authors agree that not everything is trained hard at once; the baseline still trains other qualities \"at a retention dose,\" closer to Issurin's multi-targeted blocks than to a single emphasis.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin.** Issurin's multi-targeted block model trains a minimal number of targets per block, not one ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]), and the library's brief of Issurin 2016 records the multi-targeted model's 28-study support against insufficient evidence for the concentrated-unidirectional model in team, combat and swimming preparation ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], pp. 6-7, 11). Inside the book there is a caveat a few pages later: a swimming week built from single-emphasis workouts of different kinds still \"typifies the complex (multi-faceted) form of training organisation\" and \"fails to produce a definite increase in speed, aerobic or anaerobic productivity\" (PDF pp. 387-388 / printed pp. 376-377; see [[case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength]]). So single-emphasis workouts alone are not enough; the authors want the emphasis held across a microcycle or stage. Unidirectional loading \"is effective only if it forms part of a complex of several means of a single primary emphasis involving a variety of methods\" (PDF p. 381). The argument is for highly qualified athletes; for beginners the authors keep complex training.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example of a complex unit: a workout of \"resistance training, plyometrics and sprinting,\" or a week using those means on different days (PDF p. 379). Their single-emphasis examples are the swimmer and jumper microcycles of Kudelin (1980) and Mironenko (1981) (PDF p. 381).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]], [[case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength]], [[claim--strength-and-speed-training-response-shifts-with-training-age]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b83fa382-499b-4aff-bf4b-4d7df2136c8c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
