cuting the motor tasks,\" and \"This type of work is quite compatible with the athlete's decreased functional state, provided it is executed in the optimal range of intensity of effort and frequency of movement.\"\n2. **Speed starts with the LDTE.** \"Speed work begins only at the beginning of the LDTE of strength loading (when the 'State' curve rises above the time axis), together with the necessary gradual rise in the training intensity.\"\n3. **Two anaerobic-glycolytic peaks.** High competition speed \"is determined largely by specific speed-endurance.\" The glycolytic loading \"exhibits two peaks\": the first \"is timed to end near the conclusion of the stage of concentrated strength loading. Specialised strength means are employed and the objective is to develop special strength-endurance which is primarily of a local nature\"; the second \"follows the period during which volume of speed work reaches a peak. Distance methods (in cyclic sports) or specialised means and methods (in one-on-one events and team sports) are employed... this type of work should be done in segments of small volume and combined with speed work.\"\n4. **Protect explosive strength.** \"Along with the rise in strength-endurance, explosive strength tends to decrease when the second peak of the anaerobic-glycolytic loading occurs. Therefore, in those sports where explosive strength is of great importance, it is necessary to include a sufficiently intensive, small volume of maintenance loading.\"\n\nFig 6.32(b), read from the image (PDF p. 383, no numeric scale): a shaded concentrated strength wave on the left; most curves (Vmax, S-E, Speed, STATE, An-Gly) dip or stay low during it and rise after it ends, with Vmax ending highest at the right. Individual curves are hard to follow where they cross.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give the reason for allowing coordination work during the block (it is compatible with a lowered functional state) and for the maintenance dose (explosive strength falls during the second glycolytic peak). They do not explain why speed must be excluded entirely rather than reduced. Inference: it follows from their rule that high-intensity work needs a fresh, not a depressed, functional state ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]).\n\n**When to apply** — Cyclic speed sports such as sprinting, and by the authors' mention of \"team sports\" for the second glycolytic peak, sports where repeated speed matters. Eric's baseline: \"For football specifically, the stated priority stack is: Goal 1, alactic and aerobic capacity/power for fall-camp demands; Goal 2, the same two systems for game demands; glycolytic conditioning is treated as a \\\"worst case scenario\\\" to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 115, Explicit, claimed again as Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: the authors program two deliberate glycolytic peaks, which goes further than the baseline; their own caution (small segments combined with speed work, a maintenance dose for explosive strength) is the part that fits the baseline best.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Speed-strength and complex-technical sports keep some technique and do not separate speed so sharply ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Eric's baseline also says \"staff do program it in the off-season\" for glycolytic work (line 117, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)), so the conflict with line 115 is about in-season emphasis. No volumes, durations or intensities are given for either glycolytic peak.\n\n**Example** — The authors give the schematic only. Inference: a 200 m sprinter spends a concentrated strength block doing strength-endurance circuits and relaxed technical drills with no sprinting, starts sprint work as the block ends, and adds short speed-endurance runs only after sprint volume has peaked, with a few explosive lifts kept in each week.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities]], [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"360811c4-136d-4960-a251-c7e2ffbdfe32","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
