{"parentUuid":"ed0ff040-d901-4310-a235-d325cc8759dc","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14709897 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"fb523736-76dd-4b26-a5e8-6dbddb27e2f4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:21:01.695Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14709897 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"fb523736-76dd-4b26-a5e8-6dbddb27e2f4","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmU19agk3h9eoNu9q1q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":690,"cache_read_input_tokens":289700,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":690,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":583,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffmTgc75PyHoCECX5kbG","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"b001f12f-16f4-417b-9d08-d6546a2c7943","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:21:08.204Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"b001f12f-16f4-417b-9d08-d6546a2c7943","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmU19agk3h9eoNu9q1q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LxrHYGKxSGphqdSsowUdhx","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.3\"\ntopic: mesocycle organisation variants\ncategory: [mesocycle, periodization, concentrated-loading, volume-intensity, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Two Ways to Build a Large Mesocycle: Variant A Rises Smoothly to a Plateau; Variant B Concentrates Load First, Dips, Then Rebounds Higher\n\n**What it is** — In 6.12.3 (PDF pp. 385-387 / printed pp. 374-376, all checked against the page images) the authors compare two forms of mesocycle organisation (Fig 6.33, PDF p. 386).\n\n**A terminology warning first.** \"In this chapter, *the term 'mesocycle' is reserved for what in Russian is called 'the large stage' or large mesocycle*, which extends over several months to produce a specific adaptive effect... The large mesocycle, in fact, may comprise a few conventional mesocycles and also be referred to as a phase\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 385). Its duration \"can vary (usually 3-5 months), since its calculation depends on the competition calendar and especially on the dates of the most important competitions\" (PDF p. 387).\n\n**Common practice, as the authors report it.** \"The literature reveals that three and four week stages are the most frequently used, where the last one week serves as a restoration week. There are combinations of two weeks with heavy loading and two weeks with moderate loading, or an alternation of weeks with high and moderate loading... all of them, depending on circumstances, can produce good results. It is very difficult to establish which of these is the most effective, since the criteria are extremely diverse\" (PDF p. 385). Research has concentrated on the annual cycle and the microcycle, plus the \"pre-competition training stage lasting 4-6 weeks\" (PDF p. 385).\n\n**The two variants.**\n- **Variant A** \"displays a smooth increase in the functional indicators until it reaches a distinct plateau\" and \"is a traditional form of training organisation\" (PDF p. 385). In Fig 6.33(b) its volume curve rises to a broad peak mid-cycle and falls, GPP peaks early and declines, and the fitness state rises steadily.\n- **Variant B** \"begins with a steady decline which is followed by a sharp increase in the functional indicators up to a level which significantly exceeds the maximum of the first variant\" (PDF p. 385). It has two micro-stages: a first one of concentrated special physical preparation that \"produces a steady decrease in the functional indicators,\" then lower-volume specialised and competition loading \"designed primarily to improve technique or speed so as to stimulate supercompensation.\" \"The volume increases and decreases more steeply than in the traditional scheme, while there is a slight rise in the intensity of loading... one can achieve the necessary volume of work only with a relatively low intensity of loading\" (PDF p. 385). In Fig 6.33(c) volume rises steeply and peaks early, the fitness state stays low then climbs above it, and the shaded GPP area grows across the whole cycle.\n\n**Who uses which.** \"Variant A is appropriate for athletes of medium qualification, as well as for qualified athletes needing to devote much time to perfecting technical skill. Variant B can be used best by qualified athletes who possess a high special physical work-capacity, can endure voluminous loading and display excellent sports technique\" (PDF p. 386).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation for Variant B: the concentrated special loading \"cause[s] extensive adaptation,\" and the lower-volume specialised stage that follows stimulates \"supercompensation of energy resources and increase[s] special work-capacity. The stabilisation of the body at a new functional level signifies an increase in adaptive reserves\" (PDF p. 385). Intensity is held low because \"the concentrated volume of intense loading can seriously overstrain the body and disrupt adaptation.\" They also claim Variant B avoids the falling results that come from \"exhausting the motor potential\": \"variant B leads to an increase in the athlete's special work capacity and achievements during the competition phase\" (PDF p. 386). Fig 6.33 is a schematic (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999) with no data.\n\n**When to apply** — Choosing the shape of a several-month training phase. Variant B for qualified athletes with high work capacity and sound technique who can tolerate a planned dip; Variant A for medium-level athletes and those who still need a lot of technical work. Eric's baseline: \"Undulating/non-linear periodization (Parker/Miller/Panariello), waving volume week to week (e.g., medium-high, medium, high, low over four weeks) is stated as the personally preferred model over strict linear progression\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: Eric's four-week waves are the \"three and four week stages\" the authors call common practice; the authors' A and B describe the longer phase those waves sit inside, so the two do not conflict.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not for athletes who cannot yet tolerate voluminous loading or whose technique is still unstable (the authors' own limit, PDF p. 386). \"Mesocycle\" here means a phase of 3-5 months, not the 3-6 week mesocycle of most Western texts, so the rules do not transfer to a four-week block without translation. The authors give no study for the claim that B exceeds A; the comparison rests on their adaptation theory. Both variants sit in a chapter whose models all use concentrated loading, which Issurin 2016 questions for team sports (see [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors give Fig 6.33 only. Inference: a qualified jumper's 16-week autumn phase under Variant B spends the first six or seven weeks on high-volume, moderate-intensity strength and jump work with performance tests dropping, then shifts to lower-volume technique and speed work, with test results expected to climb above their start by the end.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models]], [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":690,"cache_read_input_tokens":289700,"output_tokens":2515,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":92},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":690},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2515,"cache_read_input_tokens":289700,"cache_creation_input_tokens":690,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":690,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01LxrHYGKxSGphqdSsowUdhx":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.3\"\ntopic: mesocycle organisation variants\ncategory: [mesocycle, periodization, concentrated-loading, volume-intensity, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Two Ways to Build a Large Mesocycle: Variant A Rises Smoothly to a Plateau; Variant B Concentrates Load First, Dips, Then Rebounds Higher\n\n**What it is** — In 6.12.3 (PDF pp. 385-387 / printed pp. 374-376, all checked against the page images) the authors compare two forms of mesocycle organisation (Fig 6.33, PDF p. 386).\n\n**A terminology warning first.** \"In this chapter, *the term 'mesocycle' is reserved for what in Russian is called 'the large stage' or large mesocycle*, which extends over several months to produce a specific adaptive effect... The large mesocycle, in fact, may comprise a few conventional mesocycles and also be referred to as a phase\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 385). Its duration \"can vary (usually 3-5 months), since its calculation depends on the competition calendar and especially on the dates of the most important competitions\" (PDF p. 387).\n\n**Common practice, as the authors report it.** \"The literature reveals that three and four week stages are the most frequently used, where the last one week serves as a restoration week. There are combinations of two weeks with heavy loading and two weeks with moderate loading, or an alternation of weeks with high and moderate loading... all of them, depending on circumstances, can produce good results. It is very difficult to establish which of these is the most effective, since the criteria are extremely diverse\" (PDF p. 385). Research has concentrated on the annual cycle and the microcycle, plus the \"pre-competition training stage lasting 4-6 weeks\" (PDF p. 385).\n\n**The two variants.**\n- **Variant A** \"displays a smooth increase in the functional indicators until it reaches a distinct plateau\" and \"is a traditional form of training organisation\" (PDF p. 385). In Fig 6.33(b) its volume curve rises to a broad peak mid-cycle and falls, GPP peaks early and declines, and the fitness state rises steadily.\n- **Variant B** \"begins with a steady decline which is followed by a sharp increase in the functional indicators up to a level which significantly exceeds the maximum of the first variant\" (PDF p. 385). It has two micro-stages: a first one of concentrated special physical preparation that \"produces a steady decrease in the functional indicators,\" then lower-volume specialised and competition loading \"designed primarily to improve technique or speed so as to stimulate supercompensation.\" \"The volume increases and decreases more steeply than in the traditional scheme, while there is a slight rise in the intensity of loading... one can achieve the necessary volume of work only with a relatively low intensity of loading\" (PDF p. 385). In Fig 6.33(c) volume rises steeply and peaks early, the fitness state stays low then climbs above it, and the shaded GPP area grows across the whole cycle.\n\n**Who uses which.** \"Variant A is appropriate for athletes of medium qualification, as well as for qualified athletes needing to devote much time to perfecting technical skill. Variant B can be used best by qualified athletes who possess a high special physical work-capacity, can endure voluminous loading and display excellent sports technique\" (PDF p. 386).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation for Variant B: the concentrated special loading \"cause[s] extensive adaptation,\" and the lower-volume specialised stage that follows stimulates \"supercompensation of energy resources and increase[s] special work-capacity. The stabilisation of the body at a new functional level signifies an increase in adaptive reserves\" (PDF p. 385). Intensity is held low because \"the concentrated volume of intense loading can seriously overstrain the body and disrupt adaptation.\" They also claim Variant B avoids the falling results that come from \"exhausting the motor potential\": \"variant B leads to an increase in the athlete's special work capacity and achievements during the competition phase\" (PDF p. 386). Fig 6.33 is a schematic (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999) with no data.\n\n**When to apply** — Choosing the shape of a several-month training phase. Variant B for qualified athletes with high work capacity and sound technique who can tolerate a planned dip; Variant A for medium-level athletes and those who still need a lot of technical work. Eric's baseline: \"Undulating/non-linear periodization (Parker/Miller/Panariello), waving volume week to week (e.g., medium-high, medium, high, low over four weeks) is stated as the personally preferred model over strict linear progression\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: Eric's four-week waves are the \"three and four week stages\" the authors call common practice; the authors' A and B describe the longer phase those waves sit inside, so the two do not conflict.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not for athletes who cannot yet tolerate voluminous loading or whose technique is still unstable (the authors' own limit, PDF p. 386). \"Mesocycle\" here means a phase of 3-5 months, not the 3-6 week mesocycle of most Western texts, so the rules do not transfer to a four-week block without translation. The authors give no study for the claim that B exceeds A; the comparison rests on their adaptation theory. Both variants sit in a chapter whose models all use concentrated loading, which Issurin 2016 questions for team sports (see [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors give Fig 6.33 only. Inference: a qualified jumper's 16-week autumn phase under Variant B spends the first six or seven weeks on high-volume, moderate-intensity strength and jump work with performance tests dropping, then shifts to lower-volume technique and speed work, with test results expected to climb above their start by the end.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models]], [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffmTgc75PyHoCECX5kbG","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"0f359cb3-db7f-4cd6-bab0-b02f9f897da2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:21:28.106Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"0f359cb3-db7f-4cd6-bab0-b02f9f897da2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LxrHYGKxSGphqdSsowUdhx","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"eb87fcae-f497-4e2d-9861-daaf3aff0e27","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:21:28.227Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.3\"\ntopic: mesocycle organisation variants\ncategory: [mesocycle, periodization, concentrated-loading, volume-intensity, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Two Ways to Build a Large Mesocycle: Variant A Rises Smoothly to a Plateau; Variant B Concentrates Load First, Dips, Then Rebounds Higher\n\n**What it is** — In 6.12.3 (PDF pp. 385-387 / printed pp. 374-376, all checked against the page images) the authors compare two forms of mesocycle organisation (Fig 6.33, PDF p. 386).\n\n**A terminology warning first.** \"In this chapter, *the term 'mesocycle' is reserved for what in Russian is called 'the large stage' or large mesocycle*, which extends over several months to produce a specific adaptive effect... The large mesocycle, in fact, may comprise a few conventional mesocycles and also be referred to as a phase\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 385). Its duration \"can vary (usually 3-5 months), since its calculation depends on the competition calendar and especially on the dates of the most important competitions\" (PDF p. 387).\n\n**Common practice, as the authors report it.** \"The literature reveals that three and four week stages are the most frequently used, where the last one week serves as a restoration week. There are combinations of two weeks with heavy loading and two weeks with moderate loading, or an alternation of weeks with high and moderate loading... all of them, depending on circumstances, can produce good results. It is very difficult to establish which of these is the most effective, since the criteria are extremely diverse\" (PDF p. 385). Research has concentrated on the annual cycle and the microcycle, plus the \"pre-competition training stage lasting 4-6 weeks\" (PDF p. 385).\n\n**The two variants.**\n- **Variant A** \"displays a smooth increase in the functional indicators until it reaches a distinct plateau\" and \"is a traditional form of training organisation\" (PDF p. 385). In Fig 6.33(b) its volume curve rises to a broad peak mid-cycle and falls, GPP peaks early and declines, and the fitness state rises steadily.\n- **Variant B** \"begins with a steady decline which is followed by a sharp increase in the functional indicators up to a level which significantly exceeds the maximum of the first variant\" (PDF p. 385). It has two micro-stages: a first one of concentrated special physical preparation that \"produces a steady decrease in the functional indicators,\" then lower-volume specialised and competition loading \"designed primarily to improve technique or speed so as to stimulate supercompensation.\" \"The volume increases and decreases more steeply than in the traditional scheme, while there is a slight rise in the intensity of loading... one can achieve the necessary volume of work only with a relatively low intensity of loading\" (PDF p. 385). In Fig 6.33(c) volume rises steeply and peaks early, the fitness state stays low then climbs above it, and the shaded GPP area grows across the whole cycle.\n\n**Who uses which.** \"Variant A is appropriate for athletes of medium qualification, as well as for qualified athletes needing to devote much time to perfecting technical skill. Variant B can be used best by qualified athletes who possess a high special physical work-capacity, can endure voluminous loading and display excellent sports technique\" (PDF p. 386).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation for Variant B: the concentrated special loading \"cause[s] extensive adaptation,\" and the lower-volume specialised stage that follows stimulates \"supercompensation of energy resources and increase[s] special work-capacity. The stabilisation of the body at a new functional level signifies an increase in adaptive reserves\" (PDF p. 385). Intensity is held low because \"the concentrated volume of intense loading can seriously overstrain the body and disrupt adaptation.\" They also claim Variant B avoids the falling results that come from \"exhausting the motor potential\": \"variant B leads to an increase in the athlete's special work capacity and achievements during the competition phase\" (PDF p. 386). Fig 6.33 is a schematic (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999) with no data.\n\n**When to apply** — Choosing the shape of a several-month training phase. Variant B for qualified athletes with high work capacity and sound technique who can tolerate a planned dip; Variant A for medium-level athletes and those who still need a lot of technical work. Eric's baseline: \"Undulating/non-linear periodization (Parker/Miller/Panariello), waving volume week to week (e.g., medium-high, medium, high, low over four weeks) is stated as the personally preferred model over strict linear progression\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: Eric's four-week waves are the \"three and four week stages\" the authors call common practice; the authors' A and B describe the longer phase those waves sit inside, so the two do not conflict.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not for athletes who cannot yet tolerate voluminous loading or whose technique is still unstable (the authors' own limit, PDF p. 386). \"Mesocycle\" here means a phase of 3-5 months, not the 3-6 week mesocycle of most Western texts, so the rules do not transfer to a four-week block without translation. The authors give no study for the claim that B exceeds A; the comparison rests on their adaptation theory. Both variants sit in a chapter whose models all use concentrated loading, which Issurin 2016 questions for team sports (see [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors give Fig 6.33 only. Inference: a qualified jumper's 16-week autumn phase under Variant B spends the first six or seven weeks on high-volume, moderate-intensity strength and jump work with performance tests dropping, then shifts to lower-volume technique and speed work, with test results expected to climb above their start by the end.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models]], [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0f359cb3-db7f-4cd6-bab0-b02f9f897da2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"eb87fcae-f497-4e2d-9861-daaf3aff0e27","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14707093 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"747c35ed-7a41-4eb7-9cbd-0f3d6750595f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:21:28.243Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14707093 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"747c35ed-7a41-4eb7-9cbd-0f3d6750595f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmWXQoGsmaPQ2gFtNAh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2651,"cache_read_input_tokens":290390,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2651,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":990,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffmVeEn811GbEouw8ANZ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"1250ccf0-01ef-4f75-9eda-f7a9cf73ced6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:21:42.884Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1250ccf0-01ef-4f75-9eda-f7a9cf73ced6","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmWXQoGsmaPQ2gFtNAh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0149qA84w5LytW3WjkpchHcu","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.3\"\ntopic: bi-cyclic annual organisation\ncategory: [periodization, annual-plan, bi-cyclic, monocyclic, mesocycle]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Two Large Mesocycles a Year, the Second Peaking Higher: the Authors Question Monocyclic Periodisation but Keep It for Long-Duration Endurance\n\n**What it is** — \"Research shows that it is appropriate to use two large mesocycles in the annual cycle to promote supercompensation of the current adaptation reserves (Figure 6.34). In this case, the athlete's state displays two large peaks, with the second one producing the higher special work-capacity (i.e. fitness state). This further corroborates the value of this form of bi-cyclic organisation of annual preparation which was empirically worked out and applied successfully long ago in several sports\" (PDF p. 387 / printed p. 376, checked against the page image). \"Consequently, unquestioning adherence to monocyclic periodisation in a number of sports is not justified, and it is advisable that coaches re-examine this tradition carefully.\" Bi-cyclic periodisation may also suit sports that traditionally use monocyclic forms (skiing, Ogoltsov, 1979; middle-distance running, Turin, 1980; Sirenko, 1980) or tri-cyclic forms (boxing, Nikiforov & Viktorov, 1978; swimming, Vaitsekhovsky, 1981). \"In a number of team sports and one-on-one events, tri-cyclic periodisation may have to be used, as dictated by the competition calendar, whereas in long-duration endurance sports, monocyclic periodisation is usually recommended\" (PDF p. 387).\n\nThe first primary aim of programming (6.13) repeats it: \"It is appropriate to fit at least two large mesocycles into the annual cycle, even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period and use monocyclic periodisation\" (PDF p. 389 / printed p. 378, checked against the page image). The practical principles add: \"the optimal period for enhancing the current adaptation reserves is about 20 weeks for the most efficient loading of highly qualified athletes,\" and \"the first mesocycle should always be considered as the foundation for the second\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389, checked against the page image).\n\nFig 6.34 (PDF p. 387, read from the image, no numeric scale): two shaded strength-loading waves, V1 and V2. The fitness-state curve dips during V1, rises to a peak between the waves, dips again during V2, and then climbs to a level above the first peak at the right edge.\n\n**Contested inside the book.** Aim 1 says \"at least two large mesocycles... even in sports which traditionally... use monocyclic periodisation\" (PDF p. 389), but the same chapter says \"in long-duration endurance sports, monocyclic periodisation is usually recommended\" (PDF p. 387) and gives a monocyclic model for them with \"one competitive stage\" (PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385). The authors partly reconcile this: the monocyclic endurance model still contains \"a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training\" with two strength blocks (PDF p. 396). See [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]].\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is supercompensation of the \"current adaptation reserves\" and the claim that the second mesocycle builds on the first. They cite no data for the second peak being higher beyond \"research shows\" and long empirical use.\n\n**When to apply** — Sports with two main competition periods, or one long one where an intermediate peak is useful. Eric's baseline: \"In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: if the winter-spring and summer off-season phases are treated as the two large mesocycles, the authors' rule that the second builds on the first and peaks higher fits a fall season with a November priority window; the authors do not discuss football.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Also contested by Issurin** on the length and number of training units. Issurin's training stage \"should be about two months,\" and the number of stages \"usually varies from four to seven\" a season ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]], Block Periodization PDF pp. 38-40), against the authors' two large mesocycles of usually 3-5 months (PDF p. 387) and \"about 20 weeks\" to enhance the adaptation reserves (PDF p. 400). Bompa's cycle types as taught by Evans reserve the bicycle for \"ADVANCED OR ELITE ATHLETES\" and the monocycle for novice and junior athletes ([[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]]); the authors' models are likewise for qualified athletes. Long-duration endurance sports stay monocyclic in the authors' own scheme.\n\n**Example** — The authors' illustration is Fig 6.34 and the bi-cyclic models of 6.14.1 and 6.14.2 ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Inference: a middle-distance runner with an indoor season in winter and the main outdoor championship in late summer runs two large mesocycles of about four to five months each, the first ending at the indoor meets.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports]], [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2651,"cache_read_input_tokens":290390,"output_tokens":2461,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":146},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2651},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2461,"cache_read_input_tokens":290390,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2651,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2651,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0149qA84w5LytW3WjkpchHcu":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.3\"\ntopic: bi-cyclic annual organisation\ncategory: [periodization, annual-plan, bi-cyclic, monocyclic, mesocycle]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Two Large Mesocycles a Year, the Second Peaking Higher: the Authors Question Monocyclic Periodisation but Keep It for Long-Duration Endurance\n\n**What it is** — \"Research shows that it is appropriate to use two large mesocycles in the annual cycle to promote supercompensation of the current adaptation reserves (Figure 6.34). In this case, the athlete's state displays two large peaks, with the second one producing the higher special work-capacity (i.e. fitness state). This further corroborates the value of this form of bi-cyclic organisation of annual preparation which was empirically worked out and applied successfully long ago in several sports\" (PDF p. 387 / printed p. 376, checked against the page image). \"Consequently, unquestioning adherence to monocyclic periodisation in a number of sports is not justified, and it is advisable that coaches re-examine this tradition carefully.\" Bi-cyclic periodisation may also suit sports that traditionally use monocyclic forms (skiing, Ogoltsov, 1979; middle-distance running, Turin, 1980; Sirenko, 1980) or tri-cyclic forms (boxing, Nikiforov & Viktorov, 1978; swimming, Vaitsekhovsky, 1981). \"In a number of team sports and one-on-one events, tri-cyclic periodisation may have to be used, as dictated by the competition calendar, whereas in long-duration endurance sports, monocyclic periodisation is usually recommended\" (PDF p. 387).\n\nThe first primary aim of programming (6.13) repeats it: \"It is appropriate to fit at least two large mesocycles into the annual cycle, even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period and use monocyclic periodisation\" (PDF p. 389 / printed p. 378, checked against the page image). The practical principles add: \"the optimal period for enhancing the current adaptation reserves is about 20 weeks for the most efficient loading of highly qualified athletes,\" and \"the first mesocycle should always be considered as the foundation for the second\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389, checked against the page image).\n\nFig 6.34 (PDF p. 387, read from the image, no numeric scale): two shaded strength-loading waves, V1 and V2. The fitness-state curve dips during V1, rises to a peak between the waves, dips again during V2, and then climbs to a level above the first peak at the right edge.\n\n**Contested inside the book.** Aim 1 says \"at least two large mesocycles... even in sports which traditionally... use monocyclic periodisation\" (PDF p. 389), but the same chapter says \"in long-duration endurance sports, monocyclic periodisation is usually recommended\" (PDF p. 387) and gives a monocyclic model for them with \"one competitive stage\" (PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385). The authors partly reconcile this: the monocyclic endurance model still contains \"a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training\" with two strength blocks (PDF p. 396). See [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]].\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is supercompensation of the \"current adaptation reserves\" and the claim that the second mesocycle builds on the first. They cite no data for the second peak being higher beyond \"research shows\" and long empirical use.\n\n**When to apply** — Sports with two main competition periods, or one long one where an intermediate peak is useful. Eric's baseline: \"In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: if the winter-spring and summer off-season phases are treated as the two large mesocycles, the authors' rule that the second builds on the first and peaks higher fits a fall season with a November priority window; the authors do not discuss football.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Also contested by Issurin** on the length and number of training units. Issurin's training stage \"should be about two months,\" and the number of stages \"usually varies from four to seven\" a season ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]], Block Periodization PDF pp. 38-40), against the authors' two large mesocycles of usually 3-5 months (PDF p. 387) and \"about 20 weeks\" to enhance the adaptation reserves (PDF p. 400). Bompa's cycle types as taught by Evans reserve the bicycle for \"ADVANCED OR ELITE ATHLETES\" and the monocycle for novice and junior athletes ([[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]]); the authors' models are likewise for qualified athletes. Long-duration endurance sports stay monocyclic in the authors' own scheme.\n\n**Example** — The authors' illustration is Fig 6.34 and the bi-cyclic models of 6.14.1 and 6.14.2 ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Inference: a middle-distance runner with an indoor season in winter and the main outdoor championship in late summer runs two large mesocycles of about four to five months each, the first ending at the indoor meets.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports]], [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffmVeEn811GbEouw8ANZ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"0d388909-d4a3-4ef9-9ec9-1bb1c1ebefd7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:22:00.773Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"0d388909-d4a3-4ef9-9ec9-1bb1c1ebefd7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0149qA84w5LytW3WjkpchHcu","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"c7ce07ef-b36c-4699-a8df-3d8cff982459","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:22:00.920Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.3\"\ntopic: bi-cyclic annual organisation\ncategory: [periodization, annual-plan, bi-cyclic, monocyclic, mesocycle]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Two Large Mesocycles a Year, the Second Peaking Higher: the Authors Question Monocyclic Periodisation but Keep It for Long-Duration Endurance\n\n**What it is** — \"Research shows that it is appropriate to use two large mesocycles in the annual cycle to promote supercompensation of the current adaptation reserves (Figure 6.34). In this case, the athlete's state displays two large peaks, with the second one producing the higher special work-capacity (i.e. fitness state). This further corroborates the value of this form of bi-cyclic organisation of annual preparation which was empirically worked out and applied successfully long ago in several sports\" (PDF p. 387 / printed p. 376, checked against the page image). \"Consequently, unquestioning adherence to monocyclic periodisation in a number of sports is not justified, and it is advisable that coaches re-examine this tradition carefully.\" Bi-cyclic periodisation may also suit sports that traditionally use monocyclic forms (skiing, Ogoltsov, 1979; middle-distance running, Turin, 1980; Sirenko, 1980) or tri-cyclic forms (boxing, Nikiforov & Viktorov, 1978; swimming, Vaitsekhovsky, 1981). \"In a number of team sports and one-on-one events, tri-cyclic periodisation may have to be used, as dictated by the competition calendar, whereas in long-duration endurance sports, monocyclic periodisation is usually recommended\" (PDF p. 387).\n\nThe first primary aim of programming (6.13) repeats it: \"It is appropriate to fit at least two large mesocycles into the annual cycle, even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period and use monocyclic periodisation\" (PDF p. 389 / printed p. 378, checked against the page image). The practical principles add: \"the optimal period for enhancing the current adaptation reserves is about 20 weeks for the most efficient loading of highly qualified athletes,\" and \"the first mesocycle should always be considered as the foundation for the second\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389, checked against the page image).\n\nFig 6.34 (PDF p. 387, read from the image, no numeric scale): two shaded strength-loading waves, V1 and V2. The fitness-state curve dips during V1, rises to a peak between the waves, dips again during V2, and then climbs to a level above the first peak at the right edge.\n\n**Contested inside the book.** Aim 1 says \"at least two large mesocycles... even in sports which traditionally... use monocyclic periodisation\" (PDF p. 389), but the same chapter says \"in long-duration endurance sports, monocyclic periodisation is usually recommended\" (PDF p. 387) and gives a monocyclic model for them with \"one competitive stage\" (PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385). The authors partly reconcile this: the monocyclic endurance model still contains \"a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training\" with two strength blocks (PDF p. 396). See [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]].\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is supercompensation of the \"current adaptation reserves\" and the claim that the second mesocycle builds on the first. They cite no data for the second peak being higher beyond \"research shows\" and long empirical use.\n\n**When to apply** — Sports with two main competition periods, or one long one where an intermediate peak is useful. Eric's baseline: \"In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: if the winter-spring and summer off-season phases are treated as the two large mesocycles, the authors' rule that the second builds on the first and peaks higher fits a fall season with a November priority window; the authors do not discuss football.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Also contested by Issurin** on the length and number of training units. Issurin's training stage \"should be about two months,\" and the number of stages \"usually varies from four to seven\" a season ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]], Block Periodization PDF pp. 38-40), against the authors' two large mesocycles of usually 3-5 months (PDF p. 387) and \"about 20 weeks\" to enhance the adaptation reserves (PDF p. 400). Bompa's cycle types as taught by Evans reserve the bicycle for \"ADVANCED OR ELITE ATHLETES\" and the monocycle for novice and junior athletes ([[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]]); the authors' models are likewise for qualified athletes. Long-duration endurance sports stay monocyclic in the authors' own scheme.\n\n**Example** — The authors' illustration is Fig 6.34 and the bi-cyclic models of 6.14.1 and 6.14.2 ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Inference: a middle-distance runner with an indoor season in winter and the main outdoor championship in late summer runs two large mesocycles of about four to five months each, the first ending at the indoor meets.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports]], [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0d388909-d4a3-4ef9-9ec9-1bb1c1ebefd7","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"c7ce07ef-b36c-4699-a8df-3d8cff982459","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14704496 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"31357a0d-fa9f-4d65-b829-a319646c5157","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:22:00.933Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14704496 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"31357a0d-fa9f-4d65-b829-a319646c5157","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmYWSpJPd4jZ1d8bDFN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2590,"cache_read_input_tokens":293041,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2590,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":430,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffmY4B1aMnJCznztMPKz","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"25c69a6c-5097-42a6-b7de-f13fc07f8e3d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:22:08.968Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"25c69a6c-5097-42a6-b7de-f13fc07f8e3d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmYWSpJPd4jZ1d8bDFN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QKNuk2zZCT1UqUH5LR7vCx","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.3\"\ntopic: unidirectional weekly microcycles\ncategory: [microcycle, swimming, speed-strength, restoration, unidirectional-loading]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Weekly Microcycles for Swimmers and Speed-Strength Athletes: a Mixed Week of Single-Emphasis Workouts Gave Volume but No Clear Supercompensation; Single-Emphasis Weeks Showed 72-Hour Restoration Patterns\n\n**What it is** — The authors present three weekly microcycles (PDF pp. 387-389 / printed pp. 376-378; all pages and figures checked against the page images). They call the microcycle \"the basic units of training construction\" and say the traditional complex microcycle cannot carry the volume a large mesocycle needs (PDF p. 387).\n\n1. **A mixed week for highly qualified swimmers (Fig 6.35, PDF p. 388).** The week was built from the restoration time each workout type needs. The bars read: day 1 aerobic, day 2 speed, day 3 complex (lower bar), day 4 anaerobic, day 5 aerobic, day 6 complex (lower bar), day 7 empty. The \"trend of restoration\" curve, which \"combines the separate effects on restoration for the aerobic, anaerobic and speed components,\" wavers around a flat line with its deepest dip around days 2-3. The authors' verdict: \"despite the uni-directional character of individual workouts, the microcycle as the whole typifies the complex (multi-faceted) form of training organisation. The body cannot simultaneously differentiate between and accumulate specific reactions to every type of daily loading... this form of microcycle construction enables one to achieve a large volume of loading, but the absence of any marked supercompensation (as revealed by the trend of restoration curve) shows that it fails to produce a definite increase in speed, aerobic or anaerobic productivity\" (PDF pp. 387-388).\n2. **Single-emphasis swimming weeks (Fig 6.36, PDF p. 388; caption credits \"Kubelin, 1980,\" the text and PDF p. 381 spell it \"Kudelin\").** Each week has three aerobic (or three anaerobic) workouts on days 1, 3 and 6, with speed-strength, complex and speed-endurance workouts on days 2, 4 and 5, and day 7 empty. Text: after the aerobic week \"there was a significant depression of aerobic potential (VO2 max), the restoration of which was observed over a 72 hour period. At the same time, anaerobic potential, assessed by a swimming test of 4 x 50m, restored quickly and exceeded its initial level. In the second case, restoration of anaerobic productivity took longer and exceeded its initial level after 72 hours. It is interesting that, during this time, aerobic potential rose over a 12 hour span and then diminished.\"\n   Figure values (read by eye, \"about\"): aerobic week, VO2 max starts about 87% of initial, crosses 100% at about 36 h and reaches about 106-107% by 72 h; the curve labelled \"Training effect\" starts about 93%, reaches 100% by about 12-24 h and sits about 101-102% at 72 h. Anaerobic week, VO2 max jumps to about 112-113% by about 12 h and falls to about 106% by 72 h; \"Training effect\" starts about 96% and crosses 100% at about 30-36 h, reaching about 102-103% at 72 h.\n3. **A speed-strength week (Fig 6.37, PDF p. 389, Mironenko 1979).** Bars: day 1 technique (medium), day 2 speed-strength (medium), day 3 speed-strength (high), day 4 complex (low), day 5 speed-strength (medium), day 6 speed-strength (high), day 7 empty. The text says these microcycles combine \"workouts with large and moderate volumes of strength loading\" with \"workouts with an emphasis on technical and complex training\" (PDF pp. 388-389).\n\nThe authors close: \"the microcycles used years ago have become the weakest link in the programming of training. Naturally, the preceding examples still do not eliminate those insufficiencies, although they point the way in which scientific research should be directed\" (PDF p. 389).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason for the mixed week's failure is that the body \"cannot simultaneously differentiate between and accumulate specific reactions to every type of daily loading\" (PDF p. 387). For the single-emphasis weeks they report the restoration curves without explaining them.\n\n**When to apply** — Designing a loading week where one quality should be pushed: concentrate workouts of one emphasis in the week and expect roughly three days for the stressed quality to come back above baseline in the swimmers' data. Eric's baseline: \"The in-season game week: Sunday off; Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday; lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)). Inference: the swimmers' VO2 max took about 72 h to recover after an aerobic-emphasis week, the same window Eric leaves before kickoff; this is a coincidence of numbers from a different sport and quality, not support for the football rule.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Unclear spots.** The text says anaerobic productivity \"exceeded its initial level after 72 hours\" in the anaerobic week, but the figure's \"Training effect\" curve crosses 100% at about 30-36 h; the figure also labels that curve \"Training effect,\" not anaerobic potential, so which curve is the 4 x 50 m test is inferred from the text. The text says aerobic potential \"rose over a 12 hour span and then diminished,\" but the figure shows it still about 6% above initial at 72 h. No sample sizes, athlete numbers or units for the \"training load\" bars are given. These are elite swimmers and a Soviet-era report; the speed-strength week has no outcome data at all.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is the Kudelin swimmer data above (PDF p. 388).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]], [[periodization--bompa/heuristic--fatigue-fitness-3to1-ratio]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--microcycle-types-and-load-wave-design]], [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--popov-aerobic-strength-microcycle]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2590,"cache_read_input_tokens":293041,"output_tokens":2465,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":26},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2590},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2465,"cache_read_input_tokens":293041,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2590,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2590,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01QKNuk2zZCT1UqUH5LR7vCx":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.3\"\ntopic: unidirectional weekly microcycles\ncategory: [microcycle, swimming, speed-strength, restoration, unidirectional-loading]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Weekly Microcycles for Swimmers and Speed-Strength Athletes: a Mixed Week of Single-Emphasis Workouts Gave Volume but No Clear Supercompensation; Single-Emphasis Weeks Showed 72-Hour Restoration Patterns\n\n**What it is** — The authors present three weekly microcycles (PDF pp. 387-389 / printed pp. 376-378; all pages and figures checked against the page images). They call the microcycle \"the basic units of training construction\" and say the traditional complex microcycle cannot carry the volume a large mesocycle needs (PDF p. 387).\n\n1. **A mixed week for highly qualified swimmers (Fig 6.35, PDF p. 388).** The week was built from the restoration time each workout type needs. The bars read: day 1 aerobic, day 2 speed, day 3 complex (lower bar), day 4 anaerobic, day 5 aerobic, day 6 complex (lower bar), day 7 empty. The \"trend of restoration\" curve, which \"combines the separate effects on restoration for the aerobic, anaerobic and speed components,\" wavers around a flat line with its deepest dip around days 2-3. The authors' verdict: \"despite the uni-directional character of individual workouts, the microcycle as the whole typifies the complex (multi-faceted) form of training organisation. The body cannot simultaneously differentiate between and accumulate specific reactions to every type of daily loading... this form of microcycle construction enables one to achieve a large volume of loading, but the absence of any marked supercompensation (as revealed by the trend of restoration curve) shows that it fails to produce a definite increase in speed, aerobic or anaerobic productivity\" (PDF pp. 387-388).\n2. **Single-emphasis swimming weeks (Fig 6.36, PDF p. 388; caption credits \"Kubelin, 1980,\" the text and PDF p. 381 spell it \"Kudelin\").** Each week has three aerobic (or three anaerobic) workouts on days 1, 3 and 6, with speed-strength, complex and speed-endurance workouts on days 2, 4 and 5, and day 7 empty. Text: after the aerobic week \"there was a significant depression of aerobic potential (VO2 max), the restoration of which was observed over a 72 hour period. At the same time, anaerobic potential, assessed by a swimming test of 4 x 50m, restored quickly and exceeded its initial level. In the second case, restoration of anaerobic productivity took longer and exceeded its initial level after 72 hours. It is interesting that, during this time, aerobic potential rose over a 12 hour span and then diminished.\"\n   Figure values (read by eye, \"about\"): aerobic week, VO2 max starts about 87% of initial, crosses 100% at about 36 h and reaches about 106-107% by 72 h; the curve labelled \"Training effect\" starts about 93%, reaches 100% by about 12-24 h and sits about 101-102% at 72 h. Anaerobic week, VO2 max jumps to about 112-113% by about 12 h and falls to about 106% by 72 h; \"Training effect\" starts about 96% and crosses 100% at about 30-36 h, reaching about 102-103% at 72 h.\n3. **A speed-strength week (Fig 6.37, PDF p. 389, Mironenko 1979).** Bars: day 1 technique (medium), day 2 speed-strength (medium), day 3 speed-strength (high), day 4 complex (low), day 5 speed-strength (medium), day 6 speed-strength (high), day 7 empty. The text says these microcycles combine \"workouts with large and moderate volumes of strength loading\" with \"workouts with an emphasis on technical and complex training\" (PDF pp. 388-389).\n\nThe authors close: \"the microcycles used years ago have become the weakest link in the programming of training. Naturally, the preceding examples still do not eliminate those insufficiencies, although they point the way in which scientific research should be directed\" (PDF p. 389).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason for the mixed week's failure is that the body \"cannot simultaneously differentiate between and accumulate specific reactions to every type of daily loading\" (PDF p. 387). For the single-emphasis weeks they report the restoration curves without explaining them.\n\n**When to apply** — Designing a loading week where one quality should be pushed: concentrate workouts of one emphasis in the week and expect roughly three days for the stressed quality to come back above baseline in the swimmers' data. Eric's baseline: \"The in-season game week: Sunday off; Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday; lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)). Inference: the swimmers' VO2 max took about 72 h to recover after an aerobic-emphasis week, the same window Eric leaves before kickoff; this is a coincidence of numbers from a different sport and quality, not support for the football rule.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Unclear spots.** The text says anaerobic productivity \"exceeded its initial level after 72 hours\" in the anaerobic week, but the figure's \"Training effect\" curve crosses 100% at about 30-36 h; the figure also labels that curve \"Training effect,\" not anaerobic potential, so which curve is the 4 x 50 m test is inferred from the text. The text says aerobic potential \"rose over a 12 hour span and then diminished,\" but the figure shows it still about 6% above initial at 72 h. No sample sizes, athlete numbers or units for the \"training load\" bars are given. These are elite swimmers and a Soviet-era report; the speed-strength week has no outcome data at all.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is the Kudelin swimmer data above (PDF p. 388).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]], [[periodization--bompa/heuristic--fatigue-fitness-3to1-ratio]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--microcycle-types-and-load-wave-design]], [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--popov-aerobic-strength-microcycle]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffmY4B1aMnJCznztMPKz","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5bd2e4c0-ff22-438c-a5f4-c1e883370662","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:22:28.153Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"5bd2e4c0-ff22-438c-a5f4-c1e883370662","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QKNuk2zZCT1UqUH5LR7vCx","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"70b8c867-b61e-4d4d-9918-b90360cc1442","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:22:28.298Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.3\"\ntopic: unidirectional weekly microcycles\ncategory: [microcycle, swimming, speed-strength, restoration, unidirectional-loading]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Weekly Microcycles for Swimmers and Speed-Strength Athletes: a Mixed Week of Single-Emphasis Workouts Gave Volume but No Clear Supercompensation; Single-Emphasis Weeks Showed 72-Hour Restoration Patterns\n\n**What it is** — The authors present three weekly microcycles (PDF pp. 387-389 / printed pp. 376-378; all pages and figures checked against the page images). They call the microcycle \"the basic units of training construction\" and say the traditional complex microcycle cannot carry the volume a large mesocycle needs (PDF p. 387).\n\n1. **A mixed week for highly qualified swimmers (Fig 6.35, PDF p. 388).** The week was built from the restoration time each workout type needs. The bars read: day 1 aerobic, day 2 speed, day 3 complex (lower bar), day 4 anaerobic, day 5 aerobic, day 6 complex (lower bar), day 7 empty. The \"trend of restoration\" curve, which \"combines the separate effects on restoration for the aerobic, anaerobic and speed components,\" wavers around a flat line with its deepest dip around days 2-3. The authors' verdict: \"despite the uni-directional character of individual workouts, the microcycle as the whole typifies the complex (multi-faceted) form of training organisation. The body cannot simultaneously differentiate between and accumulate specific reactions to every type of daily loading... this form of microcycle construction enables one to achieve a large volume of loading, but the absence of any marked supercompensation (as revealed by the trend of restoration curve) shows that it fails to produce a definite increase in speed, aerobic or anaerobic productivity\" (PDF pp. 387-388).\n2. **Single-emphasis swimming weeks (Fig 6.36, PDF p. 388; caption credits \"Kubelin, 1980,\" the text and PDF p. 381 spell it \"Kudelin\").** Each week has three aerobic (or three anaerobic) workouts on days 1, 3 and 6, with speed-strength, complex and speed-endurance workouts on days 2, 4 and 5, and day 7 empty. Text: after the aerobic week \"there was a significant depression of aerobic potential (VO2 max), the restoration of which was observed over a 72 hour period. At the same time, anaerobic potential, assessed by a swimming test of 4 x 50m, restored quickly and exceeded its initial level. In the second case, restoration of anaerobic productivity took longer and exceeded its initial level after 72 hours. It is interesting that, during this time, aerobic potential rose over a 12 hour span and then diminished.\"\n   Figure values (read by eye, \"about\"): aerobic week, VO2 max starts about 87% of initial, crosses 100% at about 36 h and reaches about 106-107% by 72 h; the curve labelled \"Training effect\" starts about 93%, reaches 100% by about 12-24 h and sits about 101-102% at 72 h. Anaerobic week, VO2 max jumps to about 112-113% by about 12 h and falls to about 106% by 72 h; \"Training effect\" starts about 96% and crosses 100% at about 30-36 h, reaching about 102-103% at 72 h.\n3. **A speed-strength week (Fig 6.37, PDF p. 389, Mironenko 1979).** Bars: day 1 technique (medium), day 2 speed-strength (medium), day 3 speed-strength (high), day 4 complex (low), day 5 speed-strength (medium), day 6 speed-strength (high), day 7 empty. The text says these microcycles combine \"workouts with large and moderate volumes of strength loading\" with \"workouts with an emphasis on technical and complex training\" (PDF pp. 388-389).\n\nThe authors close: \"the microcycles used years ago have become the weakest link in the programming of training. Naturally, the preceding examples still do not eliminate those insufficiencies, although they point the way in which scientific research should be directed\" (PDF p. 389).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason for the mixed week's failure is that the body \"cannot simultaneously differentiate between and accumulate specific reactions to every type of daily loading\" (PDF p. 387). For the single-emphasis weeks they report the restoration curves without explaining them.\n\n**When to apply** — Designing a loading week where one quality should be pushed: concentrate workouts of one emphasis in the week and expect roughly three days for the stressed quality to come back above baseline in the swimmers' data. Eric's baseline: \"The in-season game week: Sunday off; Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday; lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)). Inference: the swimmers' VO2 max took about 72 h to recover after an aerobic-emphasis week, the same window Eric leaves before kickoff; this is a coincidence of numbers from a different sport and quality, not support for the football rule.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Unclear spots.** The text says anaerobic productivity \"exceeded its initial level after 72 hours\" in the anaerobic week, but the figure's \"Training effect\" curve crosses 100% at about 30-36 h; the figure also labels that curve \"Training effect,\" not anaerobic potential, so which curve is the 4 x 50 m test is inferred from the text. The text says aerobic potential \"rose over a 12 hour span and then diminished,\" but the figure shows it still about 6% above initial at 72 h. No sample sizes, athlete numbers or units for the \"training load\" bars are given. These are elite swimmers and a Soviet-era report; the speed-strength week has no outcome data at all.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is the Kudelin swimmer data above (PDF p. 388).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]], [[periodization--bompa/heuristic--fatigue-fitness-3to1-ratio]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--microcycle-types-and-load-wave-design]], [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--popov-aerobic-strength-microcycle]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5bd2e4c0-ff22-438c-a5f4-c1e883370662","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"70b8c867-b61e-4d4d-9918-b90360cc1442","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14701902 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"8da8d7fa-9398-4b8d-b567-8715a6e6e872","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:22:28.312Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14701902 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8da8d7fa-9398-4b8d-b567-8715a6e6e872","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffmb4Jn3NTeUJFCSjczq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2596,"cache_read_input_tokens":295631,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2596,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":5187,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffma577jRLzFARAPRQGE","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"cfcb54a1-aafa-4819-bb15-65a52a51a056","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:22:48.452Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"cfcb54a1-aafa-4819-bb15-65a52a51a056","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffmb4Jn3NTeUJFCSjczq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018NiLWHW6L43F7uTxkmtT1o","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--seven-primary-aims-of-programming-training.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.13\"\ntopic: primary aims of programming\ncategory: [programming, periodization, concentrated-loading, annual-plan, advanced]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Seven Primary Aims for Programming the Training of Highly Qualified Athletes\n\n**What it is** — The authors condense 6.12 into seven aims that \"become the definitive rules of programming\" for \"highly qualified athletes under modern conditions\" (PDF p. 389 / printed p. 378, and PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379, both checked against the page images). Paraphrased with the key wording:\n1. **Exploit the current adaptation reserves.** Plan the mesocycles so \"the special work capacity peaks at the time of the most important competitions,\" fitting \"at least two large mesocycles into the annual cycle, even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period.\"\n2. **Preserve the training potential of the loading** by \"systematically increasing the intensity and specificity of the training influence, according to the rise in work capacity,\" using the conjugate sequence system (5.3.3).\n3. **Concentrate extensive, specialised unidirectional loading** \"of optimal intensity, frequency and duration,\" especially special strength loading as \"a relatively independent block\" that creates \"a functional base for the subsequent special preparation.\"\n4. **Exploit the LDTE of concentrated strength loading** to regulate \"the general volume of loading in the annual cycle\" and to create \"favourable conditions for improving technique, speed and competitive preparedness.\"\n5. **Block-load means of different primary emphasis** to \"effectively utilise intrinsically incompatible loading regimes (e.g. emphasizing strength, technique or speed), eliminate or minimise negative interaction between loads of different primary emphasis, and devise combinations which provide sequential accumulation of the partial training effects.\"\n6. **Lead with special physical preparation (SPP):** \"training where SPP precedes in-depth technique or speed work.\"\n7. **Model competition activity:** reproduce \"training work that typifies competitive conditions, especially regarding execution of sporting actions at the high intensity which is encountered in competition.\"\n\nThe authors attach a condition: \"adherence to these aims will lead to success only if all the preceding preparation has been systematic and the athlete has achieved well-balanced technical and special physical preparedness. To achieve these aims, one must institute measures that take into account actual conditions, the individual characteristics of the athlete and the specifics of the sport\" (PDF p. 390).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors present the aims as conclusions from the preceding sections (\"The above findings may be expressed as a number of general aims,\" PDF p. 389) and give no separate mechanism. Each aim's support is in its own section: concentrated loading ([[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]]), the conjugate sequence ([[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]), two mesocycles ([[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]]).\n\n**When to apply** — As a checklist when reviewing an annual plan for an advanced athlete: does each phase have one main emphasis, are incompatible qualities separated into blocks, does SPP come before the heavy technique and speed work, and is there competition-like work at competition intensity? Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182, Inferred (transcript + slides)). Inference: this matches aim 5 closely.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The aims are written for highly qualified athletes, and the authors make them conditional on systematic, well-balanced earlier preparation; they are not a plan for beginners. Aim 1's \"even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period\" conflicts with the chapter's monocyclic recommendation for long-duration endurance (see [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]]). Aims 3-5 rest on the concentrated-unidirectional model that Issurin 2016 judged under-supported for team, combat and swimming preparation ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]); that challenge is recorded on the model entries, not set on this list.\n\n**Example** — The authors' worked illustrations of the aims are the annual models of 6.14 ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Inference: in a jumper's plan, aims 3 and 6 put the strength block first, aim 4 times technique for the weeks after it, and aim 7 adds full-approach jumps under meet conditions in the later preparatory weeks.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]], [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2596,"cache_read_input_tokens":295631,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2596,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_018NiLWHW6L43F7uTxkmtT1o":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--seven-primary-aims-of-programming-training.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.13\"\ntopic: primary aims of programming\ncategory: [programming, periodization, concentrated-loading, annual-plan, advanced]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Seven Primary Aims for Programming the Training of Highly Qualified Athletes\n\n**What it is** — The authors condense 6.12 into seven aims that \"become the definitive rules of programming\" for \"highly qualified athletes under modern conditions\" (PDF p. 389 / printed p. 378, and PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379, both checked against the page images). Paraphrased with the key wording:\n1. **Exploit the current adaptation reserves.** Plan the mesocycles so \"the special work capacity peaks at the time of the most important competitions,\" fitting \"at least two large mesocycles into the annual cycle, even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period.\"\n2. **Preserve the training potential of the loading** by \"systematically increasing the intensity and specificity of the training influence, according to the rise in work capacity,\" using the conjugate sequence system (5.3.3).\n3. **Concentrate extensive, specialised unidirectional loading** \"of optimal intensity, frequency and duration,\" especially special strength loading as \"a relatively independent block\" that creates \"a functional base for the subsequent special preparation.\"\n4. **Exploit the LDTE of concentrated strength loading** to regulate \"the general volume of loading in the annual cycle\" and to create \"favourable conditions for improving technique, speed and competitive preparedness.\"\n5. **Block-load means of different primary emphasis** to \"effectively utilise intrinsically incompatible loading regimes (e.g. emphasizing strength, technique or speed), eliminate or minimise negative interaction between loads of different primary emphasis, and devise combinations which provide sequential accumulation of the partial training effects.\"\n6. **Lead with special physical preparation (SPP):** \"training where SPP precedes in-depth technique or speed work.\"\n7. **Model competition activity:** reproduce \"training work that typifies competitive conditions, especially regarding execution of sporting actions at the high intensity which is encountered in competition.\"\n\nThe authors attach a condition: \"adherence to these aims will lead to success only if all the preceding preparation has been systematic and the athlete has achieved well-balanced technical and special physical preparedness. To achieve these aims, one must institute measures that take into account actual conditions, the individual characteristics of the athlete and the specifics of the sport\" (PDF p. 390).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors present the aims as conclusions from the preceding sections (\"The above findings may be expressed as a number of general aims,\" PDF p. 389) and give no separate mechanism. Each aim's support is in its own section: concentrated loading ([[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]]), the conjugate sequence ([[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]), two mesocycles ([[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]]).\n\n**When to apply** — As a checklist when reviewing an annual plan for an advanced athlete: does each phase have one main emphasis, are incompatible qualities separated into blocks, does SPP come before the heavy technique and speed work, and is there competition-like work at competition intensity? Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182, Inferred (transcript + slides)). Inference: this matches aim 5 closely.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The aims are written for highly qualified athletes, and the authors make them conditional on systematic, well-balanced earlier preparation; they are not a plan for beginners. Aim 1's \"even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period\" conflicts with the chapter's monocyclic recommendation for long-duration endurance (see [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]]). Aims 3-5 rest on the concentrated-unidirectional model that Issurin 2016 judged under-supported for team, combat and swimming preparation ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]); that challenge is recorded on the model entries, not set on this list.\n\n**Example** — The authors' worked illustrations of the aims are the annual models of 6.14 ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Inference: in a jumper's plan, aims 3 and 6 put the strength block first, aim 4 times technique for the weeks after it, and aim 7 adds full-approach jumps under meet conditions in the later preparatory weeks.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]], [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffma577jRLzFARAPRQGE","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5d1be79b-4264-4685-8f51-99fb3cce17e7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:23:04.411Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"5d1be79b-4264-4685-8f51-99fb3cce17e7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018NiLWHW6L43F7uTxkmtT1o","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--seven-primary-aims-of-programming-training.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"2303a987-e8b2-41dd-b44f-163d78f634ba","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:23:04.517Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--seven-primary-aims-of-programming-training.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.13\"\ntopic: primary aims of programming\ncategory: [programming, periodization, concentrated-loading, annual-plan, advanced]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Seven Primary Aims for Programming the Training of Highly Qualified Athletes\n\n**What it is** — The authors condense 6.12 into seven aims that \"become the definitive rules of programming\" for \"highly qualified athletes under modern conditions\" (PDF p. 389 / printed p. 378, and PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379, both checked against the page images). Paraphrased with the key wording:\n1. **Exploit the current adaptation reserves.** Plan the mesocycles so \"the special work capacity peaks at the time of the most important competitions,\" fitting \"at least two large mesocycles into the annual cycle, even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period.\"\n2. **Preserve the training potential of the loading** by \"systematically increasing the intensity and specificity of the training influence, according to the rise in work capacity,\" using the conjugate sequence system (5.3.3).\n3. **Concentrate extensive, specialised unidirectional loading** \"of optimal intensity, frequency and duration,\" especially special strength loading as \"a relatively independent block\" that creates \"a functional base for the subsequent special preparation.\"\n4. **Exploit the LDTE of concentrated strength loading** to regulate \"the general volume of loading in the annual cycle\" and to create \"favourable conditions for improving technique, speed and competitive preparedness.\"\n5. **Block-load means of different primary emphasis** to \"effectively utilise intrinsically incompatible loading regimes (e.g. emphasizing strength, technique or speed), eliminate or minimise negative interaction between loads of different primary emphasis, and devise combinations which provide sequential accumulation of the partial training effects.\"\n6. **Lead with special physical preparation (SPP):** \"training where SPP precedes in-depth technique or speed work.\"\n7. **Model competition activity:** reproduce \"training work that typifies competitive conditions, especially regarding execution of sporting actions at the high intensity which is encountered in competition.\"\n\nThe authors attach a condition: \"adherence to these aims will lead to success only if all the preceding preparation has been systematic and the athlete has achieved well-balanced technical and special physical preparedness. To achieve these aims, one must institute measures that take into account actual conditions, the individual characteristics of the athlete and the specifics of the sport\" (PDF p. 390).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors present the aims as conclusions from the preceding sections (\"The above findings may be expressed as a number of general aims,\" PDF p. 389) and give no separate mechanism. Each aim's support is in its own section: concentrated loading ([[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]]), the conjugate sequence ([[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]), two mesocycles ([[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]]).\n\n**When to apply** — As a checklist when reviewing an annual plan for an advanced athlete: does each phase have one main emphasis, are incompatible qualities separated into blocks, does SPP come before the heavy technique and speed work, and is there competition-like work at competition intensity? Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182, Inferred (transcript + slides)). Inference: this matches aim 5 closely.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The aims are written for highly qualified athletes, and the authors make them conditional on systematic, well-balanced earlier preparation; they are not a plan for beginners. Aim 1's \"even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period\" conflicts with the chapter's monocyclic recommendation for long-duration endurance (see [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]]). Aims 3-5 rest on the concentrated-unidirectional model that Issurin 2016 judged under-supported for team, combat and swimming preparation ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]); that challenge is recorded on the model entries, not set on this list.\n\n**Example** — The authors' worked illustrations of the aims are the annual models of 6.14 ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Inference: in a jumper's plan, aims 3 and 6 put the strength block first, aim 4 times technique for the weeks after it, and aim 7 adds full-approach jumps under meet conditions in the later preparatory weeks.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]], [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5d1be79b-4264-4685-8f51-99fb3cce17e7","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"2303a987-e8b2-41dd-b44f-163d78f634ba","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffmb4Jn3NTeUJFCSjczq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KfXremXYeamReumYqxbg6t","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.4\"\ntopic: technique transfer and intensity\ncategory: [technique, specificity, skill-transfer, competition-modelling, visualisation]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Technical Mastery Comes Only From Practice at Competition-Like Intensity; Skill Learned at Low Intensity Does Not Usually Transfer to Maximal Efforts\n\n**What it is** — The authors (PDF p. 399 / printed p. 388, checked against the page image): \"technical mastery can be acquired only through practice with high intensity loading which simulates competition conditions. Proficiency gained under conditions of low stimulation and intensity do not usually transfer successfully to the maximal effort situations of top-level competition. This is relevant to the use of visualisation to perfect sporting movements; this technique is not very successful if it does not offer mental simulation of the movements, the feelings and the competitive circumstances under which the movements have to be displayed. Practice only makes perfect if one practises all components of the performance process under the conditions which mimic those of the sporting event as closely as possible.\" The same idea appears as the seventh primary aim, \"Modelling competition activities\" at \"the high intensity which is encountered in competition\" (PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379), and in the medium-endurance model: \"*competitions, of course, constitute the most specific form of special preparation in any sport*\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 399).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors state the rule without a mechanism or a cited study on this page. Inference: it is the book's specificity principle applied to skill; the related Chapter 5 entry on dynamic correspondence gives the book's own criteria for when training matches the sport.\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding when and how hard to practise the competition skill: the low-intensity technique work done during a strength block ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]) is preparation, and the skill must later be practised at full intensity under competition-like conditions, starting in the preparatory period (the authors: \"it would be too late were it to commence in the competitive period,\" PDF p. 382 / printed p. 371). For mental rehearsal, include the feel and the competitive setting, not just the movement pattern. Eric's baseline: \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 103, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The same chapter allows and recommends low-intensity technique work during concentrated loading for learning components (PDF p. 383), so the rule is about where mastery is finished, not a ban on low-intensity practice. The authors say \"do not usually transfer,\" not never. Inference: for novices learning a movement for the first time, low-intensity practice is the normal starting point; the authors' context here is \"top-level competition.\"\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is visualisation that rehearses only the movement shape and so \"is not very successful\" (PDF p. 399). Inference: a shot-putter who only drills the glide at half speed in the off-season and first throws at full effort in meets would be expected to see the technique break down under maximal effort.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed]], [[framework--seven-primary-aims-of-programming-training]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2596,"cache_read_input_tokens":295631,"output_tokens":4038,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":560},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2596},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4038,"cache_read_input_tokens":295631,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2596,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2596,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01KfXremXYeamReumYqxbg6t":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.4\"\ntopic: technique transfer and intensity\ncategory: [technique, specificity, skill-transfer, competition-modelling, visualisation]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Technical Mastery Comes Only From Practice at Competition-Like Intensity; Skill Learned at Low Intensity Does Not Usually Transfer to Maximal Efforts\n\n**What it is** — The authors (PDF p. 399 / printed p. 388, checked against the page image): \"technical mastery can be acquired only through practice with high intensity loading which simulates competition conditions. Proficiency gained under conditions of low stimulation and intensity do not usually transfer successfully to the maximal effort situations of top-level competition. This is relevant to the use of visualisation to perfect sporting movements; this technique is not very successful if it does not offer mental simulation of the movements, the feelings and the competitive circumstances under which the movements have to be displayed. Practice only makes perfect if one practises all components of the performance process under the conditions which mimic those of the sporting event as closely as possible.\" The same idea appears as the seventh primary aim, \"Modelling competition activities\" at \"the high intensity which is encountered in competition\" (PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379), and in the medium-endurance model: \"*competitions, of course, constitute the most specific form of special preparation in any sport*\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 399).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors state the rule without a mechanism or a cited study on this page. Inference: it is the book's specificity principle applied to skill; the related Chapter 5 entry on dynamic correspondence gives the book's own criteria for when training matches the sport.\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding when and how hard to practise the competition skill: the low-intensity technique work done during a strength block ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]) is preparation, and the skill must later be practised at full intensity under competition-like conditions, starting in the preparatory period (the authors: \"it would be too late were it to commence in the competitive period,\" PDF p. 382 / printed p. 371). For mental rehearsal, include the feel and the competitive setting, not just the movement pattern. Eric's baseline: \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 103, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The same chapter allows and recommends low-intensity technique work during concentrated loading for learning components (PDF p. 383), so the rule is about where mastery is finished, not a ban on low-intensity practice. The authors say \"do not usually transfer,\" not never. Inference: for novices learning a movement for the first time, low-intensity practice is the normal starting point; the authors' context here is \"top-level competition.\"\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is visualisation that rehearses only the movement shape and so \"is not very successful\" (PDF p. 399). Inference: a shot-putter who only drills the glide at half speed in the off-season and first throws at full effort in meets would be expected to see the technique break down under maximal effort.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed]], [[framework--seven-primary-aims-of-programming-training]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffma577jRLzFARAPRQGE","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"2e338d23-d89b-4544-b1d3-07978503d986","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:23:16.788Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","eff