splayed. Practice only makes perfect if one practises all components of the performance process under the conditions which mimic those of the sporting event as closely as possible.\" The same idea appears as the seventh primary aim, \"Modelling competition activities\" at \"the high intensity which is encountered in competition\" (PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379), and in the medium-endurance model: \"*competitions, of course, constitute the most specific form of special preparation in any sport*\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 399).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors state the rule without a mechanism or a cited study on this page. Inference: it is the book's specificity principle applied to skill; the related Chapter 5 entry on dynamic correspondence gives the book's own criteria for when training matches the sport.\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding when and how hard to practise the competition skill: the low-intensity technique work done during a strength block ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]) is preparation, and the skill must later be practised at full intensity under competition-like conditions, starting in the preparatory period (the authors: \"it would be too late were it to commence in the competitive period,\" PDF p. 382 / printed p. 371). For mental rehearsal, include the feel and the competitive setting, not just the movement pattern. Eric's baseline: \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 103, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The same chapter allows and recommends low-intensity technique work during concentrated loading for learning components (PDF p. 383), so the rule