{"parentUuid":"188d3c33-0db6-4ae4-87f3-9b83a50de1f3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MTHZGsdZP26ZufoRhh2YyC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\nchapter: \"4.3\"","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"1c287489-d542-40a4-8e4c-c38078297fac","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:23:56.474Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"1\nchapter: \"4.3\"","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":15,"oldLines":7,"newStart":15,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **What it is** — The authors (PDF p. 399 / printed p. 388, checked against the page image): \"technical mastery can be acquired only through practice with high intensity loading which simulates competition conditions. Proficiency gained under conditions of low stimulation and intensity do not usually transfer successfully to the maximal effort situations of top-level competition. This is relevant to the use of visualisation to perfect sporting movements; this technique is not very successful if it does not offer mental simulation of the movements, the feelings and the competitive circumstances under which the movements have to be displayed. Practice only makes perfect if one practises all components of the performance process under the conditions which mimic those of the sporting event as closely as possible.\" The same idea appears as the seventh primary aim, \"Modelling competition activities\" at \"the high intensity which is encountered in competition\" (PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379), and in the medium-endurance model: \"*competitions, of course, constitute the most specific form of special preparation in any sport*\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 399)."," ","-**Why it works** — The authors state the rule without a mechanism or a cited study on this page. Inference: it is the book's specificity principle applied to skill; the related Chapter 5 entry on dynamic correspondence gives the book's own criteria for when training matches the sport.","+**Why it works** — The authors state the rule without a mechanism or a cited study on this page. Inference: it is the book's specificity principle applied to skill; the book's own criteria for when training matches the sport are in [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]] (Chapter 2)."," "," **When to apply** — Deciding when and how hard to practise the competition skill: the low-intensity technique work done during a strength block ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]) is preparation, and the skill must later be practised at full intensity under competition-like conditions, starting in the preparatory period (the authors: \"it would be too late were it to commence in the competitive period,\" PDF p. 382 / printed p. 371). For mental rehearsal, include the feel and the competitive setting, not just the movement pattern. Eric's baseline: \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 103, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1))."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"188d3c33-0db6-4ae4-87f3-9b83a50de1f3","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1c287489-d542-40a4-8e4c-c38078297fac","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice.md","snippet":"10\tingest-tier: full-text\n11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Technical Mastery Comes Only From Practice at Competition-Like Intensity; Skill Learned at Low Intensity Does Not Usually Transfer to Maximal Efforts\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — The authors (PDF p. 399 / printed p. 388, checked against the page image): \"technical mastery can be acquired only through practice with high intensity loading which simulates competition conditions. Proficiency gained under conditions of low stimulation and intensity do not usually transfer successfully to the maximal effort situations of top-level competition. This is relevant to the use of visualisation to perfect sporting movements; this technique is not very successful if it does not offer mental simulation of the movements, the feelings and the competitive circumstances under which the movements have to be displayed. Practice only makes perfect if one practises all components of the performance process under the conditions which mimic those of the sporting event as closely as possible.\" The same idea appears as the seventh primary aim, \"Modelling competition activities\" at \"the high intensity which is encountered in competition\" (PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379), and in the medium-endurance model: \"*competitions, of course, constitute the most specific form of special preparation in any sport*\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 399).\n17\t\n18\t**Why it works** — The authors state the rule without a mechanism or a cited study on this page. Inference: it is the book's specificity principle applied to skill; the book's own criteria for when training matches the sport are in [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]] (Chapter 2).\n19\t\n20\t**When to apply** — Deciding when and how hard to practise the competition skill: the low-intensity technique work done during a strength block ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]) is preparation, and the skill must later be practised at full intensity under competition-like conditions, starting in the preparatory period (the authors: \"it would be too late were it to commence in the competitive period,\" PDF p. 382 / printed p. 371). For mental rehearsal, include the feel and the competitive setting, not just the movement pattern. Eric's baseline: \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 103, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)).\n21\t\n22\t**When NOT to apply** — The same chapter allows and recommends low-intensity technique work during concentrated loading for learning components (PDF p. 383), so the rule is about where mastery is finished, not a ban on low-intensity practice. The authors say \"do not usually transfer,\" not never. Inference: for novices learning a movement for the first time, low-intensity practice is the normal starting point; the authors' context here is \"top-level competition.\"\n23\t\n24\t**Example** — The authors' own example is visualisation that rehearses only the movement shape and so \"is not very successful\" (PDF p. 399). Inference: a shot-putter who only drills the glide at half speed in the off-season and first throws at full effort in meets would be expected to see the technique break down under maximal effort.\n25\t\n26\t**Related** — [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed]], [[framework--seven-primary-aims-of-programming-training]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"da7c656e-094c-452e-ac17-9faf1131bd43","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:23:56.479Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice.md changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n10\tingest-tier: full-text\n11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Technical Mastery Comes Only From Practice at Competition-Like Intensity; Skill Learned at Low Intensity Does Not Usually Transfer to Maximal Efforts\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — The authors (PDF p. 399 / printed p. 388, checked against the page image): \"technical mastery can be acquired only through practice with high intensity loading which simulates competition conditions. Proficiency gained under conditions of low stimulation and intensity do not usually transfer successfully to the maximal effort situations of top-level competition. This is relevant to the use of visualisation to perfect sporting movements; this technique is not very successful if it does not offer mental simulation of the movements, the feelings and the competitive circumstances under which the movements have to be displayed. Practice only makes perfect if one practises all components of the performance process under the conditions which mimic those of the sporting event as closely as possible.\" The same idea appears as the seventh primary aim, \"Modelling competition activities\" at \"the high intensity which is encountered in competition\" (PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379), and in the medium-endurance model: \"*competitions, of course, constitute the most specific form of special preparation in any sport*\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 399).\n17\t\n18\t**Why it works** — The authors state the rule without a mechanism or a cited study on this page. Inference: it is the book's specificity principle applied to skill; the book's own criteria for when training matches the sport are in [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]] (Chapter 2).\n19\t\n20\t**When to apply** — Deciding when and how hard to practise the competition skill: the low-intensity technique work done during a strength block ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]) is preparation, and the skill must later be practised at full intensity under competition-like conditions, starting in the preparatory period (the authors: \"it would be too late were it to commence in the competitive period,\" PDF p. 382 / printed p. 371). For mental rehearsal, include the feel and the competitive setting, not just the movement pattern. Eric's baseline: \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 103, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)).\n21\t\n22\t**When NOT to apply** — The same chapter allows and recommends low-intensity technique work during concentrated loading for learning components (PDF p. 383), so the rule is about where mastery is finished, not a ban on low-intensity practice. The authors say \"do not usually transfer,\" not never. Inference: for novices learning a movement for the first time, low-intensity practice is the normal starting point; the authors' context here is \"top-level competition.\"\n23\t\n24\t**Example** — The authors' own example is visualisation that rehearses only the movement shape and so \"is not very successful\" (PDF p. 399). Inference: a shot-putter who only drills the glide at half speed in the off-season and first throws at full effort in meets would be expected to see the technique break down under maximal effort.\n25\t\n26\t**Related** — [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed]], [[framework--seven-primary-aims-of-programming-training]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
