{"parentUuid":"9a56f60a-4f0b-4bc3-abb9-70ad6b7cc6a8","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmhYnxA76rfRkxLR2oR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":241,"cache_read_input_tokens":304881,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":241,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":472,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffmh6hZgZzojznBB2e41","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"8e6d5d42-1026-484d-a149-b9f30f18ee0f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:24:11.636Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8e6d5d42-1026-484d-a149-b9f30f18ee0f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmhYnxA76rfRkxLR2oR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GpeJ2qZ8prni6WgBqEpVon","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14\"\ntopic: sports modelling\ncategory: [sports-modelling, needs-analysis, annual-plan, testing, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Sports Modelling: a Descriptive Model of the Sport Feeds a Prescriptive Model of the Athlete's State and the Loading Programme\n\n**What it is** — The authors split the models needed to design programmes into two classes (PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379, checked against the page image):\n- **The descriptive model** \"furnishes qualitative details of the motor qualities and fitness factors needed for proficiency in a given sport, as well as desirable quantitative kinanthropometric, kinesiological, physiological and biomechanical details which characterise movement at all stages of that sport.\" Examples of its contents: \"movement patterns, velocities and accelerations at different joint angles or stages of an event, reaction time, 'lactate threshold' (OBLA), relative muscle strength, body proportions, agonist/antagonist muscle strength ratios and timing of segments of important movements.\"\n- **The prescriptive model** \"represents a simplified analogue of the actual training process, omitting some details, but preserving the essential information concerning its composition and structure. It describes the dynamics of the athlete's fitness state and the systems of organising annual training.\" Its core is a model of the athlete's state that \"diagramatically expresses the optimal changes in the most important indicators of special work capacity over time\" and \"determines the specific periods when one should plan to achieve peak levels.\" It \"draws strongly on the data furnished by the descriptive model.\"\n- Each can be **qualitative** (the dynamics of the functional indicators, the main training contents and how components connect) or **quantitative** (\"all numerical aspects of the training... such as calculation of volume, intensity, duration of stages and loading distribution in different intensity zones\").\n\nConventions for the 6.14 annual models (PDF pp. 390-391 / printed pp. 379-380, checked against the page images): months are not named because summer and winter sports are covered; \"Each model consists of about 11 months, since, on average, one month is allotted for the final transition period\"; each model pairs a fitness-state diagram with a loading diagram; \"One can use any method that enables one to measure the values of these indicators\"; \"All models in this chapter use the principle of concentrated special strength loading, which elicits the LDTE\"; the rectangular blocks are schematic, since \"the actual loading follows a concentrated wave pattern which gradually reaches a peak, then subsides in volume\"; and stage boundaries \"can vary somewhat\" with the real calendar.\n\nOn measurement: \"It is of little value to identify which energy systems, types of fitness or movement patterns are involved in a sport unless accurate numerical determinations are regularly made of each of these factors. Even then, we have to remember that the body is not simply a machine in which input and output are deterministically related, but an organism whose behaviour displays non-linearity and fairly random deviations... Nevertheless, without this information, long-term programming becomes imprecise\" (PDF p. 391, checked against the page image). The descriptive-modelling passage names the \"Multifactorial Fitness Profile (MFP) discussed later... (Ch 7)\" and says \"Many years of Russian research have produced models for every type of sport\" (PDF pp. 390-391); it reads as Siff's framing of the Russian material.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is control: modelling \"makes it possible to manage the organisational complexity of sports activities and devise a practical scheme for describing and controlling the training process\" (PDF p. 390), and norms \"enable the coach to identify any weaknesses in the state of the athlete and thereby modify the training programme\" (PDF p. 391).\n\n**When to apply** — Before writing an annual plan: build or borrow a descriptive model of what the sport demands, with numbers, then draw the planned course of the key indicators across the year and the loading that should produce it. Eric's baseline: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 32, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)). Inference: the descriptive model is one way to state those questions in advance, so each test in the battery is tied to a factor the model says matters.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors themselves warn that the body is non-linear and deviates \"fairly random[ly]\" from quantitative prediction, so a model is a guide, not a forecast. Their example norms (Tables 6.6-6.12) come from Soviet track-and-field and weightlifting populations of the 1970s and 1980s and are \"not... all-embracing models\" (PDF p. 391; the sentence is printed with a double negative, \"None of these examples should not be regarded as all-embracing models\"). No football descriptive model is given.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own examples are the model-characteristic tables, including weightlifters' muscle and fat proportions and PWC170 by weight class (Table 6.6, Stepanova et al, 1983, PDF p. 391) and 100 m sprinter models ([[case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics]]).\n\n**Related** — [[case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics]], [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--model-characteristic-tables-exist-by-qualification]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":241,"cache_read_input_tokens":304881,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":241,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01GpeJ2qZ8prni6WgBqEpVon":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14\"\ntopic: sports modelling\ncategory: [sports-modelling, needs-analysis, annual-plan, testing, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Sports Modelling: a Descriptive Model of the Sport Feeds a Prescriptive Model of the Athlete's State and the Loading Programme\n\n**What it is** — The authors split the models needed to design programmes into two classes (PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379, checked against the page image):\n- **The descriptive model** \"furnishes qualitative details of the motor qualities and fitness factors needed for proficiency in a given sport, as well as desirable quantitative kinanthropometric, kinesiological, physiological and biomechanical details which characterise movement at all stages of that sport.\" Examples of its contents: \"movement patterns, velocities and accelerations at different joint angles or stages of an event, reaction time, 'lactate threshold' (OBLA), relative muscle strength, body proportions, agonist/antagonist muscle strength ratios and timing of segments of important movements.\"\n- **The prescriptive model** \"represents a simplified analogue of the actual training process, omitting some details, but preserving the essential information concerning its composition and structure. It describes the dynamics of the athlete's fitness state and the systems of organising annual training.\" Its core is a model of the athlete's state that \"diagramatically expresses the optimal changes in the most important indicators of special work capacity over time\" and \"determines the specific periods when one should plan to achieve peak levels.\" It \"draws strongly on the data furnished by the descriptive model.\"\n- Each can be **qualitative** (the dynamics of the functional indicators, the main training contents and how components connect) or **quantitative** (\"all numerical aspects of the training... such as calculation of volume, intensity, duration of stages and loading distribution in different intensity zones\").\n\nConventions for the 6.14 annual models (PDF pp. 390-391 / printed pp. 379-380, checked against the page images): months are not named because summer and winter sports are covered; \"Each model consists of about 11 months, since, on average, one month is allotted for the final transition period\"; each model pairs a fitness-state diagram with a loading diagram; \"One can use any method that enables one to measure the values of these indicators\"; \"All models in this chapter use the principle of concentrated special strength loading, which elicits the LDTE\"; the rectangular blocks are schematic, since \"the actual loading follows a concentrated wave pattern which gradually reaches a peak, then subsides in volume\"; and stage boundaries \"can vary somewhat\" with the real calendar.\n\nOn measurement: \"It is of little value to identify which energy systems, types of fitness or movement patterns are involved in a sport unless accurate numerical determinations are regularly made of each of these factors. Even then, we have to remember that the body is not simply a machine in which input and output are deterministically related, but an organism whose behaviour displays non-linearity and fairly random deviations... Nevertheless, without this information, long-term programming becomes imprecise\" (PDF p. 391, checked against the page image). The descriptive-modelling passage names the \"Multifactorial Fitness Profile (MFP) discussed later... (Ch 7)\" and says \"Many years of Russian research have produced models for every type of sport\" (PDF pp. 390-391); it reads as Siff's framing of the Russian material.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is control: modelling \"makes it possible to manage the organisational complexity of sports activities and devise a practical scheme for describing and controlling the training process\" (PDF p. 390), and norms \"enable the coach to identify any weaknesses in the state of the athlete and thereby modify the training programme\" (PDF p. 391).\n\n**When to apply** — Before writing an annual plan: build or borrow a descriptive model of what the sport demands, with numbers, then draw the planned course of the key indicators across the year and the loading that should produce it. Eric's baseline: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 32, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)). Inference: the descriptive model is one way to state those questions in advance, so each test in the battery is tied to a factor the model says matters.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors themselves warn that the body is non-linear and deviates \"fairly random[ly]\" from quantitative prediction, so a model is a guide, not a forecast. Their example norms (Tables 6.6-6.12) come from Soviet track-and-field and weightlifting populations of the 1970s and 1980s and are \"not... all-embracing models\" (PDF p. 391; the sentence is printed with a double negative, \"None of these examples should not be regarded as all-embracing models\"). No football descriptive model is given.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own examples are the model-characteristic tables, including weightlifters' muscle and fat proportions and PWC170 by weight class (Table 6.6, Stepanova et al, 1983, PDF p. 391) and 100 m sprinter models ([[case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics]]).\n\n**Related** — [[case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics]], [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--model-characteristic-tables-exist-by-qualification]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffmh6hZgZzojznBB2e41","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"ed0cf87b-636b-4547-a06b-ca927b106084","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:24:27.618Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ed0cf87b-636b-4547-a06b-ca927b106084","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GpeJ2qZ8prni6WgBqEpVon","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"245e7fab-3f93-4273-89de-2f15321df8f0","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:24:27.738Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14\"\ntopic: sports modelling\ncategory: [sports-modelling, needs-analysis, annual-plan, testing, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Sports Modelling: a Descriptive Model of the Sport Feeds a Prescriptive Model of the Athlete's State and the Loading Programme\n\n**What it is** — The authors split the models needed to design programmes into two classes (PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379, checked against the page image):\n- **The descriptive model** \"furnishes qualitative details of the motor qualities and fitness factors needed for proficiency in a given sport, as well as desirable quantitative kinanthropometric, kinesiological, physiological and biomechanical details which characterise movement at all stages of that sport.\" Examples of its contents: \"movement patterns, velocities and accelerations at different joint angles or stages of an event, reaction time, 'lactate threshold' (OBLA), relative muscle strength, body proportions, agonist/antagonist muscle strength ratios and timing of segments of important movements.\"\n- **The prescriptive model** \"represents a simplified analogue of the actual training process, omitting some details, but preserving the essential information concerning its composition and structure. It describes the dynamics of the athlete's fitness state and the systems of organising annual training.\" Its core is a model of the athlete's state that \"diagramatically expresses the optimal changes in the most important indicators of special work capacity over time\" and \"determines the specific periods when one should plan to achieve peak levels.\" It \"draws strongly on the data furnished by the descriptive model.\"\n- Each can be **qualitative** (the dynamics of the functional indicators, the main training contents and how components connect) or **quantitative** (\"all numerical aspects of the training... such as calculation of volume, intensity, duration of stages and loading distribution in different intensity zones\").\n\nConventions for the 6.14 annual models (PDF pp. 390-391 / printed pp. 379-380, checked against the page images): months are not named because summer and winter sports are covered; \"Each model consists of about 11 months, since, on average, one month is allotted for the final transition period\"; each model pairs a fitness-state diagram with a loading diagram; \"One can use any method that enables one to measure the values of these indicators\"; \"All models in this chapter use the principle of concentrated special strength loading, which elicits the LDTE\"; the rectangular blocks are schematic, since \"the actual loading follows a concentrated wave pattern which gradually reaches a peak, then subsides in volume\"; and stage boundaries \"can vary somewhat\" with the real calendar.\n\nOn measurement: \"It is of little value to identify which energy systems, types of fitness or movement patterns are involved in a sport unless accurate numerical determinations are regularly made of each of these factors. Even then, we have to remember that the body is not simply a machine in which input and output are deterministically related, but an organism whose behaviour displays non-linearity and fairly random deviations... Nevertheless, without this information, long-term programming becomes imprecise\" (PDF p. 391, checked against the page image). The descriptive-modelling passage names the \"Multifactorial Fitness Profile (MFP) discussed later... (Ch 7)\" and says \"Many years of Russian research have produced models for every type of sport\" (PDF pp. 390-391); it reads as Siff's framing of the Russian material.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is control: modelling \"makes it possible to manage the organisational complexity of sports activities and devise a practical scheme for describing and controlling the training process\" (PDF p. 390), and norms \"enable the coach to identify any weaknesses in the state of the athlete and thereby modify the training programme\" (PDF p. 391).\n\n**When to apply** — Before writing an annual plan: build or borrow a descriptive model of what the sport demands, with numbers, then draw the planned course of the key indicators across the year and the loading that should produce it. Eric's baseline: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 32, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)). Inference: the descriptive model is one way to state those questions in advance, so each test in the battery is tied to a factor the model says matters.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors themselves warn that the body is non-linear and deviates \"fairly random[ly]\" from quantitative prediction, so a model is a guide, not a forecast. Their example norms (Tables 6.6-6.12) come from Soviet track-and-field and weightlifting populations of the 1970s and 1980s and are \"not... all-embracing models\" (PDF p. 391; the sentence is printed with a double negative, \"None of these examples should not be regarded as all-embracing models\"). No football descriptive model is given.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own examples are the model-characteristic tables, including weightlifters' muscle and fat proportions and PWC170 by weight class (Table 6.6, Stepanova et al, 1983, PDF p. 391) and 100 m sprinter models ([[case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics]]).\n\n**Related** — [[case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics]], [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--model-characteristic-tables-exist-by-qualification]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ed0cf87b-636b-4547-a06b-ca927b106084","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"245e7fab-3f93-4273-89de-2f15321df8f0","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmhYnxA76rfRkxLR2oR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018Afkc6VHsc6h8EYdLFx7PM","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14\"\ntopic: sprinter model characteristics\ncategory: [sprinting, sports-modelling, testing-norms, relative-strength, speed-strength]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Tabachnik's Model Characteristics for 10.0 s Men and 11.0 s Women 100 m Sprinters: Speed, Relative Strength and Jump Targets\n\n**What it is** — Two tables from Tabachnik (1979) give the profile a sprinter should reach to run 10.0 s (men) or 11.0 s (women) (PDF p. 392 / printed p. 381; every value below checked against the page image).\n\n**Table 6.8** (men 10.0 s / women 11.0 s):\n- General data: initial 100 m time 11.62 / 13.0 s; age of beginning sprinting 14.6 / 14.1 years; years of sprinting 8.4 / 8.3.\n- Speed indicators: simple motor reaction time 0.110 / 0.121; starting reaction time 0.136 / 0.149; total starting time 0.37 / 0.40 s; 5 m from low start 1.18 / 1.32; 30 m from low start 3.80 / 4.25; 50 m from low start 5.70 / 6.15; last 50 m of 100 m 4.45 / 5.00 s.\n- Relative muscular strength (relative to bodymass): trunk extension 2.80 / 2.60; trunk flexion 1.20 / 1.15; knee extension 2.20 / 2.00; knee flexion 1.10 / 1.05; hip extension 3.10 / 2.80; hip flexion 1.20 / 1.05; ankle plantarflexion 3.00 / 2.80.\n- Speed-strength: standing long jump 3.20 / 2.80 m; standing triple jump 10.0 / 8.40 m; ten-step jump from standing start 36.0 / 32.0 m.\n\n**Table 6.9** (section times by goal, seconds): for a 10.0 s goal, 15 m 1.16-1.18, 30 m 3.70-3.80, first 50 m 5.60-5.70, second 50 m 4.30-4.40, last 10 m 0.900; for 10.9 s, 15 m 1.30-1.32, 30 m 4.20-4.30, first 50 m 6.00-6.10, second 50 m 4.80-4.90, last 10 m 0.990 (goals 10.2, 10.5 and 10.7 s lie between).\n\nThe tables sit among the authors' examples of descriptive modelling, alongside middle-distance (Tables 6.7, 6.10, Maximenko 1979), javelin (Table 6.11, Konstantinov 1978) and juvenile sprinter norms (Table 6.12, Alabin & Yushkevitch 1978) (PDF pp. 392-393).\n\n**Why it works** — The book gives no derivation, sample or method for the norms, and does not say how the relative-strength values were measured (the units are \"relative to bodymass\" only). The authors' general claim is that such norms \"enable the coach to identify any weaknesses in the state of the athlete\" (PDF p. 391 / printed p. 380).\n\n**When to apply** — As a reference for what a world-class sprint profile looked like in this Soviet model, and as a template for building one's own position-specific norm table. Eric's baseline: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 32, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)). Inference: a table like this only earns a place if each row answers a programming question for the roster.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Internal inconsistencies, recorded as printed.** In Table 6.8 the men's 50 m from a low start (5.70 s) plus the last 50 m (4.45 s) sum to 10.15 s, not 10.0 s; Table 6.9 gives 4.30-4.40 s for the second 50 m at a 10.0 s goal, faster than Table 6.8's 4.45 s. Women's 6.15 + 5.00 = 11.15 s against an 11.0 s goal. The sources may define the splits differently, but the book does not say. Hand timing and 1970s testing methods are not described, so the values do not convert directly to modern electronic timing. These are elite sprinters, not football players; the relative-strength values come from an unspecified test and cannot be compared with force-plate or 1RM data.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is the table itself. Inference: a coach reading the standing triple jump target (10.0 m for 10.0 s men) would check whether a sprinter with fast 30 m times but a short triple jump lacks the speed-strength the model expects.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--model-characteristic-tables-exist-by-qualification]], [[claim--absolute-leg-strength-connects-to-sprint-speed-only-indirectly-via-jump]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":241,"cache_read_input_tokens":304881,"output_tokens":4012,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":36},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":241},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4012,"cache_read_input_tokens":304881,"cache_creation_input_tokens":241,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":241,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_018Afkc6VHsc6h8EYdLFx7PM":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14\"\ntopic: sprinter model characteristics\ncategory: [sprinting, sports-modelling, testing-norms, relative-strength, speed-strength]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Tabachnik's Model Characteristics for 10.0 s Men and 11.0 s Women 100 m Sprinters: Speed, Relative Strength and Jump Targets\n\n**What it is** — Two tables from Tabachnik (1979) give the profile a sprinter should reach to run 10.0 s (men) or 11.0 s (women) (PDF p. 392 / printed p. 381; every value below checked against the page image).\n\n**Table 6.8** (men 10.0 s / women 11.0 s):\n- General data: initial 100 m time 11.62 / 13.0 s; age of beginning sprinting 14.6 / 14.1 years; years of sprinting 8.4 / 8.3.\n- Speed indicators: simple motor reaction time 0.110 / 0.121; starting reaction time 0.136 / 0.149; total starting time 0.37 / 0.40 s; 5 m from low start 1.18 / 1.32; 30 m from low start 3.80 / 4.25; 50 m from low start 5.70 / 6.15; last 50 m of 100 m 4.45 / 5.00 s.\n- Relative muscular strength (relative to bodymass): trunk extension 2.80 / 2.60; trunk flexion 1.20 / 1.15; knee extension 2.20 / 2.00; knee flexion 1.10 / 1.05; hip extension 3.10 / 2.80; hip flexion 1.20 / 1.05; ankle plantarflexion 3.00 / 2.80.\n- Speed-strength: standing long jump 3.20 / 2.80 m; standing triple jump 10.0 / 8.40 m; ten-step jump from standing start 36.0 / 32.0 m.\n\n**Table 6.9** (section times by goal, seconds): for a 10.0 s goal, 15 m 1.16-1.18, 30 m 3.70-3.80, first 50 m 5.60-5.70, second 50 m 4.30-4.40, last 10 m 0.900; for 10.9 s, 15 m 1.30-1.32, 30 m 4.20-4.30, first 50 m 6.00-6.10, second 50 m 4.80-4.90, last 10 m 0.990 (goals 10.2, 10.5 and 10.7 s lie between).\n\nThe tables sit among the authors' examples of descriptive modelling, alongside middle-distance (Tables 6.7, 6.10, Maximenko 1979), javelin (Table 6.11, Konstantinov 1978) and juvenile sprinter norms (Table 6.12, Alabin & Yushkevitch 1978) (PDF pp. 392-393).\n\n**Why it works** — The book gives no derivation, sample or method for the norms, and does not say how the relative-strength values were measured (the units are \"relative to bodymass\" only). The authors' general claim is that such norms \"enable the coach to identify any weaknesses in the state of the athlete\" (PDF p. 391 / printed p. 380).\n\n**When to apply** — As a reference for what a world-class sprint profile looked like in this Soviet model, and as a template for building one's own position-specific norm table. Eric's baseline: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 32, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)). Inference: a table like this only earns a place if each row answers a programming question for the roster.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Internal inconsistencies, recorded as printed.** In Table 6.8 the men's 50 m from a low start (5.70 s) plus the last 50 m (4.45 s) sum to 10.15 s, not 10.0 s; Table 6.9 gives 4.30-4.40 s for the second 50 m at a 10.0 s goal, faster than Table 6.8's 4.45 s. Women's 6.15 + 5.00 = 11.15 s against an 11.0 s goal. The sources may define the splits differently, but the book does not say. Hand timing and 1970s testing methods are not described, so the values do not convert directly to modern electronic timing. These are elite sprinters, not football players; the relative-strength values come from an unspecified test and cannot be compared with force-plate or 1RM data.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is the table itself. Inference: a coach reading the standing triple jump target (10.0 m for 10.0 s men) would check whether a sprinter with fast 30 m times but a short triple jump lacks the speed-strength the model expects.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--model-characteristic-tables-exist-by-qualification]], [[claim--absolute-leg-strength-connects-to-sprint-speed-only-indirectly-via-jump]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffmh6hZgZzojznBB2e41","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f2bc225d-b9d2-4cb5-832e-770aa95dbb7f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:24:42.522Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f2bc225d-b9d2-4cb5-832e-770aa95dbb7f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018Afkc6VHsc6h8EYdLFx7PM","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"851daf91-0a80-4951-9bd9-271073c551fb","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:24:42.639Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14\"\ntopic: sprinter model characteristics\ncategory: [sprinting, sports-modelling, testing-norms, relative-strength, speed-strength]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Tabachnik's Model Characteristics for 10.0 s Men and 11.0 s Women 100 m Sprinters: Speed, Relative Strength and Jump Targets\n\n**What it is** — Two tables from Tabachnik (1979) give the profile a sprinter should reach to run 10.0 s (men) or 11.0 s (women) (PDF p. 392 / printed p. 381; every value below checked against the page image).\n\n**Table 6.8** (men 10.0 s / women 11.0 s):\n- General data: initial 100 m time 11.62 / 13.0 s; age of beginning sprinting 14.6 / 14.1 years; years of sprinting 8.4 / 8.3.\n- Speed indicators: simple motor reaction time 0.110 / 0.121; starting reaction time 0.136 / 0.149; total starting time 0.37 / 0.40 s; 5 m from low start 1.18 / 1.32; 30 m from low start 3.80 / 4.25; 50 m from low start 5.70 / 6.15; last 50 m of 100 m 4.45 / 5.00 s.\n- Relative muscular strength (relative to bodymass): trunk extension 2.80 / 2.60; trunk flexion 1.20 / 1.15; knee extension 2.20 / 2.00; knee flexion 1.10 / 1.05; hip extension 3.10 / 2.80; hip flexion 1.20 / 1.05; ankle plantarflexion 3.00 / 2.80.\n- Speed-strength: standing long jump 3.20 / 2.80 m; standing triple jump 10.0 / 8.40 m; ten-step jump from standing start 36.0 / 32.0 m.\n\n**Table 6.9** (section times by goal, seconds): for a 10.0 s goal, 15 m 1.16-1.18, 30 m 3.70-3.80, first 50 m 5.60-5.70, second 50 m 4.30-4.40, last 10 m 0.900; for 10.9 s, 15 m 1.30-1.32, 30 m 4.20-4.30, first 50 m 6.00-6.10, second 50 m 4.80-4.90, last 10 m 0.990 (goals 10.2, 10.5 and 10.7 s lie between).\n\nThe tables sit among the authors' examples of descriptive modelling, alongside middle-distance (Tables 6.7, 6.10, Maximenko 1979), javelin (Table 6.11, Konstantinov 1978) and juvenile sprinter norms (Table 6.12, Alabin & Yushkevitch 1978) (PDF pp. 392-393).\n\n**Why it works** — The book gives no derivation, sample or method for the norms, and does not say how the relative-strength values were measured (the units are \"relative to bodymass\" only). The authors' general claim is that such norms \"enable the coach to identify any weaknesses in the state of the athlete\" (PDF p. 391 / printed p. 380).\n\n**When to apply** — As a reference for what a world-class sprint profile looked like in this Soviet model, and as a template for building one's own position-specific norm table. Eric's baseline: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 32, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)). Inference: a table like this only earns a place if each row answers a programming question for the roster.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Internal inconsistencies, recorded as printed.** In Table 6.8 the men's 50 m from a low start (5.70 s) plus the last 50 m (4.45 s) sum to 10.15 s, not 10.0 s; Table 6.9 gives 4.30-4.40 s for the second 50 m at a 10.0 s goal, faster than Table 6.8's 4.45 s. Women's 6.15 + 5.00 = 11.15 s against an 11.0 s goal. The sources may define the splits differently, but the book does not say. Hand timing and 1970s testing methods are not described, so the values do not convert directly to modern electronic timing. These are elite sprinters, not football players; the relative-strength values come from an unspecified test and cannot be compared with force-plate or 1RM data.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is the table itself. Inference: a coach reading the standing triple jump target (10.0 m for 10.0 s men) would check whether a sprinter with fast 30 m times but a short triple jump lacks the speed-strength the model expects.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--model-characteristic-tables-exist-by-qualification]], [[claim--absolute-leg-strength-connects-to-sprint-speed-only-indirectly-via-jump]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f2bc225d-b9d2-4cb5-832e-770aa95dbb7f","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
