{"parentUuid":"18cdda09-1528-4455-a2ba-ba43d82b56e6","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffmrojs8FmwnZzm2mmyz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GGjZJnpvrGskgLrtmMYyvv","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports.md","old_string":"Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once — priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 113, Explicit).","new_string":"Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once\" and \"priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 115, Explicit; the two phrases are joined by a dash in the baseline)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":344,"cache_read_input_tokens":316330,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":344,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01GGjZJnpvrGskgLrtmMYyvv":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports.md","old_string":"Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once — priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 113, Explicit).","new_string":"Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once\" and \"priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 115, Explicit; the two phrases are joined by a dash in the baseline)."}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffmqUFaQ6tZ8RkRHBM99","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"4bbdfc3e-2b92-4e64-a613-0338dab6a605","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:26:18.886Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"4bbdfc3e-2b92-4e64-a613-0338dab6a605","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GGjZJnpvrGskgLrtmMYyvv","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"edc938ed-942d-4006-9541-974e90fde60a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:26:19.221Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports.md","oldString":"Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once — priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 113, Explicit).","newString":"Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once\" and \"priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 115, Explicit; the two phrases are joined by a dash in the baseline).","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.2\"\ntopic: endurance annual models\ncategory: [endurance, periodization, annual-plan, concentrated-loading, bi-cyclic, monocyclic]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Annual Models for Endurance Sports: Bi-Cyclic With Two Strength Blocks for Medium Duration, Monocyclic With Two Strength Blocks for Long Duration\n\n**What it is** — Two prescriptive models from 6.14.2 and 6.14.3 (PDF pp. 395-397 / printed pp. 384-386; text and Figs 6.40 and 6.41 checked against the page images; Fig 6.46 on PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389).\n\n**Medium-duration endurance (bi-cyclic, Fig 6.40).**\n- Two mesocycles and two competition stages; in the first \"one need not compete at the fundamental distance,\" but can compete \"at other distances,\" which \"simultaneously serve as control estimates of the athlete's level of aerobic and anaerobic capacity\" (PDF p. 395).\n- \"Explosive strength peaks at the beginning of the second competition stage, after which it decreases, together with the rise in the volume of intense (anaerobic-glycolytic) cyclic work.\" Anaerobic capacity and strength-endurance \"reach their highest levels during the most important competitive stage (10th - 11th months)\" (PDF p. 395).\n- Aerobic loading predominates in the first preparatory stage; \"during the second stage the work is chiefly at the OBLA (onset of blood lactate accumulation) level\"; aerobic volume rises again in the second competition stage \"to promote restoration.\" Speed work (ATP-CP) and speed-endurance (glycolytic) are timed for the LDTE stage, \"i.e. the development of speed precedes that of speed-endurance.\" Overall sequence: \"General endurance > Speed > Speed-endurance\" (PDF p. 395).\n- Strength block contents: \"The first block emphasizes the development of explosive strength and local muscular endurance, while the second emphasizes mainly local muscular endurance\" (PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385).\n- Fig 6.40, read by eye: block 1 over about months 2-4 (taller), block 2 over about months 6-7 (shorter); phases I about months 1-4, II about month 5, III about months 6-8, IV about months 9-11. For a large mesocycle (Fig 6.46, PDF pp. 399-400), aerobic training \"covers the first half of the large mesocycle during which the GPP and concentrated strength loading take place,\" then speed, then specialised endurance, with competitions as a main way to develop it.\n\n**Long-duration endurance (monocyclic, Fig 6.41).**\n- \"Competition in long duration events requires a long preparatory period (up to 6 months) for creating stable adaptation to preserve the special work-capacity for some 4-5 months during the competitive period\" (PDF p. 396).\n- Stable results come \"if the increase in loading during the preparatory period occurs gradually (a mean monthly increase of 7-14%), if there is not a sharp drop in the volume of loading during the competitive period, and if the portion of loading in the zone of anaerobic energy production is not increased (Gilyazova, 1978)\" (PDF p. 396). See [[claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity]].\n- \"Within a monocycle of periodisation, it provides a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training\": \"The first block contains general conditioning strength work, while the second block emphasizes the development of local muscular endurance,\" carrying work capacity \"right up to the important competitions in the 10th and 11th months\" (PDF p. 396).\n- Fig 6.41, read by eye: phase I about months 1-6, phase II about months 6-11; block 1 over about months 2-3, block 2 over about months 6-7. Aerobic capacity peaks around the start of the competition period and then eases, as the text says (\"maximum aerobic productivity at the beginning of the competition period, and some decrease,\" PDF p. 397).\n\n**Why it works** — Both models rest on the LDTE of concentrated strength blocks and on timing speed and speed-endurance after them. The only cited data are Gilyazova (1978) for the monocyclic loading conditions; the figures are schematics (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999).\n\n**When to apply** — Annual planning for middle-distance (medium) or long-distance (long) endurance athletes who use strength blocks. Not a football model; the medium-duration sequence \"General endurance > Speed > Speed-endurance\" is the transferable idea. Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once — priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 113, Explicit). Inference: both models pick one energy-system emphasis per stage in that spirit.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Long-duration sports are given a monocycle, while the 6.13 aims call for \"at least two large mesocycles... even in sports which traditionally... use monocyclic periodisation\" (PDF p. 389); see [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]] for this internal conflict. The models are for qualified athletes and carry the same Issurin 2016 challenge to concentrated loading as the other 6.14 models ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Fig 6.46's caption says \"A is the block of concentrated strength loading,\" but the figure prints \"GPP\" under A and \"Strength Load\" under B; which wave is the strength block is unclear.\n\n**Example** — The authors give the schematics only. Inference: an 800 m runner competes at 400 m and 1500 m in the first competition stage as control tests, then runs the main 800 m season in months 10-11 after a smaller second block focused on local muscular endurance.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity]], [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks]], [[heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models]], [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":30,"oldLines":7,"newStart":30,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — Both models rest on the LDTE of concentrated strength blocks and on timing speed and speed-endurance after them. The only cited data are Gilyazova (1978) for the monocyclic loading conditions; the figures are schematics (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)."," ","-**When to apply** — Annual planning for middle-distance (medium) or long-distance (long) endurance athletes who use strength blocks. Not a football model; the medium-duration sequence \"General endurance > Speed > Speed-endurance\" is the transferable idea. Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once — priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 113, Explicit). Inference: both models pick one energy-system emphasis per stage in that spirit.","+**When to apply** — Annual planning for middle-distance (medium) or long-distance (long) endurance athletes who use strength blocks. Not a football model; the medium-duration sequence \"General endurance > Speed > Speed-endurance\" is the transferable idea. Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once\" and \"priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 115, Explicit; the two phrases are joined by a dash in the baseline). Inference: both models pick one energy-system emphasis per stage in that spirit."," "," **When NOT to apply** — Long-duration sports are given a monocycle, while the 6.13 aims call for \"at least two large mesocycles... even in sports which traditionally... use monocyclic periodisation\" (PDF p. 389); see [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]] for this internal conflict. The models are for qualified athletes and carry the same Issurin 2016 challenge to concentrated loading as the other 6.14 models ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Fig 6.46's caption says \"A is the block of concentrated strength loading,\" but the figure prints \"GPP\" under A and \"Strength Load\" under B; which wave is the strength block is unclear."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4bbdfc3e-2b92-4e64-a613-0338dab6a605","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"edc938ed-942d-4006-9541-974e90fde60a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffmrojs8FmwnZzm2mmyz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016ZmRCNHAnAxZJyprnnhJtB","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.3\"\ntopic: competition-period volume\ncategory: [in-season, volume, detraining, endurance, monocyclic, maintenance]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# In a Long Monocyclic Competition Period, a Prolonged Cut in Loading Volume Lowers Fitness; Competition Plus Small Special Loads Cannot Maintain It\n\n**What it is** — In the long-duration endurance model (PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385, checked against the page image): \"It has been established that the prolonged reduction in the volume of loading during the competition period (with monocyclic periodisation) leads to re-adaptation at a lower level, which is reflected in the drop of non-specific trainability. Competition and special supplementary loading (with limited volumes) cannot stimulate further development or even maintain special work-capacity. Thus, it is appropriate to periodically increase the volume of competition and special assistance loading (Baranovsky, 1969; Kryazh, 1969; Muzis, 1970; Zhikharevich, 1976).\" The paired condition from Gilyazova (1978) is that stable results need \"not a sharp drop in the volume of loading during the competitive period\" and a gradual preparatory increase (\"a mean monthly increase of 7-14%\") (PDF p. 396).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"re-adaptation at a lower level\": the body adapts down to the reduced load. They give no physiological detail and no data beyond the four citations.\n\n**When to apply** — Long competition periods (the model's is \"some 4-5 months,\" PDF p. 396) in which the athlete would otherwise only compete and do light maintenance: build in periodic increases in special and assistance volume. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \\\"maintain intensity, reduce volume\\\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Also: \"In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete\" (line 181, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' warning is against a *prolonged* volume cut in a long season; the baseline's in-season waving, and the bye-week \"speed and strength top-up\" (line 187, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)), are the kind of periodic volume increase the authors call for. A football season of about 12-15 weeks of games is roughly the length of the model's competition period, though the sport is not.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The statement is made for long-duration endurance sports under monocyclic periodisation; the authors do not extend it to strength or team sports. It does not mean intensity can be dropped. In other models the authors cut strength volume after the second block on purpose (\"It is unnecessary at this time to further increase the volume of strength work,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383), so the rule applies to special and competition loading in endurance sport, not to every quality in every sport.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: a marathoner in a five-month racing season returns every few weeks to a higher-volume week of race-pace and assistance work instead of only racing and easy running between races.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--in-season-team-sport-mini-block-microcycle]], [[claim--ligament-tendon-adaptation-and-detraining-response]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":344,"cache_read_input_tokens":316330,"output_tokens":1765,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":344},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1765,"cache_read_input_tokens":316330,"cache_creation_input_tokens":344,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":344,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_016ZmRCNHAnAxZJyprnnhJtB":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.3\"\ntopic: competition-period volume\ncategory: [in-season, volume, detraining, endurance, monocyclic, maintenance]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# In a Long Monocyclic Competition Period, a Prolonged Cut in Loading Volume Lowers Fitness; Competition Plus Small Special Loads Cannot Maintain It\n\n**What it is** — In the long-duration endurance model (PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385, checked against the page image): \"It has been established that the prolonged reduction in the volume of loading during the competition period (with monocyclic periodisation) leads to re-adaptation at a lower level, which is reflected in the drop of non-specific trainability. Competition and special supplementary loading (with limited volumes) cannot stimulate further development or even maintain special work-capacity. Thus, it is appropriate to periodically increase the volume of competition and special assistance loading (Baranovsky, 1969; Kryazh, 1969; Muzis, 1970; Zhikharevich, 1976).\" The paired condition from Gilyazova (1978) is that stable results need \"not a sharp drop in the volume of loading during the competitive period\" and a gradual preparatory increase (\"a mean monthly increase of 7-14%\") (PDF p. 396).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"re-adaptation at a lower level\": the body adapts down to the reduced load. They give no physiological detail and no data beyond the four citations.\n\n**When to apply** — Long competition periods (the model's is \"some 4-5 months,\" PDF p. 396) in which the athlete would otherwise only compete and do light maintenance: build in periodic increases in special and assistance volume. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \\\"maintain intensity, reduce volume\\\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Also: \"In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete\" (line 181, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' warning is against a *prolonged* volume cut in a long season; the baseline's in-season waving, and the bye-week \"speed and strength top-up\" (line 187, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)), are the kind of periodic volume increase the authors call for. A football season of about 12-15 weeks of games is roughly the length of the model's competition period, though the sport is not.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The statement is made for long-duration endurance sports under monocyclic periodisation; the authors do not extend it to strength or team sports. It does not mean intensity can be dropped. In other models the authors cut strength volume after the second block on purpose (\"It is unnecessary at this time to further increase the volume of strength work,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383), so the rule applies to special and competition loading in endurance sport, not to every quality in every sport.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: a marathoner in a five-month racing season returns every few weeks to a higher-volume week of race-pace and assistance work instead of only racing and easy running between races.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--in-season-team-sport-mini-block-microcycle]], [[claim--ligament-tendon-adaptation-and-detraining-response]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffmqUFaQ6tZ8RkRHBM99","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5a5ad3b5-65c4-4efd-8a24-df1e659bfa62","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:26:30.898Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"5a5ad3b5-65c4-4efd-8a24-df1e659bfa62","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e83b6a4f-63a8-4fe4-afcd-e7857787d75e","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016ZmRCNHAnAxZJyprnnhJtB","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"dd685e8d-59a3-4184-8ec0-4649559ddf18","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:26:31.534Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.3\"\ntopic: competition-period volume\ncategory: [in-season, volume, detraining, endurance, monocyclic, maintenance]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# In a Long Monocyclic Competition Period, a Prolonged Cut in Loading Volume Lowers Fitness; Competition Plus Small Special Loads Cannot Maintain It\n\n**What it is** — In the long-duration endurance model (PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385, checked against the page image): \"It has been established that the prolonged reduction in the volume of loading during the competition period (with monocyclic periodisation) leads to re-adaptation at a lower level, which is reflected in the drop of non-specific trainability. Competition and special supplementary loading (with limited volumes) cannot stimulate further development or even maintain special work-capacity. Thus, it is appropriate to periodically increase the volume of competition and special assistance loading (Baranovsky, 1969; Kryazh, 1969; Muzis, 1970; Zhikharevich, 1976).\" The paired condition from Gilyazova (1978) is that stable results need \"not a sharp drop in the volume of loading during the competitive period\" and a gradual preparatory increase (\"a mean monthly increase of 7-14%\") (PDF p. 396).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"re-adaptation at a lower level\": the body adapts down to the reduced load. They give no physiological detail and no data beyond the four citations.\n\n**When to apply** — Long competition periods (the model's is \"some 4-5 months,\" PDF p. 396) in which the athlete would otherwise only compete and do light maintenance: build in periodic increases in special and assistance volume. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \\\"maintain intensity, reduce volume\\\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Also: \"In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete\" (line 181, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' warning is against a *prolonged* volume cut in a long season; the baseline's in-season waving, and the bye-week \"speed and strength top-up\" (line 187, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)), are the kind of periodic volume increase the authors call for. A football season of about 12-15 weeks of games is roughly the length of the model's competition period, though the sport is not.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The statement is made for long-duration endurance sports under monocyclic periodisation; the authors do not extend it to strength or team sports. It does not mean intensity can be dropped. In other models the authors cut strength volume after the second block on purpose (\"It is unnecessary at this time to further increase the volume of strength work,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383), so the rule applies to special and competition loading in endurance sport, not to every quality in every sport.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: a marathoner in a five-month racing season returns every few weeks to a higher-volume week of race-pace and assistance work instead of only racing and easy running between races.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--in-season-team-sport-mini-block-microcycle]], [[claim--ligament-tendon-adaptation-and-detraining-response]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5a5ad3b5-65c4-4efd-8a24-df1e659bfa62","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
