{"parentUuid":"6576d4e1-8311-4dd1-9d96-bef03f5de33d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"18f95e19-3d7d-4312-a698-c0ed8ed1d357","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LhYE6f4SDsZF4LSmFcsZbc","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"3868a0cf-0509-48af-a406-b1256b492ae5","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:26:59.891Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md","oldString":"In other models the authors cut strength volume after the second block on purpose (\"It is unnecessary","newString":"In the explosive-strength model the second strength block deliberately carries less volume than the first (\"It is unnecessary","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.3\"\ntopic: competition-period volume\ncategory: [in-season, volume, detraining, endurance, monocyclic, maintenance]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# In a Long Monocyclic Competition Period, a Prolonged Cut in Loading Volume Lowers Fitness; Competition Plus Small Special Loads Cannot Maintain It\n\n**What it is** — In the long-duration endurance model (PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385, checked against the page image): \"It has been established that the prolonged reduction in the volume of loading during the competition period (with monocyclic periodisation) leads to re-adaptation at a lower level, which is reflected in the drop of non-specific trainability. Competition and special supplementary loading (with limited volumes) cannot stimulate further development or even maintain special work-capacity. Thus, it is appropriate to periodically increase the volume of competition and special assistance loading (Baranovsky, 1969; Kryazh, 1969; Muzis, 1970; Zhikharevich, 1976).\" The paired condition from Gilyazova (1978) is that stable results need \"not a sharp drop in the volume of loading during the competitive period\" and a gradual preparatory increase (\"a mean monthly increase of 7-14%\") (PDF p. 396).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"re-adaptation at a lower level\": the body adapts down to the reduced load. They give no physiological detail and no data beyond the four citations.\n\n**When to apply** — Long competition periods (the model's is \"some 4-5 months,\" PDF p. 396) in which the athlete would otherwise only compete and do light maintenance: build in periodic increases in special and assistance volume. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \\\"maintain intensity, reduce volume\\\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Also: \"In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete\" (line 181, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' warning is against a *prolonged* volume cut in a long season; the baseline's in-season waving, and the bye-week \"speed and strength top-up\" (line 187, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)), are the kind of periodic volume increase the authors call for. A football season of about 12-15 weeks of games is roughly the length of the model's competition period, though the sport is not.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The statement is made for long-duration endurance sports under monocyclic periodisation; the authors do not extend it to strength or team sports. It does not mean intensity can be dropped. In other models the authors cut strength volume after the second block on purpose (\"It is unnecessary at this time to further increase the volume of strength work,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383), so the rule applies to special and competition loading in endurance sport, not to every quality in every sport.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: a marathoner in a five-month racing season returns every few weeks to a higher-volume week of race-pace and assistance work instead of only racing and easy running between races.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--in-season-team-sport-mini-block-microcycle]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Long competition periods (the model's is \"some 4-5 months,\" PDF p. 396) in which the athlete would otherwise only compete and do light maintenance: build in periodic increases in special and assistance volume. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \\\"maintain intensity, reduce volume\\\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Also: \"In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete\" (line 181, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' warning is against a *prolonged* volume cut in a long season; the baseline's in-season waving, and the bye-week \"speed and strength top-up\" (line 187, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)), are the kind of periodic volume increase the authors call for. A football season of about 12-15 weeks of games is roughly the length of the model's competition period, though the sport is not."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The statement is made for long-duration endurance sports under monocyclic periodisation; the authors do not extend it to strength or team sports. It does not mean intensity can be dropped. In other models the authors cut strength volume after the second block on purpose (\"It is unnecessary at this time to further increase the volume of strength work,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383), so the rule applies to special and competition loading in endurance sport, not to every quality in every sport.","+**When NOT to apply** — The statement is made for long-duration endurance sports under monocyclic periodisation; the authors do not extend it to strength or team sports. It does not mean intensity can be dropped. In the explosive-strength model the second strength block deliberately carries less volume than the first (\"It is unnecessary at this time to further increase the volume of strength work,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383), so the rule applies to special and competition loading in endurance sport, not to every quality in every sport."," "," **Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: a marathoner in a five-month racing season returns every few weeks to a higher-volume week of race-pace and assistance work instead of only racing and easy running between races."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6576d4e1-8311-4dd1-9d96-bef03f5de33d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"3868a0cf-0509-48af-a406-b1256b492ae5","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmuoDhZEMKK7SYStGFe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JAWw2Frami6rWt7cPqi7SA","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.4\"\ntopic: tricyclic annual model\ncategory: [periodization, annual-plan, tri-cyclic, combat-sports, concentrated-loading]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Tri-Cyclic Model for Individual and One-on-One Sports: Three Mesocycles for SPP, Technical Mastery and Competition Readiness, Each With Its Own Strength Block\n\n**What it is** — 6.14.4 (PDF p. 397 / printed p. 386, checked against the page image; Fig 6.42 on PDF p. 398 / printed p. 387, checked against the image): \"The tri-cyclic model is intended primarily for individual and one-on-one sports (Fig 6.42). It comprises three mesocycles, with preparatory and competitive micro-stages. The first stage is oriented chiefly towards special physical preparation; the second, to perfecting technical mastery, and the third, to readiness for the major competitions.\" The third stage \"can be increased by shortening the duration of the second stage,\" and \"In certain cases a bi-cyclic model of training can be employed with this group of sports.\"\n- **Athlete-state model:** maximum aerobic productivity in the second stage, then some decrease; \"Maximum strength rises uniformly, while strength-endurance accelerates more during the third stage. Explosive strength reaches a maximum at the beginning of the second and at the end of the third stages.\"\n- **Three strength blocks:** the first \"to develop maximum and explosive strength; the second, to develop strength-endurance; the third, chiefly to increase explosive strength and create conditions for exploiting the LDTE of the strength loading during the fundamental competition phase.\"\n- **Energy-system pairing:** \"The first and third blocks of strength work are executed in combination with special work in the mixed (aerobic-anaerobic) zone of energy production, while the second is combined with work in the anaerobic (ATP-CP and anaerobic-glycolytic) zone. The fundamental volume of speed and technical-tactical preparation is executed against a background of the LDTE elicited by the blocks of strength work.\"\n- **Fig 6.42, read by eye:** phases I about months 1-4, II about months 5-8, III about months 9-11. Block 1 about months 1-3 (tallest), block 2 about months 5-6, block 3 about months 9-10 (shortest). At the right edge the upper-panel curves end in the order Explosive strength, Anaerobic capacity, Aerobic capacity, Strength-endurance, with Maximum strength lowest and rising nearly linearly. Shorter mesocycles of this kind \"should not shift achieving the objective of optimal enhancement of the current adaptation reserves (CAR) to the next stage\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give the structure, not a mechanism beyond the LDTE of each strength block. No data are shown; the figure is credited to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999).\n\n**When to apply** — Individual and combat sports with three main competition periods, such as the boxing and swimming examples the authors cite for tri-cyclic forms (PDF p. 387 / printed p. 376), and team sports where \"tri-cyclic periodisation may have to be used, as dictated by the competition calendar\" (PDF p. 387). Eric's baseline: \"The head coach's system of play and roster identity (e.g., a \\\"home run hitters\\\" lineup vs. a \\\"steal bases\\\" lineup) is treated as a direct input into whether a team's programming leans toward maximal strength or toward speed/power emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 188, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the model's block contents (maximum, endurance, explosive) are the kind of lever that input would set.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin for combat sports**: the library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports improved some fitness measures without reported effects on practice or competitive performance, and that Issurin judged the evidence insufficient for broad use in \"high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6; [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). This entry keeps `supported` because the tri-cyclic layout itself is not what Issurin challenges; the challenge is to the concentrated blocks inside it, recorded on [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]]. The model assumes three distinct competition peaks; it is not a template for a weekly-competition season.\n\n**Example** — The authors give the schematic only. Inference: a wrestler with a winter national championship, a spring continental event and a late-summer world championship runs a maximum-strength block in autumn, a strength-endurance block in late winter with anaerobic work, and a short explosive block before the summer peak.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1454,"cache_read_input_tokens":318721,"output_tokens":2382,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":91},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1454},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2382,"cache_read_input_tokens":318721,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1454,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1454,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01JAWw2Frami6rWt7cPqi7SA":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.4\"\ntopic: tricyclic annual model\ncategory: [periodization, annual-plan, tri-cyclic, combat-sports, concentrated-loading]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Tri-Cyclic Model for Individual and One-on-One Sports: Three Mesocycles for SPP, Technical Mastery and Competition Readiness, Each With Its Own Strength Block\n\n**What it is** — 6.14.4 (PDF p. 397 / printed p. 386, checked against the page image; Fig 6.42 on PDF p. 398 / printed p. 387, checked against the image): \"The tri-cyclic model is intended primarily for individual and one-on-one sports (Fig 6.42). It comprises three mesocycles, with preparatory and competitive micro-stages. The first stage is oriented chiefly towards special physical preparation; the second, to perfecting technical mastery, and the third, to readiness for the major competitions.\" The third stage \"can be increased by shortening the duration of the second stage,\" and \"In certain cases a bi-cyclic model of training can be employed with this group of sports.\"\n- **Athlete-state model:** maximum aerobic productivity in the second stage, then some decrease; \"Maximum strength rises uniformly, while strength-endurance accelerates more during the third stage. Explosive strength reaches a maximum at the beginning of the second and at the end of the third stages.\"\n- **Three strength blocks:** the first \"to develop maximum and explosive strength; the second, to develop strength-endurance; the third, chiefly to increase explosive strength and create conditions for exploiting the LDTE of the strength loading during the fundamental competition phase.\"\n- **Energy-system pairing:** \"The first and third blocks of strength work are executed in combination with special work in the mixed (aerobic-anaerobic) zone of energy production, while the second is combined with work in the anaerobic (ATP-CP and anaerobic-glycolytic) zone. The fundamental volume of speed and technical-tactical preparation is executed against a background of the LDTE elicited by the blocks of strength work.\"\n- **Fig 6.42, read by eye:** phases I about months 1-4, II about months 5-8, III about months 9-11. Block 1 about months 1-3 (tallest), block 2 about months 5-6, block 3 about months 9-10 (shortest). At the right edge the upper-panel curves end in the order Explosive strength, Anaerobic capacity, Aerobic capacity, Strength-endurance, with Maximum strength lowest and rising nearly linearly. Shorter mesocycles of this kind \"should not shift achieving the objective of optimal enhancement of the current adaptation reserves (CAR) to the next stage\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give the structure, not a mechanism beyond the LDTE of each strength block. No data are shown; the figure is credited to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999).\n\n**When to apply** — Individual and combat sports with three main competition periods, such as the boxing and swimming examples the authors cite for tri-cyclic forms (PDF p. 387 / printed p. 376), and team sports where \"tri-cyclic periodisation may have to be used, as dictated by the competition calendar\" (PDF p. 387). Eric's baseline: \"The head coach's system of play and roster identity (e.g., a \\\"home run hitters\\\" lineup vs. a \\\"steal bases\\\" lineup) is treated as a direct input into whether a team's programming leans toward maximal strength or toward speed/power emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 188, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the model's block contents (maximum, endurance, explosive) are the kind of lever that input would set.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin for combat sports**: the library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports improved some fitness measures without reported effects on practice or competitive performance, and that Issurin judged the evidence insufficient for broad use in \"high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6; [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). This entry keeps `supported` because the tri-cyclic layout itself is not what Issurin challenges; the challenge is to the concentrated blocks inside it, recorded on [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]]. The model assumes three distinct competition peaks; it is not a template for a weekly-competition season.\n\n**Example** — The authors give the schematic only. Inference: a wrestler with a winter national championship, a spring continental event and a late-summer world championship runs a maximum-strength block in autumn, a strength-endurance block in late winter with anaerobic work, and a short explosive block before the summer peak.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffmuTdV1LbGVCoaZYW98","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"7eef7646-fcc3-4e5c-b2bb-7725514c5b38","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:27:15.269Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"7eef7646-fcc3-4e5c-b2bb-7725514c5b38","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"18f95e19-3d7d-4312-a698-c0ed8ed1d357","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JAWw2Frami6rWt7cPqi7SA","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"71352d42-4c23-474b-b8eb-5e04a435102f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:27:15.396Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.4\"\ntopic: tricyclic annual model\ncategory: [periodization, annual-plan, tri-cyclic, combat-sports, concentrated-loading]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Tri-Cyclic Model for Individual and One-on-One Sports: Three Mesocycles for SPP, Technical Mastery and Competition Readiness, Each With Its Own Strength Block\n\n**What it is** — 6.14.4 (PDF p. 397 / printed p. 386, checked against the page image; Fig 6.42 on PDF p. 398 / printed p. 387, checked against the image): \"The tri-cyclic model is intended primarily for individual and one-on-one sports (Fig 6.42). It comprises three mesocycles, with preparatory and competitive micro-stages. The first stage is oriented chiefly towards special physical preparation; the second, to perfecting technical mastery, and the third, to readiness for the major competitions.\" The third stage \"can be increased by shortening the duration of the second stage,\" and \"In certain cases a bi-cyclic model of training can be employed with this group of sports.\"\n- **Athlete-state model:** maximum aerobic productivity in the second stage, then some decrease; \"Maximum strength rises uniformly, while strength-endurance accelerates more during the third stage. Explosive strength reaches a maximum at the beginning of the second and at the end of the third stages.\"\n- **Three strength blocks:** the first \"to develop maximum and explosive strength; the second, to develop strength-endurance; the third, chiefly to increase explosive strength and create conditions for exploiting the LDTE of the strength loading during the fundamental competition phase.\"\n- **Energy-system pairing:** \"The first and third blocks of strength work are executed in combination with special work in the mixed (aerobic-anaerobic) zone of energy production, while the second is combined with work in the anaerobic (ATP-CP and anaerobic-glycolytic) zone. The fundamental volume of speed and technical-tactical preparation is executed against a background of the LDTE elicited by the blocks of strength work.\"\n- **Fig 6.42, read by eye:** phases I about months 1-4, II about months 5-8, III about months 9-11. Block 1 about months 1-3 (tallest), block 2 about months 5-6, block 3 about months 9-10 (shortest). At the right edge the upper-panel curves end in the order Explosive strength, Anaerobic capacity, Aerobic capacity, Strength-endurance, with Maximum strength lowest and rising nearly linearly. Shorter mesocycles of this kind \"should not shift achieving the objective of optimal enhancement of the current adaptation reserves (CAR) to the next stage\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give the structure, not a mechanism beyond the LDTE of each strength block. No data are shown; the figure is credited to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999).\n\n**When to apply** — Individual and combat sports with three main competition periods, such as the boxing and swimming examples the authors cite for tri-cyclic forms (PDF p. 387 / printed p. 376), and team sports where \"tri-cyclic periodisation may have to be used, as dictated by the competition calendar\" (PDF p. 387). Eric's baseline: \"The head coach's system of play and roster identity (e.g., a \\\"home run hitters\\\" lineup vs. a \\\"steal bases\\\" lineup) is treated as a direct input into whether a team's programming leans toward maximal strength or toward speed/power emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 188, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the model's block contents (maximum, endurance, explosive) are the kind of lever that input would set.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin for combat sports**: the library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports improved some fitness measures without reported effects on practice or competitive performance, and that Issurin judged the evidence insufficient for broad use in \"high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6; [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). This entry keeps `supported` because the tri-cyclic layout itself is not what Issurin challenges; the challenge is to the concentrated blocks inside it, recorded on [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]]. The model assumes three distinct competition peaks; it is not a template for a weekly-competition season.\n\n**Example** — The authors give the schematic only. Inference: a wrestler with a winter national championship, a spring continental event and a late-summer world championship runs a maximum-strength block in autumn, a strength-endurance block in late winter with anaerobic work, and a short explosive block before the summer peak.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7eef7646-fcc3-4e5c-b2bb-7725514c5b38","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
