for combat sports\\*\\*: the library/**Issurin 2016 challenge (combat sports).** The library/; s/At the right edge the upper-panel curves end in the order/The labels at the right edge of the upper panel read, top to bottom,/' $f; grep -c \"Issurin 2016 challenge\" $f; grep -o \"labels at the right edge[^.]*\\.\" $f","description":"Adjust tricyclic wording"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffmwEsq68hNvLsPBiC7h","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d193b562-f86d-44df-9019-a347de9b5d1e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:27:30.983Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d193b562-f86d-44df-9019-a347de9b5d1e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"18f95e19-3d7d-4312-a698-c0ed8ed1d357","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UDimGSrmdN9FMwns7HXb4x","type":"tool_result","content":"1\nlabels at the right edge of the upper panel read, top to bottom, Explosive strength, Anaerobic capacity, Aerobic capacity, Strength-endurance, with Maximum strength lowest and rising nearly linearly.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"4483aa9a-7f91-420f-932e-9faffeda5704","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:27:31.499Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"1\nlabels at the right edge of the upper panel read, top to bottom, Explosive strength, Anaerobic capacity, Aerobic capacity, Strength-endurance, with Maximum strength lowest and rising nearly linearly.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":17,"oldLines":13,"newStart":17,"newLines":13,"lines":[" - **Athlete-state model:** maximum aerobic productivity in the second stage, then some decrease; \"Maximum strength rises uniformly, while strength-endurance accelerates more during the third stage. Explosive strength reaches a maximum at the beginning of the second and at the end of the third stages.\""," - **Three strength blocks:** the first \"to develop maximum and explosive strength; the second, to develop strength-endurance; the third, chiefly to increase explosive strength and create conditions for exploiting the LDTE of the strength loading during the fundamental competition phase.\""," - **Energy-system pairing:** \"The first and third blocks of strength work are executed in combination with special work in the mixed (aerobic-anaerobic) zone of energy production, while the second is combined with work in the anaerobic (ATP-CP and anaerobic-glycolytic) zone. The fundamental volume of speed and technical-tactical preparation is executed against a background of the LDTE elicited by the blocks of strength work.\"","-- **Fig 6.42, read by eye:** phases I about months 1-4, II about months 5-8, III about months 9-11. Block 1 about months 1-3 (tallest), block 2 about months 5-6, block 3 about months 9-10 (shortest). At the right edge the upper-panel curves end in the order Explosive strength, Anaerobic capacity, Aerobic capacity, Strength-endurance, with Maximum strength lowest and rising nearly linearly. Shorter mesocycles of this kind \"should not shift achieving the objective of optimal enhancement of the current adaptation reserves (CAR) to the next stage\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389).","+- **Fig 6.42, read by eye:** phases I about months 1-4, II about months 5-8, III about months 9-11. Block 1 about months 1-3 (tallest), block 2 about months 5-6, block 3 about months 9-10 (shortest). The labels at the right edge of the upper panel read, top to bottom, Explosive strength, Anaerobic capacity, Aerobic capacity, Strength-endurance, with Maximum strength lowest and rising nearly linearly. Shorter mesocycles of this kind \"should not shift achieving the objective of optimal enhancement of the current adaptation reserves (CAR) to the next stage\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389)."," "," **Why it works** — The authors give the structure, not a mechanism beyond the LDTE of each strength block. No data are shown; the figure is credited to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999)."," "," **When to apply** — Individual and combat sports with three main competition periods, such as the boxing and swimming examples the authors cite for tri-cyclic forms (PDF p. 387 / printed p. 376), and team sports where \"tri-cyclic periodisation may have to be used, as dictated by the competition calendar\" (PDF p. 387). Eric's baseline: \"The head coach's system of play and roster identity (e.g., a \\\"home run hitters\\\" lineup vs. a \\\"steal bases\\\" lineup) is treated as a direct input into whether a team's programming leans toward maximal strength or toward speed/power emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 188, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the model's block contents (maximum, endurance, explosive) are the kind of lever that input would set."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin for combat sports**: the library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports improved some fitness measures without reported effects on practice or competitive performance, and that Issurin judged the evidence insufficient for broad use in \"high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6; [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). This entry keeps `supported` because the tri-cyclic layout itself is not what Issurin challenges; the challenge is to the concentrated blocks inside it, recorded on [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]]. The model assumes three distinct competition peaks; it is not a template for a weekly-competition season.","+**When NOT to apply** — **Issurin 2016 challenge (combat sports).** The library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports improved some fitness measures without reported effects on practice or competitive performance, and that Issurin judged the evidence insufficient for broad use in \"high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6; [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). This entry keeps `supported` because the tri-cyclic layout itself is not what Issurin challenges; the challenge is to the concentrated blocks inside it, recorded on [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]]. The model assumes three distinct competition peaks; it is not a template for a weekly-competition season."," "," **Example** — The authors give the schematic only. Inference: a wrestler with a winter national championship, a spring continental event and a late-summer world championship runs a maximum-strength block in autumn, a strength-endurance block in late winter with anaerobic work, and a short explosive block before the summer peak."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d193b562-f86d-44df-9019-a347de9b5d1e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
