{"parentUuid":"72ee9cfd-b0a1-42bb-83ea-e24585e010b0","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14676507 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"803f0b41-da76-4781-8f90-df59e31e8ed4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:27:31.516Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14676507 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"803f0b41-da76-4781-8f90-df59e31e8ed4","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmxmMuoUotxSae2HeGH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":923,"cache_read_input_tokens":322842,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":923,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":468,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffmxSA1MUnkrYivPjjsv","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"aeb2782a-63b7-4130-ad77-17d3649eed41","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:27:38.067Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"aeb2782a-63b7-4130-ad77-17d3649eed41","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmxmMuoUotxSae2HeGH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Az96wmf9uMWKCMNaj4sWAp","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.5\"\ntopic: mesocycle boundaries and calendar\ncategory: [annual-plan, competition-calendar, mesocycle, periodization, recovery]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Set Mesocycle Boundaries Only by the Main Competition Dates, Treat the First Mesocycle as the Foundation for the Second, and Recuperate After Each Competitive Stage\n\n**What it is** — The authors' four practical principles for when the calendar and adaptation pull against each other (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389, checked against the page image). The conflict: \"the dates of the main competitions may change radically, while the dates of traditional competitions remain the same. A worse situation arises if the competition season is extended and the length of the preparatory stages is shortened.\"\n1. \"Organisation of training should provide favourable conditions for promoting functional adaptation of the body within the constraints of the competition calendar. The calendar should be co-ordinated with the periods and duration of the mesocycles.\"\n2. \"The optimal period for enhancing the current adaptation reserves is about 20 weeks for the most efficient loading of highly qualified athletes.\" It \"can be increased or decreased over a small range, which requires an appropriate increase or decrease in the concentration of loading. It is important in this case not to exceed the optimal time limit, since excessive intensification of training can disrupt adaptation.\"\n3. \"To determine the boundaries of the mesocycles, one should be guided only by the dates of the main competitions. This rule should not be broken for any reason, including the desire to demonstrate impressive results at the beginning of the season.\"\n4. \"The loading of the preparatory phase is more specialised and intense during the second mesocycle than the first. Therefore, the first mesocycle should always be considered as the foundation for the second. The interests of intermediate competitions should not alter this objective.\"\n\nThen, in italics in the original: \"*one should be aware of the detrimental effect that competitive loading has on the athlete's state*, since it leads to serious exhaustion of nervous energy. Therefore, during the transition to the next mesocycle, it is necessary to include a recuperation period, the duration of which is determined individually, depending on the difficulty of the competitive stage\" (PDF p. 400).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason for rules 2 and 3 is the \"current adaptation reserves\": there is an optimal time over which they can be raised, and stretching or squeezing it changes how concentrated the loading must be. They give no study for the 20-week figure on this page (\"It has already been mentioned,\" referring back to earlier sections outside this batch). The recuperation rule rests on the \"serious exhaustion of nervous energy\" from competition, without data.\n\n**When to apply** — Drawing an annual plan: place the main competitions first, cut the year into mesocycles from those dates, and do not move the boundaries to chase early-season results or to serve minor meets. Eric's baseline: \"In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: under rule 3, November and the postseason would set the boundary of the final mesocycle, and early-season games would be treated as the authors' \"intermediate competitions,\" which \"should not alter this objective.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Rule 3's \"should not be broken for any reason\" is the authors' strongest wording in the section, but it assumes one or two main competitions; in a sport with a weekly game, every game counts, and the rule does not say how to weight them. The 20-week figure is for \"highly qualified athletes.\" The recuperation period has no stated length. Issurin's competing structure uses stages of \"about two months\" ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]]); see [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example of the problem is a season that is \"extended\" while \"the length of the preparatory stages is shortened\" (PDF p. 400). Inference: a jumper whose national championship moves three weeks earlier shortens the second mesocycle by concentrating its loading more, rather than shifting the boundary to keep a full block before an early-season invitational.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":923,"cache_read_input_tokens":322842,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":923,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Az96wmf9uMWKCMNaj4sWAp":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.5\"\ntopic: mesocycle boundaries and calendar\ncategory: [annual-plan, competition-calendar, mesocycle, periodization, recovery]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Set Mesocycle Boundaries Only by the Main Competition Dates, Treat the First Mesocycle as the Foundation for the Second, and Recuperate After Each Competitive Stage\n\n**What it is** — The authors' four practical principles for when the calendar and adaptation pull against each other (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389, checked against the page image). The conflict: \"the dates of the main competitions may change radically, while the dates of traditional competitions remain the same. A worse situation arises if the competition season is extended and the length of the preparatory stages is shortened.\"\n1. \"Organisation of training should provide favourable conditions for promoting functional adaptation of the body within the constraints of the competition calendar. The calendar should be co-ordinated with the periods and duration of the mesocycles.\"\n2. \"The optimal period for enhancing the current adaptation reserves is about 20 weeks for the most efficient loading of highly qualified athletes.\" It \"can be increased or decreased over a small range, which requires an appropriate increase or decrease in the concentration of loading. It is important in this case not to exceed the optimal time limit, since excessive intensification of training can disrupt adaptation.\"\n3. \"To determine the boundaries of the mesocycles, one should be guided only by the dates of the main competitions. This rule should not be broken for any reason, including the desire to demonstrate impressive results at the beginning of the season.\"\n4. \"The loading of the preparatory phase is more specialised and intense during the second mesocycle than the first. Therefore, the first mesocycle should always be considered as the foundation for the second. The interests of intermediate competitions should not alter this objective.\"\n\nThen, in italics in the original: \"*one should be aware of the detrimental effect that competitive loading has on the athlete's state*, since it leads to serious exhaustion of nervous energy. Therefore, during the transition to the next mesocycle, it is necessary to include a recuperation period, the duration of which is determined individually, depending on the difficulty of the competitive stage\" (PDF p. 400).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason for rules 2 and 3 is the \"current adaptation reserves\": there is an optimal time over which they can be raised, and stretching or squeezing it changes how concentrated the loading must be. They give no study for the 20-week figure on this page (\"It has already been mentioned,\" referring back to earlier sections outside this batch). The recuperation rule rests on the \"serious exhaustion of nervous energy\" from competition, without data.\n\n**When to apply** — Drawing an annual plan: place the main competitions first, cut the year into mesocycles from those dates, and do not move the boundaries to chase early-season results or to serve minor meets. Eric's baseline: \"In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: under rule 3, November and the postseason would set the boundary of the final mesocycle, and early-season games would be treated as the authors' \"intermediate competitions,\" which \"should not alter this objective.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Rule 3's \"should not be broken for any reason\" is the authors' strongest wording in the section, but it assumes one or two main competitions; in a sport with a weekly game, every game counts, and the rule does not say how to weight them. The 20-week figure is for \"highly qualified athletes.\" The recuperation period has no stated length. Issurin's competing structure uses stages of \"about two months\" ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]]); see [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example of the problem is a season that is \"extended\" while \"the length of the preparatory stages is shortened\" (PDF p. 400). Inference: a jumper whose national championship moves three weeks earlier shortens the second mesocycle by concentrating its loading more, rather than shifting the boundary to keep a full block before an early-season invitational.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffmxSA1MUnkrYivPjjsv","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"70d4185d-e019-4afb-ae24-d7e056dfa73d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:27:52.456Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"70d4185d-e019-4afb-ae24-d7e056dfa73d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"18f95e19-3d7d-4312-a698-c0ed8ed1d357","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Az96wmf9uMWKCMNaj4sWAp","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"1ecc2131-ae9b-41b1-8ddf-939f157eb681","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:27:52.892Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.5\"\ntopic: mesocycle boundaries and calendar\ncategory: [annual-plan, competition-calendar, mesocycle, periodization, recovery]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Set Mesocycle Boundaries Only by the Main Competition Dates, Treat the First Mesocycle as the Foundation for the Second, and Recuperate After Each Competitive Stage\n\n**What it is** — The authors' four practical principles for when the calendar and adaptation pull against each other (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389, checked against the page image). The conflict: \"the dates of the main competitions may change radically, while the dates of traditional competitions remain the same. A worse situation arises if the competition season is extended and the length of the preparatory stages is shortened.\"\n1. \"Organisation of training should provide favourable conditions for promoting functional adaptation of the body within the constraints of the competition calendar. The calendar should be co-ordinated with the periods and duration of the mesocycles.\"\n2. \"The optimal period for enhancing the current adaptation reserves is about 20 weeks for the most efficient loading of highly qualified athletes.\" It \"can be increased or decreased over a small range, which requires an appropriate increase or decrease in the concentration of loading. It is important in this case not to exceed the optimal time limit, since excessive intensification of training can disrupt adaptation.\"\n3. \"To determine the boundaries of the mesocycles, one should be guided only by the dates of the main competitions. This rule should not be broken for any reason, including the desire to demonstrate impressive results at the beginning of the season.\"\n4. \"The loading of the preparatory phase is more specialised and intense during the second mesocycle than the first. Therefore, the first mesocycle should always be considered as the foundation for the second. The interests of intermediate competitions should not alter this objective.\"\n\nThen, in italics in the original: \"*one should be aware of the detrimental effect that competitive loading has on the athlete's state*, since it leads to serious exhaustion of nervous energy. Therefore, during the transition to the next mesocycle, it is necessary to include a recuperation period, the duration of which is determined individually, depending on the difficulty of the competitive stage\" (PDF p. 400).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason for rules 2 and 3 is the \"current adaptation reserves\": there is an optimal time over which they can be raised, and stretching or squeezing it changes how concentrated the loading must be. They give no study for the 20-week figure on this page (\"It has already been mentioned,\" referring back to earlier sections outside this batch). The recuperation rule rests on the \"serious exhaustion of nervous energy\" from competition, without data.\n\n**When to apply** — Drawing an annual plan: place the main competitions first, cut the year into mesocycles from those dates, and do not move the boundaries to chase early-season results or to serve minor meets. Eric's baseline: \"In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: under rule 3, November and the postseason would set the boundary of the final mesocycle, and early-season games would be treated as the authors' \"intermediate competitions,\" which \"should not alter this objective.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Rule 3's \"should not be broken for any reason\" is the authors' strongest wording in the section, but it assumes one or two main competitions; in a sport with a weekly game, every game counts, and the rule does not say how to weight them. The 20-week figure is for \"highly qualified athletes.\" The recuperation period has no stated length. Issurin's competing structure uses stages of \"about two months\" ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]]); see [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example of the problem is a season that is \"extended\" while \"the length of the preparatory stages is shortened\" (PDF p. 400). Inference: a jumper whose national championship moves three weeks earlier shortens the second mesocycle by concentrating its loading more, rather than shifting the boundary to keep a full block before an early-season invitational.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"70d4185d-e019-4afb-ae24-d7e056dfa73d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1ecc2131-ae9b-41b1-8ddf-939f157eb681","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmxmMuoUotxSae2HeGH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LcWwHwg2k4HrmrrQDr58GU","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.15\"\ntopic: annual programming sequence\ncategory: [annual-plan, programming, periodization, planning-process, testing]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Verkhoshansky's Eight-Step Sequence for Programming the Annual Cycle, From Target Results Down to the Microcycles\n\n**What it is** — The authors recommend \"the following sequence of logical operations, where each calculation is made by taking into account the calculation made in the preceding operation (Verkhoshansky, 1985)\" (PDF p. 401 / printed p. 390, checked against the page image). The eight steps, with their key wording:\n1. **Determine the progress in results and the dates.** \"The calculations rely on a predictive model of the dynamics of the sport results relative to the competition calendar,\" based on \"a multi-faceted objective assessment of the athlete's potential\" and what is \"realistically feasible in the current year at his level of mastery.\"\n2. **Determine the needed changes in special physical preparedness and technical-tactical proficiency,** expressed as \"specific goals, relative to the functional indicators and the characteristics of technical proficiency which must be achieved in the important competitions.\" \"Subjective ratings of perceived effort (RPE) can be very useful in offering further guidance.\"\n3. **Build quantitative models of the fitness state across the year** \"so that these indicators peak in the main competitions.\"\n4. **Select the training means and methods** by \"assessing the training potential of the means and methods.\"\n5. **Calculate the general volume of loading for all means,** based on \"the accumulated effect of the loading in all preceding stages.\"\n6. **Divide the year into mesocycles,** using \"the competition calendar and the dates necessary for optimal increase of the body's current adaptation reserves.\"\n7. **Calculate the distribution of loading over the year,** expressed as \"the quantitative dynamics of the loading imposed by the fundamental training means.\"\n8. **Organise loading over the mesocycle,** as \"a detailed training programme giving the specific distribution of loading during each of the microcycles,\" based on \"the characteristics of the LDTE of concentrated strength loading.\" \"Provision should always be made available for introducing contingency changes in programmes, necessitated by unforeseen incidents such as injuries, changes in competitive timetables and personal disasters.\"\n\nThe authors frame the difficulty first: \"Its complexity, however, lies not so much in the quantity of calculations as in the insufficient basis for choosing and quantitatively determining the main operations. Therefore in practice, even experienced coaches need to make tentative computations which may have a low probability of producing a correct prognosis\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The sequence is a top-down dependency chain: each step uses the one before. The authors give no evidence that following it improves results; it is a planning procedure.\n\n**When to apply** — Building or auditing an annual plan. Compare it with two other procedures in the library: Issurin's nine steps start from the competition calendar and end with obligatory correction after review ([[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence]]); Evans's ten steps start with a sport and position profile ([[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]]). Verkhoshansky's starts from the predicted result and the fitness changes needed to reach it, and only divides the year into mesocycles at step 6. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: step 8's contingency provision is the authors' version of that flexibility.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors concede the operations rest on an \"insufficient basis\" and may have \"a low probability of producing a correct prognosis\" (PDF p. 400). Step 1 assumes a predictive model of performance exists; in team sports there is no single result to predict. The procedure is written for individual athletes and their competitions.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked plan in 6.15. Inference: for a triple jumper, step 1 sets a target of a 40 cm improvement at the national championship in month 10, step 2 converts it to target values for the standing triple jump and approach speed, and steps 6-8 then divide the year into two mesocycles and write the weekly loading.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[framework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training]], [[principle--programme-objective-approach-quantified-goals-dictate-training]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":923,"cache_read_input_tokens":322842,"output_tokens":3665,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":55},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":923},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3665,"cache_read_input_tokens":322842,"cache_creation_input_tokens":923,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":923,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01LcWwHwg2k4HrmrrQDr58GU":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.15\"\ntopic: annual programming sequence\ncategory: [annual-plan, programming, periodization, planning-process, testing]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Verkhoshansky's Eight-Step Sequence for Programming the Annual Cycle, From Target Results Down to the Microcycles\n\n**What it is** — The authors recommend \"the following sequence of logical operations, where each calculation is made by taking into account the calculation made in the preceding operation (Verkhoshansky, 1985)\" (PDF p. 401 / printed p. 390, checked against the page image). The eight steps, with their key wording:\n1. **Determine the progress in results and the dates.** \"The calculations rely on a predictive model of the dynamics of the sport results relative to the competition calendar,\" based on \"a multi-faceted objective assessment of the athlete's potential\" and what is \"realistically feasible in the current year at his level of mastery.\"\n2. **Determine the needed changes in special physical preparedness and technical-tactical proficiency,** expressed as \"specific goals, relative to the functional indicators and the characteristics of technical proficiency which must be achieved in the important competitions.\" \"Subjective ratings of perceived effort (RPE) can be very useful in offering further guidance.\"\n3. **Build quantitative models of the fitness state across the year** \"so that these indicators peak in the main competitions.\"\n4. **Select the training means and methods** by \"assessing the training potential of the means and methods.\"\n5. **Calculate the general volume of loading for all means,** based on \"the accumulated effect of the loading in all preceding stages.\"\n6. **Divide the year into mesocycles,** using \"the competition calendar and the dates necessary for optimal increase of the body's current adaptation reserves.\"\n7. **Calculate the distribution of loading over the year,** expressed as \"the quantitative dynamics of the loading imposed by the fundamental training means.\"\n8. **Organise loading over the mesocycle,** as \"a detailed training programme giving the specific distribution of loading during each of the microcycles,\" based on \"the characteristics of the LDTE of concentrated strength loading.\" \"Provision should always be made available for introducing contingency changes in programmes, necessitated by unforeseen incidents such as injuries, changes in competitive timetables and personal disasters.\"\n\nThe authors frame the difficulty first: \"Its complexity, however, lies not so much in the quantity of calculations as in the insufficient basis for choosing and quantitatively determining the main operations. Therefore in practice, even experienced coaches need to make tentative computations which may have a low probability of producing a correct prognosis\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The sequence is a top-down dependency chain: each step uses the one before. The authors give no evidence that following it improves results; it is a planning procedure.\n\n**When to apply** — Building or auditing an annual plan. Compare it with two other procedures in the library: Issurin's nine steps start from the competition calendar and end with obligatory correction after review ([[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence]]); Evans's ten steps start with a sport and position profile ([[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]]). Verkhoshansky's starts from the predicted result and the fitness changes needed to reach it, and only divides the year into mesocycles at step 6. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: step 8's contingency provision is the authors' version of that flexibility.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors concede the operations rest on an \"insufficient basis\" and may have \"a low probability of producing a correct prognosis\" (PDF p. 400). Step 1 assumes a predictive model of performance exists; in team sports there is no single result to predict. The procedure is written for individual athletes and their competitions.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked plan in 6.15. Inference: for a triple jumper, step 1 sets a target of a 40 cm improvement at the national championship in month 10, step 2 converts it to target values for the standing triple jump and approach speed, and steps 6-8 then divide the year into two mesocycles and write the weekly loading.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[framework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training]], [[principle--programme-objective-approach-quantified-goals-dictate-training]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffmxSA1MUnkrYivPjjsv","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"9e58c92e-f666-4eaf-8d29-1470cd739d08","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:28:07.765Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"9e58c92e-f666-4eaf-8d29-1470cd739d08","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"18f95e19-3d7d-4312-a698-c0ed8ed1d357","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LcWwHwg2k4HrmrrQDr58GU","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"5f78ea68-79ae-4857-8ae1-4b4020356e32","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:28:08.322Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.15\"\ntopic: annual programming sequence\ncategory: [annual-plan, programming, periodization, planning-process, testing]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Verkhoshansky's Eight-Step Sequence for Programming the Annual Cycle, From Target Results Down to the Microcycles\n\n**What it is** — The authors recommend \"the following sequence of logical operations, where each calculation is made by taking into account the calculation made in the preceding operation (Verkhoshansky, 1985)\" (PDF p. 401 / printed p. 390, checked against the page image). The eight steps, with their key wording:\n1. **Determine the progress in results and the dates.** \"The calculations rely on a predictive model of the dynamics of the sport results relative to the competition calendar,\" based on \"a multi-faceted objective assessment of the athlete's potential\" and what is \"realistically feasible in the current year at his level of mastery.\"\n2. **Determine the needed changes in special physical preparedness and technical-tactical proficiency,** expressed as \"specific goals, relative to the functional indicators and the characteristics of technical proficiency which must be achieved in the important competitions.\" \"Subjective ratings of perceived effort (RPE) can be very useful in offering further guidance.\"\n3. **Build quantitative models of the fitness state across the year** \"so that these indicators peak in the main competitions.\"\n4. **Select the training means and methods** by \"assessing the training potential of the means and methods.\"\n5. **Calculate the general volume of loading for all means,** based on \"the accumulated effect of the loading in all preceding stages.\"\n6. **Divide the year into mesocycles,** using \"the competition calendar and the dates necessary for optimal increase of the body's current adaptation reserves.\"\n7. **Calculate the distribution of loading over the year,** expressed as \"the quantitative dynamics of the loading imposed by the fundamental training means.\"\n8. **Organise loading over the mesocycle,** as \"a detailed training programme giving the specific distribution of loading during each of the microcycles,\" based on \"the characteristics of the LDTE of concentrated strength loading.\" \"Provision should always be made available for introducing contingency changes in programmes, necessitated by unforeseen incidents such as injuries, changes in competitive timetables and personal disasters.\"\n\nThe authors frame the difficulty first: \"Its complexity, however, lies not so much in the quantity of calculations as in the insufficient basis for choosing and quantitatively determining the main operations. Therefore in practice, even experienced coaches need to make tentative computations which may have a low probability of producing a correct prognosis\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The sequence is a top-down dependency chain: each step uses the one before. The authors give no evidence that following it improves results; it is a planning procedure.\n\n**When to apply** — Building or auditing an annual plan. Compare it with two other procedures in the library: Issurin's nine steps start from the competition calendar and end with obligatory correction after review ([[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence]]); Evans's ten steps start with a sport and position profile ([[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]]). Verkhoshansky's starts from the predicted result and the fitness changes needed to reach it, and only divides the year into mesocycles at step 6. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: step 8's contingency provision is the authors' version of that flexibility.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors concede the operations rest on an \"insufficient basis\" and may have \"a low probability of producing a correct prognosis\" (PDF p. 400). Step 1 assumes a predictive model of performance exists; in team sports there is no single result to predict. The procedure is written for individual athletes and their competitions.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked plan in 6.15. Inference: for a triple jumper, step 1 sets a target of a 40 cm improvement at the national championship in month 10, step 2 converts it to target values for the standing triple jump and approach speed, and steps 6-8 then divide the year into two mesocycles and write the weekly loading.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[framework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training]], [[principle--programme-objective-approach-quantified-goals-dictate-training]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9e58c92e-f666-4eaf-8d29-1470cd739d08","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
