{"parentUuid":"d39ff163-2caf-49dc-9bb0-f6652f030465","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffn1UvnAZJYrhLCey9gh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3935,"cache_read_input_tokens":323765,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3935,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":319,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffn19TkwwbW38ZEDvCgj","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"e115481f-5ae6-4f1f-bba7-526149430abd","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:28:15.068Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"e115481f-5ae6-4f1f-bba7-526149430abd","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffn1UvnAZJYrhLCey9gh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018sPZt96w8izHEmFvCXTBJo","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--test-monthly-under-constant-conditions-and-compare-with-targets.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.16\"\ntopic: managing training by testing\ncategory: [monitoring, testing, programme-control, feedback, management]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Manage Training by Testing Once or Twice a Month Under Constant Conditions and Comparing Results With Targets Each Month\n\n**What it is** — The authors define management as \"control over the course of training and its prescription, based on a comparison of the actual dynamics of training with previous target standards,\" correcting course \"by changing the appropriate parameters of the loading\" (PDF p. 402 / printed p. 391, checked against the page image). Their guidelines (Verkhoshansky, 1985):\n1. \"One should first select the most informative fitness characteristics in order to assess the special physical preparedness, technical proficiency and psychological state of the athlete,\" using \"special laboratory methods, control exercises (pedagogical tests) or functional sporting or standardised field tests.\"\n2. \"Control over the course of training can only be effective if one regularly analyses the dynamics of the athlete's state. When doing this, (a) testing should be done regularly once or twice a month, independent of the periodisation and structure of the training stages, (b) the testing procedure should not be excessively demanding on the time and energy of the athlete, (c) the testing conditions must be kept constant to exclude the possibility of random factors affecting the results, and (d) the testing must have functional relevance.\"\n3. \"Management requires a systematic comparison (over monthly periods) of the actual results and target goals of the training. If there is a discrepancy, it is necessary to carefully analyse the situation, determine the cause for such a discrepancy and revise the training programme.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' logic is feedback control: without regular comparison against targets, deviations go unnoticed. They give no evidence for the once-or-twice-a-month frequency.\n\n**When to apply** — Setting up a testing calendar for a programme with written targets. Eric's baseline agrees on criteria (b) to (d): \"Inconsistent data from a good test is still bad data\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 34, Explicit) and \"Data collection has a cost in time, athlete compliance, and staff attention\" (line 30, Explicit). On frequency it goes further for readiness: \"Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker\" (line 74, Explicit), with a Wednesday decision read: \"Wednesdays, the thresholds should be individualized\" (line 75, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q2, Q2a)). Inference: the two operate at different scales; the authors' monthly tests track progress toward annual targets, while daily jumps track short-term readiness, a level the authors treat separately as \"largely random and at times unpredictable\" acute changes (PDF p. 378 / printed p. 367, section 6.11, text layer).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The monthly schedule is \"independent of the periodisation,\" so a test may fall in the middle of a concentrated block, when the authors expect indicators to be depressed ([[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]]); a low result then is planned, not a discrepancy. The authors do not say how big a gap counts as a discrepancy. Inference: Eric's practice of judging change against a rolling baseline and SWC (line 75) answers that question in a way the book does not.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: a jumps group tests standing triple jump and 30 m from a flying start on the first Monday of each month, same surface, same warm-up, same time of day, and compares each result with the month's target from the annual model.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training]], [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-mesocycle-duration-and-monitoring]], [[performance-technology-and-leadership/old-bull-performance-course--old-bull/principle--protocol-drift-undermines-comparability]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3935,"cache_read_input_tokens":323765,"output_tokens":1644,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":53},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3935},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1644,"cache_read_input_tokens":323765,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3935,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3935,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_018sPZt96w8izHEmFvCXTBJo":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--test-monthly-under-constant-conditions-and-compare-with-targets.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.16\"\ntopic: managing training by testing\ncategory: [monitoring, testing, programme-control, feedback, management]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Manage Training by Testing Once or Twice a Month Under Constant Conditions and Comparing Results With Targets Each Month\n\n**What it is** — The authors define management as \"control over the course of training and its prescription, based on a comparison of the actual dynamics of training with previous target standards,\" correcting course \"by changing the appropriate parameters of the loading\" (PDF p. 402 / printed p. 391, checked against the page image). Their guidelines (Verkhoshansky, 1985):\n1. \"One should first select the most informative fitness characteristics in order to assess the special physical preparedness, technical proficiency and psychological state of the athlete,\" using \"special laboratory methods, control exercises (pedagogical tests) or functional sporting or standardised field tests.\"\n2. \"Control over the course of training can only be effective if one regularly analyses the dynamics of the athlete's state. When doing this, (a) testing should be done regularly once or twice a month, independent of the periodisation and structure of the training stages, (b) the testing procedure should not be excessively demanding on the time and energy of the athlete, (c) the testing conditions must be kept constant to exclude the possibility of random factors affecting the results, and (d) the testing must have functional relevance.\"\n3. \"Management requires a systematic comparison (over monthly periods) of the actual results and target goals of the training. If there is a discrepancy, it is necessary to carefully analyse the situation, determine the cause for such a discrepancy and revise the training programme.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' logic is feedback control: without regular comparison against targets, deviations go unnoticed. They give no evidence for the once-or-twice-a-month frequency.\n\n**When to apply** — Setting up a testing calendar for a programme with written targets. Eric's baseline agrees on criteria (b) to (d): \"Inconsistent data from a good test is still bad data\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 34, Explicit) and \"Data collection has a cost in time, athlete compliance, and staff attention\" (line 30, Explicit). On frequency it goes further for readiness: \"Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker\" (line 74, Explicit), with a Wednesday decision read: \"Wednesdays, the thresholds should be individualized\" (line 75, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q2, Q2a)). Inference: the two operate at different scales; the authors' monthly tests track progress toward annual targets, while daily jumps track short-term readiness, a level the authors treat separately as \"largely random and at times unpredictable\" acute changes (PDF p. 378 / printed p. 367, section 6.11, text layer).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The monthly schedule is \"independent of the periodisation,\" so a test may fall in the middle of a concentrated block, when the authors expect indicators to be depressed ([[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]]); a low result then is planned, not a discrepancy. The authors do not say how big a gap counts as a discrepancy. Inference: Eric's practice of judging change against a rolling baseline and SWC (line 75) answers that question in a way the book does not.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: a jumps group tests standing triple jump and 30 m from a flying start on the first Monday of each month, same surface, same warm-up, same time of day, and compares each result with the month's target from the annual model.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training]], [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-mesocycle-duration-and-monitoring]], [[performance-technology-and-leadership/old-bull-performance-course--old-bull/principle--protocol-drift-undermines-comparability]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffn19TkwwbW38ZEDvCgj","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"2207710a-3937-4920-83e8-ccf14d05f19f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:28:28.291Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
