{"parentUuid":"8c9c9741-9b76-4e8a-9903-3045735eab6d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffn3NiHfXBBjcnH4TDvP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1774,"cache_read_input_tokens":327700,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1774,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1505,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffn2cUVTCrhE6UZgUdF9","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"94fd8dda-e24d-4e36-9a9f-0246e8a26b2a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:28:41.818Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"94fd8dda-e24d-4e36-9a9f-0246e8a26b2a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffn3NiHfXBBjcnH4TDvP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016kmMKnVHQTzSPX2v73kYKt","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.16\"\ntopic: training documentation\ncategory: [documentation, training-records, planning, monitoring, overtraining]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Four Coaching Documents: a Diagrammatic Annual Model, a Quantitative Model, a Mesocycle Programme and an Individual Long-Term Chart That Also Records How the Athlete Feels\n\n**What it is** — The authors call record keeping \"the simplest part of management\" and \"a source of unique material\": \"*the keeping of meticulous training records*. This is very simple and it is surprising that many coaches have not taken it seriously enough until recently\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 402 / printed p. 391, checked against the page image). Many coaches have \"a dismissive attitude towards detailed documentation... relying on their preference for intuition and standard traditional methods,\" but documentation \"is not simply a supplementary responsibility... but an important attribute of professional management upon which the success of his athletes' preparation is largely dependent,\" and \"offers a scientific, objective way of determining efficient training methods which lead to continuous progress and a decrease in overtraining and injury.\" The four recommended documents (PDF pp. 402-403 / printed pp. 391-392, both checked against the page images):\n1. **A principal model of the annual training,** \"in diagrammatic form, for example, as in Figures 6.39-6.42.\" It \"provides a good school for the coach's professional thinking and at the same time the graphic form of the model makes it easy for the coach to convey his ideas to pupils. The extent to which they understand his ideas will largely determine the success of the training.\"\n2. **A quantitative model of the training scheme,** for the individual or group, with the planned dynamics of the key indicators against the calendar and \"the general annual volume of loading for all of the training means and its distribution by months.\" \"The annual cycle of training known to be successful with qualified athletes can serve as a model.\"\n3. **A programme for the mesocycle of preparation,** \"worked out on the basis of weekly cycles.\" \"These are the fundamental working documents which the coach uses to organise and control the training.\"\n4. **The individualised long-term chart,** describing \"the long-term dynamics (volume and intensity) of the training load executed with the fundamental means, as well as the corresponding changes in the functional indicators which reflect the athlete's fitness state and sporting results,\" used \"chiefly for the analysis of its effectiveness and for making recommendations for future planning.\"\n\nOn subjective data: \"It is important to record not only the quantitative indicators of athletic state, but also the athlete's own perceptions of how he feels daily in executing the prescribed training exercises, alongside observations of general state of health and motivation. Often, the onset of a cold, reduced appetite, mood changes or altered sleep patterns may be more informative than mathematical calculations in detecting overtraining timeously. Finally, the chart would always make provision for contingency training to cope with the unexpected or unplanned, such as soreness, injury or illness\" (PDF p. 403).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are analysis and communication: the long-term chart lets the coach judge what worked, and the diagram lets athletes understand the plan. They cite no study.\n\n**When to apply** — Setting up a programme's record system: one picture of the year, one numerical plan, weekly mesocycle sheets, and a per-athlete chart that pairs load with test results. Eric's baseline: \"Data collection is judged on cost versus insight\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 30, Explicit). Inference: the long-term chart is the authors' tool for judging that insight after the fact.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Tension with Eric's baseline on daily self-report.** The authors recommend daily records of how the athlete feels, mood, appetite and sleep. Eric's baseline: \"Daily subjective wellness surveys, rolled out across multiple sports via the AMS app starting summer 2022, were abandoned after roughly one academic year because athletes learned to game the consistent, low-effort answers the survey rewarded; the data looked clean but carried no real signal\" (line 89, Explicit). The same baseline line keeps surveys \"in return-to-play scenarios and with athletes managing chronic health conditions.\" Inference: the authors assume an individual coach-athlete relationship where the coach observes these signs; a roster-wide app survey is a different collection method, and the baseline's experience is evidence about that method, not about the signs themselves. The authors' claim that such signs \"may be more informative than mathematical calculations\" is unsupported on the page.\n\n**Example** — The authors point to Figs 6.39-6.42 as examples of the principal model (PDF p. 402). Inference: the long-term chart for a sprinter would plot monthly sprint and jump volume against monthly 30 m and standing triple jump results over several years, with notes on illness and injury.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--test-monthly-under-constant-conditions-and-compare-with-targets]], [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1774,"cache_read_input_tokens":327700,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1774,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_016kmMKnVHQTzSPX2v73kYKt":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.16\"\ntopic: training documentation\ncategory: [documentation, training-records, planning, monitoring, overtraining]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Four Coaching Documents: a Diagrammatic Annual Model, a Quantitative Model, a Mesocycle Programme and an Individual Long-Term Chart That Also Records How the Athlete Feels\n\n**What it is** — The authors call record keeping \"the simplest part of management\" and \"a source of unique material\": \"*the keeping of meticulous training records*. This is very simple and it is surprising that many coaches have not taken it seriously enough until recently\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 402 / printed p. 391, checked against the page image). Many coaches have \"a dismissive attitude towards detailed documentation... relying on their preference for intuition and standard traditional methods,\" but documentation \"is not simply a supplementary responsibility... but an important attribute of professional management upon which the success of his athletes' preparation is largely dependent,\" and \"offers a scientific, objective way of determining efficient training methods which lead to continuous progress and a decrease in overtraining and injury.\" The four recommended documents (PDF pp. 402-403 / printed pp. 391-392, both checked against the page images):\n1. **A principal model of the annual training,** \"in diagrammatic form, for example, as in Figures 6.39-6.42.\" It \"provides a good school for the coach's professional thinking and at the same time the graphic form of the model makes it easy for the coach to convey his ideas to pupils. The extent to which they understand his ideas will largely determine the success of the training.\"\n2. **A quantitative model of the training scheme,** for the individual or group, with the planned dynamics of the key indicators against the calendar and \"the general annual volume of loading for all of the training means and its distribution by months.\" \"The annual cycle of training known to be successful with qualified athletes can serve as a model.\"\n3. **A programme for the mesocycle of preparation,** \"worked out on the basis of weekly cycles.\" \"These are the fundamental working documents which the coach uses to organise and control the training.\"\n4. **The individualised long-term chart,** describing \"the long-term dynamics (volume and intensity) of the training load executed with the fundamental means, as well as the corresponding changes in the functional indicators which reflect the athlete's fitness state and sporting results,\" used \"chiefly for the analysis of its effectiveness and for making recommendations for future planning.\"\n\nOn subjective data: \"It is important to record not only the quantitative indicators of athletic state, but also the athlete's own perceptions of how he feels daily in executing the prescribed training exercises, alongside observations of general state of health and motivation. Often, the onset of a cold, reduced appetite, mood changes or altered sleep patterns may be more informative than mathematical calculations in detecting overtraining timeously. Finally, the chart would always make provision for contingency training to cope with the unexpected or unplanned, such as soreness, injury or illness\" (PDF p. 403).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are analysis and communication: the long-term chart lets the coach judge what worked, and the diagram lets athletes understand the plan. They cite no study.\n\n**When to apply** — Setting up a programme's record system: one picture of the year, one numerical plan, weekly mesocycle sheets, and a per-athlete chart that pairs load with test results. Eric's baseline: \"Data collection is judged on cost versus insight\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 30, Explicit). Inference: the long-term chart is the authors' tool for judging that insight after the fact.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Tension with Eric's baseline on daily self-report.** The authors recommend daily records of how the athlete feels, mood, appetite and sleep. Eric's baseline: \"Daily subjective wellness surveys, rolled out across multiple sports via the AMS app starting summer 2022, were abandoned after roughly one academic year because athletes learned to game the consistent, low-effort answers the survey rewarded; the data looked clean but carried no real signal\" (line 89, Explicit). The same baseline line keeps surveys \"in return-to-play scenarios and with athletes managing chronic health conditions.\" Inference: the authors assume an individual coach-athlete relationship where the coach observes these signs; a roster-wide app survey is a different collection method, and the baseline's experience is evidence about that method, not about the signs themselves. The authors' claim that such signs \"may be more informative than mathematical calculations\" is unsupported on the page.\n\n**Example** — The authors point to Figs 6.39-6.42 as examples of the principal model (PDF p. 402). Inference: the long-term chart for a sprinter would plot monthly sprint and jump volume against monthly 30 m and standing triple jump results over several years, with notes on illness and injury.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--test-monthly-under-constant-conditions-and-compare-with-targets]], [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffn2cUVTCrhE6UZgUdF9","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"7b486f62-4606-43f4-866a-4612b208cc62","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:28:56.714Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"7b486f62-4606-43f4-866a-4612b208cc62","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"18f95e19-3d7d-4312-a698-c0ed8ed1d357","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016kmMKnVHQTzSPX2v73kYKt","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"72b27167-aeba-4940-b70b-5ca5231fbf58","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:28:56.848Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.16\"\ntopic: training documentation\ncategory: [documentation, training-records, planning, monitoring, overtraining]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Four Coaching Documents: a Diagrammatic Annual Model, a Quantitative Model, a Mesocycle Programme and an Individual Long-Term Chart That Also Records How the Athlete Feels\n\n**What it is** — The authors call record keeping \"the simplest part of management\" and \"a source of unique material\": \"*the keeping of meticulous training records*. This is very simple and it is surprising that many coaches have not taken it seriously enough until recently\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 402 / printed p. 391, checked against the page image). Many coaches have \"a dismissive attitude towards detailed documentation... relying on their preference for intuition and standard traditional methods,\" but documentation \"is not simply a supplementary responsibility... but an important attribute of professional management upon which the success of his athletes' preparation is largely dependent,\" and \"offers a scientific, objective way of determining efficient training methods which lead to continuous progress and a decrease in overtraining and injury.\" The four recommended documents (PDF pp. 402-403 / printed pp. 391-392, both checked against the page images):\n1. **A principal model of the annual training,** \"in diagrammatic form, for example, as in Figures 6.39-6.42.\" It \"provides a good school for the coach's professional thinking and at the same time the graphic form of the model makes it easy for the coach to convey his ideas to pupils. The extent to which they understand his ideas will largely determine the success of the training.\"\n2. **A quantitative model of the training scheme,** for the individual or group, with the planned dynamics of the key indicators against the calendar and \"the general annual volume of loading for all of the training means and its distribution by months.\" \"The annual cycle of training known to be successful with qualified athletes can serve as a model.\"\n3. **A programme for the mesocycle of preparation,** \"worked out on the basis of weekly cycles.\" \"These are the fundamental working documents which the coach uses to organise and control the training.\"\n4. **The individualised long-term chart,** describing \"the long-term dynamics (volume and intensity) of the training load executed with the fundamental means, as well as the corresponding changes in the functional indicators which reflect the athlete's fitness state and sporting results,\" used \"chiefly for the analysis of its effectiveness and for making recommendations for future planning.\"\n\nOn subjective data: \"It is important to record not only the quantitative indicators of athletic state, but also the athlete's own perceptions of how he feels daily in executing the prescribed training exercises, alongside observations of general state of health and motivation. Often, the onset of a cold, reduced appetite, mood changes or altered sleep patterns may be more informative than mathematical calculations in detecting overtraining timeously. Finally, the chart would always make provision for contingency training to cope with the unexpected or unplanned, such as soreness, injury or illness\" (PDF p. 403).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are analysis and communication: the long-term chart lets the coach judge what worked, and the diagram lets athletes understand the plan. They cite no study.\n\n**When to apply** — Setting up a programme's record system: one picture of the year, one numerical plan, weekly mesocycle sheets, and a per-athlete chart that pairs load with test results. Eric's baseline: \"Data collection is judged on cost versus insight\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 30, Explicit). Inference: the long-term chart is the authors' tool for judging that insight after the fact.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Tension with Eric's baseline on daily self-report.** The authors recommend daily records of how the athlete feels, mood, appetite and sleep. Eric's baseline: \"Daily subjective wellness surveys, rolled out across multiple sports via the AMS app starting summer 2022, were abandoned after roughly one academic year because athletes learned to game the consistent, low-effort answers the survey rewarded; the data looked clean but carried no real signal\" (line 89, Explicit). The same baseline line keeps surveys \"in return-to-play scenarios and with athletes managing chronic health conditions.\" Inference: the authors assume an individual coach-athlete relationship where the coach observes these signs; a roster-wide app survey is a different collection method, and the baseline's experience is evidence about that method, not about the signs themselves. The authors' claim that such signs \"may be more informative than mathematical calculations\" is unsupported on the page.\n\n**Example** — The authors point to Figs 6.39-6.42 as examples of the principal model (PDF p. 402). Inference: the long-term chart for a sprinter would plot monthly sprint and jump volume against monthly 30 m and standing triple jump results over several years, with notes on illness and injury.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--test-monthly-under-constant-conditions-and-compare-with-targets]], [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7b486f62-4606-43f4-866a-4612b208cc62","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"72b27167-aeba-4940-b70b-5ca5231fbf58","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffn3NiHfXBBjcnH4TDvP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LYjrjoNqUVhrjwZoGWKTtA","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.17\"\ntopic: nature of programming\ncategory: [programming, coaching-judgement, research-methods, ecological-validity]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Programming Is an Art Based on Principles, Not a Rigid Order or an Algorithm; One-Variable Short Studies Mislead It\n\n**What it is** — The chapter's closing section (PDF p. 403 / printed p. 392, checked against the page image): \"it is unacceptable to understand programming as the following of some instructions which prescribe a rigid order in the organisation of training. Furthermore, it is also senseless to view it as the creation of a precise algorithm, as is done in chess. Programming in sport is an art based on specific principles, which allows the coach creative initiative in making the final decisions.\" The authors call the chapter \"only the first steps,\" and leave to the coach \"the sequence of loading in the mesocycles and their composition in the microcycles, as well as rational ways of combining the contents of these microcycles into a system... It is the role of the coach to solve such problems.\"\n\nThey also criticise the research base: \"it is impossible to solve these problems by observing only physiological and biochemical mechanisms. It is impossible to separate, as is customary, the role of the motor processes from the mechanisms of energy production... The pitfalls of such a separation become apparent when one attempts to extract some practical recommendations from studies which change one variable at a time in an experiment of unrealistically short duration or limited complexity, using a sample of athletes of diverse levels of qualification.\" The research they call for: \"the study of the characteristics of long-term and compensatory adaptation to intense exercise and observation of the principal trends in the dynamics of the athlete's state during prolonged training stages.\"\n\nThe same stance opens 6.12.1: \"Actual conditions do not always satisfy the optimal values of these variables. Therefore, programming of training requires creative flexibility based on the skill to anticipate the effect of any form of training and select its optimal variant\" (PDF p. 379 / printed p. 368, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is complexity: training effects depend on \"the specific type of sport, the chosen loading, the period of training, the athlete's level of qualification and individual characteristics,\" which no fixed algorithm covers. They give no evidence for the claim itself.\n\n**When to apply** — Reading any model in this chapter, including the annual models, as principles to adapt, not templates to copy; and weighing short, single-variable studies on mixed-level samples with caution when they are offered as programming rules. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Eric's baseline: \"The coach's eye and objective data work in sequence: the eye flags, data investigates\" (line 33, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q6)).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not a licence to ignore numbers: the same chapter asks for quantitative models, monthly testing and detailed records ([[framework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training]], [[heuristic--test-monthly-under-constant-conditions-and-compare-with-targets]]). The critique of one-variable studies is the authors' view; the book's own Soviet sources are often reported without design details, so the critique cuts both ways. Inference: a controlled short study is weak evidence for a season plan, but an uncontrolled long observation is weak evidence for cause.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no case. Inference: a coach who reads a six-week trial in recreationally trained students showing that one exercise order beats another treats it as a hint to test in his own athletes, not as a rule for a varsity programme.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]], [[principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them]], [[principle--programme-objective-approach-quantified-goals-dictate-training]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1774,"cache_read_input_tokens":327700,"output_tokens":3705,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":249},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1774},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3705,"cache_read_input_tokens":327700,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1774,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1774,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01LYjrjoNqUVhrjwZoGWKTtA":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.17\"\ntopic: nature of programming\ncategory: [programming, coaching-judgement, research-methods, ecological-validity]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Programming Is an Art Based on Principles, Not a Rigid Order or an Algorithm; One-Variable Short Studies Mislead It\n\n**What it is** — The chapter's closing section (PDF p. 403 / printed p. 392, checked against the page image): \"it is unacceptable to understand programming as the following of some instructions which prescribe a rigid order in the organisation of training. Furthermore, it is also senseless to view it as the creation of a precise algorithm, as is done in chess. Programming in sport is an art based on specific principles, which allows the coach creative initiative in making the final decisions.\" The authors call the chapter \"only the first steps,\" and leave to the coach \"the sequence of loading in the mesocycles and their composition in the microcycles, as well as rational ways of combining the contents of these microcycles into a system... It is the role of the coach to solve such problems.\"\n\nThey also criticise the research base: \"it is impossible to solve these problems by observing only physiological and biochemical mechanisms. It is impossible to separate, as is customary, the role of the motor processes from the mechanisms of energy production... The pitfalls of such a separation become apparent when one attempts to extract some practical recommendations from studies which change one variable at a time in an experiment of unrealistically short duration or limited complexity, using a sample of athletes of diverse levels of qualification.\" The research they call for: \"the study of the characteristics of long-term and compensatory adaptation to intense exercise and observation of the principal trends in the dynamics of the athlete's state during prolonged training stages.\"\n\nThe same stance opens 6.12.1: \"Actual conditions do not always satisfy the optimal values of these variables. Therefore, programming of training requires creative flexibility based on the skill to anticipate the effect of any form of training and select its optimal variant\" (PDF p. 379 / printed p. 368, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is complexity: training effects depend on \"the specific type of sport, the chosen loading, the period of training, the athlete's level of qualification and individual characteristics,\" which no fixed algorithm covers. They give no evidence for the claim itself.\n\n**When to apply** — Reading any model in this chapter, including the annual models, as principles to adapt, not templates to copy; and weighing short, single-variable studies on mixed-level samples with caution when they are offered as programming rules. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Eric's baseline: \"The coach's eye and objective data work in sequence: the eye flags, data investigates\" (line 33, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q6)).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not a licence to ignore numbers: the same chapter asks for quantitative models, monthly testing and detailed records ([[framework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training]], [[heuristic--test-monthly-under-constant-conditions-and-compare-with-targets]]). The critique of one-variable studies is the authors' view; the book's own Soviet sources are often reported without design details, so the critique cuts both ways. Inference: a controlled short study is weak evidence for a season plan, but an uncontrolled long observation is weak evidence for cause.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no case. Inference: a coach who reads a six-week trial in recreationally trained students showing that one exercise order beats another treats it as a hint to test in his own athletes, not as a rule for a varsity programme.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]], [[principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them]], [[principle--programme-objective-approach-quantified-goals-dictate-training]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffn2cUVTCrhE6UZgUdF9","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"636d6cdb-6557-485e-b141-a9ab49e93780","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:29:09.074Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
