e if-then rule is the planning counterpart of Eric's testing rule, since each training element has to trace back to a stated objective.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The approach assumes the goal can be \"quantitatively defined\" and the work to reach it is known; in team sports the outcome (winning) is not a single measurable performance, and the authors do not address that. The \"level is harder than timing\" sentence is the authors' summary of unnamed research and runs against much of the peaking literature's focus on timing; treat it as their view.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked example on this page. Inference: a coach sets \"standing triple jump from 9.2 m to 9.6 m by the June championship\" and then derives the jump, strength and technique work and its timing from that target, rather than starting from a favourite weekly template.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--seven-primary-aims-of-programming-training]], [[principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm]], [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--eight-training-objectives]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"be2f0c3b-8237-45ff-b1ed-0d8a80b85790","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
