{"parentUuid":"cca71ee9-d35d-4575-92ec-66dc40845e0e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"18f95e19-3d7d-4312-a698-c0ed8ed1d357","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NbqZPWw9C7E9jUmSW3aRYi","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--programme-objective-approach-quantified-goals-dictate-training.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"ef84f74b-6f49-4bd4-a5fe-349a2a0bc3de","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:29:47.577Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--programme-objective-approach-quantified-goals-dictate-training.md","oldString":"is the authors' summary of unnamed research and runs against much of the peaking literature's focus on timing; treat it as their view.","newString":"is the authors' summary of unnamed research (\"Research shows\"); no study is named, so treat it as their view.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12\"\ntopic: programme-objective approach\ncategory: [programming, goal-setting, planning, periodization]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Programme-Objective Approach: Set a Quantified Goal First, and the Work Needed to Reach It Is the Strategy\n\n**What it is** — \"The basis of programming is the programme-objective approach, in accordance with which the contents, volume and organisation of training loads are determined by the *objectives* of the preparation (see Fig 6.8). The objectives consist of three main components: the desired improvement in performance; the appropriate changes in technical-tactical, psychological and competition preparedness; the necessary modification of special physical preparedness\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 379 / printed p. 368, checked against the page image). \"In practice, the selection of a programming strategy is performed by means of a logical 'if-then' approach... if one sets a specific and quantitatively defined goal, then the precise work and methods necessary to accomplish this goal constitute the appropriate strategy.\"\n\nTwo framing points on the same page:\n- **Construction aims at economy.** Construction \"involves regulating its contents to provide the effective use of loading of different primary emphasis, i.e. the organisation of the contents over time, so that the required training effect occurs with a minimal expenditure of energy\" (6.12.1). Two categories of construction follow: \"one based on structural organisation and the other on time management\" (how loads of different emphasis are combined, and how loading is regulated within cycles and stages).\n- **Level is harder than timing.** Closing section 6.11, just above the 6.12 heading: \"The art of controlling the dynamics of the athlete's fitness state consists, firstly, of achieving the necessary level of conditioning and, secondly, achieving it at the required time, as dictated by the competition calendar. Research shows that achievement of the second requirement is not especially complex. However, the probability of successfully achieving the first requirement is much smaller\" (PDF p. 379).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors present it as logic rather than evidence: a quantified target makes the required work derivable. Fig 6.8, which they cite, is outside this batch. No study is cited for the claim that reaching the level is harder than timing it.\n\n**When to apply** — Starting any plan by writing the performance target and the changes in technical and physical preparedness it requires, with numbers, before choosing exercises or a periodisation model. The eight-step sequence of 6.15 is the operational version ([[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]]). Eric's baseline: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 32, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)). Inference: the if-then rule is the planning counterpart of Eric's testing rule, since each training element has to trace back to a stated objective.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The approach assumes the goal can be \"quantitatively defined\" and the work to reach it is known; in team sports the outcome (winning) is not a single measurable performance, and the authors do not address that. The \"level is harder than timing\" sentence is the authors' summary of unnamed research and runs against much of the peaking literature's focus on timing; treat it as their view.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked example on this page. Inference: a coach sets \"standing triple jump from 9.2 m to 9.6 m by the June championship\" and then derives the jump, strength and technique work and its timing from that target, rather than starting from a favourite weekly template.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--seven-primary-aims-of-programming-training]], [[principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm]], [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--eight-training-objectives]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":23,"oldLines":7,"newStart":23,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Starting any plan by writing the performance target and the changes in technical and physical preparedness it requires, with numbers, before choosing exercises or a periodisation model. The eight-step sequence of 6.15 is the operational version ([[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]]). Eric's baseline: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 32, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)). Inference: the if-then rule is the planning counterpart of Eric's testing rule, since each training element has to trace back to a stated objective."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The approach assumes the goal can be \"quantitatively defined\" and the work to reach it is known; in team sports the outcome (winning) is not a single measurable performance, and the authors do not address that. The \"level is harder than timing\" sentence is the authors' summary of unnamed research and runs against much of the peaking literature's focus on timing; treat it as their view.","+**When NOT to apply** — The approach assumes the goal can be \"quantitatively defined\" and the work to reach it is known; in team sports the outcome (winning) is not a single measurable performance, and the authors do not address that. The \"level is harder than timing\" sentence is the authors' summary of unnamed research (\"Research shows\"); no study is named, so treat it as their view."," "," **Example** — The authors give no worked example on this page. Inference: a coach sets \"standing triple jump from 9.2 m to 9.6 m by the June championship\" and then derives the jump, strength and technique work and its timing from that target, rather than starting from a favourite weekly template."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cca71ee9-d35d-4575-92ec-66dc40845e0e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
