{"parentUuid":"451edc1b-3793-4179-b89b-7e630e67fa47","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"9ce4e1ff-e22e-47cc-b84a-20ebb6a9d3e4","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0147racgQgnqussXy58ZmMD1","type":"tool_result","content":"heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md:1\nframework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks.md:1\nframework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md:1\nclaim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md:1\nframework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports.md:1\nprogramming \"compass.\"","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"bf321899-807a-43ea-bf78-0fbf6005ec01","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:32:29.815Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md:1\nframework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks.md:1\nframework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md:1\nclaim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md:1\nframework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports.md:1\nprogramming \"compass.\"","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":17,"oldLines":7,"newStart":17,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"re-adaptation at a lower level\": the body adapts down to the reduced load. They give no physiological detail and no data beyond the four citations."," ","-**When to apply** — Long competition periods (the model's is \"some 4-5 months,\" PDF p. 396) in which the athlete would otherwise only compete and do light maintenance: build in periodic increases in special and assistance volume. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \\\"maintain intensity, reduce volume\\\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Also: \"In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete\" (line 181, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' warning is against a *prolonged* volume cut in a long season; the baseline's in-season waving, and the bye-week \"speed and strength top-up\" (line 187, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)), are the kind of periodic volume increase the authors call for. A football season of about 12-15 weeks of games is roughly the length of the model's competition period, though the sport is not.","+**When to apply** — Long competition periods (the model's is \"some 4-5 months,\" PDF p. 396) in which the athlete would otherwise only compete and do light maintenance: build in periodic increases in special and assistance volume. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Also: \"In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete\" (line 181, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' warning is against a *prolonged* volume cut in a long season; the baseline's in-season waving, and the bye-week \"speed and strength top-up\" (line 187, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)), are the kind of periodic volume increase the authors call for. A football season of about 12-15 weeks of games is roughly the length of the model's competition period, though the sport is not."," "," **When NOT to apply** — The statement is made for long-duration endurance sports under monocyclic periodisation; the authors do not extend it to strength or team sports. It does not mean intensity can be dropped. In the explosive-strength model the second strength block deliberately carries less volume than the first (\"It is unnecessary at this time to further increase the volume of strength work,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383), so the rule applies to special and competition loading in endurance sport, not to every quality in every sport."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":26,"oldLines":7,"newStart":26,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Planning a year for an explosive-strength sport with two clearly separated competition phases: put the large strength block early in the first preparation phase, a smaller and more intense block early in the second preparation phase, keep technique work going at reduced intensity inside each block, and shift to technique, speed and competition readiness as the delayed effect appears. Eric's baseline: \"In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: football has one long competition phase, not two, so the model maps onto the football year only partly: a large block in the winter off-season and a smaller, more intense block in the summer before fall camp would follow the model's block sizes, with phase IV standing in for the season. The authors do not discuss football or weekly competition."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin.** Every model in 6.14 uses the concentrated block: \"All models in this chapter use the principle of concentrated special strength loading, which elicits the LDTE\" (PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379, checked against the page image). The library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports \"improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance,\" and that Issurin judged the evidence \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6), and saw the model as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality (p. 11); see [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]. American football is a team sport. Other limits: the model is drawn for \"highly qualified athletes\" (the concentrated method \"was devised for preparing highly qualified athletes,\" PDF p. 381); the block rectangles are not literal (\"The use of rectangular blocks should not create the impression that the concentrated loads are applied suddenly at peak intensity,\" PDF p. 391 / printed p. 380); and stage boundaries \"can vary somewhat\" with the real calendar (PDF p. 391). Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \\\"maintain intensity, reduce volume\\\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: that rule fits the model's phase IV, where the strength block is over, but the model gives no in-season strength dose, and the baseline's game week (\"lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game,\" line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)) is a microcycle rule the annual model does not address.","+**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin.** Every model in 6.14 uses the concentrated block: \"All models in this chapter use the principle of concentrated special strength loading, which elicits the LDTE\" (PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379, checked against the page image). The library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports \"improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance,\" and that Issurin judged the evidence \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6), and saw the model as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality (p. 11); see [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]. American football is a team sport. Other limits: the model is drawn for \"highly qualified athletes\" (the concentrated method \"was devised for preparing highly qualified athletes,\" PDF p. 381); the block rectangles are not literal (\"The use of rectangular blocks should not create the impression that the concentrated loads are applied suddenly at peak intensity,\" PDF p. 391 / printed p. 380); and stage boundaries \"can vary somewhat\" with the real calendar (PDF p. 391). Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: that rule fits the model's phase IV, where the strength block is over, but the model gives no in-season strength dose, and the baseline's game week (\"lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game,\" line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)) is a microcycle rule the annual model does not address."," "," **Example** — The authors give the model, not a worked year (Fig 6.39, PDF p. 395). Inference: for a jumper with indoor and outdoor seasons, block 1 would sit in the first three months of autumn preparation with the jumps, weights and depth-jump sequence inside it, the indoor season would fall about month 5, block 2 would run about months 6-7 at less volume and more intensity, and the main outdoor competitions would fall in months 9-11."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":23,"oldLines":7,"newStart":23,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors give the reason for allowing coordination work during the block (it is compatible with a lowered functional state) and for the maintenance dose (explosive strength falls during the second glycolytic peak). They do not explain why speed must be excluded entirely rather than reduced. Inference: it follows from their rule that high-intensity work needs a fresh, not a depressed, functional state ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]])."," ","-**When to apply** — Cyclic speed sports such as sprinting, and by the authors' mention of \"team sports\" for the second glycolytic peak, sports where repeated speed matters. Eric's baseline: \"For football specifically, the stated priority stack is: Goal 1, alactic and aerobic capacity/power for fall-camp demands; Goal 2, the same two systems for game demands; glycolytic conditioning is treated as a \\\"worst case scenario\\\" to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 116, Explicit, claimed again as Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: the authors program two deliberate glycolytic peaks, which goes further than the baseline; their own caution (small segments combined with speed work, a maintenance dose for explosive strength) is the part that fits the baseline best.","+**When to apply** — Cyclic speed sports such as sprinting, and by the authors' mention of \"team sports\" for the second glycolytic peak, sports where repeated speed matters. Eric's baseline: \"For football specifically, the stated priority stack is: Goal 1, alactic and aerobic capacity/power for fall-camp demands; Goal 2, the same two systems for game demands; glycolytic conditioning is treated as a \"worst case scenario\" to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 116, Explicit, claimed again as Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: the authors program two deliberate glycolytic peaks, which goes further than the baseline; their own caution (small segments combined with speed work, a maintenance dose for explosive strength) is the part that fits the baseline best."," "," **When NOT to apply** — Speed-strength and complex-technical sports keep some technique and do not separate speed so sharply ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Eric's baseline also says \"staff do program it in the off-season\" for glycolytic work (line 117, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)), so the conflict with line 116 is about in-season emphasis. No volumes, durations or intensities are given for either glycolytic peak."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":21,"oldLines":7,"newStart":21,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors give the structure, not a mechanism beyond the LDTE of each strength block. No data are shown; the figure is credited to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999)."," ","-**When to apply** — Individual and combat sports with three main competition periods, such as the boxing and swimming examples the authors cite for tri-cyclic forms (PDF p. 387 / printed p. 376), and team sports where \"tri-cyclic periodisation may have to be used, as dictated by the competition calendar\" (PDF p. 387). Eric's baseline: \"The head coach's system of play and roster identity (e.g., a \\\"home run hitters\\\" lineup vs. a \\\"steal bases\\\" lineup) is treated as a direct input into whether a team's programming leans toward maximal strength or toward speed/power emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 188, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the model's block contents (maximum, endurance, explosive) are the kind of lever that input would set.","+**When to apply** — Individual and combat sports with three main competition periods, such as the boxing and swimming examples the authors cite for tri-cyclic forms (PDF p. 387 / printed p. 376), and team sports where \"tri-cyclic periodisation may have to be used, as dictated by the competition calendar\" (PDF p. 387). Eric's baseline: \"The head coach's system of play and roster identity (e.g., a \"home run hitters\" lineup vs. a \"steal bases\" lineup) is treated as a direct input into whether a team's programming leans toward maximal strength or toward speed/power emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 188, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the model's block contents (maximum, endurance, explosive) are the kind of lever that input would set."," "," **When NOT to apply** — **Issurin 2016 challenge (combat sports).** The library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports improved some fitness measures without reported effects on practice or competitive performance, and that Issurin judged the evidence insufficient for broad use in \"high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6; [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). This entry keeps `supported` because the tri-cyclic layout itself is not what Issurin challenges; the challenge is to the concentrated blocks inside it, recorded on [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]]. The model assumes three distinct competition peaks; it is not a template for a weekly-competition season."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":23,"oldLines":7,"newStart":23,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors' reason is the lowered functional state during concentrated loading (\"Noting that one's functional state will be lowered during this stage, this task is carried out at low intensity,\" PDF p. 383) and the later rise in speed-strength, which the technique is \"oriented to.\" They add that each cycle of technical improvement consists of \"the preliminary development of the motor qualities which the athlete needs to reproduce in the near future, accompanied by the immediate accommodation of technique to that level,\" and that with this system \"the athlete will not be unduly concerned about technique and his achievements will not stagnate\" (PDF p. 383). No study is cited for the two-stage scheme itself."," ","-**When to apply** — Any off-season strength block for a speed-strength athlete: keep a daily technique slot, load it with drills, rhythm work and sub-maximal whole movements while the block is heavy, and move full-speed, competition-like technique to the weeks after the block. The 6.14.1 model states the same rule (technique \"is included at reduced intensity using the separate elements and their links,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383). Eric's baseline: \"The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next) is repeatedly described as the department's programming \\\"compass.\\\"\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 101, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: the authors' scheme does not wait for strength to be built before skill work starts; it runs low-intensity skill learning alongside the strength block, which is a different reading of \"foundation\" than a strict stepwise hierarchy.","+**When to apply** — Any off-season strength block for a speed-strength athlete: keep a daily technique slot, load it with drills, rhythm work and sub-maximal whole movements while the block is heavy, and move full-speed, competition-like technique to the weeks after the block. The 6.14.1 model states the same rule (technique \"is included at reduced intensity using the separate elements and their links,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383). Eric's baseline: \"The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next) is repeatedly described as the department's programming \"compass.\"\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 101, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: the authors' scheme does not wait for strength to be built before skill work starts; it runs low-intensity skill learning alongside the strength block, which is a different reading of \"foundation\" than a strict stepwise hierarchy."," "," **When NOT to apply** — Speed sports with a cyclic structure separate the two more sharply: \"Speed work is excluded totally from the concentrated strength loading stage,\" though intermuscular coordination work is allowed (PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373; see [[framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks]]). The rule concerns the authors' concentrated-block method for qualified athletes; it says nothing about technique learning in novices outside a block. The authors do not give a dose or a proportion of time for the technique slot. Inference: in team sports the sport coach, not the strength staff, usually owns technical practice, so the strength staff can control only the timing of their own heavy work relative to it."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md"],"shared":true}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"451edc1b-3793-4179-b89b-7e630e67fa47","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
