{"parentUuid":"665bd06c-48ab-4d1a-a4d4-93e4a780e57b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md","snippet":""},"type":"attachment","uuid":"ea2534b7-1289-4cbb-b9c3-8b96fe7a15a9","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:32:29.836Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. The changes are not shown here; use Read if you need the current content.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ea2534b7-1289-4cbb-b9c3-8b96fe7a15a9","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md","snippet":"12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# In a Long Monocyclic Competition Period, a Prolonged Cut in Loading Volume Lowers Fitness; Competition Plus Small Special Loads Cannot Maintain It\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — In the long-duration endurance model (PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385, checked against the page image): \"It has been established that the prolonged reduction in the volume of loading during the competition period (with monocyclic periodisation) leads to re-adaptation at a lower level, which is reflected in the drop of non-specific trainability. Competition and special supplementary loading (with limited volumes) cannot stimulate further development or even maintain special work-capacity. Thus, it is appropriate to periodically increase the volume of competition and special assistance loading (Baranovsky, 1969; Kryazh, 1969; Muzis, 1970; Zhikharevich, 1976).\" The paired condition from Gilyazova (1978) is that stable results need \"not a sharp drop in the volume of loading during the competitive period\" and a gradual preparatory increase (\"a mean monthly increase of 7-14%\") (PDF p. 396).\n17\t\n18\t**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"re-adaptation at a lower level\": the body adapts down to the reduced load. They give no physiological detail and no data beyond the four citations.\n19\t\n20\t**When to apply** — Long competition periods (the model's is \"some 4-5 months,\" PDF p. 396) in which the athlete would otherwise only compete and do light maintenance: build in periodic increases in special and assistance volume. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Also: \"In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete\" (line 181, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' warning is against a *prolonged* volume cut in a long season; the baseline's in-season waving, and the bye-week \"speed and strength top-up\" (line 187, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)), are the kind of periodic volume increase the authors call for. A football season of about 12-15 weeks of games is roughly the length of the model's competition period, though the sport is not.\n21\t\n22\t**When NOT to apply** — The statement is made for long-duration endurance sports under monocyclic periodisation; the authors do not extend it to strength or team sports. It does not mean intensity can be dropped. In the explosive-strength model the second strength block deliberately carries less volume than the first (\"It is unnecessary at this time to further increase the volume of strength work,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383), so the rule applies to special and competition loading in endurance sport, not to every quality in every sport.\n23\t\n24\t**Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: a marathoner in a five-month racing season returns every few weeks to a higher-volume week of race-pace and assistance work instead of only racing and easy running between races.\n25\t\n26\t**Related** — [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--in-season-team-sport-mini-block-microcycle]]"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"cfafb7d7-c53b-4076-9ec7-fed3ff3fe9fa","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:32:29.836Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# In a Long Monocyclic Competition Period, a Prolonged Cut in Loading Volume Lowers Fitness; Competition Plus Small Special Loads Cannot Maintain It\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — In the long-duration endurance model (PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385, checked against the page image): \"It has been established that the prolonged reduction in the volume of loading during the competition period (with monocyclic periodisation) leads to re-adaptation at a lower level, which is reflected in the drop of non-specific trainability. Competition and special supplementary loading (with limited volumes) cannot stimulate further development or even maintain special work-capacity. Thus, it is appropriate to periodically increase the volume of competition and special assistance loading (Baranovsky, 1969; Kryazh, 1969; Muzis, 1970; Zhikharevich, 1976).\" The paired condition from Gilyazova (1978) is that stable results need \"not a sharp drop in the volume of loading during the competitive period\" and a gradual preparatory increase (\"a mean monthly increase of 7-14%\") (PDF p. 396).\n17\t\n18\t**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"re-adaptation at a lower level\": the body adapts down to the reduced load. They give no physiological detail and no data beyond the four citations.\n19\t\n20\t**When to apply** — Long competition periods (the model's is \"some 4-5 months,\" PDF p. 396) in which the athlete would otherwise only compete and do light maintenance: build in periodic increases in special and assistance volume. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Also: \"In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete\" (line 181, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' warning is against a *prolonged* volume cut in a long season; the baseline's in-season waving, and the bye-week \"speed and strength top-up\" (line 187, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)), are the kind of periodic volume increase the authors call for. A football season of about 12-15 weeks of games is roughly the length of the model's competition period, though the sport is not.\n21\t\n22\t**When NOT to apply** — The statement is made for long-duration endurance sports under monocyclic periodisation; the authors do not extend it to strength or team sports. It does not mean intensity can be dropped. In the explosive-strength model the second strength block deliberately carries less volume than the first (\"It is unnecessary at this time to further increase the volume of strength work,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383), so the rule applies to special and competition loading in endurance sport, not to every quality in every sport.\n23\t\n24\t**Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: a marathoner in a five-month racing season returns every few weeks to a higher-volume week of race-pace and assistance work instead of only racing and easy running between races.\n25\t\n26\t**Related** — [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--in-season-team-sport-mini-block-microcycle]]\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
