{"parentUuid":"ac82ad8b-2755-49df-a833-91738df65c2d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"9ce4e1ff-e22e-47cc-b84a-20ebb6a9d3e4","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WiAQSWi2qR7SYwqLoVYbhx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\n1\n1\n1","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"bceff1d3-0cce-4a65-9ee0-7917da9d95ee","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:32:47.034Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"1\n1\n1\n1","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":17,"oldLines":7,"newStart":17,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"re-adaptation at a lower level\": the body adapts down to the reduced load. They give no physiological detail and no data beyond the four citations."," ","-**When to apply** — Long competition periods (the model's is \"some 4-5 months,\" PDF p. 396) in which the athlete would otherwise only compete and do light maintenance: build in periodic increases in special and assistance volume. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Also: \"In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete\" (line 181, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' warning is against a *prolonged* volume cut in a long season; the baseline's in-season waving, and the bye-week \"speed and strength top-up\" (line 187, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)), are the kind of periodic volume increase the authors call for. A football season of about 12-15 weeks of games is roughly the length of the model's competition period, though the sport is not.","+**When to apply** — Long competition periods (the model's is \"some 4-5 months,\" PDF p. 396) in which the athlete would otherwise only compete and do light maintenance: build in periodic increases in special and assistance volume. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Also: \"In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete\" (line 181, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' warning is against a *prolonged* volume cut in a long season; the baseline's in-season waving, and the bye-week \"speed and strength top-up\" (line 187, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)), are the kind of periodic volume increase the authors call for. A football season of games is shorter than the model's 4-5 month competition period, and the sport is not an endurance sport."," "," **When NOT to apply** — The statement is made for long-duration endurance sports under monocyclic periodisation; the authors do not extend it to strength or team sports. It does not mean intensity can be dropped. In the explosive-strength model the second strength block deliberately carries less volume than the first (\"It is unnecessary at this time to further increase the volume of strength work,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383), so the rule applies to special and competition loading in endurance sport, not to every quality in every sport."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":20,"oldLines":7,"newStart":20,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," Conventions for the 6.14 annual models (PDF pp. 390-391 / printed pp. 379-380, checked against the page images): months are not named because summer and winter sports are covered; \"Each model consists of about 11 months, since, on average, one month is allotted for the final transition period\"; each model pairs a fitness-state diagram with a loading diagram; \"One can use any method that enables one to measure the values of these indicators\"; \"All models in this chapter use the principle of concentrated special strength loading, which elicits the LDTE\"; the rectangular blocks are schematic, since \"the actual loading follows a concentrated wave pattern which gradually reaches a peak, then subsides in volume\"; and stage boundaries \"can vary somewhat\" with the real calendar."," ","-On measurement: \"It is of little value to identify which energy systems, types of fitness or movement patterns are involved in a sport unless accurate numerical determinations are regularly made of each of these factors. Even then, we have to remember that the body is not simply a machine in which input and output are deterministically related, but an organism whose behaviour displays non-linearity and fairly random deviations... Nevertheless, without this information, long-term programming becomes imprecise\" (PDF p. 391, checked against the page image). The descriptive-modelling passage names the \"Multifactorial Fitness Profile (MFP) discussed later... (Ch 7)\" and says \"Many years of Russian research have produced models for every type of sport\" (PDF pp. 390-391); it reads as Siff's framing of the Russian material.","+On measurement: \"It is of little value to identify which energy systems, types of fitness or movement patterns are involved in a sport unless accurate numerical determinations are regularly made of each of these factors. Even then, we have to remember that the body is not simply a machine in which input and output are deterministically related, but an organism whose behaviour displays non-linearity and fairly random deviations... Nevertheless, without this information, long-term programming becomes imprecise\" (PDF p. 391, checked against the page image). The descriptive-modelling passage names the \"Multifactorial Fitness Profile (MFP) discussed later... (Ch 7)\" and says \"Many years of Russian research have produced models for every type of sport\" (PDF pp. 390-391). Inference: the passage reads as Siff's framing of the Russian material."," "," **Why it works** — The authors' reason is control: modelling \"makes it possible to manage the organisational complexity of sports activities and devise a practical scheme for describing and controlling the training process\" (PDF p. 390), and norms \"enable the coach to identify any weaknesses in the state of the athlete and thereby modify the training programme\" (PDF p. 391)."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":29,"oldLines":7,"newStart":29,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Choosing the shape of a several-month training phase. Variant B for qualified athletes with high work capacity and sound technique who can tolerate a planned dip; Variant A for medium-level athletes and those who still need a lot of technical work. Eric's baseline: \"Undulating/non-linear periodization (Parker/Miller/Panariello), waving volume week to week (e.g., medium-high, medium, high, low over four weeks) is stated as the personally preferred model over strict linear progression\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: Eric's four-week waves are the \"three and four week stages\" the authors call common practice; the authors' A and B describe the longer phase those waves sit inside, so the two do not conflict."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Not for athletes who cannot yet tolerate voluminous loading or whose technique is still unstable (the authors' own limit, PDF p. 386). \"Mesocycle\" here means a phase of 3-5 months, not the 3-6 week mesocycle of most Western texts, so the rules do not transfer to a four-week block without translation. The authors give no study for the claim that B exceeds A; the comparison rests on their adaptation theory. Both variants sit in a chapter whose models all use concentrated loading, which Issurin 2016 questions for team sports (see [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]).","+**When NOT to apply** — Not for athletes who cannot yet tolerate voluminous loading or whose technique is still unstable (the authors' own limit, PDF p. 386). \"Mesocycle\" here means a phase of 3-5 months, not the looser usage the authors note, \"a period of several weeks or months consisting of a certain number of microcycles\" (PDF p. 385), so the rules do not transfer to a four-week block without translation. The authors give no study for the claim that B exceeds A; the comparison rests on their adaptation theory. Both variants sit in a chapter whose models all use concentrated loading, which Issurin 2016 questions for team sports (see [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]])."," "," **Example** — The authors give Fig 6.33 only. Inference: a qualified jumper's 16-week autumn phase under Variant B spends the first six or seven weeks on high-volume, moderate-intensity strength and jump work with performance tests dropping, then shifts to lower-volume technique and speed work, with test results expected to climb above their start by the end."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — After a concentrated, high-volume block: plan a stage of reduced volume measured in weeks, not a single deload week, and do not fill it with more volume. Speed and technique work rise during this stage in the authors' models ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]])."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The rule is about the period after large accumulated volumes, not routine week-to-week loading. Tension with another library source: Bompa's step-loading paradigm uses one unloading week after three loading weeks (3:1) as the standard wave ([[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]]). The two address different situations (a routine wave versus the aftermath of a concentrated block), so this is not recorded as a contradiction, but a 3:1 deload should not be taken as enough reduction for the LDTE in the authors' sense. The authors themselves report 3- and 4-week stages with a final restoration week as the most common practice and say \"all of them, depending on circumstances, can produce good results\" (PDF p. 385 / printed p. 374). Reduced volume is not reduced intensity: in the same chapter intensity rises during the LDTE stage.","+**When NOT to apply** — The rule is about the period after large accumulated volumes, not routine week-to-week loading. Tension with another library source: Bompa's step-loading paradigm uses one unloading week after three loading weeks (3:1) as the standard wave ([[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]]). The two address different situations (a routine wave versus the aftermath of a concentrated block), so this is not recorded as a contradiction, but a 3:1 deload should not be taken as enough reduction for the LDTE in the authors' sense. The authors themselves report 3- and 4-week stages with a final restoration week as the most common practice and say \"all of them, depending on circumstances, can produce good results\" (PDF p. 385 / printed p. 374). Reduced volume is not reduced intensity: speed work begins at the LDTE stage \"together with the necessary gradual rise in the training intensity\" (PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373)."," "," **Example** — The authors give no worked case on this page. Inference: after a six-week concentrated strength block, a jumper keeps strength volume low for the following four to six weeks while jump and sprint intensity rises, instead of returning to full volume after one easy week."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ac82ad8b-2755-49df-a833-91738df65c2d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
