{"parentUuid":"599bac3e-558f-40ae-a385-119dc145f5b8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"9ce4e1ff-e22e-47cc-b84a-20ebb6a9d3e4","agentId":"ae7f316477361ec0a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YUpwy23unaecrAubWrFi55","type":"tool_result","content":"heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md:2\nheuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md:2\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 132, 133]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"8f754e94-eba4-4493-8fca-b347d0442ca7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:33:02.580Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md:2\nheuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md:2\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 132, 133]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":8,"newStart":19,"newLines":8,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — After a concentrated, high-volume block: plan a stage of reduced volume measured in weeks, not a single deload week, and do not fill it with more volume. Speed and technique work rise during this stage in the authors' models ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]])."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The rule is about the period after large accumulated volumes, not routine week-to-week loading. Tension with another library source: Bompa's step-loading paradigm uses one unloading week after three loading weeks (3:1) as the standard wave ([[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]]). The two address different situations (a routine wave versus the aftermath of a concentrated block), so this is not recorded as a contradiction, but a 3:1 deload should not be taken as enough reduction for the LDTE in the authors' sense. The authors themselves report 3- and 4-week stages with a final restoration week as the most common practice and say \"all of them, depending on circumstances, can produce good results\" (PDF p. 385 / printed p. 374). Reduced volume is not reduced intensity: speed work begins at the LDTE stage \"together with the necessary gradual rise in the training intensity\" (PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373).","+**When NOT to apply** — The rule is about the period after large accumulated volumes, not routine week-to-week loading. Section 6.10 sets out the delayed effect and its timed volume cut ([[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]]); this 6.12.2 page adds the critique that the usual 1-2 week reduction is too short and that extra volume blocks restoration. Tension with another library source: Bompa's step-loading paradigm uses one unloading week after three loading weeks (3:1) as the standard wave ([[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]]). The two address different situations (a routine wave versus the aftermath of a concentrated block), so this is not recorded as a contradiction, but a 3:1 deload should not be taken as enough reduction for the LDTE in the authors' sense. The authors themselves report 3- and 4-week stages with a final restoration week as the most common practice and say \"all of them, depending on circumstances, can produce good results\" (PDF p. 385 / printed p. 374). Reduced volume is not reduced intensity: speed work begins at the LDTE stage \"together with the necessary gradual rise in the training intensity\" (PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373)."," "," **Example** — The authors give no worked case on this page. Inference: after a six-week concentrated strength block, a jumper keeps strength volume low for the following four to six weeks while jump and sprint intensity rises, instead of returning to full volume after one easy week."," ","-**Related** — [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]], [[heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]]","+**Related** — [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]], [[heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]]"]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":25,"oldLines":8,"newStart":25,"newLines":8,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Any off-season strength block for a speed-strength athlete: keep a daily technique slot, load it with drills, rhythm work and sub-maximal whole movements while the block is heavy, and move full-speed, competition-like technique to the weeks after the block. The 6.14.1 model states the same rule (technique \"is included at reduced intensity using the separate elements and their links,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383). Eric's baseline: \"The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next) is repeatedly described as the department's programming \"compass.\"\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 101, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: the authors' scheme does not wait for strength to be built before skill work starts; it runs low-intensity skill learning alongside the strength block, which is a different reading of \"foundation\" than a strict stepwise hierarchy."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Speed sports with a cyclic structure separate the two more sharply: \"Speed work is excluded totally from the concentrated strength loading stage,\" though intermuscular coordination work is allowed (PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373; see [[framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks]]). The rule concerns the authors' concentrated-block method for qualified athletes; it says nothing about technique learning in novices outside a block. The authors do not give a dose or a proportion of time for the technique slot. Inference: in team sports the sport coach, not the strength staff, usually owns technical practice, so the strength staff can control only the timing of their own heavy work relative to it.","+**When NOT to apply** — Speed sports with a cyclic structure separate the two more sharply: \"Speed work is excluded totally from the concentrated strength loading stage,\" though intermuscular coordination work is allowed (PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373; see [[framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks]]). The rule concerns the authors' concentrated-block method for qualified athletes; it says nothing about technique learning in novices outside a block. The authors do not give a dose or a proportion of time for the technique slot. Section 6.10 already places technical training in the delayed-effect window ([[heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window]]); the 6.12.2 pages add the learning-then-perfecting split, the daily freshness rule and the low-intensity means to use inside the block. Inference: in team sports the sport coach, not the strength staff, usually owns technical practice, so the strength staff can control only the timing of their own heavy work relative to it."," "," **Example** — The authors give Fig 6.32(a) as their illustration, not a named athlete. Inference: a long jumper in a six-week strength block does approach-rhythm runs and short-approach jumps at moderate effort three or four days a week, then after the block moves to full-approach jumps at competition speed as speed-strength rises."," ","-**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice]], [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities]], [[heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]","+**Related** — [[heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice]], [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities]], [[heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]"]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"599bac3e-558f-40ae-a385-119dc145f5b8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec